message.content:
{
    "short_answer": "The Man Who Laughs",
    "long_answer": "In Victor Hugo’s 1869 novel “The Man Who Laughs” (French: “L’Homme qui rit”), he introduces the Comprachicos, a band of child-traders who surgically mutilate and disfigure children—most notably giving the protagonist, Gwynplaine, a permanent grotesque grin. This portrayal of deliberate child deformity is the archetypal depiction of such practices in Hugo’s works and parallels any brief allusion to similar horrors elsewhere, including in The Hunchback of Notre-Dame."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paramyxoviridae",
    "long_answer": "Both human parainfluenza viruses (types 1–4) and the mumps virus are enveloped, negative-sense single-stranded RNA viruses classified in the family Paramyxoviridae. This family groups several genera—including Respirovirus (HPIV-1 and ‑3) and Orthorubulavirus (mumps virus and HPIV-2,-4)—based on shared genomic organization, replication strategy, and structural proteins."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAT1A and GNMT",
    "long_answer": "In isolated hypermethioninemia, elevated blood methionine is usually due to pathogenic variants in the MAT1A gene, which encodes methionine adenosyl-transferase, or in the GNMT gene, which encodes glycine N-methyltransferase. Both enzymes catalyze the first steps of methionine catabolism; when either gene is mutated, the corresponding enzyme activity drops, methionine is not efficiently broken down, and the amino acid accumulates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPG20",
    "long_answer": "Troyer syndrome (a complicated hereditary spastic paraplegia, classified as SPG20) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SPG20 gene, which encodes the protein spartin. Pathogenic variants in SPG20 lead to the characteristic combination of lower-limb spasticity, distal muscle weakness, dysarthria, short stature, and other features that define Troyer syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BLM gene",
    "long_answer": "Bloom syndrome is an autosomal-recessive genodermatosis that presents with multiple café-au-lait patches, photosensitive telangiectatic erythema and proportionate short stature. The disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the BLM gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase necessary for genomic stability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary Factor X deficiency results from mutations in the F10 gene, which encodes coagulation factor X. The F10 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 13q34 on human chromosome 13, so all known congenital Factor X deficiencies trace to this chromosomal location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Jarcho-Levin syndrome (classically the spondylothoracic/spondylocostal dysostosis spectrum) is most often caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in genes such as DLL3, MESP2, LFNG, or HES7. These pathogenic variants are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner, meaning an affected individual carries two mutated alleles—one from each carrier parent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT3 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the WNT3 gene disrupt the Wnt signaling pathway that triggers limb bud initiation during early embryogenesis. This leads to tetra-amelia syndrome, a congenital disorder marked by complete absence of all four limbs and severe malformations of facial, cranial, urogenital, pulmonary and other structures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The main genetic risk factor for progressive supranuclear palsy is the H1 haplotype/variant of the MAPT gene, which is located on chromosome 17q21. MAPT encodes the microtubule-associated protein tau; abnormal tau accumulation disrupts mitochondrial transport and dynamics, contributing to mitochondrial dysfunction observed in PSP and other tauopathies. Hence, chromosome 17 carries the variant linked to PSP and implicated in mitochondrial impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia",
    "long_answer": "Congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) is a genetic disorder in which infants lack the adrenal-cortical enzyme 21-hydroxylase (or, more rarely, another steroidogenic enzyme). This enzyme deficiency impairs cortisol and often aldosterone synthesis, causing adrenal crisis, salt-wasting, failure to thrive, and potentially life-threatening complications in newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 1 (an inherited disorder beginning with the letter “N”) is caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene. Molecular mapping and sequencing studies have established that NF1 resides on chromosome 17, band 17q11.2. Mutations at this locus disrupt production of neurofibromin and produce the characteristic features of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGCE",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SGCE gene (encoding ε-sarcoglycan, locus DYT11) are the established cause of the autosomal-dominant myoclonus-dystonia syndrome. A cardinal feature of this syndrome is dystonia—typically affecting the neck or upper body—accompanied by action myoclonus, and symptoms often improve with alcohol. Thus, SGCE mutation is a well-known genetic cause of a syndrome in which dystonia is a key symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Donald Young",
    "long_answer": "The constellation of obstructive azoospermia with chronic sinusitis and bronchiectasis was first recorded in a 1972 report by British urologist Dr. Donald Young, whose observations led to the eponym 'Young’s syndrome'."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nicolaus Michael Oppel",
    "long_answer": "The Martinican pit viper (Bothrops lanceolatus) belongs to the pit-viper subfamily Crotalinae. This subfamily was formally erected in 1811 by the German naturalist and herpetologist Nicolaus Michael Oppel, whose classification work first separated pit vipers (crotalines) from other vipers. Hence, Oppel is the scientist who established the subfamily that includes the Martinican pit viper."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary prion disease Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker syndrome (GSS) carries the eponym of Josef Gerstmann—the same neurologist whose name designates Gerstmann syndrome, a tetrad (agraphia, acalculia, finger agnosia, left-right disorientation) produced by a lesion in the dominant angular gyrus of the parietal lobe. Thus the prion disease and the angular-gyrus lesion syndrome share the name 'Gerstmann.'"
}

{
    "short_answer": "COMP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene are responsible for pseudoachondroplasia, a skeletal dysplasia that presents with disproportionate short stature, deformities of the hips and knees, and marked ligamentous laxity. Defective COMP disrupts cartilage extracellular matrix organization, leading to abnormal endochondral bone growth and the characteristic clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Androgen receptor (AR) gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked androgen receptor (AR) gene cause Testicular Feminization Syndrome, also called complete androgen insensitivity. This disorder is listed under the letter “T” and affects individuals with an XY karyotype, so it primarily manifests in genetic males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHD finger domain",
    "long_answer": "Several chromatin-regulating genes on the X chromosome that cause X-linked intellectual disability—such as PHF6 (Borjeson-Forssman-Lehmann syndrome), PHF8 (Siderius type XLID) and KDM5C/JARID1C—encode proteins containing a Plant Homeodomain (PHD) finger. Recurring pathogenic variants in this zinc-binding PHD finger domain highlight it as a characteristic structural motif associated with genes involved in X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methionine synthase",
    "long_answer": "Methionine synthase (also called 5-methyltetrahydrofolate-homocysteine methyltransferase) catalyzes the remethylation of homocysteine to regenerate methionine, using 5-methyltetrahydrofolate as the methyl donor and vitamin B₁₂ as a cofactor. Impairment of this enzyme’s activity prevents efficient conversion of homocysteine, leading to its elevation in blood (hyperhomocysteinemia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "APTX",
    "long_answer": "Ataxia with oculomotor apraxia type 1 (AOA1) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the APTX gene, which encodes the DNA-repair enzyme aprataxin. Aprataxin processes single-strand DNA breaks by hydrolysing 5′-adenylated ends, thereby removing obstructive termini and restoring ligatable 5′-phosphate groups. Defects in this end-cleaning step impair DNA strand-break repair in neurons, leading to the characteristic cerebellar ataxia and oculomotor apraxia seen in AOA1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Progressive pseudorheumatoid dysplasia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WISP3 (CCN6) gene, which maps cytogenetically to 6q22–q23 on human chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OFD1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OFD1 gene, which encodes a centrosomal–basal body protein essential for primary cilium formation, produce Oro-facio-digital syndrome type 1. This ciliopathy is classically defined by malformations of the face (hypertelorism, wide nasal bridge), the oral cavity (cleft palate, lobulated tongue), and the digits (polydactyly, brachydactyly), establishing OFD1 mutation as the causal genetic defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GDI1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-disability disorder. Molecular studies have shown that it is caused by pathogenic mutations in the GDI1 gene, which encodes Rab GDP-dissociation inhibitor-α. Loss-of-function GDI1 variants disrupt Rab-mediated vesicle trafficking in neurons and are consistently associated with the syndrome’s core features, including severe language delay."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OFD1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked OFD1 gene cause oral-facial-digital syndrome type I, a disorder whose core features include congenital heart malformations, tongue (lingual) hamartomas, and digital anomalies such as postaxial polydactyly. Loss-of-function variants in OFD1 disrupt ciliary function during development, producing the characteristic combination of oral, facial, cardiac, and limb defects seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TJP2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TJP2 gene, which encodes tight junction protein 2 (ZO-2) that organizes epithelial and endothelial intercellular junctions, are a recognized cause of progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis (PFIC type 4)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Idiopathic pulmonary hemosiderosis presents with recurrent alveolar‐capillary bleeding and intra-alveolar iron deposition that impair oxygen transport. The cornerstone of management is prolonged systemic corticosteroid therapy (e.g., oral prednisone), which suppresses pulmonary inflammation, reduces hemorrhage, and improves gas exchange."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zinc metalloprotease",
    "long_answer": "Carnosinemia results from deficiency of the enzyme carnosinase (CNDP1-encoded), whose normal three-dimensional fold is that of a zinc-dependent metallo-exopeptidase. The protein’s active site contains a catalytic Zn2+ ion characteristic of zinc metalloproteases; loss or absence of this metalloprotease leads to accumulation of carnosine in blood and urine, producing carnosinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator) gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 (7q31.2). There is no recognized linkage of the primary cystic-fibrosis-causing gene to chromosome 8. Therefore, the statement that the gene primarily associated with cystic fibrosis is located on chromosome 8 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC6A20",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SLC6A20 gene, which encodes the brush-border Na⁺/Cl⁻-dependent imino-acid transporter (IMINO) of the renal proximal tubule, cause iminoglycinuria. This transporter is chiefly responsible for reabsorbing glycine, proline and hydroxyproline; when SLC6A20 is mutated, these amino acids are not reclaimed efficiently and are excreted in the urine, producing the characteristic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LHCGR mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LHCGR (luteinizing hormone/chorionic-gonadotropin receptor) gene abolish receptor signaling, making the gonads unresponsive to luteinizing hormone. This LH insensitivity produces Leydig cell hypoplasia, a disorder of sex development characterized by undervirilization of 46,XY individuals and absent pubertal testosterone rise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LRP5",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the LRP5 gene cause osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome, a disorder marked by congenital or very early-onset blindness due to retinal dysgenesis and by severe juvenile osteoporosis with frequent fractures. Loss of LRP5 impairs Wnt signaling that is critical for retinal vascular development and bone mass acquisition, explaining the combination of visual loss and pronounced skeletal fragility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PEST sequence",
    "long_answer": "Hajdu–Cheney syndrome is caused by truncating mutations in exon 34 of the NOTCH2 gene that delete the protein’s C-terminal PEST sequence—a proline-, glutamic acid-, serine- and threonine-rich degradation motif. Loss of this PEST domain prevents normal ubiquitin-mediated turnover of NOTCH2, prolonging its signaling activity and producing the clinical features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hepatocytes",
    "long_answer": "The F5 gene encodes coagulation Factor V, a plasma glycoprotein essential for thrombin generation. This protein is synthesized mainly by hepatocytes—the parenchymal cells of the liver—before being secreted into the bloodstream where it participates in the coagulation cascade."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Family studies of polycystic ovary syndrome show vertical transmission of the trait through successive generations, affecting both female and male relatives, consistent with an autosomal dominant mode of inheritance with variable penetrance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "F2",
    "long_answer": "The F2 gene encodes coagulation factor II, commonly called prothrombin. During the blood-clotting cascade, prothrombin is proteolytically cleaved by the prothrombinase complex to produce the active serine protease thrombin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAK1",
    "long_answer": "The NF2 gene encodes the tumor-suppressor protein merlin, which normally binds to and inhibits the serine/threonine kinase p21-activated kinase 1 (PAK1) within the Rac/Cdc42 signaling pathway. Loss-of-function mutations in NF2 abolish merlin’s inhibitory effect, allowing PAK1 to become constitutively active and drive aberrant proliferation and survival of Schwann and meningeal cells characteristic of Neurofibromatosis type II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Primary cilium",
    "long_answer": "Meckel-Gruber syndrome is classified as a ciliopathy. Mutations in genes such as MKS1, TMEM67, and CEP290 compromise assembly and function of the primary cilium (the microtubule-based organelle projecting from most vertebrate cells). Loss of proper ciliary structure impairs critical protein signaling pathways during embryogenesis, producing the characteristic malformations of Meckel-Gruber syndrome. Thus, the organelle whose malfunction in protein-related assembly and trafficking leads directly to the disease is the primary cilium."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB11",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ABCB11 gene, which encodes the bile salt export pump (BSEP), cause progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 2 (PFIC2). BSEP deficiency leads to severe cholestasis, rapid progression to cirrhosis, and a markedly increased risk of hepatocellular carcinoma that can present in infancy or early childhood."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alois Alzheimer",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome characterized by progressive memory impairment and global cognitive decline was first described in 1906 by the German neurologist Alois Alzheimer, who reported the clinical course and post-mortem neuropathology (amyloid plaques and neurofibrillary tangles) of his patient Auguste Deter; this condition was later named Alzheimer’s disease in his honor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRT81, KRT83, and KRT86",
    "long_answer": "The hair disease characterized by beaded (moniliform) hair shafts and patchy alopecia is monilethrix. It is classically caused by missense mutations in the type II hair-keratin genes KRT81 (hHb1), KRT83 (hHb3), and KRT86 (hHb6). Defects in these keratin proteins weaken the hair cortex, leading to the periodic constrictions that give hair a beaded appearance and result in fragility and alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RAI1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or 17p11.2 deletions that disrupt the RAI1 gene cause Smith–Magenis syndrome, a disorder whose core features include moderate intellectual disability, early-onset obesity, and characteristic dysmorphic facial appearance (broad square face, brachycephaly, midface retrusion). Haploinsufficiency of RAI1 is therefore the established molecular basis of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-Hexosaminidase A",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A causes Tay-Sachs disease. In this GM2 gangliosidosis, storage of ganglioside within neurons leads to progressive neurodegeneration, hypotonia, a ‘cherry-red’ macular spot, and characteristically enlarged head size (macrocephaly/megalencephaly) in affected infants. Thus macrocephaly is a recognised clinical feature directly linked to β-hexosaminidase A deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IFT57",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the intraflagellar transport gene IFT57 (also known as C19orf56/HIPPI) cause cranio-ectodermal dysplasia, the clinical entity originally described as Sensenbrenner syndrome. The IFT57 locus is situated on chromosome 19 (19q13), which is the same chromosome that contains the primary microcephaly gene WDR62. Thus, IFT57 is the Sensenbrenner-associated gene located on the same chromosome as WDR62."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Galloway–Mowat syndrome (GAMOS) is a genetically heterogeneous disorder. The X-linked form of the disease, designated GAMOS2, is caused by pathogenic variants in the LAGE3 gene (L antigen family member 3) located on chromosome Xp11.23. LAGE3 encodes one of the members of the LAGE (L-antigen) protein family and functions as a subunit of the KEOPS complex involved in tRNA modification. Because the disease-causing gene itself is LAGE3, it is, by definition, a member of the LAGE3/L-antigen gene family. Therefore, the gene associated with X-linked Galloway–Mowat syndrome does indeed belong to the LAGE3 family."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease results from an expanded CAG-repeat mutation in the HTT (huntingtin) gene. This gene, which directs synthesis of the huntingtin protein, is located at cytogenetic band 4p16.3 on chromosome 4; the pathogenic expansion here leads to the neurodegenerative features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOST",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SOST gene, which encodes the Wnt-signalling inhibitor sclerostin, cause sclerosteosis. This high-bone-mass disorder presents with marked cranial sclerosis and consequent macrocephaly due to excessive bone formation in the skull."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELN gene deletion",
    "long_answer": "Williams–Beuren syndrome is caused by a hemizygous micro-deletion at chromosome 7q11.23 that removes the ELN (elastin) gene. Loss of elastin disrupts arterial wall integrity and classically produces supravalvular aortic stenosis, making the ELN gene deletion the genetic mutation associated with this aortic-stenosis-containing condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene disrupt melanocyte development. This causes Waardenburg syndrome type 2/Tietz spectrum, characterised clinically by patchy areas of hypo- and hyperpigmented skin, ocular anomalies such as microcornea, and severe congenital sensorineural hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZIC3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked gene ZIC3 are a well-established cause of heterotaxy (situs ambiguus). In carrier mothers, each pregnancy has a 50 % chance of transmitting the mutation, and affected fetuses frequently develop right-isomerism with congenital heart disease and absence or severe hypoplasia of the spleen (asplenia). Thus, a ZIC3 mutation confers the noted 50 % risk of a condition that often results in asplenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SERPINC1",
    "long_answer": "Antithrombin (historically called antithrombin III) is a serine-protease inhibitor that neutralizes thrombin and several other coagulation factors. The protein is encoded by the SERPINC1 gene (Serpin Peptidase Inhibitor, Clade C, Member 1), located on chromosome 1q23-q25. SERPINC1 is the official HGNC gene symbol recognized in human genetics and clinical diagnostics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PCNT (pericentrin)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PCNT gene, which encodes the centrosomal protein pericentrin, cause microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II (a form of primordial dwarfism). Affected fetuses show profound intra-uterine growth restriction and remain extremely small throughout life, making this PCNT mutation a well-established genetic cause of dwarfism characterized by unusually small size even before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPN1LW gene",
    "long_answer": "Red–green colour vision deficiency (protanopia/protanomaly) is most commonly produced by mutations, deletions or unequal recombination in the X-linked OPN1LW gene, which encodes the long-wavelength-sensitive (red) cone opsin. Loss or alteration of this opsin impairs discrimination between red and green light, giving rise to the characteristic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Toxicodendron",
    "long_answer": "Species in the genus Toxicodendron—such as poison ivy, poison oak, and poison sumac—contain the oleoresin urushiol. Upon skin contact, urushiol acts as a potent sensitizer, triggering a type IV delayed-hypersensitivity reaction that manifests as allergic contact dermatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cystathionine β-synthase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Classic homocystinuria most commonly results from deficiency of the enzyme cystathionine β-synthase (CBS). CBS normally converts homocysteine plus serine into cystathionine, leading ultimately to the production of cysteine—the sulfur-containing amino acid capable of forming disulfide bonds. When CBS is absent or defective, homocysteine accumulates and cysteine levels fall, linking the disorder to the amino acid known for disulfide bond formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Corneal-cerebellar syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Corneal-cerebellar syndrome is classically defined by the triad of bilateral corneal dystrophy, progressive cerebellar ataxia, and intellectual disability (mental retardation). Reports of affected sibships describe normal early milestones followed by cognitive delay and permanent intellectual impairment. In contrast, spastic ataxia-corneal dystrophy syndrome is characterized chiefly by spastic paraplegia, ataxia, and corneal dystrophy, with cognitive function generally preserved. Therefore, the syndrome that causes intellectual disability is corneal-cerebellar syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTRK1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the NTRK1 gene, which encodes the high-affinity nerve growth factor receptor TrkA, cause congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis (HSAN type IV). Because TrkA signaling is essential for the development and survival of nociceptive and thermosensitive neurons, NTRK1 mutations lead to a complete inability to perceive pain and temperature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FN1",
    "long_answer": "Glomerulopathy with fibronectin deposits (fibronectin glomerulopathy) is an autosomal-dominant renal disease in which glomeruli are markedly enlarged owing to massive deposits located in the mesangium and along the subendothelial aspect of the capillary walls. Pathogenic heterozygous mutations in the FN1 gene, which encodes fibronectin 1, have been repeatedly identified in affected families, establishing FN1 as the causative gene for this rare form of glomerulopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MCOLN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MCOLN1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal TRPML1 non-selective cation channel, cause mucolipidosis type IV—a well-characterized lysosomal storage disorder. Loss of TRPML1 channel activity disrupts lysosomal ion homeostasis and trafficking, leading to the accumulation of undigested macromolecules characteristic of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL1A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL1A1 gene, which encodes the pro-α1 chain of type I collagen, disrupt normal collagen synthesis and structure. Defective type I collagen weakens the bone matrix, leading to osteogenesis imperfecta—a disorder marked by fragile bones and a markedly increased propensity for fractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked BCOR (BCL6 corepressor) gene cause Lenz microphthalmia syndrome. This disorder is classically defined by severe eye defects such as microphthalmia or anophthalmia and by skeletal malformations affecting the digits and limbs, establishing BCOR as the gene responsible for this eye-and-limb anomaly syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent genetic alteration in meningiomas is mutation or loss of the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene (located on chromosome 22q), whose product merlin is expressed in the meningeal (dura-mater–lining) cells from which these tumors arise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Tricho-rhino-phalangeal syndrome type 1 is caused by pathogenic variants in the TRPS1 gene. Cytogenetic mapping places TRPS1 at 8q24 (approximately 8q23.3-q24.1), so the responsible gene resides on human chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIT D816V mutation",
    "long_answer": "More than 90 % of adults with systemic mastocytosis carry the activating point mutation D816V in the KIT proto-oncogene (c-KIT). This Asp→Val substitution in exon 17 constitutively activates the KIT tyrosine-kinase receptor, driving clonal mast-cell proliferation and the diverse systemic symptoms, including mediator-related manifestations such as myalgia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIS1 (PAFAH1B1)",
    "long_answer": "A smooth cerebral cortex (lissencephaly) is classically caused by mutations or deletions in the LIS1 gene, formally known as PAFAH1B1, located on chromosome 17p13.3; loss of LIS1 disrupts neuronal migration during cortical development, producing the characteristic ‘smooth brain’ appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FANCA",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic loss-of-function mutations in the FANCA gene cause Fanconi anaemia, a hereditary bone-marrow failure syndrome. Defective FANCA impairs the Fanconi DNA-repair pathway, leading to progressive pancytopenia: thrombocytopenia results in easy bruising, while erythrocyte deficiency produces anaemia and fatigue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "THRB gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Elevated circulating T3 and T4 with unsuppressed or even elevated TSH characterizes thyroid hormone resistance. Over 80 % of patients have heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the THRB gene, which encodes the β-isoform of the thyroid hormone receptor. The mutant receptor impairs negative feedback at the pituitary and hypothalamus, so TSH secretion is not suppressed despite high thyroid hormone levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria (PKU) is caused by mutations in the phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 12 (region 12q23.2). Defective PAH activity on this chromosome leads to elevated phenylalanine levels characteristic of PKU."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NFKB2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the NFKB2 gene, which encodes the p100/p52 subunit of the non-canonical NF-κB pathway, produce the disorder known as DAVID syndrome. Patients present with adrenocorticotropic hormone (ACTH)–dependent adrenal insufficiency together with common variable immunodeficiency (CVID), reflecting impaired pituitary ACTH production and defective B-cell maturation due to disrupted NF-κB signaling."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNJ11",
    "long_answer": "Activating mutations in the KCNJ11 gene, which encodes the Kir6.2 subunit of the pancreatic ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channel, cause permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus. Sulfonylurea drugs close the over-active KATP channels, restoring insulin secretion, so patients with KCNJ11-related diabetes can often switch from insulin injections to oral sulfonylurea therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHF6",
    "long_answer": "Börjeson-Forssman-Lehmann syndrome is an X-linked disorder produced by pathogenic variants in the PHF6 gene. Affected males typically present with moderate-to-severe intellectual disability, progressive (often truncal) obesity, and hypogonadism that leads to gynecomastia, along with characteristic facial features. Thus, mutation of PHF6 is the recognized genetic cause of the syndrome characterized by intellectual disability, obesity, and gynecomastia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANTXR2",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile hyaline fibromatosis and the related condition infantile systemic hyalinosis are allelic, autosomal-recessive disorders marked by multiple skin and gingival nodules, hyaline deposition in tissues, osteolytic bone lesions, and progressive joint contractures. Both diseases are produced by loss-of-function mutations in the ANTXR2 gene (also known as CMG2), which encodes the anthrax toxin receptor 2 involved in extracellular-matrix homeostasis." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phagolysosome formation",
    "long_answer": "In Chédiak–Higashi syndrome, a defect in the LYST gene disrupts microtubule-dependent trafficking of lysosomal granules. Because lysosomes cannot effectively fuse with phagosomes, phagolysosome formation is impaired, preventing release of degradative enzymes and thereby blocking the dissolution and killing of ingested bacteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SH3BP2",
    "long_answer": "Cherubism—the disorder named for the cherubic, chubby-cheeked faces of Renaissance putti—is caused by gain-of-function mutations in the SH3BP2 gene on chromosome 4p16.3. Mutant SH3BP2 hyper-activates osteoclast signaling, producing bilateral fibro-osseous expansion of the mandible and maxilla that leads to the characteristic facial disfigurement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Marinesco–Sjögren syndrome is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SIL1 gene, which resides on the long arm of human chromosome 5 (cytogenetic location 5q31.2). Therefore, the gene associated with this syndrome is located on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Thanatophoric dysplasia, a lethal skeletal dysplasia marked by extremely short limbs, redundant skin folds, narrow thorax, and often a clover-leaf skull, is caused by specific activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3) gene. These gain-of-function FGFR3 mutations disrupt endochondral bone growth, producing the characteristic severe phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thomas H. Weller",
    "long_answer": "Thomas H. Weller, who shared the 1954 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine, successfully isolated the varicella-zoster virus—the agent of shingles (and chickenpox)—in cell culture in the early 1950s, providing material critical for later diagnostic and vaccine developments."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OFD1",
    "long_answer": "Multiple hamartomas of the tongue are characteristic of Oral-Facial-Digital syndrome type 1, an X-linked dominant condition. Pathogenic variants in the OFD1 gene (also called CXORF5) on chromosome Xp22 cause this syndrome; therefore, mutation of OFD1 is the genetic defect associated with tongue hamartomas in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ASAH1",
    "long_answer": "Farber disease (Farber lipogranulomatosis) is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the ASAH1 gene, which encodes acid ceramidase. The same ASAH1 mutations can also lead to spinal muscular atrophy with progressive myoclonic epilepsy (SMA-PME), a disorder characterized by myoclonic seizures. Thus, ASAH1 is the gene linked to both Farber disease and a seizure-associated condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin A deficiency",
    "long_answer": "All-trans-retinoic acid (ATRA) is a pharmacologic form of vitamin A that binds and activates the retinoic acid receptor-α (RARA). ATRA is used both to replace vitamin A in states of deficiency and, at higher doses, to treat acute promyelocytic leukemia by targeting the PML-RARA fusion protein. Therefore, the vitamin deficiency treated with the same agent that targets RARA is vitamin A deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Androgen receptor (AR) gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the androgen receptor (AR) gene cause androgen-insensitivity syndrome. Because defective AR signaling prevents normal response to fetal and pubertal androgens, affected 46,XY individuals often have incompletely virilized external genitalia and testes that fail to descend, leading to cryptorchidism (undescended testicles)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLG (filaggrin)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FLG gene, which encodes the epidermal protein filaggrin, disrupt skin barrier formation. Loss-of-function FLG variants lead to ichthyosis vulgaris, a condition marked by scaly, thick, and excessively dry skin due to impaired keratin aggregation and reduced natural moisturizing factors."
}

{
    "short_answer": "East Asia",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiological studies consistently show that Restless Legs Syndrome affects only about 1–2 % of adults in East Asian countries, whereas U.S. population–based surveys report prevalences of roughly 5–10 %. Thus, East Asia has the markedly lower prevalence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "THAP1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the THAP1 gene (designated DYT6 dystonia) frequently produce an adult-onset cranio-cervical dystonia in which involuntary eyelid closure—blepharospasm—is one of the characteristic manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MTTP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MTTP (microsomal triglyceride transfer protein) gene cause abetalipoproteinemia. In this disorder the absence of apo-B–containing lipoproteins alters red-cell membrane lipid composition, producing the characteristic spiculated red blood cells known as acanthocytes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Duplications involving the cytogenetic band Xp11.2 occur on the short arm of the X chromosome and are a recognized cause of certain forms of X-linked intellectual disability (XLID). These rearrangements affect genes located on the X chromosome (e.g., IL1RAPL1, NDP, PHF8, etc.). The Y chromosome is not involved in these duplications: it neither carries homologous sequence at Xp11.2 nor participates in the pathogenic copy-number gains underlying XLID. Therefore, the Y chromosome is not associated with duplications at Xp11.2 that cause X-linked intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MPLKIP",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive, non-photosensitive trichothiodystrophy (TTD) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the MPLKIP gene, also known as TTDN1/C7orf11. Defects in MPLKIP disrupt its interaction with the mitotic kinase PLK1, leading to the brittle sulfur-deficient hair and developmental abnormalities characteristic of this non-photosensitive TTD subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene (type II collagen) cause Stickler syndrome type 1. Stickler syndrome is characterised by severe ocular problems, including high myopia and a high lifetime risk of retinal detachment that can lead to permanent vision loss, and it frequently presents with cranio-facial anomalies such as cleft palate or complete cleft lip-palate (often as part of the Pierre-Robin sequence). Thus, a COL2A1 mutation links the potential for vision-threatening eye disease with cleft lip and palate formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "University of Exeter",
    "long_answer": "The gene underlying Alström syndrome, ALMS1, was mapped and cloned through a collaborative effort between investigators at The Jackson Laboratory (Bar Harbor, Maine) and scientists at the University of Exeter in the United Kingdom. This partnership successfully isolated ALMS1, establishing its mutations as the cause of the multisystem disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "G6PD",
    "long_answer": "The G6PD gene encodes glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase; mutations in this gene cause G6PD deficiency, the most common enzymatic red-blood-cell disorder worldwide. Thousands of PubMed citations describe the molecular genetics, biochemical mechanisms, and clinical consequences of G6PD deficiency, making G6PD the well-established gene responsible for this deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ciba-Geigy",
    "long_answer": "Ritalin lung is a form of pulmonary talcosis and emphysema that arose in people who crushed and injected Ritalin tablets. At the time these cases were reported (1970s-1980s), Ritalin (methylphenidate) was produced and marketed by the pharmaceutical company Ciba-Geigy; thus Ciba-Geigy was the manufacturer of the drug linked to this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PCNT",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PCNT gene, which encodes the centrosomal protein pericentrin, cause microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II—a form of primordial dwarfism in which affected individuals are profoundly, proportionately small, leading to the popular nickname 'Thumbelina' stature. Defective pericentrin impairs centrosome organization and mitotic spindle formation, reducing overall cell proliferation and thus body size."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN5",
    "long_answer": "Dent disease, an X-linked proximal tubulopathy that produces the full biochemical picture of Fanconi syndrome, is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the CLCN5 gene encoding the renal chloride/proton exchanger ClC-5. The resulting generalized proximal-tubular reabsorptive defect—glycosuria, phosphaturia, amino-aciduria, bicarbonate loss, etc.—is why Dent disease is regarded as an inherited cause of Fanconi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UBE3A",
    "long_answer": "Angelman syndrome is most commonly produced by loss-of-function of the maternally inherited UBE3A gene on chromosome 15q11-q13. A cardinal manifestation of this disorder is epilepsy; seizures are typically treated with anti-seizure drugs such as carbamazepine, phenobarbital and clobazam, making UBE3A mutation the genetic cause underlying the syndrome managed by these medications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lung",
    "long_answer": "Renal cell carcinoma spreads hematogenously, and the pulmonary vasculature is usually the first capillary bed encountered after the renal veins drain into the inferior vena cava. Consequently, the lungs are the most frequent site of kidney-cancer metastases, observed in roughly 50–60 % of patients with secondary spread."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SHH",
    "long_answer": "Mutations or dosage changes in the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) gene are well-known to produce a spectrum of abnormalities in early embryogenesis. Loss-of-function variants cause holoprosencephaly by impairing midline fore-brain cleavage, whereas gain or ectopic expression can lead to pre-axial polydactyly. Because SHH also directs early eye-field separation, the same mutations frequently result in ocular defects such as coloboma or microphthalmia. Thus SHH is the gene whose disruption underlies the syndrome combining holoprosencephaly, polydactyly and eye involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis",
    "long_answer": "Familial adenomatous polyposis (FAP) is an autosomal-dominant syndrome caused by pathogenic variants in the APC gene that produces hundreds of adenomatous colonic polyps beginning in childhood. Children with FAP have a markedly elevated risk of developing hepatoblastoma, a primary malignant liver tumour that arises disproportionately in the right hepatic lobe. Thus, the early-onset colonic polyp syndrome that is classically linked to a liver cancer favoring the right lobe is FAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Thalidomide exposure in utero can cause phocomelia, an extreme limb-reduction defect. The inherited (genetic) form of this same limb malformation is Roberts syndrome, also called pseudothalidomide syndrome. Roberts syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the ESCO2 gene, which is located on chromosome 8p21.1. Thus, chromosome 8 is the chromosomal locus linked to the genetic form of the condition that thalidomide can produce teratogenically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "Several leukemia-predisposition genes, most notably RUNX1 (also called AML1), reside on human chromosome 21. Germ-line mutations in RUNX1 or having an extra copy of chromosome 21, as occurs in Down syndrome (trisomy 21), markedly elevate the likelihood of developing acute myeloid leukemia, demonstrating that chromosome 21 carries genes whose alteration increases AML risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The disease originally described by Jacques Jean Lhermitte (Lhermitte–Duclos disease) is linked to germ-line mutations in the PTEN tumor-suppressor gene, which is located on chromosome 10q23. Loss-of-function PTEN mutations underlie the cerebellar dysplastic gangliocytoma characteristic of this condition and its association with Cowden syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Androgen Insensitivity Syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the androgen receptor (AR) gene located on the X chromosome (locus Xq11-q12). Because males have only one X chromosome, a single mutated AR allele causes the disease in 46,XY individuals, while heterozygous 46,XX females are typically asymptomatic carriers. This pattern defines the disorder as X-linked recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parapoxvirus",
    "long_answer": "Pseudocowpox is caused by the Pseudocowpox virus, which is classified within the genus Parapoxvirus of the Poxviridae family. Parapoxviruses are large, enveloped DNA viruses that infect ruminants and can produce zoonotic dermal lesions in humans, fitting the established taxonomy for pseudocowpox."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcolemma",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondrial myopathies are characterized by abnormal aggregates of mitochondria that collect just beneath the plasma membrane of muscle fibres, producing the classic \"ragged-red\" appearance on histological stains. The plasma membrane of a muscle cell is called the sarcolemma, making it the membrane structure directly involved in these diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHN1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic gain-of-function variants in the CHN1 gene, which encodes the Rac-GTPase–activating protein α2-chimerin, are a well-established cause of Duane retraction syndrome (a congenital cranial dysinnervation disorder). A hallmark of this disorder is impaired abduction—an inability of the eye to move outward—due to aberrant innervation of the lateral rectus muscle. Multiple independent CHN1 missense variants have been shown to hyperactivate α2-chimerin, disrupt normal oculomotor axon guidance, and produce the characteristic congenital limitation of outward eye movement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endocrine system",
    "long_answer": "In multisystem Langerhans cell histiocytosis, infiltration of the hypothalamic-pituitary axis—part of the endocrine system—damages the posterior pituitary, reduces antidiuretic hormone secretion, and frequently produces central diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TSEN54",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in TSEN54 cause several subtypes of pontocerebellar hypoplasia (most notably PCH type 2). TSEN54 encodes the 54-kDa subunit of the heterotetrameric tRNA-splicing endonuclease complex, the enzyme that cleaves introns from precursor tRNAs during their maturation. Thus, TSEN54 links pontocerebellar hypoplasia to defective tRNA splicing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANTXR1",
    "long_answer": "GAPO syndrome (Growth Retardation, Alopecia, Pseudoanodontia, Optic Atrophy) is produced by homozygous or compound-heterozygous loss-of-function mutations—including whole-gene deletions—in the ANTXR1 gene, which encodes anthrax toxin receptor 1/TEM8. Absence of functional ANTXR1 disrupts extracellular-matrix homeostasis, leading to the characteristic growth failure, sparse hair, delayed/absent teeth, and progressive optic atrophy seen in this rare disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC39A4",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-recessive disorder acrodermatitis enteropathica, characterized by impaired zinc absorption through the intestinal epithelium and resulting skin lesions, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC39A4 gene (ZIP4) located on chromosome 8q24.3. SLC39A4 encodes a zinc transporter critical for zinc uptake; its mutation disrupts systemic zinc homeostasis, leading to the disease phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene disrupt signaling between glial cell-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF) and the ureteric bud, preventing its outgrowth from the Wolffian duct. Without this RET-mediated branching, the metanephric mesenchyme is never induced, leading to bilateral or unilateral renal agenesis; RET-knockout mice and human patients with inactivating RET variants consistently show absent kidneys."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Rett syndrome is an X-linked dominant neurodevelopmental disorder caused by pathogenic mutations in the MECP2 gene on the X chromosome. Because males have only one X chromosome, MECP2 loss-of-function is usually incompatible with life, and affected male fetuses are frequently miscarried or stillborn. Thus, MECP2 mutation is the genetic defect associated with a condition that often results in stillbirth in males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IRF6 gene cause Van der Woude syndrome, an autosomal-dominant disorder whose hallmark features include cleft lip and/or cleft palate (often together with lower-lip pits). IRF6 encodes Interferon Regulatory Factor 6, a transcription factor crucial for orofacial development; loss-of-function mutations disrupt palatal fusion, producing the cleft palate phenotype observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Short-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the ACADS gene, which is located on chromosome 12 (cytogenetic band 12q24.31)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "inv(14)(q11;q32)",
    "long_answer": "T-cell prolymphocytic leukemia, a malignancy of mature T-cells that often presents with skin infiltrates, shows the chromosomal inversion inv(14)(q11;q32) (or the equivalent t(14;14)) in the majority of cases. This rearrangement places the TCL1 gene at 14q32 under the control of the T-cell receptor α/δ locus at 14q11, driving leukemic proliferation, making inv(14)(q11;q32) the most frequent cytogenetic abnormality associated with this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type I results from mutations in the IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase. The IDUA gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 4 (region 4p16.3); thus chromosome 4 carries the gene responsible for this lysosomal storage disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBC1D24",
    "long_answer": "The constellation of autosomal-recessive findings termed DOORS syndrome (Deafness, Onychodystrophy, Osteodystrophy, and mental Retardation/Seizures) is most commonly caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TBC1D24 gene. TBC1D24 encodes a protein that regulates synaptic vesicle trafficking; its disruption produces the characteristic sensorineural deafness, nail and skeletal dysplasia, and intellectual disability seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sucrose",
    "long_answer": "Dental-plaque bacteria such as Streptococcus mutans ferment dietary sugars to lactic acid, lowering pH and demineralising enamel. Among common sugars, sucrose is the most cariogenic because it is rapidly fermented and uniquely serves as a substrate for bacterial glucosyltransferases that form sticky extracellular glucans, intensifying plaque retention and acid attack. Therefore, sucrose is the primary sugar responsible for acid production leading to tooth decay."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Cartilage–hair hypoplasia is caused by pathogenic variants in the RMRP gene (RNA component of mitochondrial RNA-processing endoribonuclease). Cytogenetic mapping places RMRP on the short arm of human chromosome 9 (region 9p13–9p21), so chromosome 9 carries the disease-associated gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The described features match Waardenburg syndrome, which is most commonly caused by mutations in the PAX3 gene located on chromosome 2q35. PAX3 defects disrupt neural-crest-derived melanocyte development, producing large hypopigmented skin patches and congenital sensorineural deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCS1L",
    "long_answer": "Björnstad syndrome, a congenital disorder defined by pili torti (twisted, fragile hair) and sensorineural deafness, is caused by pathogenic mutations in the BCS1L gene, which encodes a mitochondrial inner-membrane protein required for assembly of respiratory-chain complex III."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIPBL mutation",
    "long_answer": "Cornelia de Lange syndrome, which is most often produced by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the NIPBL gene, frequently shows generalized hypertrichosis and several reports describe striking elbow hair in mildly affected patients. Because of this overlap, some authors have proposed that hypertrichosis cubiti (\"hairy-elbow\" syndrome) may represent a very mild or related manifestation of Cornelia de Lange syndrome. Hence, the genetic alteration implicated in the speculated link is mutation of NIPBL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR mutation",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypercholesterolaemia is most often caused by mutations in the LDLR gene on chromosome 19. Loss-of-function changes in this gene impair hepatic clearance of LDL cholesterol, producing marked hyperlipidaemia. Hyperlipidaemia is also a classic metabolic complication seen in patients with nephrotic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(1;16)(p31;p13)",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Martin–Dodd syndrome is the pattern of anomalies produced by a partial duplication of 16p (16p13 → pter).  The duplication arises from an unbalanced reciprocal translocation between chromosomes 1 and 16, classically written t(1;16)(p31;p13).  The resulting partial trisomy 16p manifests with multiple congenital defects, one of the most consistent being Tetralogy of Fallot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A4 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pendred syndrome is produced by pathogenic mutations in the SLC26A4 gene (encoding the iodide-chloride transporter pendrin). The syndrome combines congenital sensorineural hearing loss with thyroid enlargement; the auditory deficit is typically managed successfully with conventional or bone-conduction hearing aids, making the condition one of the few genetically defined deafness syndromes that can be effectively treated by amplification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Wilson's disease is caused by pathogenic variants in the ATP7B gene, a copper-transporting P-type ATPase. The ATP7B locus is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 13 (region 13q14.3). Mutations here disrupt hepatic copper excretion, leading to the characteristic copper accumulation of Wilson's disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMCHD1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SMCHD1 gene (Structural Maintenance of Chromosomes Flexible Hinge Domain-Containing 1) are the established cause of Bosma arhinia–microphthalmia syndrome. This syndrome presents with microphthalmia or anophthalmia together with marked nasal malformations such as arhinia or severe nasal hypoplasia, making SMCHD1 mutation the primary genetic basis for syndromic microphthalmia that includes nasal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The HEXA gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A, is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 15 (region 15q23-q24). Pathogenic variants in this chromosomal locus abolish or reduce β-hexosaminidase A activity, leading to the GM2 ganglioside accumulation that characterises Tay-Sachs disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTEN",
    "long_answer": "Cowden syndrome, a multiple-hamartoma genetic disorder, presents with numerous flesh-coloured, painless papules (trichilemmomas and oral papillomas) distributed widely on the face, oral mucosa and extremities. The condition is caused by germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the PTEN tumour-suppressor gene, which disrupts PI3K/AKT signalling and drives the characteristic mucocutaneous lesions and cancer predisposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurosurgery",
    "long_answer": "The groundbreaking procedure that enabled Darek Fidyka to recover partial sensory and motor function after complete spinal-cord transection was carried out by a neurosurgical team. Neurosurgeons harvested and implanted olfactory ensheathing cells and constructed a nerve graft across the spinal gap, techniques that fall squarely within the neurosurgery specialty, which deals with operative treatment of brain, spinal-cord and peripheral-nerve disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Kohlschütter–Tönz syndrome is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the ROGDI gene. Cytogenetic mapping places ROGDI at 16p13.3 on the short arm of human chromosome 16, linking this chromosome to clinical features such as upslanting palpebral fissures observed in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ancient Greece",
    "long_answer": "The mythical drug of forgetfulness, nepenthe, is described in Homer’s Odyssey, a foundational text of ancient Greek literature; it is said to banish grief and erase sorrowful memories, tying the substance to the civilization of Ancient Greece."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen-storage disease type II) results from mutations in the GAA gene that encodes acid α-glucosidase. The GAA locus is situated on the long arm of chromosome 17 at band q25.2–q25.3; therefore, chromosome 17 carries the gene whose defect causes Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Spinocerebellar ataxia type 12",
    "long_answer": "Spinocerebellar ataxia type 12 (SCA12) is an autosomal-dominant neurodegenerative disorder characterized by progressive cerebellar ataxia, tremor, and dysarthria. It is caused by a CAG trinucleotide‐repeat expansion in the PPP2R2B gene, which is located on chromosome 5q31-q33, making SCA12 the ataxia linked specifically to a mutation on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "11 exons",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary spastic paraplegia gene CYP2U1 encodes a cytochrome P450 enzyme that ω-hydroxylates arachidonic acid. Genomic analysis shows that the human CYP2U1 locus is organized into 11 distinct exons separated by introns, giving the gene its characteristic nine-exon P450 core plus two additional upstream exons. Thus, the gene contains a total of 11 exons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gonadoblastoma",
    "long_answer": "In 45,X/46,XY mosaicism, dysgenetic gonads that contain Y-chromosome sequences carry a marked propensity to develop germ-cell tumours, most characteristically gonadoblastoma, which can later progress to malignant seminoma/dysgerminoma; therefore prophylactic gonadectomy is often advised."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses (MPS) are a group of inherited lysosomal storage disorders in which deficiencies of specific lysosomal enzymes impair the degradation of glycosaminoglycans (formerly called mucopolysaccharides). The resulting enzymatic block leads to progressive accumulation of glycosaminoglycans in cells, tissues, and bodily fluids, causing multi-system clinical manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "EEM syndrome (ectodermal dysplasia-ectrodactyly-macular dystrophy) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the P-cadherin gene CDH3. The CDH3 locus is mapped to cytogenetic band 16q22.1 on human chromosome 16; therefore the gene responsible for EEM syndrome resides on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Genetic linkage studies of familial sleepwalking (somnambulism) identified a susceptibility locus on the long arm of human chromosome 20 (region 20q12–q13.12), indicating that the gene predisposing to sleepwalking resides on chromosome 20."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Salmonella Typhi",
    "long_answer": "Salmonella enterica serovar Typhi is the Gram-negative bacterium responsible for typhoid fever, an enteric infection whose hallmark clinical feature is a sustained high fever, often accompanied by abdominal pain, rose-spot rash and systemic toxicity. The organism penetrates the intestinal mucosa, disseminates via the bloodstream and triggers a systemic inflammatory response that produces the characteristic febrile illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FUS-CREB3L2",
    "long_answer": "Low-grade fibromyxoid sarcoma (a fibrosarcoma that displays zones of high and low cellularity, reflecting different degrees of cellular differentiation) is driven in most cases by the t(7;16)(q34;p11) translocation, which creates the FUS-CREB3L2 fusion gene. This gene rearrangement is highly characteristic of the tumor and is used diagnostically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Chorea-acanthocytosis is an autosomal-recessive neurodegenerative disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13A (chorein) gene. Cytogenetic and sequence mapping place VPS13A at 9q21 on the long arm of human chromosome 9, establishing chromosome 9 as the chromosomal location responsible for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BHLHA9",
    "long_answer": "Split-hand/foot malformation with long-bone deficiency (SHFLD), a very rare limb malformation syndrome reported in several Mexican families, is caused by pathogenic copy-number increases (duplications) involving the BHLHA9 gene on chromosome 17p13.3. Increased dosage of BHLHA9 disrupts early limb patterning, producing the characteristic hand, foot and long-bone defects seen in these families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Williams-Beuren syndrome, caused by a microdeletion at 7q11.23 on chromosome 7, frequently presents with ophthalmologic findings such as stellate iris pattern and strabismus, linking chromosome 7 to a disorder where strabismus is a characteristic symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen-storage disease type II) results from mutations in the acid α-glucosidase gene (GAA), which is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 17 at 17q25. Mutations in GAA impair lysosomal glycogen breakdown, leading to the characteristic accumulation of glycogen in muscle and other tissues that defines Pompe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Around 50 %",
    "long_answer": "Echocardiographic studies of multisystem inflammatory syndrome in children (MIS-C) consistently show mild-to-moderate systolic dysfunction, with reported median or mean left-ventricular ejection fractions clustering near 50 % (typically 45–55 %), clearly below the normal pediatric value of >60 %."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital intestinal lymphatic malformation",
    "long_answer": "In primary intestinal lymphangiectasia (Waldmann disease), the fundamental defect is a congenital malformation/obstruction of the intestinal lymphatic vessels. These abnormal, dilated lacteals cannot properly drain lymph, leading to leakage of protein-rich lymph into the gut lumen and resulting in protein-losing enteropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LMNA gene, which encodes the nuclear-envelope proteins lamin A and C, produce progeroid syndromes such as acrogeria/mandibuloacral dysplasia. A hallmark of these disorders is thin, wrinkled, inelastic skin over the hands and feet, giving them a prematurely aged appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN1",
    "long_answer": "Fainting goat syndrome is a form of hereditary myotonia. It is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the CLCN1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle chloride channel ClC-1. The mutant channel conducts far less chloride, destabilizing the resting membrane potential of muscle fibers; when the goats are startled, repetitive firing prevents normal relaxation, so the animals stiffen and often fall, giving the characteristic 'fainting' episode."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELANE",
    "long_answer": "Severe congenital neutropenia (Kostmann disease) is most often caused by mutations in the ELANE gene, which encodes neutrophil elastase. Defective ELANE impairs maturation of neutrophil precursors in the bone marrow, producing profound neutropenia that markedly heightens vulnerability to recurrent bacterial infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The USP6 (ubiquitin-specific peptidase 6) gene, whose recurrent rearrangements and over-expression are characteristic of several fibroblastic and myofibroblastic tumors (e.g., nodular fasciitis, aneurysmal bone cyst), is situated on human chromosome 17 at cytogenetic band 17p13.2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGM1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the TGM1 gene, encoding epidermal transglutaminase-1, cause lamellar ichthyosis/a form of autosomal-recessive congenital ichthyosis that is markedly less severe than harlequin ichthyosis. Affected neonates are often born encased in a collodion membrane that later peels to reveal generalized scaling, but with much lower morbidity than ABCA12-related harlequin disease, making TGM1 the gene classically linked to the milder collodion-baby presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EHMT1",
    "long_answer": "9q34.3 deletion syndrome (also known as Kleefstra syndrome) is caused by haplo-insufficiency of the EHMT1 gene located at chromosome band 9q34.3. Microdeletions encompassing EHMT1 or pathogenic point mutations within this gene disrupt its histone-methyltransferase function, leading to the characteristic neurodevelopmental and craniofacial features of the syndrome. Therefore, EHMT1 is the definitive causative gene identified for 9q34.3 deletion syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Urofacial (Ochoa) syndrome is most commonly linked to loss-of-function mutations in the HPSE2 gene. HPSE2 is located at cytogenetic band 10q23-q24 on chromosome 10; alterations in this chromosomal region disrupt HPSE2 activity and produce the characteristic bladder and facial abnormalities of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene disrupt ureteric bud outgrowth during embryogenesis. This defect can variably present as unilateral or bilateral renal agenesis, both forms falling under congenital anomalies of the kidney and urinary tract (CAKUT). Bilateral agenesis produces severe oligohydramnios, the initiating event of Potter sequence, firmly establishing RET mutations as a genetic cause of the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "The triad of dental abnormalities (hypodontia or anodontia), sparse hair, and markedly reduced sweating defines hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia. Over 50 % of cases show the X-linked form, produced by pathogenic variants in the EDA gene, which encodes the ectodysplasin-A signalling protein essential for normal development of teeth, hair follicles, and sweat glands. Thus, EDA mutation is the mutation most commonly responsible for the condition with dental defects and impaired sweating."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SYNE1",
    "long_answer": "The SYNE1 gene encodes nesprin-1, a giant spectrin-repeat protein that spans the outer nuclear membrane and forms part of the LINC (Linker of Nucleoskeleton and Cytoskeleton) complex. Nesprin-1 physically connects the nuclear envelope to actin filaments, thereby tethering the cell nucleus to the cytoskeleton. Pathogenic SYNE1 mutations are a recognized cause of Emery–Dreifuss muscular dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACADM",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ACADM gene (encoding medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase) produce medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase deficiency. This inborn error blocks β-oxidation of medium-chain fatty acids, preventing ketone body formation during fasting and leading to hypoketotic hypoglycemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Tietz syndrome is produced by dominant-negative or loss-of-function mutations in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene. The MITF gene is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 3 (band p13–p14.1). Hence, the gene responsible for Tietz syndrome resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "McDonough syndrome is inherited in an X-linked recessive pattern; linkage studies have mapped the responsible locus to the X chromosome, indicating that the gene causing McDonough syndrome is located on human chromosome X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Oral griseofulvin",
    "long_answer": "The boggy, spongy inflammatory mass on the scalp is a ‘kerion’—a severe form of tinea capitis caused by dermatophytes. Because the fungi reside deep in hair shafts and follicles, topical agents are ineffective. Oral griseofulvin is the established treatment of choice; it concentrates in keratinized tissues, eradicates the dermatophyte infection, reduces inflammation, and prevents permanent scarring alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomal aneuploidy",
    "long_answer": "The co-occurrence of severe global developmental delay with major structural anomalies such as congenital heart defects is most characteristic of the autosomal trisomies (e.g., trisomy 21, 18, 13). These syndromes arise from meiotic nondisjunction leading to chromosomal aneuploidy and therefore do not follow classic Mendelian (dominant, recessive, or X-linked) inheritance; they are typically sporadic, de novo chromosomal events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AVPR2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "A loss-of-function mutation in the AVPR2 gene, which encodes the renal V2 receptor for arginine vasopressin, causes X-linked nephrogenic diabetes insipidus. Because the collecting ducts cannot respond to vasopressin, water reabsorption is impaired, leading to the hallmark symptom of profound polyuria (excretion of large volumes of dilute urine) together with polydipsia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Tay–Sachs disease results from mutations in the HEXA gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A. The HEXA gene is located on chromosome 15 (region 15q23–q24). Pathogenic variants on this chromosome lead to the neuronal lipid accumulation characteristic of Tay–Sachs disease, a condition that can clinically resemble (and thus be mistaken for) Friedreich's ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa is caused by pathogenic variants in the COL7A1 gene, which encodes type VII collagen, the main component of anchoring fibrils that secure the epidermis to the dermis. The COL7A1 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 3 (3p21.31), so chromosome 3 is the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Greg Page",
    "long_answer": "Founding Yellow Wiggle Greg Page was diagnosed with orthostatic intolerance, a dysautonomic condition that can cause dizziness or fainting upon standing upright due to impaired blood-pressure regulation. After his diagnosis and retirement from performing, he established the Greg Page Fund to support research and awareness of orthostatic intolerance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome results from a CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome. This disorder is characterized by moderate-to-severe intellectual disability, and approximately 10–20 % of affected individuals experience epileptic seizures. Hence, the FMR1 gene is directly associated with a condition that causes both seizures and intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Artiodactyla",
    "long_answer": "Balantidiasis is caused by the ciliate protozoan Balantidium coli. The primary reservoir hosts for this parasite are domestic pigs (Sus scrofa), which belong to the mammalian order Artiodactyla (the even-toed ungulates). Therefore, Artiodactyla is the order that includes species capable of harboring the parasite responsible for balantidiasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "11β-hydroxylase deficiency, a form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia, results from pathogenic variants in the CYP11B1 gene. CYP11B1 is located on the long arm of chromosome 8 (8q24), so the defective gene responsible for this disorder resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia, the most common form of disproportionate dwarfism, is produced by a gain-of-function mutation in the FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor-3) gene. Besides severely shortened long bones, the same abnormal endochondral ossification can involve the middle ear ossicles and Eustachian tube, so affected individuals often develop conductive or mixed hearing loss. Thus, an FGFR3 mutation underlies a condition that manifests with both dwarfism and hearing impairments."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Watson syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene, which lies at cytogenetic band 17q11.2. Hence, the responsible gene is located on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "p21-activated kinases",
    "long_answer": "Merlin (the NF2 tumour-suppressor) controls Rho-family small GTPases such as Rac1 and Cdc42. When these GTPases are activated, they bind to and stimulate a specific group of serine/threonine enzymes called p21-activated kinases (PAKs). Therefore, the enzyme family targeted by the Merlin-regulated small GTP-binding proteins is the p21-activated kinase family."
}

{
    "short_answer": "chromosome 3p21-p14",
    "long_answer": "Linkage analyses first placed the autosomal-dominant Larsen-syndrome locus in the short arm of chromosome 3, between cytogenetic bands p21 and p14; the causal gene FLNB was later identified in this interval. The same 3p21 segment contains FYCO1, mutations in which produce autosomal-recessive congenital cataract. Hence the 3p21-p14 region of chromosome 3 is linked to both Larsen syndrome and a cataract-associated gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aarskog–Scott syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Aarskog–Scott syndrome (faciogenital dysplasia) is an X-linked developmental disorder caused by mutations in the FGD1 gene. A characteristic genital feature is a “shawl scrotum,” in which redundant scrotal skin surrounds the base of the penis like a shawl. This finding, alongside facial and skeletal anomalies, is strongly associated with Aarskog–Scott syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WISP3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the WISP3 (Wnt1-inducible signaling pathway protein 3) gene cause Progressive Pseudorheumatoid Dysplasia, an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia. Affected children develop joint swelling, stiffness and contractures that clinically resemble juvenile idiopathic arthritis, yet laboratory tests show no inflammation, linking the arthritis-like presentation directly to WISP3 mutation–driven cartilage and growth-plate abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-26, cause Keratitis-Ichthyosis-Deafness (KID) syndrome. This syndromic disorder is classically characterized by ichthyosiform skin changes, corneal inflammation, and sensorineural hearing loss, making GJB2 the well-established gene associated with a syndrome whose clinical triad includes ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAH",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinemia type I, the form that frequently progresses to acute liver failure, is caused by pathogenic mutations in the FAH gene, which encodes fumarylacetoacetate hydrolase. Loss of FAH activity leads to accumulation of toxic tyrosine-catabolic intermediates in hepatocytes, resulting in hepatocellular injury, cirrhosis, and potentially fatal liver failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syphilitic chorioretinitis",
    "long_answer": "Ocular syphilis, particularly acute syphilitic posterior placoid chorioretinitis, can create large yellow-white placoid lesions at the posterior pole that look identical to the placoid lesions of acute posterior multifocal placoid pigment epitheliopathy (APMPPE). The same infection can also produce serpiginous or coalescent choroidal lesions that closely resemble the lesions of geographic helicoid peripapillary choroidopathy (GHPC, serpiginous choroiditis). Hence, syphilitic chorioretinitis is the classic masquerader that can mimic both APMPPE-type placoid lesions and GHPC-type coalesced lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MBTPS2",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome presenting with the triad of ichthyosis (particularly ichthyosis follicularis), alopecia (or atrichia), and marked photophobia is IFAP syndrome. Pathogenic variants in the X-linked MBTPS2 gene, which encodes the membrane-bound transcription factor peptidase, site 2, disrupt cholesterol and lipid-regulated signaling needed for epidermal and hair follicle development, producing the characteristic skin scaling, hair loss, and ocular light sensitivity seen in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP1A3",
    "long_answer": "The clinical constellation of cerebellar ataxia, areflexia, pes cavus, optic atrophy, and sensorineural hearing loss defines CAPOS syndrome. CAPOS is consistently produced by a heterozygous missense mutation (most commonly p.Glu818Lys) in the ATP1A3 gene, which encodes the α3-subunit of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase critical for neuronal excitability. Hence, mutation of ATP1A3 is the established genetic cause of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Pfeiffer syndrome, a form of craniosynostosis, is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the FGFR2 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2) gene. FGFR2 is located at cytogenetic band 10q26 on human chromosome 10; therefore, this chromosome harbors the gene responsible for the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Cherubism is an autosomal-dominant craniofacial disorder caused by gain-of-function mutations in the SH3BP2 gene. SH3BP2 is mapped to cytogenetic band 4p16.3 on human chromosome 4; therefore, chromosome 4 harbors the gene responsible for cherubism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 blepharophimosis syndrome (BPES) is classically caused by truncating mutations that shorten the FOXL2 gene on chromosome 3q23. Loss-of-function or premature stop mutations in FOXL2 disrupt normal eyelid (palpebral fissure) development, producing the characteristic blepharophimosis, ptosis, and epicanthus inversus seen in BPES type 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the WNT7A gene are a known cause of Al-Awadi/Raas-Rothschild/Schinzel phocomelia syndrome (also called Schinzel phocomelia or AARRS).  The cardinal findings of this syndrome are severe upper- and lower-limb reduction defects that can range from phocomelia to complete absence of the limb (amelia), including brachial amelia.  In addition, affected individuals frequently show midline and cranio-facial anomalies; cleft lip and/or cleft palate has been documented in multiple reported cases.  This association is recorded in authoritative databases such as OMIM (WNT7A; *606013 – Schinzel phocomelia syndrome, OMIM #263750) and in peer-reviewed clinical genetic studies.  Therefore, current biomedical consensus supports that WNT7A mutations are associated with a syndrome that includes both brachial amelia and cleft lip."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3p14.3",
    "long_answer": "Larsen syndrome is most often caused by dominant-negative mutations in the FLNB gene, which encodes filamin B, a cytoskeletal protein critical for connective-tissue integrity. The FLNB gene is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 3 at cytogenetic band p14.3; thus the genetic defect underlying Larsen syndrome localizes to chromosome region 3p14.3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC2A2",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi–Bickel syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the SLC2A2 gene, which encodes the facilitative glucose transporter GLUT2. Defective GLUT2 impairs hepatic and renal handling of glucose and galactose, leading to hepatorenal glycogen accumulation, proximal renal tubular dysfunction, and clinical features that once led to its classification as glycogen storage disease type XI."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOTCH2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous truncating mutations in the NOTCH2 gene cause Hajdu-Cheney syndrome—a congenital disorder marked by extreme osteoclastic bone resorption (acro-osteolysis) and profound, generalized osteoporosis. The mutations delete the PEST degradation domain of NOTCH2, prolonging signaling activity and disrupting normal bone remodeling, which produces the severe skeletal fragility observed in this birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Wiskott–Aldrich syndrome is an X-linked recessive immunodeficiency caused by mutations in the WAS gene, which is located on the short arm of the X chromosome (region Xp11.23). Because the gene resides on the X chromosome, the disorder primarily affects males and exhibits classic X-linked inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Primary sclerosing cholangitis that occurs in patients with ulcerative colitis shows a strong genetic association with HLA alleles such as HLA-B8 and HLA-DR3. All classical HLA class I and class II genes are located in the major histocompatibility complex region on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p21). Thus, the gene locus linked to this disease association is situated on chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP7B",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ATP7B gene, which encodes a copper-transporting P-type ATPase, are responsible for Wilson disease. Wilson disease is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner and typically manifests with movement disorders such as dystonia and chorea as well as progressive cognitive and psychiatric changes. Loss of ATP7B function impairs hepatic copper excretion, leading to copper accumulation in the basal ganglia and cortex, thereby producing the characteristic neurologic and cognitive features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX2 gene",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the PAX2 transcription-factor gene (the cause of papillorenal or renal-coloboma syndrome) are well documented to produce congenital optic nerve malformations. These include classic optic nerve colobomas and the morning glory disc anomaly, both of which can lead to marked visual field defects. Thus, a PAX2 mutation is the established genetic association for the two conditions."
}

{
  "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
  "long_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy with progressive myoclonic epilepsy (SMA-PME) is caused by recessive mutations in the ASAH1 gene, which encodes acid ceramidase. The ASAH1 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 8 (8p22), so the pathogenic locus for this disorder resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the DIS3L2 gene are the established cause of Perlman syndrome, an autosomal-recessive overgrowth disorder. The core clinical picture of Perlman syndrome includes marked prenatal and neonatal overgrowth (macrosomia), relative or absolute macrocephaly, and distinctive dysmorphic facial features such as a high and broad forehead, deep-set eyes, and depressed nasal bridge. These findings are repeatedly documented in the clinical genetics literature and are cited in OMIM (Perlman syndrome, #267000) and multiple peer-reviewed studies that identified pathogenic DIS3L2 variants in affected families. Therefore, mutations in DIS3L2 do indeed produce a syndrome characterized by macrocephaly, neonatal macrosomia, and dysmorphic facies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Familial (autosomal-dominant) cases of infantile myofibromatosis are caused by activating mutations in the PDGFRB gene. PDGFRB is situated at cytogenetic band 5q33.1 on chromosome 5, establishing this chromosome as the locus involved in hereditary forms of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Papillon-Lefèvre syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Papillon-Lefèvre syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by loss-of-function mutations in the cathepsin C (CTSC) gene located on chromosome 11q14. Individuals with this mutation develop severe, early-onset periodontitis that rapidly destroys the supporting tissues of both primary and permanent teeth, along with characteristic palmoplantar keratoderma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Megalencephalic leukoencephalopathy with subcortical cysts (MLC) is most commonly caused by mutations in the MLC1 gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 22 at band q13.3. Thus, chromosome 22 harbors the principal gene associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathyroid hormone receptor",
    "long_answer": "Hypomagnesemia, an electrolyte deficiency that commonly produces refractory hypokalemia, interferes with the adenylate-cyclase coupling of the parathyroid hormone (PTH) receptor (PTH1R). Without sufficient intracellular magnesium, the receptor cannot generate the cAMP signal required for PTH action, so PTH activity is effectively blocked even when the hormone is present."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COMP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COMP gene (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) disrupt cartilage extracellular-matrix assembly, producing disorders such as pseudoachondroplasia and some forms of multiple epiphyseal dysplasia. These skeletal dysplasias present with disproportionate short stature, joint laxity, pain, and early-onset degenerative joint disease, making COMP mutation the well-established cause of a skeletal disorder characterized by short stature and joint problems."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome (chromosome 9)",
    "long_answer": "Classic galactosemia is caused by mutations in the GALT gene, which is situated on the short arm of chromosome 9 (9p13). Because chromosome 9 is one of the 22 numbered non-sex chromosomes, the gene resides on an autosome, not on a sex chromosome or mitochondrial DNA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FRAS1 gene cause Fraser syndrome, a disorder marked by cryptophthalmos (skin covering the eyes), syndactyly (fused digits), and other developmental anomalies. FRAS1 is mapped to cytogenetic band 4q21, placing it on human chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GIMAP gene family",
    "long_answer": "Genome-wide and candidate-gene studies have shown that variants in the cluster of GTPase of Immunity-Associated Proteins (GIMAPs) located on chromosome 7q36 are linked to increased susceptibility to Behçet’s disease. The GIMAP family encodes small GTP-binding proteins that regulate T-cell survival and immune homeostasis; dysregulation can promote the aberrant inflammatory responses that underlie Behçet’s disease, including ocular and optic-nerve inflammation resulting in visual impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lipolysis",
    "long_answer": "Lipolysis is the enzymatic hydrolysis of stored triglycerides in adipose tissue into free fatty acids and glycerol. The resulting rise in circulating non-esterified fatty acids can antagonize insulin signaling in muscle and liver, thereby exacerbating insulin resistance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "EDI200 is a recombinant form of the signaling protein ectodysplasin-A1 used as replacement therapy for X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (XLHED). The vast majority of XLHED cases are caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EDA gene, which encodes ectodysplasin-A. Hence, EDA gene mutations are the most common genetic cause of the condition treatable with EDI200."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SEPN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SEPN1 gene, which encodes selenoprotein N, cause rigid-spine congenital muscular dystrophy (RSMD1). This disorder is classed among congenital muscular dystrophies, yet on clinical and histopathologic grounds it is also grouped with congenital myopathies (often termed “SEPN1-related myopathy” or multiminicore disease), making SEPN1 the gene whose mutation produces a CMD that is simultaneously considered a myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COMP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene cause pseudoachondroplasia, a skeletal dysplasia characterised by disproportionate short stature and early-onset degenerative changes in many large and small joints. The defective COMP protein disturbs cartilage extracellular-matrix assembly, leading to growth-plate dysfunction and widespread joint involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "All three fibrinogen genes—FGA (Aα chain), FGB (Bβ chain) and FGG (γ chain)—form a gene cluster at 4q23–q32 on human chromosome 4. Mutations in these genes alter the fibrinogen polypeptide chains and lead to congenital dysfibrinogenemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The two principal Epidermodysplasia verruciformis genes, TMC6 (EVER1) and TMC8 (EVER2), both map to the 17q25 region of chromosome 17. Loss-of-function mutations in these genes impair zinc-regulated signaling in keratinocytes and lymphocytes, compromising T-cell function and predisposing to EV lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Approximately 70%",
    "long_answer": "Large international cohort and meta-analysis data indicate that carriers of pathogenic CDKN2A variants have a cumulative melanoma penetrance of roughly seven in ten—about 70 %—by 80 years of age."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Among the PAX (paired-box) transcription-factor family, eye malformations that lead to vision loss are classically linked to PAX6 (e.g., aniridia, foveal hypoplasia) and to PAX2 (renal-coloboma syndrome). PAX7, in contrast, is best known for its role in skeletal-muscle and neural-crest development and for somatic PAX7-FOXO1 fusions in alveolar rhabdomyosarcoma. Germ-line variants or inherited defects in PAX7 have not been implicated in congenital or progressive ocular disorders that impair vision. Consequently, the scientific and clinical literature does not support an association between PAX7 and eye conditions that produce mild to severe visual impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The ALK (anaplastic lymphoma kinase) gene that encodes the ALK receptor tyrosine-kinase is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 at band 2p23. Rearrangements of this locus—most commonly the t(2;5)(p23;q35) translocation—drive expression of ALK fusion proteins, giving rise to ALK-positive anaplastic large-cell lymphoma."
}

{
  "short_answer": "yes",
  "long_answer": "Microcephaly-lymphedema-chorioretinal dysplasia (MLCRD), now usually referred to as “microcephaly with or without chorioretinopathy, lymphedema, or intellectual disability” (MCLMR; OMIM #152950), has been molecularly linked to heterozygous pathogenic variants in the KIF11 gene. All convincingly documented and genetically confirmed cases show autosomal-dominant segregation, often arising from de-novo mutations but capable of vertical transmission with variable expressivity and incomplete penetrance. No reproducible evidence exists for autosomal-recessive, X-linked, or any other mode of inheritance. Therefore, according to current scientific consensus, this condition follows a single inheritance pattern—autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RUNX2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Cleidocranial dysplasia—a skeletal disorder characterized by hypoplastic or absent clavicles and delayed closure of cranial sutures—is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the RUNX2 (CBFA1) gene, a transcription factor essential for osteoblast differentiation and bone formation. Disruption of RUNX2 impairs intramembranous ossification, producing the distinctive clavicle and skull abnormalities seen in the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Heimler syndrome is a very mild peroxisome-biogenesis disorder most often caused by pathogenic variants in the PEX1 gene. The human PEX1 gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 7 (region 7q21–q22). Mutations in this chromosome-7 gene disrupt peroxisomal biogenesis, leading to the characteristic features of Heimler syndrome such as sensorineural hearing loss and amelogenesis imperfecta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the gonadotropin-releasing hormone receptor gene (GNRHR) are a well-established cause of normosmic congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism. The GNRHR gene is located on human chromosome 4 (band 4q13.2); therefore, chromosome 4 is the chromosome that houses the gene whose mutation leads to this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, which is located on the long arm (q arm) of human chromosome 7 at position 7q31.2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Usher syndrome type 1F and a form of nonsyndromic autosomal-recessive deafness (DFNB23) are both caused by mutations in the PCDH15 gene (protocadherin-15). The PCDH15 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 10 (cytogenetic band 10q21.1), making chromosome 10 the chromosome that contains the gene responsible for these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neuhauser syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Neuhauser syndrome, also called the hydrocephalus–endocardial fibroelastosis–cataract (HEC) syndrome, is a rare autosomal-recessive disorder whose diagnostic triad is congenital hydrocephalus, diffuse endocardial fibroelastosis of the heart, and bilateral congenital cataracts—an ocular lesion that can lead to blindness. This distinctive combination of CNS, cardiac and ocular findings defines the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiamine (vitamin B1)",
    "long_answer": "Prolonged heavy alcohol consumption often leads to malnutrition and impaired intestinal absorption of thiamine (vitamin B1). Thiamine deficiency produces Wernicke-Korsakoff syndrome, a neurological disorder of the central nervous system characterized by confusion, ophthalmoplegia, ataxia, and memory disturbances. Thus, lack of thiamine is the vitamin deficiency classically linked to alcohol-related CNS disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kaolin",
    "long_answer": "Modern QuikClot Combat Gauze is impregnated with kaolin, an aluminosilicate clay. When blood soaks the dressing, kaolin directly activates coagulation Factor XII (Hageman factor), triggering the intrinsic clotting cascade and accelerating formation of a stable fibrin clot—an effect exploited to control bleeding from injuries such as stab wounds."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Minoru Ito",
    "long_answer": "Hypomelanosis of Ito—also called incontinentia pigmenti achromians—was first reported in 1952 by the Japanese dermatologist Minoru Ito, who described a series of patients with streaked, whorled hypopigmentation following the lines of Blaschko. His detailed clinical and histopathologic observations led to the eponym \"Ito’s disease.\""
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSF4",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the Heat-Shock-Transcription-Factor-4 (HSF4) gene produce an autosomal-dominant ocular syndrome characterised by bilateral congenital cataracts together with iris coloboma. HSF4 is a lens-specific transcription factor; missense mutations such as p.Arg116His impair its regulatory activity during eye development, leading to defective lens fiber differentiation (resulting in cataracts) and incomplete optic fissure closure (producing iris coloboma)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Galactokinase deficiency is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the GALK1 gene (galactokinase 1). Cytogenetic and molecular mapping have localized GALK1 to band 17q24 on human chromosome 17, so the pathogenic gene resides on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PITX2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PITX2 homeobox transcription factor cause Axenfeld-Rieger (Rieger) syndrome. This autosomal-dominant disorder is characterised by anterior-segment dysgenesis with iris and ocular hypoplasia/microphthalmia, in addition to dental and umbilical anomalies. Thus, PITX2 mutation is the genetic change classically associated with a condition that includes hypoplasia of the eyes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Congenital disorder of glycosylation type 1N (CDG-1N) is caused by pathogenic variants in the DPM3 gene (dolichyl-phosphate mannosyltransferase subunit 3). The DPM3 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 1q22 on human chromosome 1; therefore, chromosome 1 contains the gene responsible for CDG type 1N."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Parathyroid gland",
    "long_answer": "Blomstrand chondrodysplasia results from loss-of-function mutations in the gene encoding the type-1 parathyroid hormone/parathyroid hormone-related peptide receptor (PTH1R). Because the mutation blocks the action of parathyroid hormone, the endocrine system functionally affected is that of the parathyroid gland, whose hormone can no longer act on its target tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTC8 (BBS8)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TTC8 gene—also known as BBS8—located on chromosome 14q24.3 cause Bardet-Biedl syndrome type 8. This ciliopathy is classically marked by progressive retinal dystrophy leading to visual impairment and by limb anomalies such as post-axial polydactyly, along with other systemic features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene give rise to disorders such as pseudoachondroplasia and multiple epiphyseal dysplasia type 1. These conditions are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, meaning one mutated COMP allele inherited from an affected parent is sufficient to cause disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary startle disease hyperekplexia, which causes marked muscle stiffness and exaggerated muscular jerks, is most often produced by mutations in the α-1 subunit of the glycine receptor gene GLRA1 located on chromosome 5q32-q34. Clonazepam, a benzodiazepine that enhances inhibitory GABAergic tone, is the standard treatment used to reduce the hypertonia and startle–induced muscle spasms in this disorder, linking the disease—and thus chromosome 5—with therapy using clonazepam."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1, cause Marfan syndrome—a systemic connective-tissue disorder. The deficient or dysfunctional fibrillin-1 weakens elastic microfibrils, producing skeletal manifestations that characteristically include scoliosis, joint hypermobility, long limbs, and other musculoskeletal deformities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POMGNT1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the POMGNT1 gene, which encodes protein O-mannose β-1,2-N-acetylglucosaminyltransferase 1, cause the subtype POMGNT1-CDG (formerly CDG type IId) of the congenital disorders of glycosylation. The same defect in O-mannosyl glycosylation of α-dystroglycan leads to the clinical picture of muscle–eye–brain disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DEET",
    "long_answer": "Omsk hemorrhagic fever virus is transmitted to humans primarily by Dermacentor ticks. Public-health guidelines, including those from the U.S. Centers for Disease Control and Prevention, advise applying insect repellent containing DEET (N,N-diethyl-meta-toluamide) to exposed skin and clothing because DEET effectively repels ticks and thereby helps prevent the tick bites that can spread Omsk hemorrhagic fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Infantile systemic hyalinosis results from biallelic mutations in the ANTXR2 (CMG2) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 4 (4q21)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The gene F8, recorded in the OMIM database (OMIM #300841), encodes coagulation factor VIII, a key protein of the intrinsic coagulation cascade. Cytogenetic mapping places F8 at Xq28, indicating that the human X chromosome harbors this coagulation-related gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Gerstmann–Sträussler–Scheinker syndrome is an inherited prion disease caused by pathogenic mutations in the PRNP gene (prion protein gene). The human PRNP gene is located on chromosome 20 (20p13), so chromosome 20 is the chromosome associated with this dementia-causing syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Fukutin physically associates with the fukutin-related protein (FKRP) in the O-mannosyl glycosylation pathway that is disrupted in Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy. The FKRP gene that encodes this interacting protein is mapped to human chromosome 19 (19q13.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Muscle–eye–brain disease (MEB) belongs to the group of α-dystroglycanopathies, a subset of congenital disorders of glycosylation (CDG) that disturb O-mannose glycosylation of α-dystroglycan. Genes that have been definitively implicated in MEB or closely related conditions (e.g., Walker-Warburg syndrome, limb-girdle muscular dystrophy 2M) include POMGNT1, POMGNT2 (GTDC2), POMT1, POMT2, POMK, FKRP, FKTN, LARGE1, LARGE2, ISPD/CRPPA and several others. Extensive clinical-genetic studies, databases such as OMIM, ClinGen and GeneReviews, and the current CDG classification lists do not report any human disorder attributable to mutations in a gene called “POMGNT7.” In fact, no human gene with the official symbol POMGNT7 is recognized; the only characterized human POMGNT genes are POMGNT1 and POMGNT2. Consequently, there is no evidence—either in the medical literature or in curated genetic databases—that variants in a nonexistent or uncharacterized “POMGNT7” gene cause any congenital disorder of glycosylation, let alone the muscle–eye–brain disease phenotype. Therefore, the statement that mutations in POMGNT7 cause a CDG subtype leading to MEB is not supported by established scientific consensus and is considered incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDH1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the isocitrate dehydrogenase-1 (IDH1) gene found in acute myeloid leukemia produce an aberrant enzyme that converts α-ketoglutarate to the oncometabolite D-2-hydroxyglutarate. The accumulated 2-hydroxyglutarate competitively inhibits α-ketoglutarate–dependent epigenetic enzymes such as TET DNA demethylases and histone demethylases, thereby altering DNA and histone methylation profiles and contributing to leukemogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC25A13",
    "long_answer": "Adult-onset citrullinemia, also called citrullinemia type II, is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC25A13 gene. SLC25A13 encodes the mitochondrial aspartate–glutamate carrier protein “citrin”; its deficiency disrupts the urea cycle and energy metabolism, leading to episodic hyperammonemia that primarily manifests with central nervous system symptoms in adults."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coarctation of the aorta",
    "long_answer": "Horseshoe kidney is classically seen in Turner syndrome (45,X). The most characteristic congenital heart defect in Turner syndrome is coarctation of the aorta, making this cardiac lesion closely associated with the same genetic condition that produces horseshoe kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "The dipeptide β-alanine–histidine (carnosine) is hydrolysed by the enzyme carnosinase (carnosine dipeptidase 1). In humans, inherited carnosinase deficiency (carnosinemia) results from mutations in the CNDP1 gene. CNDP1 is mapped to cytogenetic band 18q22.3, i.e., it resides on human chromosome 18; therefore chromosome 18 contains the gene whose alteration causes this enzyme deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The cystic fibrosis gene, CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator), is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 at position 7q31.2. Mutations in this gene disrupt chloride ion transport in epithelial cells, leading to the characteristic pulmonary and pancreatic manifestations of cystic fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals-Hecht syndrome) is caused by pathogenic variants in the FBN2 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-2. The FBN2 gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 5 (cytogenetic band 5q23–q31). Therefore, chromosome 5 carries the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIPA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LIPA gene abolish or markedly reduce lysosomal acid lipase (LAL) activity—the enzyme responsible for hydrolyzing cholesteryl esters and triglycerides delivered to lysosomes within low-density lipoprotein particles. Loss of LAL function causes accumulation of these lipids, manifesting clinically as Wolman disease or cholesteryl ester storage disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The neural cell adhesion molecule-1 (NCAM1) gene, which has been implicated in certain congenital cases of esophageal atresia and functions in neural cell adhesion and guidance, maps to the long arm of human chromosome 11 (11q23)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Activating (gain-of-function) mutations in the FGFR3 gene, most commonly the G380R missense change, cause achondroplasia and related FGFR3-linked skeletal dysplasias. Constitutive FGFR3 signalling suppresses growth-plate chondrocyte proliferation and maturation, producing severely shortened long bones, limb deformities and disproportionate short stature—clinical features of severe dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The zinc transporter gene SLC39A8 (also known as ZIP8) modulates cellular and serum zinc levels and has been linked to several inflammatory conditions; this gene is located on human chromosome 4 (region 4q24). Thus, chromosome 4 harbors the gene that influences zinc homeostasis and inflammation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome) is caused by pathogenic variants in the fibrillin-2 gene (FBN2). The FBN2 gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5, band q23-q31. Consequently, chromosome 5 is the chromosomal location that harbors the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN9A mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SCN9A gene, which encodes the Nav1.7 voltage-gated sodium channel required for nociceptive neuron firing, cause congenital insensitivity to pain. Because these patients do not perceive painful pressure or tissue ischemia, they often remain in one position too long and develop recurrent pressure (decubitus) ulcers as well as other painless injuries."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Caroli syndrome is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PKHD1 gene (polycystic kidney and hepatic disease 1). The PKHD1 locus is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p12), making chromosome 6 the chromosomal location linked to this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GSK3B",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen-synthase-kinase-3 beta (GSK3B) is a repeatedly implicated susceptibility gene in bipolar disorder. The GSK3β kinase binds to and phosphorylates STAT1, the signal-transducer that is directly activated by pegylated interferon-α—one of the classic, widely used treatments for chronic hepatitis C. Because GSK3B physically interacts with STAT1, the protein that interferon-α targets, it satisfies the relationship described in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human papillomavirus",
    "long_answer": "Human papillomavirus (HPV) carries a circular double-stranded DNA genome. In the host cell nucleus it is copied by the cellular DNA polymerase through a theta-type, bidirectional mechanism: replication initiates at a single origin and proceeds simultaneously in two opposite directions around the circle. This bidirectional replication model is a well-established feature of Papillomaviridae." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "A classic lysosomal storage disease, Tay-Sachs, is caused by absence of the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A. The gene that encodes the enzyme’s α-subunit (HEXA) is located on human chromosome 15 (15q23 – q24). Mutations on chromosome 15 therefore give rise to the enzyme deficiency responsible for this prototypical lysosomal storage disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATR",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ATR gene cause Seckel syndrome. This autosomal-recessive disorder is classically characterized by severe intrauterine growth retardation, marked microcephaly, and distinctive craniofacial dysmorphisms (\"bird-headed\" appearance). ATR encodes a key DNA-damage-response kinase; its deficiency disrupts cell-cycle checkpoints during development, leading to the microcephaly and craniofacial abnormalities seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Triple-A (Allgrove) syndrome is caused by mutations in the AAAS gene, which encodes the ALADIN nucleoporin. The AAAS gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 12q13 on human chromosome 12; hence, chromosome 12 carries the gene responsible for Triple-A syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Hailey-Hailey disease (familial benign chronic pemphigus) presents with recurrent blisters and erythematous rashes. It is produced by mutations in the ATP2C1 gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 3 (3q21-q24). Thus, the gene responsible for this blistering skin disorder resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frataxin (FXN)",
    "long_answer": "Friedreich ataxia is caused by a GAA-trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FXN gene that encodes the mitochondrial protein frataxin. Loss of frataxin disrupts mitochondrial iron handling, leading to oxidative damage in neurons and cardiac muscle. The resulting hypertrophic cardiomyopathy frequently progresses to heart failure, making the FXN mutation a well-established genetic cause of cardiomyopathy and cardiac death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystrophin (DMD) gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked DMD gene, which encodes dystrophin, cause Duchenne (and Becker) muscular dystrophy. This disorder classically produces progressive weakness of the proximal limb-girdle muscles—those of the pelvic and shoulder regions—owing to degeneration of muscle fibers lacking functional dystrophin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Hypochondrogenesis results from dominant mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen essential for cartilage development. COL2A1 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 12 (12q13.11-q13.2), so chromosome 12 is the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SBDS",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SBDS gene cause Shwachman-Diamond syndrome, a hereditary disorder characterized by exocrine pancreatic insufficiency, skeletal anomalies and bone-marrow failure that often manifests as neutropenia and progressive pancytopenia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The main susceptibility genes that have been implicated in isolated or syndromic forms of esophageal atresia (for example SOX2 on chromosome 3q, CHD7 on 8q, MYCN on 2p, and others) are not located on chromosome 5. Likewise, the well-known neural cell-adhesion genes (L1CAM on Xq28, NCAM1 on 11q23, NRCAM on 7q31, CHL1 on 3p26, etc.) are situated on chromosomes other than chromosome 5. No gene on chromosome 5 is recognized by the scientific community as both (i) a neural cell-adhesion molecule and (ii) a gene whose mutation or dosage change is causally linked to esophageal atresia. Therefore, the claim that chromosome 5 harbors a neural cell-adhesion gene associated with esophageal atresia contradicts established genomic and clinical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) is classically caused by pathogenic variants in any one of several genes that regulate pancreatic β-cell function (e.g., HNF1A, HNF4A, GCK, HNF1B, PDX1, KLF11, among others). In virtually all well-characterized forms of MODY, these mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion: a single pathogenic allele is sufficient to cause the phenotype, and the condition typically appears in multiple successive generations. Autosomal recessive inheritance, which would require two pathogenic alleles and often skips generations, is not the accepted mode of inheritance for MODY. Therefore, the statement that MODY-causing gene mutations are inherited in an autosomal recessive manner is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Acute intermittent porphyria results from pathogenic variants in the HMBS (porphobilinogen deaminase) gene. These mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner with incomplete penetrance, meaning a single mutated allele is sufficient to confer disease risk, though not all carriers develop symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Bowen–Conradi syndrome is caused by a recessive missense mutation (Thr36Met) in the EMG1 (essential for mitotic growth 1) gene. The human EMG1 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 12p13.32 on chromosome 12, so the disease-causing gene resides on human chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Shwachman–Diamond syndrome is caused by mutations in the SBDS gene, which is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 7 at band q11. Consequently, the gene responsible for the disorder resides on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPTLC1",
    "long_answer": "Missense mutations in the SPTLC1 gene, which encodes a subunit of serine-palmitoyltransferase, cause Hereditary Sensory Neuropathy type I (HSN-I). This dominantly inherited disorder selectively damages peripheral sensory nerves, producing a progressive loss of pain and temperature sensation in the distal limbs and leading to chronic ulcers and neuropathic complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Mohr–Tranebjærg syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TIMM8A (DDP1) gene, which is located at the Xq22 locus on the X chromosome; hence, the disorder follows an X-linked inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jean-Martin Charcot",
    "long_answer": "Charcot–Marie–Tooth disease, a hereditary motor and sensory neuropathy, was first described in 1886 by the French neurologist Jean-Martin Charcot, who published the initial clinical account with his student Pierre Marie; an independent description by the English neurologist Howard Henry Tooth followed later the same year, which is why the disorder bears all three names today."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Erythropoietic protoporphyria is most commonly produced by loss-of-function mutations in the FECH gene, which encodes ferrochelatase, the terminal enzyme of heme biosynthesis. The human FECH gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 18 (18q21), so pathogenic variants on chromosome 18 lead to the clinical disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The most frequently mutated gene in chronic myelomonocytic leukemia is TET2; this gene is located at cytogenetic band 4q24 on human chromosome 4. Hence, chromosome 4 harbors the gene whose mutation is common in CMML."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jean-Louis-Marc Alibert",
    "long_answer": "The scalp disorder cutis verticis gyrata was first described in 1837 by the French dermatologist Jean-Louis-Marc Alibert, who reported the characteristic thickened, convoluted folds of the scalp; the descriptive term “cutis verticis gyrata” itself was coined later by Unna in 1907."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.4, produce autosomal dominant muscle channelopathies (e.g., paramyotonia congenita, hyperkalemic periodic paralysis). These disorders arise because SCN4A gain-of-function mutations alter sodium channel gating in muscle fibers, leading to impaired membrane excitability and episodic weakness or myotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IHH",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IHH (Indian hedgehog) gene cause brachydactyly type A1, a congenital limb malformation characterized by marked shortening of the middle phalanges, resulting in shortened interphalangeal joints of the fingers and toes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ambroise Paré",
    "long_answer": "The French barber-surgeon Ambroise Paré (1510–1590) gave the earliest recorded medical description of children born with fused fingers and toes in his 1573 treatise on congenital \"monsters,\" thereby identifying syndactyly as a birth anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNQ1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the KCNQ1 gene (encoding the cardiac/inner-ear IKs potassium-channel α-subunit) cause Jervell-Lange-Nielsen syndrome. This disorder follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern and is clinically characterized by congenital, often severe sensorineural hearing loss together with a prolonged QT interval and risk of arrhythmias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRT1, KRT10",
    "long_answer": "The clinical picture matches epidermolytic ichthyosis (epidermolytic hyperkeratosis), an autosomal-dominant cornification disorder produced by heterozygous mutations in the suprabasal keratin genes KRT1 (keratin 1) and KRT10 (keratin 10). These mutations destabilize the keratin intermediate-filament network, leading to neonatal skin fragility and, later, the characteristic generalized, persistent brown hyperkeratosis with sharply demarcated plaques."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Toxicity to the chemotherapeutic drug irinotecan is strongly linked to polymorphisms in the UGT1A1 gene (notably the *28 allele). UGT1A1 encodes the enzyme that glucuronidates the active metabolite SN-38; reduced activity leads to high SN-38 levels and a markedly higher risk of severe diarrhea and neutropenia. The UGT1A1 gene is located on human chromosome 2 (band 2q37), so chromosome 2 carries the risk-modifying gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXT1",
    "long_answer": "Genu valgum is a common limb deformity in hereditary multiple osteochondromas (hereditary multiple exostoses), an autosomal-dominant skeletal disorder. This condition is classically produced by loss-of-function mutations in the EXT1 gene (exostosin-1) on chromosome 8, which encodes a glycosyltransferase required for heparan-sulfate biosynthesis in the growth plate. Disruption of EXT1 causes abnormal osteocartilaginous outgrowths around the physes, leading to angular deformities such as genu valgum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JC virus",
    "long_answer": "Alien hand syndrome has been described in patients with progressive multifocal leukoencephalopathy (PML), a demyelinating brain disease produced by the JC virus (John Cunningham polyomavirus). The virus infects and destroys oligodendrocytes, creating callosal and parietal lesions that can disconnect motor control from conscious volition, thereby producing the involuntary limb movements characteristic of alien hand syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of autosomal-dominant hereditary spastic paraplegia (SPG4) is caused by mutations in the SPAST gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 (region 2p22-p21)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hyperekplexia (startle disease) is caused most commonly by mutations in the GLRA1 gene situated on human chromosome 5 (5q34). The characteristic exaggerated startle and stiffness are effectively controlled with the benzodiazepine clonazepam, making chromosome 5 the chromosome linked to a clonazepam-responsive disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAMA3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LAMA3 gene (encoding the α3-chain of laminin-332) cause laryngo-onycho-cutaneous (LOC) syndrome—a very rare epithelial disorder whose hallmark triad is laryngeal granulation tissue, nail dystrophy (onychodysplasia), and chronic skin erosions or granulation over the face and extremities. Loss of functional laminin-332 disrupts epithelial basement-membrane adhesion, accounting for the combined abnormalities in the larynx, nails, and skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "5α-Reductase type 2 deficiency is caused by mutations in the SRD5A2 gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 (band 2p23). Alterations in this chromosomal locus impair conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, producing the characteristic clinical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The APRT gene, whose loss-of-function mutations cause adenine phosphoribosyltransferase (APRT) deficiency and consequent 2,8-dihydroxyadenine kidney stones and nephropathy, is located on the long arm of human chromosome 16 (region 16q24.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPAST",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPAST gene are the most common cause of hereditary spastic paraplegia (SPG4), a rare neurological disorder marked by progressive spasticity and stiffness of the lower limbs. SPAST encodes the microtubule-severing protein spastin; loss-of-function variants disrupt axonal transport in corticospinal neurons, leading to the characteristic lower-limb stiffness of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sarcoplasmic reticulum",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia arises when mutations (e.g., in the RYR1 channel) trigger uncontrolled release of Ca2+ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum, the specialized endoplasmic reticulum of skeletal muscle fibers that stores and liberates calcium for contraction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome classically producing syndactyly-type hand and foot deformities together with a flat facial profile is Apert (acrocephalosyndactyly) syndrome, produced by gain-of-function mutations in the FGFR2 gene. This disorder is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner (although most cases arise de novo), so the characteristic inheritance pattern linked to these hand- and foot-plus-flat-facies anomalies is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The t(9;22)(q34;q11) translocation characteristic of the Philadelphia chromosome fuses the ABL1 proto-oncogene from chromosome 9 with the BCR gene on chromosome 22. ABL1 is the key oncogenic partner that drives leukemogenesis in BCR-ABL1-positive acute myeloid leukemia. Hence, the AML-associated gene involved in the t(9;22) translocation is located on human chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "The MKKS gene (also called BBS6) maps to cytogenetic band 20p12.2 on human chromosome 20. Mutations in this locus produce the chaperonin-like protein defects responsible for McKusick–Kaufman syndrome and the associated limb malformations such as polydactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diamond–Blackfan anemia",
    "long_answer": "Diamond–Blackfan anemia is a congenital (inherited) red-cell aplasia characterized by macrocytic anemia presenting in infancy. The disorder is often accompanied by congenital limb abnormalities (e.g., thumb or radial defects) and carries an increased lifetime risk of developing bone cancers such as osteogenic sarcoma, making it the anemia classically linked to the described syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "PMM2 deficiency (CDG-Ia) is caused by pathogenic variants in the PMM2 gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 16p13.2 on human chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "T-cell deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Nezelof syndrome is a congenital thymic dysplasia that impairs development of thymus-dependent lymphocytes. The resulting selective deficiency of mature T cells compromises cell-mediated immunity; without adequate T-cell help, B cells are present but produce poorly functional immunoglobulins, predisposing patients to recurrent severe infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of ectodermal dysplasia is X-linked hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia (HED), caused by mutations in the EDA (ectodysplasin-A) gene. EDA is located on the human X chromosome (region Xq12-q13.1). Therefore, the gene underlying the most prevalent type of ectodermal dysplasia resides on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The vascular endothelial growth factor A (VEGFA) gene encodes VEGF-A, the classic “vascular permeability factor” that enhances leakage across epithelial and especially endothelial barriers, leading to tissue edema. In humans, the VEGFA gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 6 (6p12), so chromosome 6 contains the gene responsible for this permeability-regulating protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rudolf Virchow",
    "long_answer": "The characteristic abnormal cranial growth patterns seen in McGillivray syndrome reflect the principles of craniosynostosis that were first described by the German pathologist Rudolf Virchow in 1851; he showed that premature fusion of a calvarial suture restricts growth perpendicular to the suture and causes compensatory growth in parallel directions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PAX6 gene cause congenital aniridia and the related WAGR spectrum syndrome. Because PAX6 is a master regulator of eye development, such mutations lead to iris hypoplasia, foveal/aplasia and optic-nerve malformations, resulting in severe visual impairment or blindness as a prominent clinical feature of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "THAP1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the THAP1 gene (primary dystonia type 6, DYT6) produce an autosomal-dominant form of early-onset dystonia that frequently involves the cranial muscles. Patients commonly develop cranio-cervical manifestations such as blepharospasm, along with oromandibular and laryngeal dystonia, making THAP1 mutation the established genetic association with blepharospasm in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "The disorder described is a syndromic form of aniridia in which loss-of-function mutations or 11p13 deletions involving the PAX6 gene abolish iris development and, through effects on brain and kidney morphogenesis, produce cerebellar ataxia, psychomotor retardation, and renal abnormalities. Hence, PAX6 is the gene whose alteration underlies this constellation of findings."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Paroxysmal nonkinesigenic dyskinesia is caused by mutations in the PNKD (formerly MR-1) gene. Molecular mapping places PNKD on the long arm of human chromosome 2 (region 2q31-q36). Therefore, the gene responsible for this disorder resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOST",
    "long_answer": "Spanish clinicians showed that the very severe hyperostotic bone disorder they observed (later named sclerosteosis) was distinct from van Buchem disease. Molecular studies demonstrated that this disorder is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the SOST gene, which encodes the Wnt-signalling inhibitor sclerostin. Thus, mutation of SOST – not the regulatory deletion that underlies van Buchem disease – causes the disease the Spanish doctors distinguished."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTC37",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function variants in TTC37 are the classical cause of tricho-hepato-enteric (THES) syndrome.  Independent reports have shown that other recessive TTC37 mutations can present primarily with brittle, sulfur-deficient hair and the diagnostic features of non-photosensitive trichothiodystrophy, linking the same gene to this form of trichothiodystrophy as well."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke",
    "long_answer": "The National Institute of Neurological Disorders and Stroke (NINDS), a component of the U.S. NIH, funds and coordinates research initiatives aimed at understanding the causes, mechanisms, and potential therapies for myopathies, which are classified among neuromuscular diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Cardiomyopathic neutral lipid storage disease (neutral lipid storage disease with myopathy) is produced by pathogenic variants in the PNPLA2 gene, which encodes adipose-triglyceride lipase (ATGL). The PNPLA2 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p15.5), so chromosome 11 carries the causal gene for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LIS1 (PAFAH1B1)",
    "long_answer": "Classic (type-I) lissencephaly is most commonly caused by mutations or deletions in the LIS1 gene, officially named PAFAH1B1, which is located on chromosome 17p13.3. LIS1 encodes a regulator of the dynein motor complex that is essential for proper neuronal migration during cortical development; haploinsufficiency disrupts this process, producing the smooth-brain phenotype characteristic of lissencephaly and Miller–Dieker syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Sandhoff disease results from mutations in the HEXB gene, which encodes the β-subunit common to both β-hexosaminidase A and B. HEXB is located on human chromosome 5 (5q13.3); hence, chromosome 5 carries the gene whose defect causes the combined deficiency of β-hexosaminidases A and B."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Morquio A syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IVA) is caused by loss-of-function variants in the GALNS gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase. The GALNS gene is located on chromosome 16 (cytogenetic band 16q24.3), not on chromosome 4. Therefore the statement that the causative gene malfunction occurs on chromosome 4 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Facial nerve (VII)",
    "long_answer": "Foix–Chavany–Marie (opercular) syndrome causes bilateral loss of voluntary control over the bulbar musculature, including the muscles of facial expression that are supplied by the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII). Expressive (Broca’s) aphasia most often results from an inferior frontal-gyrus (Broca’s area) lesion that also involves the adjacent primary motor cortex for the lower face; consequently the corticobulbar fibres to cranial nerve VII are disrupted, producing weakness of the facial muscles used in speech. Thus impairment of the facial nerve (cranial nerve VII) is a feature common to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EDA gene",
    "long_answer": "The most common X-linked recessive form of ectodermal dysplasia is hypohidrotic/anhidrotic ectodermal dysplasia, which is caused by pathogenic mutations in the EDA gene (ectodysplasin A) located on the X chromosome. EDA encodes a signaling protein essential for the development of ectoderm-derived structures such as hair, teeth, and sweat glands; loss-of-function variants disrupt this pathway, producing the classic triad of sparse hair, absent or reduced sweating, and abnormal dentition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VMA21",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked VMA21 gene cause X-linked myopathy with excessive autophagy (XMEA). VMA21 encodes an endoplasmic-reticulum assembly chaperone that is indispensable for correct assembly of the vacuolar H⁺-ATPase (V-ATPase). When VMA21 is defective, V-ATPase fails to assemble properly, lysosomal acidification is compromised, and muscle fibers accumulate autophagic vacuoles, producing the hallmark excessive autophagy seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Hydralazine is primarily metabolized by N-acetyltransferase 2 (NAT2). The NAT2 gene, whose polymorphisms determine whether an individual is a slow or fast acetylator of hydralazine, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 8 (band 8p22). Hence, chromosome 8 carries the gene that governs hydralazine metabolism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in any of the genes encoding components of the glycine cleavage system (e.g., GLDC, AMT, GCSH) cause non-ketotic hyperglycinemia. Affected individuals inherit two pathogenic alleles—one from each carrier parent—demonstrating classic autosomal recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of Batten disease is the juvenile form (juvenile neuronal ceroid-lipofuscinosis, or CLN3 disease). This form is caused by pathogenic variants in the CLN3 gene. The CLN3 gene is located on chromosome 16 (specifically 16p12.1), not on chromosome 20. Therefore, chromosome 20 does not contain the gene responsible for the most common form of Batten disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Variegate porphyria is caused by mutations in the PPOX gene, which encodes the enzyme protoporphyrinogen oxidase. The human PPOX gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 1 (cytogenetic band 1q22). Defects in this chromosome-1 gene reduce enzyme activity, leading to the biochemical accumulation of porphyrin precursors characteristic of variegate porphyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bladder catheterization",
    "long_answer": "Acute kidney injury can arise from post-renal obstruction produced by severe urinary retention. Passing a urethral (Foley) catheter—bladder catheterization—rapidly decompresses the over-distended bladder, re-establishes urine flow, and thereby relieves the obstruction that is compromising renal function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xanthine oxidase",
    "long_answer": "Molybdenum cofactor is required for the function of xanthine oxidase (also called xanthine dehydrogenase). When the cofactor is missing, the enzyme is completely inactive. Xanthine oxidase normally catalyzes the final steps of purine degradation—converting hypoxanthine → xanthine and xanthine → uric acid—so loss of its activity is the reason purine catabolism and uric-acid homeostasis are disturbed in molybdenum cofactor deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAN2B1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MAN2B1 gene, which is mapped to chromosome 19p13.1-p13.2, cause the lysosomal storage disorder alpha-mannosidosis. Individuals with alpha-mannosidosis develop progressive skeletal-muscle involvement that often produces proximal muscle weakness and a characteristic waddling (myopathic) gait, linking the mutation in MAN2B1 to the described clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The first gene shown to cause Adams–Oliver syndrome is ARHGAP31, which is located at cytogenetic band 3q13.31 on human chromosome 3; pathogenic mutations in ARHGAP31 lead to the characteristic scalp and limb defects of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AGK",
    "long_answer": "Sengers syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the AGK gene (acylglycerol kinase, mitochondrial). AGK encodes the mitochondrial lipid kinase acylglycerol kinase, which phosphorylates mono- and diacylglycerols to lysophosphatidic and phosphatidic acids—key intermediates in cardiolipin biosynthesis. Disrupted cardiolipin metabolism impairs mitochondrial energy production and leads to cardiomyopathy, linking AGK to cardiovascular disease and establishing it as the gene responsible for Sengers syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kew Gardens, Queens",
    "long_answer": "The first recognized outbreak of rickettsialpox was documented in 1946 among residents of the Kew Gardens neighborhood in the borough of Queens, New York City. Epidemiologic investigation by Dr. Robert Huebner traced the febrile, vesicular illness to mites (Liponyssoides sanguineus) carried by house mice in apartment buildings there, establishing Kew Gardens as the locus of the initial rickettsialpox outbreak."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ISCU",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary myopathy with exercise intolerance (a mitochondrial myopathy presenting as lifelong exercise intolerance) is caused by mutations in the ISCU gene, which lies on chromosome 12q24. ISCU encodes the scaffold protein needed for iron–sulfur-cluster assembly in mitochondrial respiratory-chain enzymes; defective ISCU impairs complexes I, II and III, producing the characteristic exercise-intolerant myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "Börjeson-Forssman-Lehmann syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the PHF6 gene located on the X chromosome. Because males are hemizygous for the X chromosome, a single mutated PHF6 allele produces the full clinical picture, while heterozygous females are typically unaffected or only mildly affected depending on X-inactivation. This pattern of transmission is therefore classified as X-linked recessive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1q25–q31",
    "long_answer": "Stocco dos Santos syndrome is the original description of camptodactyly-arthropathy-coxa vara-pericarditis (CACP) syndrome. The disorder results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PRG4 (lubricin) gene, which was mapped to the chromosomal locus 1q25–q31. Hence, the camptodactyly seen in Stocco dos Santos syndrome is linked to the 1q25–q31 region of chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AK2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the AK2 gene (adenylate kinase 2) produce reticular dysgenesis, the most severe form of severe combined immunodeficiency. AK2 deficiency blocks both myeloid and lymphoid hematopoiesis, leading to agranulocytosis and lymphopenia, and also disrupts inner-ear development, resulting in congenital sensorineural deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NDP",
    "long_answer": "The X-linked form of congenital zonular (lamellar) cataract accompanied by nystagmus is produced by pathogenic variants in the NDP gene on Xp11.3, which encodes the signaling protein norrin. Mutations in NDP explain both the ocular phenotype and the X-linked pattern of inheritance observed in affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia results from recessive mutations in the TTC7A gene (tetratricopeptide repeat domain 7A). TTC7A is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 2 at band 2p16.3, establishing chromosome 2 as the chromosomal location of the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Absence of fingerprints-congenital milia syndrome (also called adermatoglyphia) is caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the skin-specific isoform of the SMARCAD1 gene. SMARCAD1 is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 4 (4q22.3). Thus, the responsible gene resides on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Gunther disease (congenital erythropoietic porphyria) is caused by autosomal-recessive mutations in the UROS gene, which encodes uroporphyrinogen III synthase. The UROS gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 10 (10q25.2-q26.3), making chromosome 10 the chromosomal location responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "USH2A",
    "long_answer": "The USH2A gene is mutated in Usher syndrome type II. It encodes the trans-membrane protein usherin, which is essential for the proper development and long-term maintenance of stereocilia in inner-ear hair cells and of photoreceptor cells in the retina. Loss-of-function variants in USH2A disrupt these structures, leading to the characteristic hearing impairment and progressive retinitis pigmentosa seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Worth syndrome (endosteal hyperostosis) results from mutations in the LRP5 gene. LRP5 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 11 (region 11q13), so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Isolated microphthalmia type 1 (MCOP1) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the homeobox transcription factor gene VSX2 (also known as CHX10). The VSX2/CHX10 gene is mapped to the 14q24.3 cytogenetic band, placing it on human chromosome 14."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The common juvenile form of Batten disease (juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis) is caused by pathogenic variants in the CLN3 gene. The human CLN3 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 16p12.1 on chromosome 16, making this chromosome the locus for the gene underlying the most prevalent form of Batten disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RPE65 gene, which is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (band 1p31), are a well-established cause of Leber congenital amaurosis. This chromosomal location was determined through linkage and positional cloning studies, and the gene’s role has been confirmed by successful gene-replacement therapies that restore vision in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The Wilms tumor protein is encoded by the WT1 gene, which is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p13). This chromosomal location was identified through linkage and cytogenetic studies of patients with Wilms tumor and related syndromes, firmly establishing chromosome 11 as the site of the WT1 gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prenatal ultrasonography",
    "long_answer": "Fetal (prenatal) ultrasound can visualize characteristic skeletal abnormalities—such as bowing, fractures, and cortical thickening—caused by autosomal-dominant COL1A1 pathogenic variants (e.g., in osteogenesis imperfecta or Caffey disease). Therefore, prenatal ultrasonography is the key diagnostic tool used to identify affected pregnancies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoachondroplasia is an autosomal-dominant skeletal dysplasia produced by pathogenic variants in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene. The COMP gene is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 19 (region 19p13.1-p12), so chromosome 19 is the chromosomal location responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous-gene deletion at band 11p13 on chromosome 11 that removes or disrupts the WT1 and PAX6 genes. Loss of these genes produces the characteristic features—Wilms tumor, aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, and intellectual disability—linking the disorder specifically to chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of Alagille syndrome result from pathogenic variants or deletions in the JAG1 gene, which is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 20 (band 20p12). Mutations in this chromosome-20-encoded gene disrupt JAG1–NOTCH signaling, leading to the characteristic multisystem features of Alagille syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kenji Bandō",
    "long_answer": "Kenji Bandō wrote the story that became the Japanese film and novel “Taiyō no Uta” (Midnight Sun), in which the male protagonist falls in love with Kaoru Amane, a girl suffering from xeroderma pigmentosum—a DNA-repair disorder that makes exposure to ultraviolet light potentially lethal. The plot revolves around her need to avoid sunlight and the romance that develops in spite of this medical condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The gene C4orf26 (Chromosome 4 open reading frame 26), whose loss-of-function mutations cause Kohlschütter–Tönz syndrome, is physically located on human chromosome 4 (band 4q21). Hence chromosome 4 harbors this disease-associated gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF11 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in KIF11 (kinesin family member 11) are known to cause the syndrome of microcephaly with or without chorioretinopathy, lymphedema, and mental retardation (MCLMR). A characteristic neuroimaging finding in affected patients is microlissencephaly—a markedly reduced brain size with a simplified or nearly smooth cortical pattern—together with congenital peripheral lymphedema. Thus, a pathogenic KIF11 mutation is the established genetic cause of this rare form of microlissencephaly accompanied by congenital lymphedema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTPN11",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the PTPN11 gene (coding for the SHP-2 tyrosine-phosphatase) are the most frequent cause of Noonan syndrome, a condition whose cardinal clinical features include proportionate short stature and characteristic craniofacial dysmorphisms such as a broad forehead, hypertelorism, down-slanting palpebral fissures and low-set ears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The major Alzheimer’s disease susceptibility gene is APOE (apolipoprotein E). The ε4 allele of APOE markedly increases the risk of late-onset Alzheimer’s disease, and this gene is located on chromosome 19q13.2. Hence, chromosome 19 harbors the principal genetic locus most strongly linked to Alzheimer’s susceptibility in the general population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The glypican-1 gene (GPC1), whose variation has been implicated in susceptibility to biliary atresia, is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q37)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXC2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FOXC2 gene, which encodes a forkhead-box transcription factor, cause lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome. A hallmark of this condition is distichiasis—a congenital double row of eyelashes—making FOXC2 the gene most clearly associated with this clinical feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Macroglossia seen in α-mannosidosis is caused by deficiency of lysosomal α-mannosidase encoded by the MAN2B1 gene. MAN2B1 is located on chromosome 19 (region 19p13–q13); therefore, chromosome 19 contains the gene responsible for this mannosidosis-related form of macroglossia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "A gain-of-function mutation in the FGFR3 gene (most commonly the p.G380R substitution) underlies achondroplasia, the prototypic skeletal dysplasia characterized by rhizomelic shortening of the long bones—including marked shortening of the femur—due to impaired endochondral ossification at the growth plates."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EPM2A and NHLRC1",
    "long_answer": "Lafora disease (progressive myoclonic epilepsy type 2) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in two genes: EPM2A, which encodes the dual-specificity phosphatase laforin, and NHLRC1 (also called EPM2B), which encodes the E3 ubiquitin-ligase malin. Defects in either gene disrupt glycogen metabolism, leading to accumulation of insoluble polyglucosan (Lafora) bodies in neurons and other tissues, producing the characteristic neurodegeneration and myoclonic seizures of Lafora disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gliosarcoma",
    "long_answer": "Gliosarcoma, a rare variant of glioblastoma containing both glial and sarcomatous components, is documented to metastasize extracranially (e.g., to lung, liver, bone) more often than other cerebral gliomas. The sarcomatous element is believed to confer greater vascular invasiveness, which underlies its higher propensity for systemic spread compared with typical gliomas confined largely to the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The gene most commonly implicated in hereditary spastic paraplegia that alters endoplasmic reticulum morphology is ATL1 (atlastin-1, SPG3A). ATL1 encodes a dynamin-like GTPase that mediates homotypic fusion of ER tubules; mutations disturb ER network structure and cause the early-onset, autosomal-dominant form of HSP. The ATL1 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 14 (region 14q22.1), so chromosome 14 carries this disease-linked locus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The nuclear-encoded gene COX20 (also known as FAM36A) produces the COX20 protein, an inner-mitochondrial-membrane assembly factor essential for the biogenesis and stabilization of cytochrome c oxidase subunit II (COX2). The human COX20 gene is located on Chromosome 1 (band 1q44), so Chromosome 1 contains the gene responsible for this function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EFEMP1 (Fibulin-3)",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal-dominant, hereditary form of macular dystrophy known as Doyne honeycomb retinal dystrophy / malattia Leventinese is produced by a missense mutation (classically p.R345W) in the EFEMP1 gene, which encodes the extracellular-matrix protein fibulin-3. Mutant EFEMP1 accumulates beneath the retinal pigment epithelium, forming drusen that progress to macular dystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTRK1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the NTRK1 gene, which encodes the high-affinity receptor (TrkA) for nerve growth factor (NGF), cause Hereditary Sensory and Autonomic Neuropathy type IV (congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis). Loss-of-function variants impair NGF signaling required for survival and differentiation of sensory and autonomic neurons, leading to the characteristic neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Familial Mediterranean fever is caused by mutations in the MEFV gene, which encodes the inflammation-regulating protein pyrin. The MEFV gene is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (16p13.3); defects here lead to the recurrent peritoneal inflammation characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the IRF6 gene cause Van der Woude syndrome, an autosomal-dominant disorder whose cardinal features include cleft lip and/or cleft palate (malformations of the palate) and lower-lip pits. IRF6 encodes a transcription factor essential for epithelial differentiation during craniofacial development; when mutated, it disrupts palatogenesis, producing the characteristic palatal anomalies seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Adolphe Franceschetti",
    "long_answer": "Swiss ophthalmologist Adolphe Franceschetti was the first to describe the constellation of optic-nerve coloboma (papillary dysplasia) and renal malformations that later became known as Papillorenal or renal-coloboma syndrome. Molecular studies have since shown that many patients with this syndrome carry heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the transcription-factor gene PAX2, confirming the genetic basis of the disorder he originally delineated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex arises from mutations in either the TSC1 or TSC2 genes. Mutations in TSC2, located on human chromosome 16p13.3, are consistently associated with a more severe clinical phenotype than mutations in TSC1 on chromosome 9. Hence, chromosome 16 harbors the gene whose defects produce the more severe form of tuberous sclerosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Fumarase (fumarate hydratase) deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the FH gene. The FH gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 1q42.1 on human chromosome 1, so chromosome 1 is the chromosomal location responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Primary congenital glaucoma (PCG) is most frequently caused by pathogenic variants in genes such as CYP1B1, LTBP2, TEK, FOXC1, and PITX2. By contrast, the only gene that has been conclusively linked, by numerous independent studies, to Peters-plus syndrome is B3GLCT (formerly B3GALTL), an autosomal-recessive glycosyltransferase gene. Pathogenic B3GLCT variants produce the full Peters-plus phenotype (Peters anomaly with short stature, brachydactyly, developmental delay, craniofacial anomalies, etc.).\n\nNo peer-reviewed reports or databases (e.g., OMIM, ClinVar, HGMD) document B3GLCT mutations causing isolated primary congenital glaucoma, nor do genes classically mutated in PCG (CYP1B1, LTBP2, TEK, FOXC1, PITX2, MYOC) produce Peters-plus syndrome. Therefore, according to established genetic evidence, there is currently no mutation known to be shared between primary congenital glaucoma and Peters-plus syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary haemochromatosis, an iron-overload disorder that can disturb thermoregulation, is most commonly caused by mutations in the HFE gene. The HFE gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p21.3-p22). Consequently, chromosome 6 carries the gene responsible for this iron-related thermoregulatory condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nephrolithiasis (kidney stones)",
    "long_answer": "In primary hyperparathyroidism, excess parathyroid hormone causes hypercalcemia and marked hypercalciuria. The high urinary calcium fosters the formation of calcium-containing renal calculi (nephrolithiasis). Recurrent or large kidney stones can obstruct the urinary tract, cause infection, and progressively damage renal parenchyma, ultimately leading to chronic kidney disease and renal failure. Thus, nephrolithiasis is a common and clinically significant complication of primary hyperparathyroidism that can progress to kidney failure if untreated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pancreatic alpha cells",
    "long_answer": "In multiple endocrine neoplasia type 1, one possible pancreatic tumor is a glucagonoma. This tumor arises from pancreatic alpha cells, which normally secrete the hormone glucagon. Excess glucagon stimulates hepatic gluconeogenesis and glycogenolysis, opposing insulin action and producing marked hyperglycemia. Therefore, the alpha cell is the pancreatic cell type whose hormone secretion can lead to hyperglycemia in MEN 1 patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile Neuronal Ceroid Lipofuscinosis (Batten disease, JNCL) is caused by mutations in the CLN3 gene. The CLN3 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 16p12.1 on human chromosome 16, establishing chromosome 16 as the chromosomal location responsible for JNCL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Keratinocytes",
    "long_answer": "Erythrokeratodermia variabilis is caused by mutations in connexin genes (e.g., GJB3/Connexin-31 and GJB4/Connexin-30.3) that encode gap-junction proteins highly expressed in epidermal keratinocytes. The defective connexins impair intercellular communication among these keratinocytes, leading to the characteristic transient erythema and hyperkeratotic plaques of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Northern epilepsy syndrome (also known as Finnish progressive epilepsy with mental retardation or EPMR) is produced by recessive mutations in the CLN8 gene, which is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 8 (region 8p23.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Beta thalassemia results from mutations in the beta-globin (HBB) gene, which is located on the short arm of chromosome 11 (region 11p15.5). Alterations in this gene disrupt β-globin chain production, leading to the characteristic imbalance of globin chains that underlies the clinical manifestations of beta thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "γ-Secretase complex",
    "long_answer": "NCSTN (nicastrin), PSEN1 (presenilin-1), and PSENEN (PEN-2) encode core components of the γ-secretase complex. Loss-of-function mutations in any of these subunits impair γ-secretase activity, disrupt Notch signaling in follicular epithelium, and are a well-established genetic cause of familial hidradenitis suppurativa."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cryptorchidism",
    "long_answer": "The reported Primrose syndrome patient had congenital cryptorchidism (an undescended testis), a well-established risk factor that predisposes germ cells to malignant transformation and thus likely contributed to the development of his testicular tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Hypochondroplasia is caused by activating mutations in the FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3) gene, which regulates bone growth during embryonic development. FGFR3 is localized to the short arm of human chromosome 4 (4p16.3); therefore, chromosome 4 harbors the gene whose alteration leads to hypochondroplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Canavan disease, a leukodystrophy that characteristically presents with megalencephaly (macrocephaly), is caused by pathogenic variants in the ASPA gene located on human chromosome 17 (17p13.2). Mutations here impair aspartoacylase activity, leading to spongiform white-matter degeneration and the clinical picture of macrocephaly-associated leukodystrophy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HBA1 and HBA2",
    "long_answer": "ATR-16 (α-thalassemia/mental-retardation) syndrome is produced by large deletions on chromosome 16p13.3 that remove the α-globin gene cluster. The critical genes lost for the haematological phenotype are the two closely linked α-globin genes, HBA1 and HBA2; their absence abolishes α-globin synthesis, producing the α-thalassemia component of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Spastic paraplegia type 11 (SPG11) results from pathogenic variants in the SPG11 gene (encoding the protein spatacsin). Cytogenetic mapping places SPG11 on the long arm of human chromosome 15 (15q21.1), establishing chromosome 15 as the locus responsible for this hereditary spastic paraplegia subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6q21–q22",
    "long_answer": "Craniometaphyseal dysplasia exists in two genetic forms. The autosomal-dominant form is linked to ANKH on chromosome 5 and is generally milder. The autosomal-recessive form is clinically much more severe, often causing extreme skull bone overgrowth with optic-canal narrowing and consequent visual impairment. Linkage studies have mapped this recessive, severe form to the locus on chromosome 6q21–q22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochrane Collaboration",
    "long_answer": "A Cochrane Database of Systematic Reviews analysis concluded that conjunctival autograft surgery was significantly less likely to show pterygium recurrence at 6 months than amniotic membrane transplantation, highlighting lower risk ratios for recurrence in the autograft group."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNB1",
    "long_answer": "Adult-onset autosomal dominant leukodystrophy (ADLD), a disorder marked by progressive central nervous system demyelination together with prominent autonomic failure (e.g., orthostatic hypotension, bladder and bowel dysfunction), is produced by a genomic duplication of the LMNB1 gene. The duplicated LMNB1 allele leads to over-expression of the nuclear envelope protein lamin-B1, which disrupts myelin gene regulation, resulting in the characteristic demyelination and autonomic dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Niemann–Pick disease type C is caused in the great majority of cases by mutations in the NPC1 gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 18 (band 18q11.2). Defects in NPC1 disrupt intracellular cholesterol trafficking, leading to the characteristic lipid accumulation seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mutations in GALNT3, FGF23, and KL (Klotho)",
    "long_answer": "The peri-articular, tumor-like form of calcinosis cutis—known as tumoral calcinosis and also encountered as a complication in patients on chronic renal dialysis—has been linked to loss-of-function mutations in three phosphate-regulating genes: GALNT3, FGF23, and KL (Klotho). These mutations disrupt the FGF23-mediated pathway that maintains phosphate homeostasis, leading to hyperphosphatemia and massive calcium-phosphate deposition around joints."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Schinzel–Giedion syndrome is caused by de-novo pathogenic variants in the SETBP1 gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 18 (18q12.3). Because SETBP1 resides on an autosome, it is not present on either human sex chromosome (X or Y)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome and chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The Fragile X mental-retardation 1 (FMR1) gene that causes X-linked intellectual disability resides on the X chromosome, while the huntingtin (HTT) gene responsible for Huntington’s disease is located on chromosome 4 (locus 4p16.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Spastic paraplegia autosomal dominant type 42 (SPG42) is caused by pathogenic variants in the SLC33A1 gene, which encodes the acetyl-CoA transporter 1. The SLC33A1 gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 3 at band q25.33; therefore, the gene responsible for SPG42 resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The ABO gene, located at 9q34 on human chromosome 9, encodes the glycosyl-transferases that determine ABO blood groups. Individuals with blood group O (a result of a specific ABO allele) show markedly reduced Plasmodium falciparum rosetting of infected erythrocytes, diminishing microvascular obstruction and thereby lowering the risk of severe malaria. Hence, the chromosomal location relevant to this rosetting-reducing gene is chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Harkhuf",
    "long_answer": "The Old Kingdom governor Harkhuf, whose tomb inscription records several expeditions to Nubia, famously returned with a living pygmy (dwarf) for Pharaoh Pepi II. His autobiography at Qubbet el-Hawa describes the mission and the king’s delight, making Harkhuf the official historically linked to bringing a pygmy—stature sometimes associated with microcephaly—from Nubia to Egypt."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tyrosinase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Loss of the copper-dependent enzyme tyrosinase stops the conversion of tyrosine to DOPA-melanin, producing oculocutaneous albinism. Without melanin, the skin lacks pigment, so underlying blood vessels give it a pink-reddish tint and the skin becomes extremely sensitive to sunlight and other cutaneous insults."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OSGEP",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OSGEP gene, which encodes the catalytic subunit of the KEOPS complex responsible for N6-threonyl-carbamoylation (t6A) of adenosine-37 in ANN-type tRNAs, have been repeatedly identified in patients with Galloway–Mowat syndrome. Loss-of-function mutations impair t6A modification, leading to the neuro-renal manifestations characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Meleda disease (autosomal-recessive palmoplantar keratoderma) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLURP1 gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 8 (region 8q24). Hence, the responsible gene resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vaginal bleeding",
    "long_answer": "Choriocarcinoma is a highly vascular malignant trophoblastic tumor that aggressively invades uterine tissue and blood vessels. This invasion frequently produces profuse vaginal bleeding, which can be severe enough to cause significant blood loss and resultant hypovolemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SALL1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the transcription-factor gene SALL1 cause Townes-Brocks syndrome, whose core features include external ear anomalies such as pre-auricular pits or tags and a high frequency of renal malformations. Unlike branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome, clinically significant hearing loss is typically absent or mild, so the presence of preauricular pits with renal disease but no hearing impairment points to a pathogenic SALL1 mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACOX1",
    "long_answer": "Acyl-CoA oxidase deficiency (also called pseudoneonatal adrenoleukodystrophy) is caused by pathogenic variants in the ACOX1 gene, which encodes peroxisomal straight-chain acyl-CoA oxidase 1—the first enzyme of the peroxisomal β-oxidation pathway. Loss of ACOX1 activity blocks degradation of very-long-chain fatty acids, leading to their pathological accumulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iron-deficiency anemia",
    "long_answer": "Among patients with inflammatory bowel disease, a very common extraintestinal complication is iron-deficiency anemia. Chronic intestinal bleeding and impaired iron absorption lead to iron depletion, resulting in reduced hemoglobin synthesis and anemia, which manifests outside the gastrointestinal tract."
}

{
    "short_answer": "László Seitelberger",
    "long_answer": "László Seitelberger, a Hungarian-born neurologist and neuropathologist, was one of the first investigators to describe and analyse infantile neuroaxonal dystrophy (later linked to PLA2G6 mutations). In the early literature this childhood neurodegenerative disorder was frequently mistaken for a form of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis because both conditions show intracellular autofluorescent storage material. Seitelberger’s clinicopathological studies were pivotal in recognising it as a distinct disease, separating it from the NCLs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The inherited form of triphalangeal thumb maps to the 7q36 region of chromosome 7. Mutations or duplications in the ZRS regulatory element within the LMBR1 gene on this chromosome alter Sonic hedgehog (SHH) signaling during limb development, producing a thumb with three phalanges that often necessitates corrective plastic surgery."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLA (α-galactosidase A)",
    "long_answer": "Fabry disease, an X-linked lysosomal storage disorder that can manifest as coronary vasospasm and variant (Prinzmetal) angina, is caused by pathogenic mutations in the GLA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-galactosidase A. Loss-of-function mutations in GLA lead to accumulation of globotriaosylceramide in vascular smooth-muscle and endothelial cells, predisposing affected patients to the vasospastic chest pain characteristic of variant angina."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOG gene",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the NOG gene, which encodes the bone morphogenetic protein antagonist noggin, cause disorders such as stapes ankylosis with broad thumbs and toes/multiple synostosis syndrome. These conditions present with congenital, non-progressive conductive hearing loss due to stapes fixation together with characteristic skeletal deformities (digital broadening, joint fusions, other bone abnormalities)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Two cases",
    "long_answer": "According to the case reports catalogued in the dysmorphology and OMIM literature, the combination of congenital distichiasis with structural congenital heart defects and mixed peripheral vascular malformations has been documented in only two individual patients. No additional patients or families have been reported since those original descriptions, making the total number of published cases two."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Tangier disease results from loss-of-function mutations in the ATP-binding cassette transporter A1 (ABCA1) gene. The ABCA1 gene resides on chromosome 9q31; defects in this transporter impair cholesterol efflux from cells, leading to the markedly low HDL levels characteristic of Tangier disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Myxoid liposarcomas are commonly driven by the t(12;16)(q13;p11) translocation that fuses the FUS gene on chromosome 16p11 with the DDIT3 gene on chromosome 12q13. Thus, the FUS gene resides on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clubfoot",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis multiplex congenita often involves severe, rigid deformities of the feet; congenital talipes equinovarus (clubfoot) occurs in the majority of affected infants and is therefore regarded as a common birth defect associated with arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neurons",
    "long_answer": "After a traumatic brain injury, excessive glutamatergic signalling can cause hyperactivity of neurons—particularly excitatory cortical and hippocampal pyramidal cells. This sustained neuronal overexcitation produces abnormal synchronous firing and network hyperexcitability, a well-established mechanism that can progress to post-traumatic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A4",
    "long_answer": "Pendred syndrome, in which children typically progress to profound sensorineural hearing loss by about 10 years of age, is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC26A4 gene (encoding the anion transporter pendrin)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GIF",
    "long_answer": "The human GIF gene encodes gastric intrinsic factor, the glycoprotein that binds vitamin B12 (cobalamin) in the duodenum and carries it to the ileum for absorption. Crystal structures of intrinsic factor–B12 complexes have been solved and deposited in the Protein Data Bank, confirming the gene-to-protein relationship."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SRD5A2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SRD5A2 gene, which encodes the type-2 5-α-reductase enzyme that converts testosterone to the more potent androgen dihydrotestosterone (DHT), cause 5-α-reductase deficiency. Affected 46,XY individuals are under-virilized at birth and may present with predominantly female external genitalia, a condition that older literature labeled “male pseudohermaphroditism.” Thus, loss-of-function mutations in SRD5A2 underlie the disorder historically referred to by that term."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBA1A",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the TUBA1A gene, located on chromosome 12q13, disrupt α-tubulin function and neuronal microtubule dynamics. This impairs neuronal migration during cortical development and produces the tubulinopathy form of lissencephaly (often called LIS3). Hence, TUBA1A mutations on chromosome 12 are a well-established genetic cause of lissencephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The key negative regulator of prolactin release is dopamine acting through the pituitary D2 dopamine receptor. The gene encoding this receptor (DRD2), which governs dopamine-mediated inhibition of prolactin secretion, is located on human chromosome 11 (band 11q22-q23)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Abdallat–Davis–Farrage syndrome has been documented in siblings born to non-affected, consanguineous parents, with males and females equally affected. This segregation pattern—affected children of healthy carrier parents and no vertical transmission—establishes that the disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive manner."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Left upper quadrant",
    "long_answer": "The spleen normally resides high in the left upper quadrant of the abdomen, tucked beneath the left hemidiaphragm and protected by the 9th–11th ribs. In Felty’s syndrome—characterized by rheumatoid arthritis, neutropenia, and splenomegaly—the organ enlarges in this same left upper quadrant location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTC gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OTC (ornithine transcarbamylase) gene disrupt the urea cycle enzyme ornithine transcarbamylase, preventing efficient conversion of ammonia to urea. This enzymatic block causes toxic accumulation of ammonia in the bloodstream, the hallmark of the most common inherited urea-cycle disorder, ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Heterozygous",
    "long_answer": "Fryns-Aftimos syndrome is caused by a single pathogenic variant present in only one of the two ACTB alleles. Reported patients harbor de-novo or dominantly inherited heterozygous mutations in ACTB; no homozygous or compound-heterozygous cases have been documented. Hence, the disease-causing ACTB mutation is heterozygous."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Inflammation vs inheritance",
    "long_answer": "Sporadic inclusion body myositis is an acquired, inflammatory myopathy (with endomysial T-cell infiltrates), whereas hereditary inclusion body myopathies are genetically inherited, non-inflammatory muscle disorders; the key distinction is the presence of immune-mediated inflammation in IBM versus purely genetic (inherited) causation in hIBM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CAPN3 gene, located at 15q15 on human chromosome 15, cause limb-girdle muscular dystrophy type 2A (LGMD2A). CAPN3 encodes the skeletal-muscle–specific protease calpain-3, and loss-of-function variants impair muscle fiber maintenance, producing the characteristic proximal muscle weakness of this LGMD subtype. Hence, the critical LGMD-linked gene resides on chromosome 15."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral neuropathy",
    "long_answer": "Conditions that impair fat absorption—such as cholestatic liver disease, cystic fibrosis, or biliary atresia—can lead to vitamin E deficiency. Lack of vitamin E allows oxidative damage to long-axon neurons and posterior column pathways, frequently manifesting as peripheral neuropathy (loss of vibration/position sense, distal weakness, paresthesias). Thus, peripheral neuropathy is a well-recognized neurological complication of vitamin E deficiency arising from these malabsorptive disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OAT gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OAT gene, which encodes ornithine aminotransferase, cause gyrate atrophy of the choroid and retina. Loss of this enzyme blocks normal ornithine metabolism, producing marked hyper-ornithinemia in blood and leading to progressive retinal degeneration that culminates in blindness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The first and most widely cited autosomal-dominant locus for hereditary spastic paraplegia (SPG3A/ATL1) was mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 14 (14q22-q24). Mutations in this chromosome-14 gene are a well-established cause of classical hereditary spastic paraplegia, confirming that the responsible gene is located on chromosome 14."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Jackson–Weiss syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants of the FGFR2 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2) gene. FGFR2 is mapped to band q26 on human chromosome 10 (10q26). Thus, chromosome 10 is the chromosome that harbors the causative gene for this craniosynostosis syndrome affecting the occipital bone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The PEX6 gene, whose pathogenic variants cause Heimler syndrome (a mild peroxisome-biogenesis disorder), is mapped to human chromosome 6 (cytogenetic band 6p21). Mutations in this chromosome-6 gene impair the PEX6 ATPase needed for peroxisomal protein import, leading to the clinical features of Heimler syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HRAS",
    "long_answer": "Gain-of-function mutations in the HRAS gene, located on chromosome 11p15.5, cause Costello syndrome. This Rasopathy presents with characteristic facial features, cardiac defects, growth delay, and moderate to severe intellectual disability, making HRAS mutation a well-established genetic cause of a disorder that includes intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TYR",
    "long_answer": "The TYR gene, which encodes the enzyme tyrosinase, has been repeatedly identified in genetic studies of vitiligo. Tyrosinase is a key catalytic enzyme in the melanin-biosynthetic pathway within melanocytes and is not an immune-system protein. Variants of TYR alter melanin production and also serve as autoantigenic targets in vitiligo, explaining the gene’s association with the disease while its primary biological role remains melanin synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes 2 and 4",
    "long_answer": "Gilbert syndrome is caused by mutations in the UGT1A1 gene, which is mapped to human chromosome 2 (band 2q37). The gene encoding serum albumin (ALB) resides on human chromosome 4 (band 4q13). Thus, the Gilbert-syndrome gene is on chromosome 2, whereas the albumin gene is on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Focal palmoplantar and gingival keratosis is a hereditary palmoplantar keratoderma. The disorder is characterized by well-circumscribed hyperkeratotic plaques (callosities) confined to sites of pressure on the palms and soles and by a whitish or verrucous hyperkeratosis of the attached gingiva and, occasionally, other oral mucosa. It does not manifest as round or oval, thin plaques distributed mainly on the trunk. Truncal macules or plaques are not part of the clinical picture that defines this entity. Therefore the statement describing trunk-predominant, discrete patches or thin plaques is inconsistent with the established clinical presentation of focal palmoplantar and gingival keratosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Apert syndrome—classed among first-branchial-arch (mandibular) disorders—results from activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor-2 (FGFR2) gene. FGFR2 is located on chromosome 10q25-q26, and the altered receptor disturbs neural-crest/mesenchymal signalling during development of first arch derivatives, producing the characteristic craniofacial malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary hypertension",
    "long_answer": "In patients with emphysema, chronic destruction of alveolar tissue causes hypoxic vasoconstriction and loss of pulmonary capillaries, raising pulmonary arterial pressure. This comorbid pulmonary hypertension increases right-ventricular afterload and frequently progresses to cor pulmonale, a form of right-sided heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The human UMPS gene encodes the bifunctional enzyme orotate phosphoribosyl-transferase/orotidine-5′-phosphate decarboxylase, which catalyzes the final two reactions that generate uridine monophosphate (UMP) in de novo pyrimidine biosynthesis. Cytogenetic and sequence mapping place the UMPS gene on chromosome 3 (region 3q13-q21)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Pascual-Castroviejo syndrome type 1 is the X-linked form of the blepharophimosis–intellectual-disability (Ohdo) spectrum and is caused by pathogenic variants in the MED12 gene. MED12 is situated on the long arm of the human X chromosome (region Xq13), so the gene responsible for this syndrome is carried on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC9",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the ABCC9 gene, which encodes the SUR2 regulatory subunit of ATP-sensitive potassium channels, cause Cantú syndrome. One of the hallmark clinical features of Cantú syndrome is congenital generalized hypertrichosis that is especially pronounced on the eyebrows and eyelashes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The myeloperoxidase (MPO) gene—which, when deficient, alters the crosstalk between neutrophils and dendritic cells in lymph nodes—is situated on chromosome 17 (band 17q23.1)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cotton dust",
    "long_answer": "Inhalation of cotton (and other vegetable-fiber) dust by textile workers produces byssinosis, an occupational pneumonitis. With continued exposure the airway inflammation and obstruction characteristic of byssinosis can progress to chronic bronchitis and ultimately emphysema."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Testosterone",
    "long_answer": "Because 3β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type II is needed to convert Δ5 precursors to the Δ4 steroid androstenedione, its absence blocks the testicular pathway that produces testosterone. Genetic (46,XY) males with 3β-HSD congenital adrenal hyperplasia therefore have markedly reduced testosterone levels, leading to undervirilization, cryptorchidism and later impaired spermatogenesis—key aspects of compromised testicular function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The HEXA gene, located on human chromosome 15 (15q23-q24), encodes the α-subunit of β-hexosaminidase A. Pathogenic mutations in HEXA lead to Tay-Sachs disease, a lysosomal storage disorder characterized by GM2 ganglioside accumulation and severe degeneration of the central nervous system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paraplegia",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neurocutaneous angioma (Von Hippel–Lindau disease) can produce hemangioblastomas within the spinal cord. Expansion or hemorrhage of these vascular tumors compresses the descending corticospinal tracts, and this may result in paraplegia—loss of motor function in both lower limbs—which is a well-documented neurological complication of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MTHFR",
    "long_answer": "A common missense mutation (e.g., C677T) in the methylenetetrahydrofolate-reductase (MTHFR) gene disrupts folate metabolism and raises homocysteine levels during early embryogenesis. Impaired folate availability hampers closure of the cranial neural tube, producing severe defects such as anencephaly, in which the fetus develops little or no functional brain tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse that results from defects in primary cilia has been linked to pathogenic variants in the DZIP1 gene, which encodes a protein required for ciliogenesis in valve cells. The DZIP1 gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 13 (13q32), making chromosome 13 the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for this cilia-related form of mitral valve prolapse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mask-like facies",
    "long_answer": "In Pashayan (blepharo-naso-facial) syndrome, the combination of blepharophimosis, telecanthus, a broad flat nasal bridge and reduced facial expression makes the face appear rigid and expressionless; clinicians therefore describe it as having a ‘mask-like’ appearance, comparing the facies to a mask."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, which is located on the long arm (region 7q31.2) of chromosome 7. Defects in this chloride channel gene disrupt epithelial ion transport, leading to the characteristic pulmonary and pancreatic manifestations of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dihydropteridine reductase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "A deficiency of the enzyme dihydropteridine reductase (DHPR) impairs regeneration of the cofactor tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4), which is required for phenylalanine hydroxylase activity. This causes hyperphenylalaninemia, but, unlike classic phenylketonuria, it also blocks BH4-dependent synthesis of the neurotransmitters dopamine and serotonin. The resulting neurotransmitter deficiency produces progressive neurologic deterioration, seizures, and developmental delay despite dietary phenylalanine restriction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TTC7A gene (Tetratricopeptide repeat domain 7A). TTC7A is mapped to cytogenetic band 2p16.2, locating the disease-causing gene on human chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRRT2",
    "long_answer": "A variant form of benign familial infantile epilepsy, often combined with paroxysmal kinesigenic dyskinesia, is caused by mutations in the PRRT2 gene (proline-rich transmembrane protein 2) located on chromosome 16p11.2. Pathogenic PRRT2 variants disrupt synaptic regulation, leading to the characteristic self-limited infantile seizures observed in this epilepsy variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZNHIT3",
    "long_answer": "PEHO syndrome (Progressive Encephalopathy with Edema, Hypsarrhythmia, and Optic atrophy) belongs to the Finnish disease heritage and is caused by biallelic mutations in the ZNHIT3 gene. A Finnish founder variant, c.92C>T (p.Ser31Leu), in ZNHIT3 disrupts the zinc-finger HIT-type protein it encodes, leading to the characteristic neurodevelopmental deterioration, infantile spasms, edema, and optic atrophy seen in PEHO syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A",
    "long_answer": "A heterozygous mutation in the TUBB4A gene (the DYT4 dystonia locus) has been identified in families with the extremely rare “whispering” form of spasmodic dysphonia. This mutation in the β-tubulin 4A protein disrupts microtubule function, producing a focal laryngeal dystonia that presents clinically as spasmodic dysphonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Familial chordoma susceptibility is linked to duplication or variants of the T (TBXT, brachyury) gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 6 at 6q27. Hence, chromosome 6 harbors the gene associated with inherited risk for chordoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Rabson–Mendenhall syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the INSR gene, which encodes the insulin receptor and underlies the syndrome’s profound insulin resistance. The INSR gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 19 (19p13.2), so chromosome 19 is the chromosome that carries the causative gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MITF",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the MITF (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor) gene impair melanocyte differentiation. The resulting deficiency of melanin causes generalized albinism of skin, intermittent dark (black) hair locks, and congenital sensorineural deafness—clinical features classically seen in Tietz/Waardenburg type 2 albinism-deafness syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "American Journal of Human Genetics",
    "long_answer": "Petrus J. Waardenburg formally delineated the condition later termed Waardenburg syndrome type 1 in his 1951 paper entitled “A new syndrome combining developmental anomalies of the eyelids, eyebrows and nose root with pigmentary defects of the iris and head hair and with congenital deafness,” published in the American Journal of Human Genetics. This inaugural description established the clinical entity and its characteristic features, making that journal the first venue in which the syndrome was described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1 gene",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line mutations in the NF1 gene (coding for neurofibromin) cause neurofibromatosis type 1. A common early sign of NF1 is infantile horizontal nystagmus produced by optic-pathway involvement, and the same mutation predisposes patients to optic pathway gliomas—a low-grade brain tumor. Thus, the NF1 gene mutation links nystagmus with a specific type of brain tumor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TTC7A gene (tetratricopeptide repeat domain 7A). The TTC7A locus is situated at 2p16 on human chromosome 2; therefore, the pathogenic mutation resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The glycogen storage disease involving the liver isoform of phosphorylase b kinase is caused by mutations in the PHKA2 gene, which encodes the α-subunit specific to the hepatic isoform of this enzyme. PHKA2 is located on the short arm of the X chromosome (Xp22). Thus, the relevant gene—and the disease it causes—maps to the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anemia",
    "long_answer": "Iron is required for hemoglobin synthesis; when iron stores are insufficient, hemoglobin production falls, causing iron-deficiency anemia. Severe anemia reduces the blood’s oxygen-carrying capacity, leading to tissue hypoxia, cardiac strain, heart failure, and, if uncorrected, death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Inherited chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis can be caused by loss-of-function mutations in IL-17F, the Th17-derived cytokine critical for mucocutaneous antifungal defense. The human IL17F gene is located at cytogenetic band 6p12 on chromosome 6; thus chromosome 6 contains the gene encoding the cytokine affected in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FXN GAA repeat expansion",
    "long_answer": "Friedreich ataxia is produced by a GAA trinucleotide repeat expansion in the frataxin (FXN) gene on chromosome 9. Loss of frataxin impairs mitochondrial iron handling, leading to degeneration of the spinocerebellar, dorsal column and corticospinal pathways as well as peripheral nerves. Clinically this causes progressive gait ataxia with absent or markedly reduced deep-tendon reflexes (hyporeflexia), together with other features such as pes cavus and hypertrophic cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human papillomavirus",
    "long_answer": "Human papillomavirus (HPV) is a prevalent sexually transmitted infection. Certain HPV types infect the cervical epithelium, producing inflammation that presents clinically as cervicitis, and persistent infection with high-risk oncogenic strains (e.g., HPV-16, HPV-18) drives malignant transformation of cervical cells, making HPV the principal etiologic agent of cervical cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOTCH3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the NOTCH3 gene cause CADASIL (Cerebral Autosomal Dominant Arteriopathy with Sub-cortical Infarcts and Leukoencephalopathy), a hereditary small-vessel disease. Defective NOTCH3 signaling injures smooth muscle cells in cerebral arterioles, leading to thickened and fragile vessel walls, recurrent ischemic strokes, and other cerebrovascular events."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Familial Mediterranean fever is caused by mutations in the MEFV gene, which is located at cytogenetic band 16p13.3 on human chromosome 16. This autosomal-recessive gene defect accounts for the high prevalence of the disease among Mediterranean populations, including Assyrians."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Vici syndrome is caused by recessive mutations in the EPG5 gene (Ectopic P-granules autophagy protein 5). The EPG5 locus is situated at 18q12.3 on human chromosome 18, so chromosome 18 harbors the gene responsible for the immunodeficiency seen in Vici syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type 0 results from deficiency of the liver isoform of glycogen synthase, encoded by the GYS2 gene. The human GYS2 gene is located on chromosome 12 (cytogenetic band 12p12). Therefore, chromosome 12 harbors the gene responsible for the deficient liver enzyme in GSD-0."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "The most prevalent form, leukocyte adhesion deficiency type I, arises from mutations in the ITGB2 gene encoding the β2-integrin (CD18) subunit. ITGB2 is mapped to human chromosome 21 (region 21q22.3), making chromosome 21 the chromosomal location responsible for the commonest LAD variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Factor V Leiden is caused by a single-nucleotide mutation (Arg506Gln) in the coagulation factor V gene (F5). The F5 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 1 at band 1q23, so the mutation resides on chromosome 1, leading to the hypercoagulable state characteristic of Factor V Leiden."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2 (connexin-26)",
    "long_answer": "The ichthyosis hystrix variant that presents with bilateral sensorineural hearing loss is caused by pathogenic mutations in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-26. GJB2 mutations disrupt intercellular communication in both epidermal keratinocytes and cochlear cells, producing the characteristic hyperkeratotic skin (ichthyosis hystrix) together with bilateral hearing impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL7A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL7A1 gene, which encodes type VII collagen, cause dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa. In this disorder, skin blisters form after minimal trauma and heal with pronounced atrophic or hypertrophic scarring, especially on exposed areas such as the face, leading to the characteristic facial scarring seen in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN1A",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SCN1A gene, which encodes the α-subunit of the neuronal voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.1, are the primary cause of Dravet syndrome—a severe developmental epileptic encephalopathy. Patients with SCN1A mutations typically present in infancy with febrile and afebrile seizures that evolve into multiple seizure types alongside cognitive and motor impairments, firmly linking SCN1A mutations to a neurological disorder in which seizures are a defining symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith-Wiedemann syndrome, an overgrowth disorder evident at birth (macrosomia, macroglossia, organomegaly), is produced by mutations or imprinting defects in the 11p15.5 region (genes such as CDKN1C, IGF2, H19) located on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Fibrin is generated when the blood-borne precursor fibrinogen is cleaved by thrombin. All three fibrinogen polypeptide chains—α (FGA), β (FGB), and γ (FGG)—are encoded by a tight gene cluster on the long arm of human chromosome 4 (region 4q28-q31). Thus chromosome 4 contains the genes whose products assemble into fibrin during blood clotting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "POLR3A and POLR3B",
    "long_answer": "Missense or truncating mutations in the POLR3A and POLR3B genes, which encode the two largest subunits of RNA polymerase III, cause the hypomyelinating leukodystrophy known as 4H/Pol III-related leukodystrophy. This disorder (4H, ADDH, TACH) was originally described as ‘CAMFAK-like’ because it presents with childhood-onset cerebellar ataxia, mental retardation, peripheral neuropathy and other features characteristic of CAMFAK syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Björnstad syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the BCS1L gene, which encodes a mitochondrial complex III assembly factor. The BCS1L gene is located on human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q33–q35); therefore, chromosome 2 carries the gene responsible for Björnstad syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq (long arm of the X chromosome)",
    "long_answer": "Recurrent microduplications involving the long arm of the X chromosome, particularly the Xq28 region (e.g., MECP2 duplication syndrome), are a well-established cause of X-linked intellectual disability. Thus, duplications on the Xq arm are characteristically associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Congenital aniridia is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PAX6 transcription factor gene. PAX6 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (region 11p13). Thus, the chromosome harboring the gene responsible for aniridia is chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Placental abruption",
    "long_answer": "Untreated maternal hypothyroidism increases the risk of placental abruption—the premature separation of the placenta from the uterine wall. Abruption acutely disrupts fetal oxygen and nutrient supply, leading to fetal hypoxia, stillbirth, or neonatal death, thereby contributing significantly to perinatal mortality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RMRP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RMRP gene, which encodes the RNA component of mitochondrial RNA-processing endoribonuclease (RNase MRP), cause cartilage-hair hypoplasia. This mitochondrial disorder disrupts chondrocyte growth and leads to metaphyseal chondrodysplasia, producing marked skeletal deformities such as short-limbed dwarfism, bowed long bones and spinal curvature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudohypoparathyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Several patients described with the syndrome of anonychia-onychodystrophy accompanied by brachydactyly type B and ectrodactyly have also been diagnosed with pseudohypoparathyroidism. This endocrine disorder (Albright hereditary osteodystrophy) characteristically produces shortening of the 4th and 5th metacarpal (and metatarsal) bones, the same skeletal feature noted in these individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Down syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is well documented to carry a high prevalence of ocular motility disorders: 20–50 % of affected individuals develop esotropia or another form of strabismus. Brown’s syndrome, by contrast, is itself a specific mechanical subtype of strabismus rather than a systemic condition that increases general risk. Consequently, Down syndrome is the syndrome classically associated with a higher overall risk of developing strabismus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "The classic triad of ectrodactyly, ectodermal dysplasia, and facial clefts defines EEC (Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal Dysplasia-Clefting) syndrome. Pathogenic variants in the TP63 gene, which encodes a p53-family transcription factor crucial for limb and ectodermal development, are the well-established molecular cause of EEC syndrome. Therefore, TP63 is the gene associated with this skin and developmental disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAM83H",
    "long_answer": "Amelogenesis imperfecta is a hereditary disorder of enamel formation. Among the genes identified, pathogenic variants in FAM83H (Family with sequence similarity 83 member H) are reported most often, making FAM83H mutations the single most frequent known genetic cause of this enamel-defect disorder. Loss-of-function or truncating mutations in FAM83H disrupt the maturation/mineralization stage of enamel development, producing the characteristic hypocalcified enamel seen in many affected families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the NBN (previously NBS1) gene. This gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 8 at band 8q21.3; therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Tricho-hepato-enteric syndrome is most commonly produced by loss-of-function mutations in the TTC37 gene. TTC37 transcripts are detectable in cells from amniotic fluid and other fetal tissues, and the gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 5 (band q15). Hence, chromosome 5 is the chromosome that harbours the gene expressed in amniotic fluid and linked to this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX6 gene are the established cause of aniridia, an eye condition characterized by partial or complete absence of the iris. Aniridia can lead to a spectrum of visual impairment—from relatively mild reduction in acuity to severe vision loss—due to associated complications such as cataract, glaucoma, and foveal hypoplasia. PAX6 encodes a transcription factor critical for ocular development; loss-of-function variants disrupt normal eye formation, explaining the variable but often serious impact on vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Familial Alzheimer’s disease type 3 is caused by autosomal‐dominant mutations in the presenilin-1 (PSEN1) gene, which is located on human chromosome 14 (band 14q24.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Acrofrontofacionasal dysostosis (also called acromelic frontonasal dysostosis) is now known, in most patients investigated, to result from heterozygous missense mutations in the gene ZSWIM6.  ZSWIM6 (Zinc-finger, SWIM-type-containing 6) is mapped to cytogenetic band 5q12 on the long arm of chromosome 5 (GRCh38 coordinates ≈ chr5:58.7-59.1 Mb).  The recurrent hotspot mutation p.Arg391Cys/His in this gene has been repeatedly documented in affected individuals, establishing ZSWIM6 as the principal gene responsible for the disorder.  Because the causative gene is situated on chromosome 5, the statement that the major gene associated with Acrofrontofacionasal dysostosis is located on chromosome 5 is supported by current genetic evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Waardenburg anophthalmia syndrome results from pathogenic variants in the PAX3 gene. PAX3 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q35–q36). Hence, the gene responsible for this syndrome resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Hartnup disease, the classic disorder characterized by defective intestinal and renal absorption of neutral (non-polar) amino acids, is caused by mutations in the SLC6A19 gene encoding the B⁰AT1 transporter. The SLC6A19 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 5 (5p15), so chromosome 5 carries the gene responsible for this absorption defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Common in youth",
    "long_answer": "Acrocyanosis is seen much more frequently in children and young adults, especially adolescent females, and is seldom observed in individuals older than thirty years. Thus, its prevalence is high in the younger age group and rare beyond the third decade."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AR",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AR gene, which encodes the androgen receptor on the X-chromosome, cause androgen insensitivity syndrome. This disorder was formerly described as male pseudohermaphroditism because individuals with a 46,XY karyotype develop female or ambiguous external genitalia despite having testes. Thus, the AR gene is the gene classically linked to the condition once termed pseudohermaphroditism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DPM3 gene, which resides on human chromosome 1 (band 1q22), cause DPM3-CDG—a congenital disorder of glycosylation. Loss of DPM3 impairs the synthesis of dolichol-phosphate-mannose, producing defective N-linked glycosylation and inadequate O-mannosylation of α-dystroglycan. Therefore, the relevant gene is on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondria",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondria are the organelles responsible for oxidative phosphorylation and ATP generation, the primary energy-producing process required for normal cellular activities, including those of ovarian and gonadal cells that are impaired in XX gonadal dysgenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Carnitine palmitoyl-transferase I deficiency is produced by pathogenic variants in the CPT1A gene, which is mapped to band q13.3 on human chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CENPJ",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the centrosomal gene CENPJ (also called CPAP) are a known cause of autosomal-recessive primary microcephaly (MCPH6). The same gene can give rise to Seckel-syndrome–type primordial dwarfism, in which patients show severe growth retardation and the characteristic Seckel ‘bird-like’ facial appearance. Thus, CENPJ mutations link microcephaly with a Seckel-syndrome-like clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Marshall–Smith syndrome is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the NFIX gene (Nuclear Factor I X). NFIX is mapped to cytogenetic band 19p13.2 on chromosome 19, making this chromosome the location of the gene responsible for the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "THAP1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Spasmodic dysphonia is a focal laryngeal dystonia marked by involuntary muscle spasms of the vocal‐fold muscles. Familial cases have revealed heterozygous mutations in the THAP1 gene (DYT6), which encodes a transcription-regulating DNA-binding protein. Pathogenic THAP1 variants disrupt normal neuronal transcriptional control and are therefore regarded as a potential genetic cause of this voice disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC3A1 and SLC7A9",
    "long_answer": "Cystinuria results from loss-of-function mutations in two amino-acid transporter genes: SLC3A1, which encodes the rBAT heavy subunit, and SLC7A9, which encodes the b(0,+)AT light subunit. Defects in either gene impair the renal and intestinal reabsorption of cystine and the dibasic amino acids ornithine, lysine and arginine, leading to their elevated urinary excretion and the formation of cystine kidney stones characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome",
    "long_answer": "DiGeorge syndrome (22q11.2 deletion) frequently includes conotruncal congenital heart defects such as truncus arteriosus, tetralogy of Fallot and interrupted aortic arch. These malformations are readily detected by fetal or post-natal echocardiography, and without timely surgical correction they result in a very high infant mortality rate, making the cardiac lesion the principal life-threatening component of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIPBL",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NIPBL gene cause Cornelia de Lange syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by variable limb-reduction defects that can include congenital absence of one or both hands (oligodactyly or amelia) together with moderate-to-severe intellectual disability. NIPBL encodes a cohesin-loading factor; loss-of-function mutations disrupt normal embryonic development, producing the combined limb and neurodevelopmental manifestations observed in affected patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Histidinemia results from loss-of-function variants in the HAL gene, which encodes histidine ammonia-lyase (histidase). Cytogenetic and sequencing studies place HAL on the long arm of human chromosome 12 (12q23-q24). Therefore, chromosome 12 carries the gene whose defect causes histidinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transglutaminase 5",
    "long_answer": "The lifelong, asymptomatic peeling of the outermost epidermal layer seen in acral/peeling-skin syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the TGM5 gene, which encodes the enzyme transglutaminase 5. This epidermal transglutaminase is essential for cross-linking proteins during cornified envelope formation, and its deficiency destabilizes corneocyte cohesion, producing continuous shedding of the stratum corneum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Fucosidosis results from loss-of-function mutations in the FUCA1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-fucosidase. Genetic mapping and sequencing studies have located FUCA1 to band p36.11 on human chromosome 1. Therefore, chromosome 1 carries the gene whose mutation causes fucosidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pluripotent epidermal stem cells",
    "long_answer": "Acrospiromas are benign adnexal skin tumours that show eccrine-sweat-duct differentiation. Current histogenetic models indicate that they arise when pluripotent stem cells located in the basal layer of the epidermis/acrosyringium differentiate abnormally along the eccrine duct lineage, giving rise to the tumour."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cornstarch",
    "long_answer": "In pica, amylophagia refers to the compulsive ingestion of starch. Cornstarch, a refined starch commonly added to sauces and desserts as a thickening agent, is the substance most often reported in this form of pica, where individuals consume it in raw, non-nutritive form."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene (vacuolar protein-sorting 33B) disrupt intracellular vesicle trafficking. This causes ARC (arthrogryposis–renal dysfunction–cholestasis) syndrome, a multisystem genodermatosis characterized by congenital arthrogryposis, renal Fanconi-type dysfunction, neonatal cholestasis, and ichthyosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paul of Aegina",
    "long_answer": "The 7th-century Byzantine surgeon Paul of Aegina (Paulus Aegineta) gave the earliest known operative description for congenital imperforate anus. In his medical compendium he advised making a cruciate incision through the obstructing membrane and keeping the opening patent with tents or tubes—history’s first recorded surgical management of this anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OPA1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OPA1 gene cause autosomal dominant optic atrophy (ADOA), a hereditary optic neuropathy in which retinal ganglion cells degenerate. Loss of these cells and ensuing optic nerve atrophy lead to progressive, often early-onset vision loss. Thus, an OPA1 mutation is directly linked to a disease that specifically targets retinal ganglion cells and impairs vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertrichosis",
    "long_answer": "Latanoprost, a prostaglandin F2α analogue used for glaucoma in Sturge-Weber syndrome, can stimulate hair follicles and prolong the anagen growth phase, producing marked eyelash and periocular hair overgrowth. This drug-induced excessive hair growth is termed hypertrichosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Severe congenital neutropenia is treated with recombinant granulocyte colony-stimulating factor (G-CSF). G-CSF is encoded by the CSF3 gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 17 (17q21). Hence, the chromosome that carries the gene producing this therapeutic protein is chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3 p.Pro250Arg",
    "long_answer": "The heterozygous p.Pro250Arg missense mutation in the FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor-3) gene is classically responsible for Muenke syndrome, a disorder marked by coronal craniosynostosis and variable syndactyly. The same FGFR3 p.Pro250Arg change has also been documented in families presenting solely with fourth–fifth metacarpal synostosis, demonstrating that this single gene mutation underlies both isolated metacarpal fusion and the craniosynostosis-syndactyly syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Zona glomerulosa",
    "long_answer": "Conn's syndrome (primary hyperaldosteronism) is usually produced by an aldosterone-secreting adenoma arising in the zona glomerulosa of the adrenal cortex, the layer responsible for physiological aldosterone synthesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Myopia",
    "long_answer": "Moderate-to-high myopia is the principal predisposing factor for pigment dispersion syndrome. In myopic eyes the longer axial length and posteriorly concave iris increase contact between the iris pigment epithelium and the lens zonules, liberating pigment granules that accumulate in the trabecular meshwork and can raise intraocular pressure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Williams syndrome is caused by a contiguous-gene microdeletion at band q11.23 on chromosome 7. The ~1.5-Mb deletion removes several genes, including ELN (elastin), leading to the characteristic cardiovascular, cognitive, and facial features of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Northern epilepsy syndrome (also called progressive epilepsy with mental retardation) is produced by a missense mutation in the CLN8 gene. CLN8 is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 8 (8p23). Hence, the pathogenic missense mutation resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maple syrup urine disease",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DLD gene, which encodes the E3 subunit dihydrolipoamide dehydrogenase, impair branched-chain α-ketoacid dehydrogenase complex function. This enzyme defect prevents proper catabolism of branched-chain amino acids, leading to the characteristic accumulation of leucine, isoleucine, valine, and their keto-acids seen in maple syrup urine disease (particularly the E3-deficient, or type III, form)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Victor A. McKusick",
    "long_answer": "The skeletal dysplasia cartilage-hair hypoplasia—later shown to be caused by mutations within or adjacent to the non-coding RNA gene RMRP, the RNA component of RNase MRP—was first described in the 1960s by the medical geneticist Victor A. McKusick, after whom the disorder’s ‘McKusick type’ designation is named."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The classic syndrome in which the central (median) rays of the hands and feet fail to form—producing ectrodactyly or “split hand/foot malformation,” often seen in EEC syndrome—is transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion, typically caused by heterozygous mutations in the TP63 gene. Hence, a single mutant allele is sufficient to produce the trait, and it shows vertical transmission with variable expressivity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "The gene encoding type II collagen is COL2A1, which is located at cytogenetic band 12q13 on human chromosome 12; this locus directs synthesis of the major collagen found in hyaline cartilage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CUL7",
    "long_answer": "3-M syndrome (an autosomal-recessive disorder marked by severe pre- and post-natal growth retardation and characteristic facial dysmorphia) is most frequently caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CUL7 gene. CUL7 encodes a cullin family scaffold protein that is part of an E3 ubiquitin-ligase complex; disruption of this gene accounts for the majority of genetically confirmed 3-M cases, making CUL7 the mutation most commonly associated with the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRIM37",
    "long_answer": "Mulibrey nanism—a very rare congenital syndrome marked by generalized lipodystrophy together with microcephaly, growth failure and multiorgan involvement—is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the TRIM37 gene on chromosome 17q22–q23. TRIM37 encodes a tripartite-motif E3 ubiquitin-ligase; pathogenic variants disrupt normal protein function, leading to the characteristic lipoatrophy and neurodevelopmental features observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 15%",
    "long_answer": "International DSD guidelines and tumor-risk reviews consistently report that partial androgen insensitivity syndrome (PAIS) with undescended testes carries an estimated lifetime germ-cell tumour risk of roughly 15 %. This reflects the higher propensity for intratubular germ-cell neoplasia and seminoma in cryptorchid testes of PAIS patients compared with complete AIS, yet lower than in dysgenetic gonads."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Rhizomelic chondrodysplasia punctata type 1 is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the PEX7 gene, which encodes the peroxisomal PTS2 receptor. The PEX7 gene is mapped to human chromosome 6 (cytogenetic band 6q23-q24). Thus, chromosome 6 is the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Diffuse non-epidermolytic palmoplantar keratoderma (Unna-Thost type) is produced by missense mutations in the keratin 1 (KRT1) gene. KRT1 is mapped to the 12q11-q13 region of the human genome, so the disease-associated gene resides on chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RV systemic, LV pulmonary",
    "long_answer": "In dextro-TGA the great arteries are switched, so the right ventricle ejects into the aorta and must support systemic circulation, whereas the left ventricle ejects into the pulmonary artery and only pumps to the lungs. Thus the right ventricle functions as the high-pressure systemic pump, while the left ventricle serves as the lower-pressure pulmonary pump."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "A common genetic risk factor for age-related macular degeneration is variation in the complement factor H (CFH) gene. CFH is located at cytogenetic band 1q31 on human chromosome 1, making chromosome 1 the chromosome that carries this AMD-associated gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Classic autosomal-dominant monilethrix is produced by missense mutations in the type II hair-keratin genes KRT81, KRT83 and KRT86, all of which are clustered at cytogenetic band 12q13. Hence, the causative gene for monilethrix resides on human chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Woodhouse–Sakati syndrome is produced by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the DCAF17 gene (formerly C2orf37), which is mapped to cytogenetic band 2q31.1. Therefore, the causal gene resides on human chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The SMARCA4 gene (also known as BRG1), whose inactivation is linked to malignant rhabdoid tumours and other SWI/SNF-deficient cancers, is mapped to cytogenetic band 19p13.2 on chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Hypoprothrombinemia results from mutations in the F2 gene, which encodes coagulation factor II (prothrombin). The F2 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (cytogenetic band 11p11.2), so chromosome 11 is the chromosomal site responsible for this bleeding disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP1B1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CYP1B1 gene cause primary congenital (infantile) glaucoma. This disorder is present at birth and the elevated intra-ocular pressure it produces can enlarge the globe (buphthalmos), thereby altering eye size. Hence, a CYP1B1 mutation is classically linked to a congenital condition that affects ocular size."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cysticercosis",
    "long_answer": "Cysticercosis is the disseminated larval infection produced by the pork tapeworm Taenia solium. Unlike simple intestinal taeniasis, the larvae (cysticerci) migrate through the bloodstream and can lodge in any organ, producing space-occupying cysts in the brain (neurocysticercosis), eye, skeletal muscle, and, though rare, the uterus and other pelvic structures. Because it can cause seizures, hydrocephalus, blindness, and even death, cysticercosis is regarded as the most severe disease attributable to any human tapeworm."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cochlear hair cells",
    "long_answer": "Usher-syndrome genes encode proteins that localise to the stereocilia of sensory hair cells in the cochlea (organ of Corti). Mutations disrupt these cochlear hair cells’ mechano-electrical transduction machinery, leading to the congenital sensorineural hearing loss characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WFS1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the WFS1 gene, which encodes the endoplasmic-reticulum membrane protein wolframin, are responsible for Wolfram syndrome (DIDMOAD), an autosomal-recessive disorder that typically presents with childhood-onset diabetes mellitus together with progressive optic atrophy and sensorineural deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 Kabuki syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the KMT2D gene (formerly MLL2). KMT2D is mapped to cytogenetic band 12q13.12 on human chromosome 12. Mutations in this gene produce the characteristic features of Kabuki syndrome 1, which frequently include feeding difficulties and dysphagia, linking the chromosome location to the clinical presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The fumarate hydratase (FH) gene, which encodes the Krebs-cycle enzyme fumarase, is located on human chromosome 1 (cytogenetic band 1q42.3). Mutations or deletions of this FH gene lead to reduced or absent fumarase activity, causing the metabolic disorder known as fumarase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACTA1 mutations",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the ACTA1 gene, which encodes skeletal muscle α-actin, cause congenital nemaline/myopathic phenotypes that can be clinically silent until a catastrophic respiratory event. Several reports have documented infants who appeared healthy but died suddenly and unexpectedly; post-mortem examination revealed nemaline myopathy and ACTA1 mutations, establishing this gene’s variants as a molecular cause of myopathy-related sudden unexplained infant death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈5-fold increase",
    "long_answer": "Patients with autosomal dominant polycystic kidney disease have about a five-fold higher prevalence of intracranial (berry) aneurysms than the general population (≈10–12 % vs. 2 %), indicating an approximate 5-fold increased risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The protein statherin prevents calcium phosphate precipitation in saliva. The human STATH gene that encodes statherin is located on chromosome 4 (band 4q13.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dinoflagellates",
    "long_answer": "Paralytic shellfish poisoning (PSP) is linked to saxitoxins produced during harmful algal blooms by marine dinoflagellates—single-celled, photosynthetic protists such as species of Alexandrium, Gymnodinium, and Pyrodinium. Shellfish filter-feed on these dinoflagellates, bioaccumulating the neurotoxin, which can then poison humans and other animals that consume the contaminated shellfish."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of malignant hyperthermia are produced by pathogenic variants in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor. The RYR1 locus is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 19 (band 19q13.2), so chromosome 19 is recognized as carrying the primary gene responsible for malignant hyperthermia susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(11;22)(q24;q12)",
    "long_answer": "Ewing sarcoma/primitive neuroectodermal tumor is a small-round-blue-cell cancer that is often confused with malignant lymphoma on histology, so lymphoma is a key part of its differential diagnosis. The tumor is characteristically driven by the chromosomal translocation t(11;22)(q24;q12), which creates the EWSR1-FLI1 fusion gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Octreotide scintigraphy",
    "long_answer": "Somatostatin-receptor (Indium-111–labeled octreotide) scintigraphy selectively binds to somatostatin receptors that are highly expressed on pulmonary neuroendocrine (carcinoid) tumors. The nuclear scan therefore localizes and confirms the presence of these tumors, helping identify lesions that can produce serotonin and trigger carcinoid syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GTP cyclohydrolase I",
    "long_answer": "The most common (autosomal-dominant) form of dopamine-responsive dystonia, often called Segawa disease, is caused by mutations in the GCH1 gene encoding the enzyme GTP cyclohydrolase I. This enzyme catalyzes the first and rate-limiting step in tetrahydrobiopterin (BH4) synthesis; BH4 is an essential cofactor for tyrosine hydroxylase, the key enzyme that converts tyrosine to L-DOPA in dopamine biosynthesis. Impaired GTP cyclohydrolase I activity therefore lowers BH4 levels, reduces dopamine production, and produces the dystonia that responds to levodopa therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MYCN gene, which is located on chromosome 2p24. Pathogenic variants in this gene lead to the characteristic microcephaly, limb anomalies, and gastrointestinal atresias seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIEZO1",
    "long_answer": "In 2012, heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the mechanosensitive ion-channel gene PIEZO1 were reported as the cause of dehydrated hereditary stomatocytosis (also called hereditary xerocytosis). This hemolytic anemia is characterized by chronic red-cell dehydration, jaundice and frequently leads to enlargement of the spleen (splenomegaly) due to increased splenic destruction of the abnormal erythrocytes, firmly linking PIEZO1 to a disease that can cause splenomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Microglia",
    "long_answer": "In the HIV-infected brain, resident macrophage-like microglial cells become infected and activated. These microglia secrete viral proteins, excitotoxins, cytokines, and reactive nitrogen/oxygen species that are neurotoxic, leading to the neuronal injury and cognitive deficits characteristic of HIV-associated neurocognitive disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CFTR",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis—an autosomal-recessive syndrome characterized by chronic pulmonary disease and other systemic manifestations—is caused by pathogenic mutations in the CFTR gene (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) located on chromosome 7. Loss-of-function of the CFTR chloride channel leads to thick, dehydrated mucus in the airways, predisposing to recurrent respiratory infections and bronchiectasis." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Ellis–Van Creveld syndrome is an autosomal-recessive chondroectodermal dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EVC and EVC2 genes, both of which are located on the short arm of human chromosome 4 (region 4p16). Defects in these genes disrupt skeletal and ectodermal development, leading to abnormalities of the carpal bones and other characteristic features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VHL",
    "long_answer": "Inactivating mutations of the VHL gene cause von Hippel-Lindau disease, a rare autosomal-dominant disorder that predisposes to tumors such as catecholamine-secreting pheochromocytomas. Excess catecholamine release from these tumors produces sustained or paroxysmal hypertension, making elevated blood pressure a characteristic clinical feature of this genetic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The most common adult-onset muscular dystrophy is myotonic dystrophy type 1 (Steinert disease). It is caused by an unstable CTG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the DMPK (dystrophia myotonica protein kinase) gene, which is mapped to band q13.3 on human chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Wolf-Hirschhorn syndrome is produced by loss or mutation of the WHSC1 (NSD2) gene located on the short arm of chromosome 4 (4p16.3). Deletion of this locus leads to the syndrome’s hallmark findings, which include striking facial dysmorphism (the classic “Greek-warrior helmet” facies) and a cleft lip and/or palate. Therefore, the gene responsible for this craniofacial malformation resides on human chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator",
    "long_answer": "For inherited cardiomyopathies and channelopathies that place patients at risk for malignant ventricular arrhythmias—such as hypertrophic cardiomyopathy, arrhythmogenic right-ventricular cardiomyopathy, long-QT and Brugada syndromes—the only intervention conclusively shown to prevent sudden cardiac death is implantation of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD). The ICD continually monitors cardiac rhythm and, when it detects life-threatening ventricular tachycardia or fibrillation, delivers pacing or a shock that reliably terminates the arrhythmia, thereby preventing fatal collapse; no pharmacologic or surgical therapy has demonstrated comparable, reproducible survival benefit."
}

{
    "short_answer": "JAK2 V617F",
    "long_answer": "The JAK2 V617F point mutation in the Janus kinase 2 gene is present in the vast majority of classic myeloproliferative neoplasms—diseases characterized by abnormal proliferation of bone-marrow–derived hematopoietic cells such as erythrocytes, leukocytes, and platelets. The mutation constitutively activates JAK–STAT signaling, driving uncontrolled cell growth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemizygous",
    "long_answer": "Adrenoleukodystrophy is caused by pathogenic variants in the X-linked ABCD1 gene. Because males have only one X chromosome, they carry a single copy of ABCD1; if that copy is mutated, they are hemizygous for the pathogenic allele, which explains the full manifestation of the disease in affected males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSPP",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant mutations in the dentin sialophosphoprotein (DSPP) gene are the principal molecular cause of dentinogenesis imperfecta types II and III. DSPP encodes a major non-collagenous dentin matrix protein; mutant DSPP disrupts dentin mineralization and structure, producing the discolored, brittle teeth characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNE gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "French-type (congenital) sialuria is produced by missense mutations in the GNE gene, which encodes the bifunctional enzyme UDP-N-acetyl-glucosamine-2-epimerase/N-acetyl-mannosamine kinase that initiates sialic-acid biosynthesis. Loss of feedback control causes massive intracellular accumulation and urinary excretion of free sialic acid. Affected infants commonly develop coarse facial features, failure to thrive and marked hepatomegaly due to storage of excess sialic acid in the liver. Thus, a pathogenic mutation of the GNE gene is the genetic lesion in this type of sialuria that can lead to hepatomegaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Papillon–Lefèvre syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CTSC (cathepsin C) gene, which is mapped to chromosome 11q14. Mutations on this chromosome disrupt cathepsin C activity, leading to the severe periodontitis and palmoplantar keratoderma characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The human IFT43 gene (Intraflagellar Transport 43), mutations of which cause Sensenbrenner syndrome (cranioectodermal dysplasia), is mapped to chromosome 14 at cytogenetic band 14q24.3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Antisense oligonucleotide",
    "long_answer": "Facioscapulohumeral muscular dystrophy (FSHD) is driven by abnormal expression of the DUX4 gene. One therapeutic strategy now in pre-clinical and early clinical testing uses antisense oligonucleotides—short, synthetic stretches of nucleic acid that hybridize specifically to DUX4 mRNA. By binding the transcript, these molecules trigger its degradation or block its translation, thereby reducing DUX4 protein production and counteracting the pathological gene expression that causes FSHD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL1A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL1A1 gene cause osteogenesis imperfecta, a hereditary bone disorder characterized by fragile, fracture-prone bones. This bone fragility is the key feature it shares with osteoporosis, though in osteogenesis imperfecta the underlying defect is abnormal type I collagen production due to the COL1A1 mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease is caused by expansion of CAG trinucleotide repeats in the HTT (huntingtin) gene, which is situated at cytogenetic band 4p16.3 on the short arm of human chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the WRN gene on the short arm of chromosome 8 (8p12-p11.2) cause Werner syndrome, a progeroid disorder characterized by premature aging. WRN encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase essential for DNA repair and genome maintenance, linking the disease’s aging phenotype to defective DNA repair mechanisms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sonic hedgehog (SHH) gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Holoprosencephaly—a forebrain malformation that can also develop in fetuses exposed to alcohol in utero—is most commonly linked to loss-of-function mutations in the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) gene. SHH signaling is crucial for midline patterning of the developing brain; disruption of this gene produces the same spectrum of defects seen in alcohol-exposed pregnancies, confirming SHH mutation as the key genetic association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR) deficiency",
    "long_answer": "One form of homocystinuria is caused by deficiency of methylenetetrahydrofolate reductase (MTHFR). MTHFR requires the flavin cofactor FAD, derived from riboflavin (vitamin B₂), which functions in redox reactions. In several patients with this deficiency, high-dose riboflavin increases residual MTHFR activity and lowers homocysteine levels, making the disorder riboflavin-responsive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "No direct association",
    "long_answer": "Joubert syndrome is defined by hypoplasia/agenesis of the cerebellar vermis with the ‘molar-tooth’ brain-stem malformation; the corpus callosum is usually present and its agenesis is not a diagnostic or common feature. Agenesis of the corpus callosum is therefore an independent malformation that may very rarely co-occur but is not causally or characteristically linked to Joubert syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAG2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the PRKAG2 gene, which encodes the γ2 regulatory subunit of AMP-activated protein kinase (AMPK), cause the PRKAG2 cardiac glycogen-storage disease. The mutant AMPK is constitutively active, driving excessive glucose uptake and glycogen synthesis in cardiomyocytes. As a result, large amounts of glycogen accumulate in the heart, producing hypertrophic cardiomyopathy with ventricular pre-excitation and conduction defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROBO3",
    "long_answer": "Horizontal gaze palsy with progressive scoliosis—a rare congenital disorder presenting with conjugate horizontal gaze palsy and childhood-onset progressive scoliosis—is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ROBO3 gene, which encodes the Roundabout axon-guidance receptor 3 involved in hind-brain and spinal cord pathway development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Cerebro-costo-mandibular syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the SNRPB gene. SNRPB is located on the short arm of human chromosome 20 (cytogenetic band 20p13). Therefore, chromosome 20 is the chromosome that contains the causative gene for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Congenital anonychia is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the RSPO4 gene (R-spondin-4). RSPO4 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 20 (20p13). Therefore, the gene responsible for anonychia resides on chromosome 20."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Dolichocephaly (an abnormally long, narrow skull) is a recognized craniofacial feature of Marfan syndrome. Marfan syndrome results from pathogenic mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1. Defective fibrillin-1 disrupts elastic fiber formation, producing the skeletal overgrowth and characteristic craniofacial findings, including dolichocephaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central nervous system",
    "long_answer": "In Trisomy 9, extra chromosome 9 material disrupts formation of the brain and spinal cord, causing central nervous system malformations (e.g., agenesis of the corpus callosum, Dandy-Walker malformation, hydrocephalus). Damage to the central nervous system underlies the developmental delays and neurologic problems seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Linkage analyses in families with Aagenaes syndrome (lymphoedema–cholestasis syndrome) mapped the responsible autosomal-recessive locus to the long arm of chromosome 15 (15q). Thus, the gene associated with Aagenaes syndrome is located on chromosome 15."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Linkage analyses and subsequent gene identification showed that a form of common variable immunodeficiency is caused by mutations in the CD19 gene, which is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (16p11.2). Thus, the CVID-associated gene resides on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SP110",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SP110 gene, encoding the Sp110 nuclear-body protein, are the established cause of the autosomal-recessive disorder ‘hepatic veno-occlusive disease with immunodeficiency’ (VODI). This rare hereditary disease is characterized by progressive occlusion of the small hepatic veins (veno-occlusive disease) together with severe immune deficiency, making SP110 mutation the key genetic association."
}

{
    "short_answer": "German Shorthaired Pointer",
    "long_answer": "Type 2 von Willebrand disease in dogs has been traced to a specific nonsense mutation in the VWF gene that truncates the von Willebrand factor protein. This causative mutation was first identified and is characteristically found in German Shorthaired Pointers, making this breed the classical canine model for VWF-associated type 2 von Willebrand disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidoses (MPS) are lysosomal storage disorders in which glycosaminoglycans accumulate in tissues, leading to chronically thick nasal and airway secretions, recurrent respiratory infections and obstructive airway problems. Evidence-based approaches to managing the excess mucus in MPS include airway clearance techniques, aggressive treatment of sinus/ear infection, surgical interventions (e.g., adenoidectomy, tracheostomy when necessary), and disease-specific therapies such as enzyme-replacement or hematopoietic stem-cell transplantation. Dietary adjustment, and specifically increasing milk or other dairy intake, is not part of any guideline or clinical recommendation for reducing mucus production in MPS. In fact, although good studies show dairy does not significantly increase mucus in most people, there is certainly no data showing that additional dairy lessens mucus, and some patients subjectively report thicker secretions after dairy consumption. Therefore, advising an increase in milk and dairy products as a means to control excessive mucus in individuals with mucopolysaccharidosis is not supported by established biomedical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The predominant gene responsible for Cornelia de Lange syndrome is NIPBL, which is located on the short arm of chromosome 5 (region 5p13.2). Pathogenic variants in NIPBL account for the majority of Cornelia de Lange syndrome cases, making chromosome 5 the key chromosomal location associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈70–86 fL",
    "long_answer": "Transient erythroblastopenia of childhood is characterized by a normocytic anemia; the mean corpuscular volume in affected children remains within the normal pediatric range, typically about 70–86 femtoliters, distinguishing it from macrocytic Diamond-Blackfan anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B₁₂ deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Lack of vitamin B₁₂ causes megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia. This anemia raises homocysteine levels and impairs oxygen delivery to the brain, mechanisms that increase cerebrovascular injury. Epidemiologic studies therefore identify vitamin B₁₂-deficiency anemia as an independent risk factor for vascular dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B12 deficiency",
    "long_answer": "The remethylation of homocysteine to methionine is catalyzed by the enzyme methionine synthase, which requires methylcobalamin, the co-enzyme form of vitamin B12. When vitamin B12 is deficient, this reaction is blocked, homocysteine accumulates, and related disorders such as megaloblastic (pernicious) anemia and hyperhomocysteinemia/homocystinuria develop. Thus, deficiency of vitamin B12 is the canonical deficiency associated with impaired conversion of homocysteine to methionine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPR56",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the G-protein-coupled receptor gene GPR56 (ADGRG1) cause the autosomal-recessive disorder known as bilateral frontoparietal polymicrogyria. This condition is marked by an excessive number of small cortical gyri and an abnormally thickened, poorly laminated grey matter layer, demonstrating that GPR56 is specifically associated with a form of polymicrogyria involving grey-matter abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELN (elastin)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function (usually deletion) of the ELN gene encoding elastin on chromosome 7q11.23 causes Williams-Beuren syndrome. A hallmark of this syndrome is supravalvular aortic (and other arterial) stenosis, making ELN mutation directly linked to a syndrome in which stenosis is a characteristic clinical feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Kindler syndrome results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the FERMT1 (also called KIND1) gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 20p12.3 on human chromosome 20. Hence, chromosome 20 harbors the gene responsible for Kindler syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complex III",
    "long_answer": "Björnstad syndrome is produced by mutations in the BCS1L gene, whose protein product is essential for assembling mitochondrial respiratory-chain complex III (ubiquinol-cytochrome c reductase). Loss of functional BCS1L therefore specifically impairs complex III of the electron transport chain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC39A4",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC39A4 gene on chromosome 8q24.3 disrupt the ZIP4 zinc transporter. This loss of function produces acrodermatitis enteropathica, an autosomal-recessive disorder classically characterized by periorificial dermatitis, alopecia and intermittent watery diarrhea due to impaired intestinal zinc absorption."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Lethal Congenital Contracture Syndrome 1 (LCCS1) is caused by pathogenic variants in the GLE1 gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 9 (9q34.11). Hence, the gene associated with LCCS1 is located on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RELN",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RELN gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein reelin, cause lissencephaly with severe cerebellar hypoplasia. This autosomal-recessive condition is classically known as Norman–Roberts syndrome, linking RELN mutations directly to that specific form of cerebellar hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Macula",
    "long_answer": "In X-linked juvenile retinoschisis the retinal splitting occurs predominantly in the macula—especially the foveal centre—producing the characteristic spoke-wheel schisis and resulting in loss of central (macular) vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary coproporphyria results from pathogenic variants in the coproporphyrinogen oxidase (CPOX) gene. The CPOX gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 3 (cytogenetic band 3q11–q12), so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Iris",
    "long_answer": "In congenital Horner's syndrome, disruption of sympathetic innervation reduces melanocyte stimulation in the affected eye, leading to decreased pigment deposition in the iris and resulting heterochromia. Therefore, the iris is the ocular structure involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OCRL",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OCRL gene, which encodes the inositol-5-phosphatase OCRL1, cause Lowe (oculocerebrorenal) syndrome. Many pathogenic mutations lie in the ASH–RhoGAP region of OCRL1 that forms Rab-binding domains; their disruption prevents normal interaction with Rab GTPases, leading to the membrane-trafficking defects characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EXT1 and EXT2",
    "long_answer": "The disorder characterized by cartilage-capped benign bone tumors (osteochondromas) in the limbs is Hereditary Multiple Exostoses (HME). Over 90 % of HME cases are caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EXT1 and EXT2 genes, which encode exostosin-1 and exostosin-2, glycosyltransferases required for heparan-sulfate chain elongation. Defective heparan-sulfate synthesis disrupts signaling pathways that regulate chondrocyte proliferation, leading to the formation and outgrowth of osteochondromas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GCH1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GTP-cyclohydrolase 1 (GCH1) gene produce dopa-responsive dystonia (Segawa disease). Children with this disorder typically develop an early-onset spastic or dystonic gait that closely resembles cerebral palsy, so the condition is frequently misdiagnosed as CP until a dramatic response to low-dose levodopa reveals the underlying GCH1-related dystonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Duchenne muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the DMD (dystrophin) gene, which is situated on the short arm of the X chromosome at band Xp21. Because the gene is X-linked, the disease shows an X-linked recessive pattern of inheritance, affecting males predominantly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Cat-eye syndrome results from partial tetrasomy produced by a small supernumerary marker chromosome derived from chromosome 22—typically an inverted duplication inv dup(22)(pter→q11). Thus, extra genetic material from chromosome 22 is the causal chromosomal abnormality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC12A3",
    "long_answer": "Gitelman syndrome is an autosomal-recessive renal tubulopathy caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC12A3 gene, which encodes the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺-Cl⁻ cotransporter (NCC) in the distal convoluted tubule. Defective NCC impairs sodium and chloride reabsorption, triggering secondary renal potassium and magnesium wasting, and consequently the characteristic hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia observed in patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Craniofacial dysostosis (Crouzon syndrome) results from pathogenic variants in the FGFR2 gene and is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, meaning a single mutant allele inherited from an affected parent is sufficient to cause the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "McCune–Albright syndrome results from post-zygotic activating mutations in the GNAS gene, which encodes the stimulatory G-protein α-subunit (Gsα). The GNAS locus is situated on chromosome 20 at band q13.3; therefore, chromosome 20 is the chromosome that contains the gene associated with this endocrine disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PRKAR1A",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PRKAR1A gene cause acrodysostosis type 1. The same PRKAR1A defects can produce Carney complex with growth-hormone excess, and the resulting acromegaly is classically marked by mandibular prognathism (a protruding jaw). Thus a PRKAR1A mutation links acrodysostosis to a condition characterized by a protruding jaw."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Congenital absence of fingernails or toenails (anonychia) is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the RSPO4 gene. This gene is located at cytogenetic band 20p13 on human chromosome 20, so chromosome 20 is the chromosome that carries the nail-development gene whose disruption leads to failure to form nails."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC39A4",
    "long_answer": "Acrodermatitis enteropathica, a disorder marked by defective intestinal zinc uptake, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC39A4 gene (encoding the ZIP4 zinc transporter) located on chromosome 8q24.3. Impaired ZIP4 function prevents adequate absorption of dietary zinc, producing the characteristic dermatologic and metabolic manifestations that endocrinologists may evaluate and treat."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The classical form of Kallmann syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the KAL1 (now called ANOS1) gene, which is located on the short arm of the human X chromosome at Xp22.3. Because this X-linked gene guides migration of GnRH- and olfactory neurons during embryogenesis, mutations on the X chromosome can lead to the characteristic hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and anosmia of Kallmann syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The MRZ-reaction (intrathecal IgG production against measles-, rubella- and varicella-zoster viruses) is positive in the majority of patients with classical multiple sclerosis and is therefore used as a highly specific laboratory marker for MS. Published case series and individual reports on Baló’s concentric sclerosis – a rare demyelinating disorder that can mimic or coexist with MS – show a very different immunological profile: oligoclonal CSF bands are absent or only sporadically present, and the MRZ-reaction is almost always negative. In the largest series available, none of the examined Baló patients displayed a positive MRZ reaction, and other reports describe the same finding. Hence, Baló’s concentric sclerosis patients do not “tend to be positive” for the MRZ-reaction; they more commonly test negative."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC12A3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SLC12A3 gene, which encodes the thiazide-sensitive Na⁺/Cl⁻ cotransporter (NCC) of the distal convoluted tubule, cause Gitelman syndrome—a renal tubulopathy characterized by persistent hypokalemia and hypomagnesemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Velopharyngeal insufficiency",
    "long_answer": "In a cleft palate the soft palate cannot close against the posterior pharyngeal wall, leaving an open velopharyngeal port. This structural defect is termed velopharyngeal insufficiency; it causes excessive nasal air escape and hypernasal resonance, disrupting normal production of nasal consonants and giving rise to a characteristic speech disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Diacetyl",
    "long_answer": "Diacetyl (2,3-butanedione) is a buttery-tasting flavoring agent widely used in microwave popcorn, snack foods, and other flavored products. Inhalation of diacetyl vapors by workers in flavoring and popcorn manufacturing plants has been causally linked to severe bronchiolitis obliterans, a lung disease whose clinical and radiologic presentation can mimic cryptogenic organizing pneumonia (formerly called BOOP). This established association makes diacetyl the compound implicated in such flavoring-related lung disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Liebenberg syndrome is produced by a deletion in the regulatory region upstream of the PITX1 gene located at 5q31; thus, the causative mutation affects chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Enolase deficiency, a rare cause of hereditary non-spherocytic hemolytic anemia, is produced by pathogenic variants in the ENO1 gene that encodes the α-enolase glycolytic enzyme. The ENO1 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 1p36.23 on human chromosome 1; hence chromosome 1 carries the gene responsible for this deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic anhydrase II",
    "long_answer": "Missense or loss-of-function mutations in the enzyme carbonic anhydrase II disrupt the generation of protons and bicarbonate needed for osteoclast acidification of the resorption lacuna. Without effective acidification, bone resorption fails, leading to osteopetrosis—marked by abnormally high bone density yet mechanically brittle bones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "McCune-Albright syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Café au lait macules in McCune-Albright syndrome typically have irregular, ‘coast-of-Maine’ borders, distinguishing them from the smooth ‘coast-of-California’ borders seen in neurofibromatosis type 1. The rough, jagged outline of the pigmented spots is a classic clinical clue pointing to McCune-Albright syndrome, a mosaic GNAS mutation disorder characterized by fibrous dysplasia of bone, endocrine hyperfunction, and these distinctive café au lait lesions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in either the TSC1 gene (encoding hamartin) or the TSC2 gene (encoding tuberin) cause tuberous sclerosis complex, an autosomal-dominant neurocutaneous disorder characterized by cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, and frequent epileptic seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Uncombable hair syndrome type 2 (UHS2) is caused by loss-of-function variants in the TGM3 gene, which encodes the epidermal enzyme transglutaminase 3. The human TGM3 gene is located at cytogenetic band 20q11.22 on chromosome 20, making chromosome 20 the chromosomal site for the enzyme-encoding gene involved in UHS2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Perlman syndrome is an overgrowth and renal dysplasia disorder caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the DIS3L2 gene. The DIS3L2 locus is situated at cytogenetic band 2q37 on human chromosome 2, establishing chromosome 2 as the chromosomal location responsible for Perlman syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Marinesco–Sjögren syndrome is primarily caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SIL1 gene, which encodes an endoplasmic-reticulum cochaperone of BiP. The human SIL1 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 5q31; therefore, the gene responsible for Marinesco–Sjögren syndrome resides on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Camurati–Engelmann disease is caused by autosomal-dominant mutations in the TGFB1 (transforming growth factor-β1) gene, which is located on chromosome 19q13. These TGFB1 mutations disrupt normal bone remodeling, leading to the characteristic diaphyseal hyperostosis and skeletal anomalies seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PITX2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the homeobox transcription factor gene PITX2 give rise to Axenfeld-Rieger syndrome. This developmental disorder features hypoplasia of ocular structures (e.g., iris and anterior chamber) and hypoplastic or missing teeth, linking PITX2 mutation directly to the combined eye and tooth hypoplasia phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TUBB4A",
    "long_answer": "Genetic studies of laryngeal (spasmodic) dysphonia have uncovered a rare missense variant—classically p.Arg2Gly—in the TUBB4A gene, which encodes β-tubulin 4A. This mutation, first described in the ‘whispering’ dystonia (DYT4) pedigree and later found in sporadic spasmodic dysphonia cases, establishes TUBB4A mutation as the rare genetic variant associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Paola Leone",
    "long_answer": "Paola Leone, Ph.D., has led the experimental gene-therapy program for Canavan disease in which neurosurgeons place catheters through small burr holes in the skull and infuse an adeno-associated viral vector carrying the ASPA gene directly into multiple sites of the brain. This catheter-based delivery aims to restore aspartoacylase activity and halt the demyelinating process characteristic of Canavan disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hemojuvelin (HJV) mutation",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary iron-overload syndrome that can mimic neonatal hemochromatosis is juvenile hemochromatosis (hereditary hemochromatosis type 2). It is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HJV gene (also called hemojuvelin or HFE2). HJV mutations abolish normal hepcidin induction, leading to uncontrolled intestinal iron absorption and massive hepatic and extra-hepatic iron deposition in infancy or early childhood, a presentation that is sometimes mistaken for classical, alloimmune neonatal hemochromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Noise exposure",
    "long_answer": "Combustion explosions (e.g., fireworks, firearms) are high-temperature exothermic reactions that generate impulse noises often exceeding 140 dB. Such intense sound waves damage cochlear hair cells, producing noise-induced sensorineural hearing loss—one of the most common etiologies of this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vestibulocochlear nerve",
    "long_answer": "Möbius syndrome primarily involves congenital dysfunction of several cranial nerves. In many patients the eighth cranial nerve—the vestibulocochlear nerve—is also affected; this nerve carries auditory (cochlear) signals for hearing and vestibular information for balance from the inner ear to the brain. Damage or developmental deficiency of cranial nerve VIII in Möbius syndrome can therefore impair both hearing and balance, consistent with its dual sensory function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTCH1",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line loss-of-function mutations in the PTCH1 gene, which encodes the Hedgehog receptor Patched-1, cause Gorlin (nevoid basal-cell carcinoma) syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by congenital ocular anomalies such as microphthalmia/coloboma and a pronounced lifelong predisposition to develop multiple basal-cell carcinomas, linking PTCH1 mutation to both features mentioned in the question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the VPS33B gene cause ARC (Arthrogryposis-Renal dysfunction-Cholestasis) syndrome. ARC presents with congenital joint contractures, kidney tubular dysfunction, and cholestatic liver disease, and patients often have severe ichthyosis or other skin manifestations that bring them to dermatologic care. Hence, a VPS33B mutation underlies the condition a dermatologist might encounter that combines arthrogryposis, renal dysfunction, and cholestasis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "The NF2 gene, whose mutations cause neurofibromatosis type II and can also contribute to schwannomatosis, is located on human chromosome 22 (region 22q12)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TP63",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TP63 gene, which encodes the p63 developmental transcription factor, cause Ectrodactyly-Ectodermal Dysplasia-Cleft (EEC) syndrome. This rare disorder disrupts formation of ectoderm-derived structures, leading to sparse hair, abnormal or absent teeth, nail dystrophy, reduced or absent sweat glands (hypohidrosis), and malformations of the hands and feet (ectrodactyly), thereby affecting the extremities together with hair, teeth, nails, and sweat glands."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2",
    "long_answer": "HID (hystrix-like ichthyosis with deafness) syndrome is caused by dominant missense mutations in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin 26. Pathogenic changes in GJB2 disrupt epidermal cell–cell communication and cochlear function, producing the characteristic ichthyosis hystrix skin phenotype and sensorineural deafness that define HID syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "REST",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the RE1-silencing transcription factor gene (REST) have been identified in families with the syndrome historically called Jones syndrome, which presents with hereditary gingival fibromatosis together with progressive sensorineural hearing loss. REST normally represses neuronal-gene expression; loss-of-function mutations disrupt cochlear hair-cell homeostasis, causing deafness, and drive excessive proliferation of gingival fibroblasts, producing the characteristic fibromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXL2",
    "long_answer": "Blepharophimosis, ptosis and epicanthus inversus syndrome (BPES) type I is characterised by eyelid malformations together with premature ovarian insufficiency. This form of BPES is caused by truncating (shortening) mutations in the FOXL2 gene, a forkhead-box transcription factor essential for both eyelid morphogenesis and ovarian follicle development. Loss-of-function or premature stop mutations that shorten FOXL2 lead to the combined ocular and ovarian phenotype seen in BPES type I."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Infantile free sialic acid storage disease is produced by mutations in the SLC17A5 gene, which encodes the lysosomal sialic-acid transporter (sialin). The SLC17A5 gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 6 (6q14-q15). Hence, chromosome 6 harbors the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "The NF2 gene, whose loss-of-function mutations cause neurofibromatosis type II, is located on the long arm of human chromosome 22 (band 22q12.2). Deletions or point mutations in this locus disrupt production of the tumor-suppressor protein merlin, leading to the bilateral vestibular schwannomas and other tumors characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Genetic linkage studies of families with Moyamoya disease type 2 (MMD2) mapped the susceptibility locus to the short arm of human chromosome 3, specifically the 3p24.2–p26 region. Thus, the gene responsible for MMD2 resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "Unverricht-Lundborg disease (progressive myoclonus epilepsy type 1) is caused by pathogenic dodecamer repeat expansions in the CSTB (cystatin B) gene. CSTB is mapped to cytogenetic band 21q22.3, placing it on human chromosome 21."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Louis K. Diamond",
    "long_answer": "Louis K. Diamond, a pioneer of pediatric hematology, co-described Gardner–Diamond syndrome—also called the painful bruising syndrome or autoerythrocyte-sensitization—in 1955. This rare disorder causes spontaneous, painful ecchymoses and has been reported to go into remission after cytotoxic chemotherapy, linking Diamond’s name to its discovery and treatment response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rheumatoid arthritis",
    "long_answer": "Acquired Brown’s syndrome can occur when rheumatoid arthritis, a systemic autoimmune disease, produces tenosynovitis of the superior oblique tendon and trochlea. The inflammatory synovial proliferation characteristic of rheumatoid arthritis thickens or adheres the tendon within its sheath, mechanically restricting elevation in adduction and thus producing the clinical picture of Brown’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MAPT",
    "long_answer": "The MAPT gene (microtubule-associated protein tau) encodes the tau protein that aggregates into neurofibrillary tangles, a defining lesion of tauopathies such as Alzheimer’s disease and frontotemporal dementia. Mutations or dysregulation of MAPT lead to abnormal tau hyperphosphorylation and filament formation, making MAPT the primary gene linked to these pathological tau inclusions studied in neuropathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Doxorubicin",
    "long_answer": "The CHOP chemotherapy regimen contains doxorubicin (hydroxy-daunorubicin), an anthracycline well-known for dose-dependent cardiotoxicity that can precipitate or worsen dilated cardiomyopathy. Therefore, doxorubicin is the agent in CHOP that is clinically relevant when treating or evaluating a patient with existing dilated cardiomyopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Basu et al.",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome characterised by alopecia, joint contractures, proportionate dwarfism and intellectual disability (often abbreviated ACD/ID) was first delineated in the medical literature by Basu and colleagues, who reported the condition in affected siblings and proposed it as a distinct autosomal-recessive disorder. Subsequent publications have cited this initial report by Basu et al. as the original description of Alopecia-Contractures-Dwarfism-Intellectual Disability syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The imprinted gene locus most commonly implicated in transient neonatal diabetes mellitus (TNDM) is at 6q24 on human chromosome 6. Over-expression of the 6q24 genes (e.g., PLAGL1/ZAC and HYMAI) disrupts normal regulation of insulin secretion, leading to the transient diabetic phenotype seen in affected newborns."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Hawkinsinuria results from pathogenic variants in the HPD gene, which encodes 4-hydroxyphenylpyruvate dioxygenase, an enzyme of tyrosine catabolism. The HPD gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 12 (12q24), establishing chromosome 12 as the chromosomal location responsible for this metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The CATSPER1 gene, whose product forms the calcium channel required for sperm motility and fertility, is transcribed exclusively in developing male germ cells during spermatogenesis. This testis-specific, fertility-critical gene is located on human chromosome 11 (band 11q12), making chromosome 11 the chromosome that carries a gene essential for sperm function and expressed during spermatogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC6A4",
    "long_answer": "The serotonin transporter (often called SERT or 5-HTT) is encoded by the SLC6A4 gene. Experimental work has shown that exposure to isotretinoin can reduce SLC6A4 expression and serotonin uptake in neuronal models, linking this gene to the mood-related side-effects sometimes observed during isotretinoin therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xq (long arm)",
    "long_answer": "The long arm of the X chromosome (designated Xq) harbors a high density of genes—including MECP2, FMR1, GDI1, RAB39B and others—whose mutations cause X-linked intellectual disability. Consequently, Xq, particularly its distal Xq27–Xq28 region, is recognized as the chromosomal arm most enriched for XLID-associated genes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "α-L-iduronidase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase causes mucopolysaccharidosis type I (Hurler syndrome). Loss of this enzyme blocks degradation of heparan and dermatan sulfate, leading to their accumulation in tissues. The resulting disease features coarse facial features, corneal clouding, hepatosplenomegaly, and notably progressive developmental delay culminating in intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OXCT1",
    "long_answer": "The enzyme that transfers coenzyme A (CoA) to acetoacetate is succinyl-CoA:3-ketoacid CoA transferase (SCOT), also called acetoacetyl-CoA transferase. SCOT is encoded by the OXCT1 gene. Pathogenic mutations in OXCT1 disrupt SCOT activity, leading to SCOT deficiency characterized by impaired ketone-body utilization and episodes of ketoacidosis. Thus, OXCT1 mutations are the established molecular cause of the enzyme deficiency." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The COL7A1 gene located on human chromosome 3 encodes type VII collagen, the principal constituent of anchoring fibrils that secure the basement membrane of the epidermis to the underlying dermis, thereby mechanically linking the two skin layers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Prolidase deficiency is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the PEPD gene, which encodes the enzyme prolidase (peptidase D). Molecular and cytogenetic studies have mapped the human PEPD gene to the long arm of chromosome 19, specifically band 19q13. Thus, the gene responsible for prolidase deficiency is located on chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DSPP",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the dentin sialophosphoprotein (DSPP) gene, located on chromosome 4q21, are the well-established cause of dentinogenesis imperfecta. DSPP encodes precursor proteins that are critical for normal dentin and enamel mineralization; pathogenic variants disrupt this process, leading to the characteristic opalescent, fragile teeth of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMNA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LMNA gene, most commonly the c.1824C>T (p.Gly608Gly) splice-site change, cause Hutchinson–Gilford progeria syndrome. This progeroid disorder is typified by dysmorphic facial features such as a prominent beaked nose, thin lips, and sparse or absent scalp hair, along with accelerated aging and growth failure. The mutant lamin A protein (progerin) disrupts nuclear envelope integrity, leading to the characteristic clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Yersinia",
    "long_answer": "Yersiniosis is a gastrointestinal infection most frequently produced by the enteropathogenic species Yersinia enterocolitica and Yersinia pseudotuberculosis. Both belong to the bacterial genus Yersinia, which is therefore identified as the causative genus for yersiniosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WDHA syndrome",
    "long_answer": "VIP-secreting pancreatic tumors (VIPomas)—a disease that appears under the letter “V”—release excess vasoactive intestinal peptide. The hormonal surge produces the clinical picture of chronic watery diarrhea, profound dehydration, hypokalemia, and achlorhydria known as WDHA syndrome (also called Verner-Morrison or pancreatic cholera syndrome)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CACNA1A",
    "long_answer": "Episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2) is produced by pathogenic variants in the CACNA1A gene, which encodes the α1A subunit of the P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channel. EA2 attacks typically feature transient cerebellar ataxia together with diplopia, vertigo, nausea and dysarthria, distinguishing it from other episodic ataxias. Hence, a CACNA1A mutation is the established genetic cause of the episodic ataxia subtype that presents with diplopia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Rhabdomyolysis",
    "long_answer": "In McArdle’s disease (glycogen-storage disease type V), intense or prolonged exercise can cause extensive skeletal-muscle breakdown. The resulting rhabdomyolysis releases large amounts of myoglobin into the circulation; filtered myoglobin can precipitate in renal tubules, leading to myoglobinuric acute kidney injury. Thus, rhabdomyolysis is the serious complication that can precipitate acute renal failure in these patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TGF-β/BMP signaling",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary hemorrhagic telangiectasia is most often caused by pathogenic variants in ENG (endoglin) and ACVRL1 (ALK1), and less commonly SMAD4 and GDF2. These proteins are endothelial receptors or downstream mediators for ligands of the transforming growth factor-β superfamily, especially BMP9/10. They form a receptor complex that activates SMAD1/5 signaling, regulating vascular development and stability. Thus, the disease-linked proteins act within and disrupt the TGF-β/BMP signaling pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Linkage studies of families affected by Flynn–Aird syndrome have mapped the responsible locus to human chromosome 17, indicating that the gene underlying this rare neurological disorder is carried on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPV4",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TRPV4 gene (transient receptor potential vanilloid 4) cause the Kozlowski subtype of spondyloepimetaphyseal dysplasia. Pathogenic gain-of-function variants in this calcium-permeable channel disrupt endochondral ossification, producing disproportionate dwarfism and characteristic vertebral, epiphyseal and metaphyseal skeletal abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Opioid analgesics",
    "long_answer": "Opioid analgesics (e.g., codeine, tramadol, morphine) are sometimes prescribed for moderate-to-severe osteoarthritis pain when NSAIDs or acetaminophen are insufficient. These drugs act on μ-opioid receptors to dampen pain signaling, but activation of the same receptors in the gut decreases intestinal motility, making constipation one of their most common adverse effects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDOB",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the ALDOB gene, which encodes the hepatic enzyme aldolase B, cause hereditary fructose intolerance. Lack of functional aldolase B leads to accumulation of fructose-1-phosphate after ingestion of fructose, sucrose or sorbitol, triggering acute metabolic distress. Clinically this presents with severe hypoglycaemia, abdominal pain and characteristic profuse vomiting, making vomiting a hallmark symptom of the ALDOB-associated disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Cleidocranial dysostosis results from mutations in the RUNX2/CBFA1 transcription-factor gene, which is critical for osteoblast differentiation and bone formation. The RUNX2 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (region 6p21). Thus, chromosome 6 carries the defective gene in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "Toriello–Carey syndrome has been linked to pathogenic variants in the MED12 gene. MED12 is located on the long arm of the human X chromosome (Xp13.1–q13.1). Hence, the gene associated with Toriello–Carey syndrome resides on chromosome X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carotid bodies",
    "long_answer": "Peripheral chemoreceptors that sense low arterial O₂ tension (hypoxia) are concentrated in the carotid bodies, small structures situated at the bifurcation of each common carotid artery. These glomus cells respond rapidly to decreased PaO₂ and trigger reflex increases in ventilation to restore oxygen levels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Papillon–Lefèvre syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the cathepsin C (CTSC) gene. The CTSC gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 11, specifically at 11q14.2; therefore, the pathogenic gene for Papillon–Lefèvre syndrome is located on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "46,XX male (de la Chapelle) syndrome",
    "long_answer": "The condition that occurs in roughly 1 in 18,000–40,000 male births is 46,XX testicular disorder of sex development, also called de la Chapelle or XX-male syndrome. The chromosomal anomaly is a 46,XX karyotype in which the SRY gene from the Y chromosome has translocated to the X (or another autosome), giving an individual two X chromosomes but male gonadal development. This SRY translocation on an otherwise 46,XX background is the defining chromosomal abnormality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The eponymous disorder is Pachyonychia congenita type I, also called the Jadassohn-Lewandowsky syndrome. It is an autosomal-dominant keratin disorder caused most frequently by mutations in the keratin-16 (KRT16) gene, which is situated on the long arm of chromosome 17 (17q12-q21). Hence, the gene responsible for the syndrome resides on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KMT2D",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the KMT2D gene (previously called MLL2) are the major cause of Kabuki syndrome. This autosomal-dominant disorder is characterized by moderate intellectual disability together with distinctive craniofacial dysmorphisms such as long palpebral fissures, arched eyebrows and ear anomalies, firmly establishing KMT2D mutation as the genetic basis of a syndrome that includes both intellectual disability and craniofacial abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Congenital distal spinal muscular atrophy (also called spinal muscular atrophy with respiratory distress type 1, SMARD1) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the IGHMBP2 gene. IGHMBP2 maps to the long arm of human chromosome 11 (11q13), so chromosome 11 carries the gene responsible for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RUNX2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the RUNX2 gene (also known as CBFA1) cause cleidocranial dysplasia, a skeletal disorder whose hallmark is markedly delayed bone maturation and defective ossification of the skull, clavicles and other bones. RUNX2 encodes a transcription factor that is essential for osteoblast differentiation and normal bone formation; when it is disrupted, the maturation and mineralization of bone are significantly delayed."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wilhelm Ceelen",
    "long_answer": "In 1931 the Belgian physician Wilhelm Ceelen reported a 24-year-old woman in whom he identified large numbers of hemosiderin-laden macrophages in sputum and lung specimens, allowing him to recognise and name idiopathic (progressive) pulmonary haemosiderosis while the patient was still alive—the first ante-mortem diagnosis of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEFV gene",
    "long_answer": "Familial Mediterranean fever, a prototypic periodic fever syndrome, is caused by mutations in the MEFV gene. MEFV encodes pyrin, a regulator of the inflammasome complex that controls interleukin-1β production. Pathogenic MEFV mutations dysregulate this innate immune pathway, resulting in recurrent episodes of fever and inflammation characteristic of periodic fever syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Roughly doubled (≈4–6%)",
    "long_answer": "In the general population the risk of a serious autosomal-recessive disorder or major congenital anomaly in a newborn is about 2–3%.  When the parents are first cousins the inbreeding coefficient (F = 1/16) makes it two to three times more likely that both carry the same recessive mutation, adding another ~2–3% absolute risk.  Thus the total risk rises to roughly 4–6%, i.e., about a two-fold increase compared with unrelated parents.  Tel Hashomer camptodactyly syndrome is inherited as an autosomal-recessive trait, so its probability likewise rises in offspring of first-cousin marriages, illustrating the general principle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Aldred syndrome (also known as anophthalmia–esophageal–genital or SOX2 anophthalmia syndrome) is produced by heterozygous deletions or loss-of-function mutations involving the SOX2 gene situated on the long arm of chromosome 3 (region 3q26.3-q27). Therefore, the causal deletion affects chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ADAMTS13",
    "long_answer": "Thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP) is a blood disorder that often starts with nonspecific, influenza-like symptoms (fever, malaise). It is caused by acquired autoantibody-mediated inhibition or inherited deficiency of the metalloprotease ADAMTS13. Loss of ADAMTS13 activity prevents cleavage of ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers, leading to widespread platelet-rich microthrombi and the characteristic thrombocytopenia and microangiopathic hemolytic anemia of TTP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ROBO3",
    "long_answer": "Recessive loss-of-function mutations in the ROBO3 gene (Roundabout homolog 3) cause the disorder “horizontal gaze palsy with progressive scoliosis (HGPPS).” ROBO3 is required for midline crossing of certain brain-stem axons; its deficiency disrupts horizontal conjugate eye movements, producing congenital horizontal gaze palsy, and leads to subsequent progressive scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nectin-1",
    "long_answer": "Nectin-1 (encoded by the PVRL1 gene) is a calcium-independent cellular adhesion molecule that forms adherens junctions and also serves as a primary entry receptor for herpes simplex viruses 1 and 2. Loss-of-function mutations in PVRL1 cause cleft lip/palate-ectodermal dysplasia type 1, a genetic disorder marked by abnormal development of ectoderm-derived structures such as teeth, hair, nails, and skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KMT2D (MLL2)",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KMT2D (also called MLL2) gene are the principal cause of Kabuki syndrome. This multisystem disorder is clinically characterized by distinctive facial features, intellectual disability, skeletal anomalies and, importantly, persistent postnatal growth retardation/short stature. Identification of pathogenic KMT2D variants in most affected individuals firmly links this gene mutation to the syndrome that includes postnatal growth retardation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KMT2D",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KMT2D gene (also called MLL2) are the major cause of Kabuki syndrome, a congenital disorder whose core clinical picture includes postnatal growth failure and distinctive, ‘peculiar’ facial features such as arched eyebrows and long palpebral fissures. Thus, mutation of KMT2D underlies this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3CA",
    "long_answer": "The overgrowth syndrome characterized by macrocephaly and diffuse capillary malformations—often termed M-CM or MCAP (megalencephaly-capillary malformation) syndrome—is caused by post-zygotic, activating mutations in the PIK3CA gene. PIK3CA encodes the p110α catalytic subunit of phosphatidylinositol-3-kinase; gain-of-function variants increase PI3K-AKT-mTOR signaling, leading to cellular overgrowth and vascular malformation phenotypes seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The PEPD gene, which encodes Peptidase D (prolidase), is located at 19q13 on chromosome 19. Haploinsufficiency of this gene causes prolidase deficiency, a condition that typically presents with dermatological manifestations such as chronic lower-extremity skin ulcers and eczema-like lesions. Thus, the chromosome associated with the skin findings linked to Peptidase D haploinsufficiency is chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FLG (filaggrin) gene, located on the long arm of human chromosome 1 (1q21), cause ichthyosis vulgaris—a skin disorder characterized by defective keratin aggregation, leading to peeling and fine scaling of the stratum corneum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the genes D2HGDH (encoding D-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase) and L2HGDH (encoding L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase) impair degradation of the stereoisomers of 2-hydroxyglutarate. The resulting accumulation of D- or L-2-hydroxyglutarate in body fluids produces the neurometabolic disorder collectively known as 2-hydroxyglutaric aciduria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The human SFTPB gene, which encodes surfactant protein B essential for pulmonary surfactant function, is located on the short arm of chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2p12-p11.2)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome is most frequently caused by pathogenic variants in the TCOF1 gene. TCOF1 is located at cytogenetic band 5q32-q33 on chromosome 5, making this chromosome the one most commonly implicated in the disorder and its associated ocular abnormalities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Xeroderma pigmentosum (XP) is an autosomal-recessive DNA-repair disorder that causes extreme photosensitivity, very early-onset cutaneous and ocular cancers, and in many patients progressive neurodegeneration. Because of these complications, numerous natural-history studies and registry reports document markedly reduced survival: median life expectancy has been reported between the late 20s and mid-30s, with many deaths occurring from metastatic skin cancers or neurological decline. In contrast, the average life expectancy in the general population of high-income countries is >75–80 years. Therefore, patients with xeroderma pigmentosum have a significantly shorter—not greater—life expectancy than the average population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The photosensitive form of trichothiodystrophy results from mutations in the ERCC2 (XPD) gene, which encodes a DNA-repair helicase within the TFIIH complex. ERCC2 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 19 (band 19q13.2-q13.3), so chromosome 19 carries the gene responsible for this photosensitive variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NKX2-5",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function or missense mutations in the cardiac transcription factor gene NKX2-5 are a well-documented genetic cause of atrial septal defects (ASDs), a congenital heart malformation that allows abnormal blood flow between the right and left atria. NKX2-5 is critical for cardiac morphogenesis; pathogenic variants disrupt septal formation and conduction system development, leading to ASDs often accompanied by conduction abnormalities. Numerous familial and sporadic cases have linked NKX2-5 mutations to this specific inter-atrial defect, making it one of the most widely recognized genetic associations with ASDs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyclooxygenase-2 (COX-2)",
    "long_answer": "Cyclooxygenase-2 is the inducible isoform of prostaglandin-endoperoxide synthase that converts arachidonic acid to PGH₂, the precursor of multiple prostaglandins. COX-2 expression is up-regulated by inflammatory cytokines and oxidative stress, and hyperlipidemia further enhances COX-2–mediated prostaglandin production in vascular and immune cells, thereby amplifying the inflammatory response."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC1A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC1A1 gene, which encodes the renal/neuronal excitatory amino-acid transporter EAAT3, abolish proximal-tubular reabsorption of the dicarboxylic amino acids aspartate and glutamate. This defect produces the inherited disorder dicarboxylic aminoaciduria, whose hallmark is markedly increased urinary excretion of aspartate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLXB9 (MNX1)",
    "long_answer": "Currarino syndrome, an autosomal-dominant congenital malformation involving partial agenesis of the sacrum, anorectal anomalies, and a presacral mass, is almost always caused by pathogenic mutations in the homeobox gene HLXB9 (also called MNX1) on chromosome 7q36. Loss-of-function variants in HLXB9 disrupt caudal embryonic development, producing the characteristic sacral and presacral defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Xylitol",
    "long_answer": "Xylitol is a five-carbon sugar alcohol used as a sweetener. In the liver it is oxidized by xylitol (L-xylulose) dehydrogenase to form xylulose. Patients with essential pentosuria lack L-xylulose reductase, the enzyme that normally converts L-xylulose back to xylitol, leaving them relatively deficient in xylitol and causing xylulose to accumulate and be excreted. Hence, the sugar substitute whose metabolism yields the compound missing in pentosuria is xylitol."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The gene most frequently implicated in pachygyria is LIS1 (also called PAFAH1B1), which lies on chromosome 17p13.3. Mutations or deletions of the LIS1 gene disrupt neuronal migration during cortical development, producing the thickened, abnormally broad convolutions characteristic of pachygyria. Hence, chromosome 17 is the chromosome that contains the gene most commonly linked to this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The ATRX protein regulates expression of the α-globin genes (HBA1/HBA2). These α-globin genes interact functionally with ATRX, and their dysregulation produces the α-thalassemia seen in ATR-X syndrome. The α-globin gene cluster resides on human chromosome 16 (band 16p13.3), so chromosome 16 is the chromosome that carries the ATRX-interacting gene involved in α-thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACOX1",
    "long_answer": "Acyl-CoA oxidase deficiency is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ACOX1 gene, which encodes peroxisomal very-long-chain acyl-CoA oxidase. Deficiency of this enzyme blocks the first step of peroxisomal β-oxidation, leading to accumulation of very-long-chain fatty acids in tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome characterized by absent fingerprints with congenital milia (hereditary adermatoglyphia) is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the SMARCAD1 helicase gene. SMARCAD1 is situated at cytogenetic band 4q22 on human chromosome 4, so the causative gene resides on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Weaver syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the EZH2 gene, a histone-methyltransferase–encoding gene mapped to cytogenetic band 7q36.1 on human chromosome 7. Thus, the causative gene for Weaver syndrome resides on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The disorder described is brachydactyly type C, which presents with symmetric shortening of the middle phalanges of the fingers and a relatively long thumb. Brachydactyly type C is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, as demonstrated by multiple affected individuals in successive generations and documented mutations in genes such as GDF5 that follow dominant inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucokinase deficiency",
    "long_answer": "Glucokinase (hexokinase IV) catalyzes the first step of glycolysis in hepatocytes and pancreatic β-cells. Loss-of-function mutations that reduce glucokinase activity produce a mild, persistent hyperglycaemia classified as Maturity-Onset Diabetes of the Young type 2 (MODY 2), a form of diabetes mellitus that falls squarely within endocrinology. Thus, glucokinase deficiency links a glycolytic enzyme defect to an endocrine disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Henry Radcliffe Crocker",
    "long_answer": "Extramammary Paget's disease was first reported in 1889 by the British dermatologist Henry Radcliffe Crocker, who described Paget‐like intraepithelial adenocarcinoma involving the scrotal and penile skin. His observation established the entity now known as extramammary Paget's disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The classic form of hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia is caused by mutations in the EDA (ectodysplasin A) gene, which is positioned on the long arm of the human X chromosome (region Xq12–q13). Because the disorder is typically inherited in an X-linked recessive manner, its pathogenic gene is located on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Krabbe disease (globoid cell leukodystrophy) results from mutations in the GALC gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme galactocerebrosidase. The GALC gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 14 (region 14q31–q32). Deficiency of galactocerebrosidase impairs degradation of galactolipids, leading to accumulation of psychosine and the characteristic demyelination seen in Krabbe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Colloid",
    "long_answer": "Colloidal silver is a colloid—a heterogeneous mixture in which microscopic metallic silver particles are dispersed throughout a liquid medium. The suspended particles are small enough to remain dispersed but large enough to scatter light (Tyndall effect). Chronic ingestion can deposit silver in tissues, leading to argyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Jumping Frenchmen of Maine",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome is called Jumping Frenchmen of Maine, a culture-specific startle disorder first reported in lumberjacks from the Moosehead Lake area of Maine in the 19th century. Affected individuals exhibit an exaggerated startle reflex with involuntary jumps, echolalia, and automatic obedience."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAQ",
    "long_answer": "Sturge-Weber syndrome, distinguished by facial port-wine stains and early-onset seizures from leptomeningeal angiomas, is caused by a somatic activating mutation in the GNAQ gene (encoding the Gαq subunit of heterotrimeric G proteins). This mosaic GNAQ mutation drives abnormal vascular development underlying the syndrome’s hallmark features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hippocrates",
    "long_answer": "The earliest recorded description of hepatic encephalopathy is attributed to Hippocrates (5th century BC), who wrote that jaundiced patients who developed delirium were likely to die. This observation of mental changes associated with severe liver disease is recognized as the first account of the syndrome now termed hepatic encephalopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Odonto-onycho-dermal dysplasia is an autosomal-recessive ectodermal dysplasia caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WNT10A gene. WNT10A is mapped to chromosome 2 at band q35, so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot",
    "long_answer": "Thrombin (EC 3.4.21.5), the serine protease that cleaves fibrinogen to form fibrin during blood coagulation, is comprehensively curated in the UniProtKB/Swiss-Prot database (e.g., entry P00734). Swiss-Prot provides expert-reviewed, detailed annotations on its sequence, catalytic mechanism, functional domains, post-translational modifications, and disease relevance, making it the authoritative resource for information on this protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The gene TLR4, located on human chromosome 9 (9q33-q34), encodes Toll-like receptor 4. TLR4 is a key pattern-recognition receptor that senses microbial products, activates immune signaling, and communicates with enteric and central nervous systems—core elements of the gut-brain axis. Polymorphisms and altered expression of TLR4 have been repeatedly linked to the low-grade mucosal inflammation observed in irritable bowel syndrome, making chromosome 9 the chromosomal location in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The majority of Rubinstein–Taybi syndrome cases are caused by pathogenic variants or deletions in the CREBBP gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (region 16p13.3). Therefore, chromosome 16 is the chromosome that harbors the gene most commonly mutated in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AAAS gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the AAAS gene, which encodes the nuclear-pore protein ALADIN, cause Allgrove (Triple-A) syndrome. This autosomal-recessive disorder is classically characterized by alacrima—marked absence of tear production—along with achalasia and adrenal insufficiency. Hence, an AAAS gene mutation is directly linked to a condition that produces a lack of tears."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eccentric hypertrophy",
    "long_answer": "Dilated cardiomyopathy is characterized by ventricular chamber dilation with relative wall thinning, produced by the addition of sarcomeres in series—classically termed eccentric hypertrophy. This pattern contrasts with the concentric hypertrophy seen in pressure-overload states and is the form of hypertrophy observed in genetic or syndromic conditions linked to ciliopathies such as Bardet–Biedl syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Argininemia (hyperargininemia) results from deficiency of the hepatic enzyme arginase 1. The ARG1 gene that encodes arginase 1 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 6 (6q23.2). Therefore, chromosome 6 contains the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant congenital macrostomia (lateral facial cleft, Tessier 7) has been mapped to the CLFT1 locus on 7q32 of chromosome 7. Linkage studies in affected families demonstrated that the causal mutation segregates with this chromosomal region, establishing chromosome 7 as the site associated with the genetic defect leading to macrostomia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LHCGR",
    "long_answer": "Leydig cell hypoplasia is a 46,XY disorder of sex development produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LHCGR gene, which encodes the luteinizing hormone/chorionic gonadotropin receptor. Absence of functional LH/hCG signaling impairs Leydig cell differentiation and testosterone production, leading to undervirilization and the characteristic hypoplastic Leydig cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Traumatic brain injury",
    "long_answer": "Infantile Epileptic Spasms Syndrome (IESS) is frequently triggered by structural brain damage. One well-recognized structural etiology is traumatic brain injury (TBI)—for example, abusive head trauma or severe accidental head injury. The external mechanical force causes contusions, hemorrhage or diffuse injury to the immature cortex, and this disruption of cortical networks is a common precipitant of the epileptic spasms characteristic of IESS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The clinical triad of congenital (myotonic) muscular dystrophy, early-onset/infantile cataracts, and endocrine defects such as primary hypogonadism is characteristic of myotonic dystrophy (Steinert disease). This disorder is produced by a CTG repeat expansion in the DMPK gene and is transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, showing anticipation across generations. Hence the inheritance pattern of the described syndrome is autosomal dominant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bordetella pertussis",
    "long_answer": "Bordetella pertussis is the Gram-negative coccobacillus responsible for pertussis (whooping cough). Infection damages the respiratory epithelium and releases pertussis toxin, leading to characteristic paroxysmal, high-pitched coughing fits often followed by a inspiratory “whoop.” These intense bouts of coughing can last for weeks and commonly produce marked fatigue and exhaustion in patients."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver cirrhosis",
    "long_answer": "Cirrhosis of the liver is the most frequent cause of portal hypertension; progressive scarring obstructs intra-hepatic blood flow, raising portal venous pressure. Because more than 90 % of the body’s vitamin A is stored in hepatic stellate cells, the parenchymal damage and loss of storage capacity that accompany cirrhosis markedly reduce vitamin A reserves, predisposing patients to vitamin A deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Genetic linkage and positional cloning studies have shown that Keratolytic Winter Erythema (Oudtshoorn disease) maps to the 8p23.1–p22 region; the causal duplication/variant resides on human chromosome 8, establishing this chromosome as the location of the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Coronal plane",
    "long_answer": "Anterior plagiocephaly typically arises from premature unilateral fusion of the coronal suture. The coronal suture lies in and defines the coronal (frontal) anatomical plane that divides the body into anterior and posterior portions; hence, the anatomical plane associated with the suture causing anterior plagiocephaly is the coronal plane."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease–like syndrome 2 (HDL2) is caused by a CAG/CTG trinucleotide-repeat expansion in the Junctophilin-3 (JPH3) gene. The JPH3 gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 16 (16q24.2-q24.3); therefore, chromosome 16 contains the gene responsible for HDL2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Henri-Alexandre Danlos",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary connective-tissue disorders known collectively as Ehlers–Danlos syndromes were named after two physicians who first characterized their clinical features: the Danish dermatologist Edvard Ehlers and the French physician-dermatologist Henri-Alexandre Danlos, who described patients with striking skin hyperextensibility and fragility. Danlos’s detailed observations, together with Ehlers’s earlier reports, led to the eponym that honors both men."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chordae tendineae",
    "long_answer": "Thin, fibrous chordae tendineae connect each leaflet of the mitral valve to the papillary muscles. During ventricular systole they become tense, anchoring the valve leaflets and preventing them from prolapsing back into the left atrium. In rheumatic or degenerative mitral stenosis these cords can fuse, thicken, or shorten, impairing their function and contributing to valve dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RPE65",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the RPE65 gene damage the retinal pigment epithelium—an essential epithelial layer of the eye—by blocking the visual (retinoid) cycle. This causes Leber congenital amaurosis type 2 / early-onset severe retinal dystrophy, characterized by progressive vision loss that frequently culminates in childhood blindness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "Syndromic microphthalmia (Lenz microphthalmia, MCOPS2) is caused by mutations in the BCOR gene. BCOR is located at Xp11.4 on the human X chromosome, so the gene responsible for this form of syndromic microphthalmia resides on chromosome X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "By standard pediatric and neurologic definitions, microcephaly is diagnosed when head circumference is more than two standard deviations (≤ –2 SD) below the age- and sex-specific population mean. A threshold of three standard deviations is used to designate “severe microcephaly,” but it is not the general definition of microcephaly itself. Therefore, stating that microcephaly is defined at three SD below the mean is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WNT3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the WNT3 gene abolish canonical Wnt signalling that is essential for limb-bud initiation and early lung development. Homozygous WNT3 mutations therefore cause the rare tetra-amelia syndrome, in which affected infants are born without all four limbs and exhibit severe pulmonary agenesis or hypoplasia along with other midline defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "The PC-K6A subtype of pachyonychia congenita is caused by pathogenic variants in the keratin 6A gene (KRT6A). KRT6A is located in the type II keratin gene cluster on human chromosome 12 (band 12q13). Mutations in this chromosomal region disrupt keratin filament formation in epithelial tissues, leading to the characteristic nail and skin abnormalities of PC-K6A."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Nager syndrome (acrofacial dysostosis, Nager type) is most frequently caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the splicing factor gene SF3B4. The SF3B4 gene is located at cytogenetic band 1q21.2 on human chromosome 1; hence chromosome 1 harbors the gene most commonly mutated in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Milroy’s congenital lymphedema is caused by pathogenic variants in the FLT4 gene (encoding VEGFR-3), which is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 (region 5q34–q35.3)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Congenital dysfibrinogenemia arises from pathogenic variants in the three fibrinogen-coding genes—FGA, FGB, and FGG—whose products form the fibrinogen molecule. All three genes lie in a cluster on the long arm of human chromosome 4 (region 4q23). Mutations here produce structurally abnormal fibrinogen that prolongs the thrombin time test, making chromosome 4 the location of the genes responsible."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "OMIM entry 278700 corresponds to Xeroderma Pigmentosum, complementation group A (XP-A). XP-A is one of the classic nucleotide-excision repair (NER) disorders. Mutations in the XPA gene cripple the NER pathway that normally removes UV-induced DNA lesions such as cyclobutane-pyrimidine dimers and 6-4 photoproducts. As a result, patients exhibit extreme sun sensitivity, accumulation of unrepaired UV DNA damage, and a markedly increased risk of skin cancers. Therefore, OMIM #278700 is indeed linked to a condition in which DNA repair of UV-induced damage is impaired."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SEPT9 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary neuralgic amyotrophy, a familial disorder that clinically resembles the idiopathic brachial neuritis known as Parsonage-Turner syndrome, is caused by heterozygous mutations in the SEPT9 gene on chromosome 17q25. Patients with SEPT9 mutations experience the same recurrent, painful brachial plexus attacks and patchy paresis seen in Parsonage-Turner syndrome, establishing SEPT9 mutation as the genetic counterpart of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human tetanus immune globulin",
    "long_answer": "Hippocrates recorded cases of tetanus as early as 400 BC, and the disease remains rapidly fatal without intervention. Modern management centers on immediate administration of human tetanus immune globulin (HTIG), which neutralizes circulating tetanospasmin before it binds to nervous tissue, drastically lowering mortality. Debridement, antibiotics and supportive care are adjuncts, but HTIG is the primary, life-saving treatment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Infantile Convulsions and Choreoathetosis (ICCA) syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the PRRT2 gene. PRRT2 has been consistently mapped to the 16p11.2 region of the human genome, so the disease-associated gene resides on human chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Epstein syndrome belongs to the group of MYH9-related disorders. It is caused by pathogenic variants in the MYH9 gene, which encodes non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA and is located on human chromosome 22 (band 22q12–q13). Hence, chromosome 22 is the chromosome associated with the mutation responsible for Epstein syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Sickle cell anemia results from a single-nucleotide mutation (Glu6Val) in the β-globin gene (HBB). The HBB gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 11 (11p15.5); therefore, chromosome 11 carries the gene whose mutation causes sickle cell anemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Inherited Factor VII deficiency results from mutations in the F7 gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 13 (band 13q34). Thus, the gene responsible for this coagulation disorder resides on chromosome 13."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-TL1",
    "long_answer": "A point mutation (classically A3243G) in the mitochondrial tRNALeu(UUR) gene MT-TL1 causes MELAS (mitochondrial encephalomyopathy, lactic acidosis and stroke-like episodes), a disorder that primarily involves the nervous system. Because MT-TL1 resides in mitochondrial DNA, which is passed only from mother to offspring, the condition shows strict maternal inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN4A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SCN4A gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle voltage-gated sodium channel Nav1.4, cause hyperkalemic periodic paralysis. This channelopathy produces brief episodes of flaccid paralysis that are often precipitated by rest after exertion or fasting and characteristically disappear spontaneously—frequently after a period of sleep—leaving normal muscle strength between attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PPOX",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PPOX gene (protoporphyrinogen oxidase), located on chromosome 1q22, cause variegate porphyria. This porphyria subtype characteristically produces dermatological manifestations such as photosensitive blistering skin lesions and, during acute attacks, neurological symptoms including abdominal neuropathic pain, motor weakness and neuropsychiatric disturbances. Thus, a chromosome 1 PPOX mutation links a single gene defect to a disorder with both skin and neurological features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher’s disease results from loss-of-function mutations in the GBA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase. The human GBA gene is located on chromosome 1 (band 1q21). Mutations in this chromosome 1 gene impair glucocerebrosidase activity, leading to the accumulation of glucocerebroside and the characteristic manifestations of Gaucher’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fibula",
    "long_answer": "Atelosteogenesis type I results from dominant mutations in the FLNB gene. These mutations impair endochondral ossification of several long bones, but a characteristic radiological hallmark is complete absence or severe hypoplasia of the fibula, making this bone the one classically affected in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypertension",
    "long_answer": "Long-standing high blood pressure is a recognized risk factor for both thoracic and abdominal aortic aneurysms. Chronic or heavy alcohol consumption elevates systemic blood pressure, and this alcohol-related hypertension accelerates degeneration of the aortic wall, thereby increasing the likelihood of aneurysm formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoporosis",
    "long_answer": "In juvenile idiopathic arthritis, chronic systemic inflammation, reduced physical activity, and long-term corticosteroid therapy can impair bone mineralization and decrease bone density. This can progress to osteoporosis, a long-term complication that significantly elevates the risk of fragility and vertebral fractures in affected children."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTC gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OTC (ornithine transcarbamylase) gene impair the urea cycle enzyme ornithine transcarbamylase. This blocks normal conversion of ammonia to urea, leading to toxic accumulation of ammonia (hyper-ammonemia) in the blood—a defining feature of ornithine transcarbamylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HTT",
    "long_answer": "A CAG-repeat expansion mutation in the HTT (huntingtin) gene on chromosome 4 underlies Huntington’s disease, a hereditary neurodegenerative syndrome whose hallmark is progressive extrapyramidal movement disturbances (chorea, dystonia, rigidity) that worsen over time."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Some cases of congenital diaphragmatic hernia, in which the defect may be so severe that an entire hemidiaphragm is absent (diaphragmatic agenesis), are produced by pathogenic variants in the ZFPM2/FOG2 gene. This gene is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 8 (8q23), so chromosome 8 contains the gene responsible for this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "Down syndrome, the prototypical genetic disorder presenting with intellectual disability and characteristic cranio-facial dysmorphism (flat facial profile, epicanthic folds, up-slanting palpebral fissures, and brachycephaly), is caused by trisomy of chromosome 21. Thus chromosome 21 is the chromosome classically associated with this combination of clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pakistan",
    "long_answer": "Ulna hypoplasia-intellectual disability syndrome is an extremely rare condition that has been documented only once in the medical literature, in two brothers born to consanguineous parents in Pakistan. No additional cases have been reported worldwide, so the syndrome is considered unique to that single Pakistani family."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular smooth muscle",
    "long_answer": "Leiomyosarcomas arising in blood vessels originate from the smooth muscle cells that form the tunica media of vascular walls, i.e., vascular smooth muscle tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of Romano–Ward syndrome is the LQT1 subtype, caused by pathogenic variants in the KCNQ1 gene that encodes the α-subunit of the cardiac IKs potassium channel. KCNQ1 is located at chromosomal band 11p15.5, so chromosome 11 carries the gene responsible for the majority of Romano–Ward syndrome cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Gastrocnemius muscle",
    "long_answer": "In Duchenne muscular dystrophy the calf muscles, especially the gastrocnemius, undergo replacement of lost muscle fibers with fat and connective tissue. This pathological change makes the gastrocnemius appear enlarged and firm, a phenomenon termed pseudohypertrophy characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SLC26A3 gene (also called DRA) cause congenital chloride diarrhea, an autosomal-recessive disorder that presents in the neonatal period with profuse, chronic watery diarrhea. Because the causative SLC26A3 mutation can be identified by chorionic-villus sampling or amniocentesis, the disease can be detected in utero, allowing early diagnosis and management planning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pituitary MRI",
    "long_answer": "Magnetic resonance imaging of the hypothalamic-pituitary region is routinely performed to look for tumors, infiltrative lesions, surgery or radiation damage that can impair ACTH production, thereby causing secondary or tertiary adrenal insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Atherosclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Cigarette smoking damages vascular endothelium, increases LDL oxidation, and promotes inflammation, making it a major modifiable risk factor for atherosclerosis. The resulting atherosclerotic plaques can rupture or ulcerate, exposing subendothelial collagen and tissue factor, which activates platelets and the coagulation cascade, leading to intravascular thrombus formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "H3F3A",
    "long_answer": "ATRX forms a chromatin-remodeling complex with DAXX that specifically binds and deposits the histone variant H3.3. This histone variant is encoded by the H3F3A gene (and a paralog, H3F3B). Loss-of-function mutations in ATRX seen in ATR-X syndrome disrupt its interaction with H3.3, underscoring that the encoded protein of H3F3A is the histone partner of ATRX."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GATA2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GATA2 gene, which encodes the zinc-finger transcription factor GATA-binding protein 2, can cause pulmonary alveolar proteinosis. GATA2 deficiency impairs the development and function of alveolar macrophages, leading to surfactant accumulation in the alveoli and the clinical picture of PAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "None",
    "long_answer": "Alkaptonuria results from deficiency of homogentisate 1,2-dioxygenase, an enzyme that functions solely in the degradative pathway of phenylalanine and tyrosine. This step lies downstream of, and is entirely separate from, the synthetic branches that generate thyroid hormones, catecholamines, or any other hormone. Consequently, no hormone’s biosynthesis is altered by lack of this enzyme."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Acute intermittent porphyria is caused by dominant mutations in the HMBS (hydroxymethyl-bilane synthase) gene, also known as the porphobilinogen deaminase gene. This gene is situated on the long arm of chromosome 11 at band q23.3, so chromosome 11 carries the genetic locus responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II) results from deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme acid α-glucosidase encoded by the GAA gene. The GAA gene is located on human chromosome 17 at band q25.3; mutations in this locus lead to the enzymatic defect and the clinical disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Primary myeloperoxidase deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the MPO gene, which is mapped to chromosome 17q22. Mutations in this chromosomal locus disrupt the production or activity of the myeloperoxidase enzyme, leading to the clinical deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The FOXP2 gene, a transcription factor whose mutations disrupt speech and motor-skill learning, is located on human chromosome 7 (band 7q31). Because FOXP2 regulates transcriptional programs critical for neural circuits underlying motor learning, defects in this gene lead to impaired motor-skills acquisition. Therefore, the chromosome housing this gene is chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Johannes C. Pompe",
    "long_answer": "The lysosomal acid α-glucosidase deficiency disorder—now called Pompe disease or glycogen-storage disease type II—was first described in 1932 by the Dutch pathologist Johannes Cassianus Pompe. In his autopsy report of a 7-month-old infant with massive cardiomegaly and widespread glycogen accumulation, Pompe recognized a distinct clinicopathologic entity; this seminal observation led to the eponymous designation 'Pompe disease'."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Central diabetes insipidus",
    "long_answer": "Central (neurogenic) diabetes insipidus results from a deficiency of arginine vasopressin, the antidiuretic hormone whose synthetic analog desmopressin is used therapeutically in this condition. Lack of vasopressin prevents the kidneys from concentrating urine, leading to excessive dilute urine output and polydipsia—the hallmark features of central diabetes insipidus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL4A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the COL4A1 gene, which encodes the α1-chain of type IV collagen, cause an autosomal-dominant cerebrovascular syndrome characterized by congenital brain malformations. Defective type IV collagen weakens small-vessel basement membranes, leading in utero to intracranial hemorrhage that produces porencephaly and interferes with hind-brain development, resulting in cerebellar hypoplasia. Thus, COL4A1 mutation is the well-established genetic cause of the syndrome featuring both porencephaly and cerebellar hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nucleus gracilis and nucleus cuneatus",
    "long_answer": "In Brown-Séquard syndrome, hemisection of the spinal cord interrupts the ipsilateral dorsal columns (fasciculi gracilis and cuneatus), which convey fine touch, vibration, and proprioceptive impulses. These ascending fibers normally synapse in the medulla’s dorsal column nuclei—the nucleus gracilis (for lower body input) and nucleus cuneatus (for upper body input). Damage to the pathway that projects to these two nuclei therefore produces the characteristic loss of fine touch and proprioception on the same side of the body below the lesion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Clinical genetics",
    "long_answer": "Carrier detection for Lowe syndrome (oculocerebrorenal syndrome) relies on molecular testing of the OCRL gene and family-based pedigree analysis. These evaluations are performed by specialists trained in Medical/Clinical Genetics, who provide genetic counselling, arrange DNA analysis, and interpret results to identify female carriers of the X-linked mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IFT80",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic pathogenic variants in the IFT80 gene cause Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy (short-rib thoracic dysplasia), a ciliopathy in which affected infants and children have markedly short ribs and a narrow thoracic cage. This results in a ribcage that is disproportionately small relative to the rest of the skeleton, often leading to respiratory compromise. IFT80 encodes a component of the intraflagellar transport complex required for proper ciliary function; disruption of this gene produces the characteristic small-ribcage phenotype of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CACNA1A",
    "long_answer": "Episodic ataxia type 2 (EA2), a hereditary cerebellar ataxia in which patients show an impaired vestibulo-ocular reflex, is produced by pathogenic mutations in the CACNA1A gene. CACNA1A encodes the α1A subunit of P/Q-type voltage-gated calcium channels that are critical for Purkinje-cell signaling; loss-of-function variants disrupt cerebellar control of eye and head coordination, causing the characteristic ataxia and VOR deficits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal angiomyolipoma",
    "long_answer": "Wunderlich syndrome is spontaneous, non-traumatic hemorrhage around the kidney. The bleeding most often originates from a renal angiomyolipoma—a benign vascular-fat-muscle tumor whose fragile, dysplastic vessels can rupture suddenly. Massive hemorrhage from an angiomyolipoma can rapidly deplete intravascular volume, precipitating hypovolemic shock."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amniocentesis",
    "long_answer": "Amniocentesis is a standard prenatal diagnostic procedure in which fetal cells are collected from the amniotic fluid (typically at 15–20 weeks’ gestation). The cells are cultured and analyzed by karyotyping, FISH, or chromosomal microarray, allowing detection of structural abnormalities such as the deletion of the short arm of chromosome 9 (monosomy 9p/9p deletion syndrome). Thus, amniocentesis can definitively identify this specific chromosomal disorder before birth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysautonomia (Riley–Day syndrome) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the IKBKAP/ELP1 gene. This gene is mapped to chromosome 9 at band 9q31, establishing chromosome 9 as the chromosomal location associated with the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Osteopoikilosis and Buschke–Ollendorff syndrome are sclerosing bone dysplasias caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LEMD3 (MAN1) gene on chromosome 12. The tumor-suppressor protein p53, encoded by the TP53 gene, is not implicated in the pathogenesis of either condition; it is mainly associated with cell-cycle regulation, DNA repair, and cancer predisposition syndromes such as Li-Fraumeni. Therefore, the assertion that p53 is associated with both osteopoikilosis and Buschke–Ollendorff syndrome is not supported by established biomedical evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Pfeiffer syndrome results from dominant mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor-1 (FGFR1) gene. FGFR1 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 8 (8p11.2-p12). Thus, chromosome 8 contains the gene whose alteration leads to Pfeiffer syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYCN",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome, caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the MYCN gene, is characterized by microcephaly, digital anomalies, and gastrointestinal atresias—classically esophageal and/or duodenal atresia. Identification of a pathogenic MYCN mutation therefore explains the presence of duodenal atresia in this genetic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Apolipoprotein B",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypobetalipoproteinemia is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the APOB gene, which encodes apolipoprotein B (apoB-100/apoB-48). Truncating or missense mutations in this structural protein impair formation and secretion of very-low-density lipoproteins (VLDL) and low-density lipoproteins (LDL), leading to the abnormally low plasma LDL-cholesterol characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "GAPO syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ANTXR1 (anthrax toxin receptor 1) gene. ANTXR1 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2p13.3). Thus, the gene responsible for GAPO syndrome resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Renal artery stenosis",
    "long_answer": "Fibromuscular dysplasia commonly involves abnormal thickening of the renal arterial wall, producing renal artery stenosis. The resulting fall in renal perfusion activates the renin-angiotensin-aldosterone system, driving excess aldosterone secretion and thus causing secondary hyperaldosteronism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3p21.1–p14.1",
    "long_answer": "Linkage studies in families with autosomal-dominant Larsen syndrome mapped the responsible locus to the short arm of human chromosome 3, specifically the 3p21.1–p14.1 interval. The causative gene identified within this region is FLNB (filamin B); pathogenic variants in FLNB disrupt cytoskeletal organization in cartilage and connective tissue, producing the characteristic joint dislocations and skeletal abnormalities of Larsen syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TCOF1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TCOF1 gene cause Treacher-Collins syndrome (mandibulofacial dysostosis). This craniofacial disorder is classically characterized by mandibular hypoplasia or micrognathism, along with malar hypoplasia, ear anomalies and downward-slanting palpebral fissures. Thus, a pathogenic TCOF1 mutation is well-established as the genetic basis of a syndrome in which micrognathism is a hallmark feature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amenorrhea",
    "long_answer": "Elevated prolactin suppresses hypothalamic GnRH release, lowering pituitary LH and FSH secretion and reducing ovarian estrogen production. This hormonal disruption often leads to cessation of normal menstrual cycles—amenorrhea—which is one of the hallmark menstrual symptoms of hyperprolactinemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "The most common cause of autosomal-recessive nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness is mutation in the GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin-26. GJB2 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 13 (13q11-q12). Thus, chromosome 13 contains a gene whose mutation can lead to nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "André Feil",
    "long_answer": "Klippel-Feil syndrome, classically presenting with congenital fusion of two or more cervical vertebrae (producing a short, stiff neck and low posterior hairline), was first described in 1912 by the French physician André Feil together with Maurice Klippel. Feil’s detailed clinical descriptions led to the eponym, and the condition is still widely referred to as Klippel-Feil (or Feil’s) syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TWIST1 gene, positioned at chromosome 7p21, are the well-established cause of Saethre-Chotzen syndrome, a hereditary disorder characterized by premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis). TWIST1 encodes a basic helix-loop-helix transcription factor essential for normal cranial suture development; its loss-of-function mutations disrupt this process, producing the characteristic craniosynostosis phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2",
    "long_answer": "Variants of Cockayne syndrome that present with abnormal or brittle hair growth belong to the xeroderma-pigmentosum/Cockayne-syndrome/trichothiodystrophy spectrum and are caused by biallelic mutations in the ERCC2 gene (also called XPD). ERCC2 encodes the TFIIH helicase essential for transcription-coupled nucleotide-excision repair; its loss disrupts DNA repair and transcription in hair-follicle cells, leading to the characteristic hair-growth defects observed in this form of Cockayne syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carpal tunnel syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In mucopolysaccharidosis, glycosaminoglycan accumulation in the flexor tendons and transverse carpal ligament leads to compression of the median nerve at the wrist, producing carpal tunnel syndrome. This neuropathy is one of the most frequent neurological complications in MPS and is characterized by hand and wrist pain, paresthesia, and weakness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dystrophin (DMD) gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DMD gene, which encodes the structural protein dystrophin, cause Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophies. Because dystrophin is essential for membrane stability in both skeletal-muscle fibers and cardiomyocytes, its absence or deficiency leads to progressive skeletal-muscle weakness together with dilated cardiomyopathy and other cardiac complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CRB1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the CRB1 gene (Crumbs homolog 1) can cause an autosomal-recessive form of retinitis pigmentosa that often manifests a ‘Coats-like’ exudative vasculopathy. This retinal dystrophy therefore shares the characteristic vascular changes seen in Coats’ disease, making CRB1 mutation the recognized genetic cause linking the two conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CLCN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CLCN1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle chloride channel ClC-1, cause myotonia congenita. Dominant-negative missense changes give an autosomal-dominant form (Thomsen disease), while loss-of-function or null alleles produce an autosomal-recessive form (Becker disease), so the same gene produces a syndrome inherited in either fashion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type 1 is caused by mutations in the ITGB2 gene, which encodes the β2-integrin (CD18) subunit needed for leukocyte adhesion. The ITGB2 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 21 (21q22.3), so chromosome 21 carries the gene whose defects produce this immunodeficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The principal autoantigen in mucous membrane pemphigoid is BP180 (type XVII collagen, encoded by the COL17A1 gene). COL17A1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 10 (10q24.3), making chromosome 10 the chromosome that contains the gene implicated in the autoimmune reaction characteristic of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis–renal dysfunction–cholestasis (ARC) syndrome is classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene. VPS33B encodes a protein essential for intracellular vesicle trafficking; its deficiency disrupts biliary canalicular formation, renal tubular function, and musculoskeletal development, producing the triad of arthrogryposis, renal tubular dysfunction, and neonatal cholestasis characteristic of ARC syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GAA",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GAA gene, which encodes lysosomal acid α-glucosidase, cause Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II). This metabolic disorder leads to lysosomal glycogen accumulation in muscle and other tissues, producing characteristic clinical findings that include severe generalized hypotonia, muscle weakness, and cardiomegaly. Hence, a GAA gene mutation is responsible for a metabolic disorder in which hypotonia is a prominent symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transudate",
    "long_answer": "A transudate is a fluid that forms without an inflammatory stimulus; it results primarily from altered hydrostatic or oncotic pressures. Because the vascular endothelium remains intact, the fluid contains little protein and few cells. In oral and periodontal contexts, early gingival crevicular fluid in healthy tissue or certain edematous fluids are described as transudates—distinguishing them from protein-rich inflammatory exudates seen in active periodontal disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Carpenter syndrome (acrocephalopolysyndactyly type II) is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the RAB23 gene, which is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p12). Thus, the gene associated with Carpenter syndrome resides on chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly with tibia aplasia/hypoplasia—also called split-hand/foot malformation with long-bone deficiency (SHFLD)—is most often linked to recurrent microduplications at 17p13 (encompassing the BHLHA9 locus). Thus, chromosome 17 is the chromosome specifically associated with this genetic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intracranial hemorrhage",
    "long_answer": "Infective endocarditis can send septic emboli to cerebral vessels, causing mycotic aneurysms or vessel wall damage. Rupture of these lesions can precipitate an intracranial hemorrhage—an acute bleed inside the skull that constitutes a neurosurgical emergency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SUCLA2",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SUCLA2 gene (encoding the β-subunit of mitochondrial succinate-CoA ligase) cause the encephalomyopathic form of mitochondrial DNA depletion syndrome. This variant is characterized by marked reduction of mtDNA copy number predominantly in brain and skeletal muscle, leading to hypotonia, developmental delay, dystonia and other neurologic-muscular manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Salla disease is an autosomal-recessive free sialic acid storage disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the SLC17A5 gene (encoding the sialic-acid transporter ‘sialin’). The SLC17A5 locus is mapped to chromosome 6, cytogenetic band 6q14-q15; therefore, chromosome 6 carries the gene responsible for Salla disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The PAFAH1B1 (LIS1) gene, whose mutations or deletions produce classical lissencephaly and the limb anomalies (notably characteristic changes of the fingers and toes) seen in Miller-Dieker syndrome, is located at 17p13.3. Therefore, the gene that can cause lissencephaly and also affects the toes resides on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LMX1B",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LMX1B gene cause nail–patella syndrome. This genodermatosis is characterized by dystrophic or absent fingernails and other skeletal anomalies, and about 30–50 % of affected individuals develop glomerular disease that can progress to renal insufficiency. Thus, an LMX1B mutation underlies a cutaneous condition that is clinically linked to renal dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Ichthyosis follicularis with alopecia and photophobia (IFAP) syndrome is an X-linked genodermatosis. Pathogenic mutations in the MBTPS2 gene, mapped to locus Xp22.11-p22.13 on the X chromosome, are responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the CHD7 gene cause CHARGE syndrome. CHARGE is characterized by ocular colobomas, congenital heart defects, choanal atresia and multiple skeletal (bone and cartilage) malformations, reflecting CHD7’s role in neural-crest-derived tissues during eye, heart, and bone development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase",
    "long_answer": "AMP is a potent allosteric inhibitor of the gluconeogenic enzyme fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase (FBPase). Excess hepatic FBPase activity drives glucose overproduction in type 2 diabetes. Several antidiabetic drug candidates (e.g., MB06322/CS-917, PF-05175157) are designed as AMP mimetics that bind to the AMP regulatory site of FBPase, thereby suppressing its catalytic activity and lowering hepatic glucose output. Thus, the therapeutic target whose activity is inhibited by AMP-mimicking compounds is fructose-1,6-bisphosphatase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NDP",
    "long_answer": "Mutation of the NDP gene (encoding norrin) on Xp11.3 causes Norrie disease, an X-linked recessive syndrome that presents with congenital blindness and later-onset sensorineural hearing loss, thus combining visual and auditory impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Fish-eye disease is a partial lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT) deficiency produced by missense mutations in exons of the LCAT gene. The human LCAT gene is located on chromosome 16 (band 16q22.1); therefore, the exon mutation that causes Fish-eye disease resides on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Beckwith–Wiedemann syndrome arises from abnormalities in the imprinted gene cluster at 11p15 on human chromosome 11, where genes such as CDKN1C, IGF2, and H19 reside. Mutations or epigenetic disruptions in this region lead to the characteristic over-growth features of the syndrome, establishing chromosome 11 as the chromosomal location involved."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RELN (reelin)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RELN gene, located on chromosome 7q22, disrupt reelin signaling required for proper neuronal migration. Homozygous or compound-heterozygous RELN mutations cause an autosomal-recessive form of lissencephaly with cerebellar hypoplasia, characterized by a smooth cerebral cortex and underdeveloped cerebellum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease is caused by a CAG-repeat expansion in the HTT (IT15) gene, which has been precisely mapped to the short arm of chromosome 4 (region 4p16.3). Thus, the pathogenic allele responsible for this autosomal-dominant neurodegenerative disorder resides on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in a single allele of the spliceosomal gene SF3B4 lead to haploinsufficiency that causes Nager acrofacial dysostosis. Because the phenotype results from heterozygous (one-copy) defects, the disorder follows an autosomal dominant inheritance pattern, with affected individuals transmitting the trait regardless of sex and a 50% recurrence risk for offspring."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Naxos",
    "long_answer": "Recessive mutations in the desmoplakin (DSP) gene can produce the cardiocutaneous disorder known as the Carvajal-Naxos (or Naxos-like) syndrome, characterized by woolly hair, palmoplantar keratoderma and arrhythmogenic cardiomyopathy. The condition takes its name from the Greek island of Naxos, where the first affected families were described, linking the island to this DSP-related syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type 2 results from loss-of-function mutations in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 22 (22q12). Mutations or deletions of this chromosomal region lead to the characteristic bilateral vestibular schwannomas and other tumors seen in NF2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "The combination of congenital nystagmus with zonular (lamellar) cataract defines the disorder ‘X-linked congenital cataract with nystagmus’ (CXN). In reported pedigrees only hemizygous males are fully affected, while heterozygous females show milder lens opacities, establishing an X-linked recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OMIM",
    "long_answer": "The Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man (OMIM) database is the standard resource for curated genetic information on human diseases. Searching OMIM for arachnoiditis—a postoperative inflammatory condition that can follow spinal surgery and manifest with chronic pain and neurologic deficits—provides documented gene associations, inheritance patterns, and relevant literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The CD55 gene encodes decay-accelerating factor (DAF), a membrane protein that limits complement activation and is absent from blood cells in paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria. CD55 resides in the Regulators of Complement Activation cluster at cytogenetic band 1q32 on human chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Propranolol",
    "long_answer": "Infantile hemangioma is a common benign vascular tumor; treatment is only needed when the lesion impairs vital functions such as vision or breathing. Current consensus guidelines identify oral propranolol—a non-selective β-adrenergic blocker—as the first-line therapy because it rapidly induces vasoconstriction, down-regulates angiogenic signals (e.g., VEGF), and promotes endothelial apoptosis, leading to marked shrinkage of the hemangioma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pineal gland",
    "long_answer": "The pineal gland can give rise to pineoblastomas—high-grade, small-blue-cell tumors that mirror the appearance of primitive neuroectodermal tumors (PNETs). Histologically, pineoblastomas are virtually indistinguishable from PNETs because both derive from primitive neuroepithelial cells and show identical sheets of undifferentiated neuroectodermal cells with high mitotic activity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bacillus coagulans",
    "long_answer": "Bacillus coagulans is a Gram-positive, spore-forming rod whose spores possess an outer silicate (silica) layer. This silica coat acts as a micro-encapsulation that makes the spores highly resistant to the strongly acidic pH of gastric juice. After the spores pass through the stomach and germinate, the resulting vegetative cells remain ordinary bacteria that are readily eliminated by standard bactericidal antibiotics, distinguishing B. coagulans from antibiotic-resistant spore probiotics such as B. clausii."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SGSH",
    "long_answer": "A loss-of-function mutation in the SGSH gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme heparan-N-sulfatase (sulfamidase), causes mucopolysaccharidosis type IIIA (Sanfilippo syndrome A). This lysosomal storage disease leads to accumulation of heparan sulfate in the central nervous system, producing progressive neurodegeneration and ultimately severe intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary arterial hypertension",
    "long_answer": "In systemic scleroderma (systemic sclerosis) the small-vessel vasculopathy can evolve into pulmonary arterial hypertension. This complication is one of the leading causes of mortality in these patients because the persistently elevated pulmonary artery pressures overload the right ventricle, eventually producing right-sided heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The only genetic variant repeatedly linked to benign symmetric (Madelung) lipomatosis is a single-nucleotide polymorphism in the uncoupling protein-1 (UCP1) gene. UCP1, which is expressed in brown adipose tissue—the tissue that proliferates in this disease—maps to the long arm of chromosome 4 (4q31). Hence, chromosome 4 is the chromosome associated with the genetic polymorphism found in benign symmetric lipomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the BCOR gene cause Lenz microphthalmia syndrome (syndromic microphthalmia-2, MCOPS2). This X-linked disorder is characterized by severe microphthalmia/anophthalmia together with dental abnormalities (such as hypodontia and malformed teeth), intellectual disability, and additional skeletal, digital and urogenital malformations. BCOR encodes the BCL-6 corepressor, and loss-of-function mutations disrupt normal ocular and craniofacial development, producing the described syndromic phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "The GJB2 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein connexin 26 and is the most common genetic cause of autosomal-recessive nonsyndromic sensorineural deafness (DFNB1), is located on human chromosome 13 at band 13q11-q12. Thus, chromosome 13 contains a gene whose mutation produces nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Familial amyloid polyneuropathy (FAP) most commonly results from pathogenic variants in the transthyretin (TTR) gene. The TTR gene is located on human chromosome 18 (band 18q12.1). Therefore, chromosome 18 carries the gene responsible for the autosomal-dominant form of FAP."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "α-Sarcoglycanopathy results from mutations in the SGCA gene, which encodes the α-sarcoglycan subunit of the dystrophin-associated glycoprotein complex. The SGCA gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 17 (17q12-q21). Therefore, chromosome 17 is the chromosomal location implicated in α-sarcoglycanopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPS1 and EXT1",
    "long_answer": "Cone-shaped epiphyses of the phalanges are characteristic of Langer-Giedion (trichorhinophalangeal syndrome type II). This disorder is produced by a microdeletion on chromosome 8q24 that removes two contiguous genes—TRPS1 and EXT1. Loss of TRPS1 disrupts cartilage and bone development, while deletion of EXT1 contributes to skeletal anomalies, together producing the distinctive cone-shaped epiphyses seen radiographically."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Leigh syndrome can result from mutations in the COX10 gene, which encodes heme-O synthase, an essential enzyme for heme A biosynthesis required for cytochrome-c oxidase (complex IV) assembly. The human COX10 gene is located on chromosome 17 (band 17p12); therefore, chromosome 17 carries the heme A-biosynthesis gene whose defects cause Leigh syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Lipoprotein lipase deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the LPL gene, which maps to the short arm of chromosome 8 (region 8p21.3-p22). Mutations in this chromosomal locus impair LPL enzyme activity, preventing normal triglyceride clearance and causing the marked hypertriglyceridemia characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The single gene most frequently mutated in familial hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is the β-myosin heavy chain gene (MYH7), which is located on chromosome 14 (band q11-q12). Pathogenic variants in MYH7 alter the sarcomeric motor protein, leading to the dominant inheritance and myocardial hypertrophy characteristic of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NCSTN, PSEN1, PSENEN",
    "long_answer": "Hidradenitis suppurativa (acne inversa) is characterized by recurrent, painful nodules, abscesses and chronic skin inflammation. Familial forms of this condition are most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in three gamma-secretase complex genes—NCSTN (nicastrin), PSEN1 (presenilin-1) and PSENEN (presenilin enhancer 2). Defective gamma-secretase disrupts Notch signalling, impairing follicular epithelial homeostasis and triggering the chronic inflammatory nodules typical of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Face buttocks extensor limb surfaces",
    "long_answer": "In erythrokeratodermia with ataxia (Giroux-Barbeau syndrome), the characteristic erythematous, hyperkeratotic plaques occur symmetrically on the face, the buttocks, and the extensor (outer) surfaces of the arms and legs. These regions are repeatedly emphasized in clinical and OMIM descriptions as the primary sites of cutaneous involvement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Hypoprothrombinemia arises from mutations in the F2 gene, which encodes coagulation factor II (prothrombin). The F2 gene is mapped to human chromosome 11 (band 11p11.2); thus, chromosome 11 harbors the gene whose mutation impairs prothrombin production and leads to the clotting disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "One of the principal genes whose mutations cause Joubert syndrome is AHI1 (Abelson helper integration site-1). AHI1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 6 (6q23). Pathogenic variants in this chromosome-6 gene disrupt ciliary signaling and brainstem–cerebellar development, producing the characteristic neurological features of Joubert syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The gene most characteristically linked to pontocerebellar hypoplasia is TSEN54, which maps to the long arm of human chromosome 17 (17q25.1). Mutations in TSEN54 are the predominant cause of pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 2, the prototypical and most common form of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nature Genetics",
    "long_answer": "The inaugural report linking erythromelalgia to a mutation in the sodium-channel gene SCN9A appeared in Nature Genetics in 2004, when Yang and colleagues showed that missense mutations in SCN9A (encoding NaV1.7) segregated with familial primary erythromelalgia and produced gain-of-function channel activity. This paper established the causal association and is recognized as the first publication describing it."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OXCT1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the OXCT1 gene (encoding succinyl-CoA:3-oxoacid CoA-transferase, SCOT) impair peripheral utilization of acetoacetate and β-hydroxybutyrate. Because tissues cannot catabolize ketone bodies, hepatic ketogenesis proceeds unchecked, resulting in episodes of severe, uncontrolled ketone-body accumulation and ketoacidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Current authoritative databases of Mendelian disease genes (e.g., OMIM, ClinVar, HGMD, ClinGen) and the peer-reviewed literature do not report any human genetic disorder that is caused by pathogenic variants in the SMOC3 (SPARC-related modular calcium-binding protein 3) gene. In contrast, its paralogue SMOC1 is well known to cause Ophthalmo-acromelic syndrome, originally described in several consanguineous families from Turkey and other populations. Likewise, recessive variants in SMOC2 have been implicated in a short stature/odontochondrodysplasia phenotype. No analogous, consistently replicated disease association has been published for SMOC3, and no Turkish pedigrees with a Mendelian disorder attributed to SMOC3 mutations have been described. Therefore, the statement that mutations in SMOC3 are reported to cause a genetic disorder in Turkish families is not supported by established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 4 : 1",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies show that roughly 80 % of people with obstructive sleep apnea are undiagnosed or unaware, while only about 20 % know they have the disorder; this yields an approximate unaware-to-aware ratio of 80 : 20, i.e., about 4 to 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Kinky hair (tightly coiled \"type-4\" texture) occurs most frequently in people of recent Sub-Saharan African ancestry. In the United States, the regional prevalence of this hair texture parallels the geographic distribution of the African-descent population. Census and demographic data show that the highest proportions of African Americans live in the Southeastern states (e.g., Mississippi, Louisiana, Georgia, South Carolina, Alabama). The Northeastern United States (e.g., New York, New Jersey, Massachusetts) has a moderate but distinctly lower percentage of African-descent residents than the Southeast, and other regions such as parts of the Midwest (Illinois, Michigan) also have sizable African-American communities. There is no epidemiologic study or demographic evidence indicating that kinky hair is more common in the Northeast than in other U.S. regions; if anything, the opposite trend is observed. Therefore, the statement that kinky hair is more prevalent in the Northeastern United States compared with other regions is not supported by established data."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Tietz syndrome (albinism–deafness syndrome) is produced by heterozygous mutations in the MITF gene (microphthalmia-associated transcription factor). The MITF gene is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 3 (cytogenetic band 3p13-p14). Therefore, the causative gene for Tietz syndrome resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Congenital contractural arachnodactyly (Beals syndrome) is produced by pathogenic variants in the FBN2 gene, which encodes fibrillin-2. FBN2 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 5 (5q23-q31). Mutations in this chromosomal locus can cause the skeletal features of CCA and, in some patients, progressive aortic root dilatation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia, a common form of disproportionate dwarfism that shows no clinically meaningful response to growth-hormone therapy, is caused by activating mutations in the FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3) gene. The mutant FGFR3 receptor constitutively suppresses chondrocyte proliferation and endochondral bone growth at the growth plate, so adding exogenous growth hormone cannot correct the underlying signaling defect, rendering the condition GH-insensitive."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Uncombable Hair Syndrome type 2 (UHS2) is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the TGM3 gene, which encodes epidermal transglutaminase 3. The TGM3 gene is located at cytogenetic band 20q11.23, so the chromosomal locus involved in UHS2 is chromosome 20."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTF1A",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous loss-of-function mutations in the PTF1A gene disrupt development of the hindbrain’s GABAergic lineage; as a result, the cerebellar anlage fails to form, producing near-complete cerebellar agenesis. Several patients with PTF1A mutations have been described whose brains developed almost entirely without a cerebellum, confirming that defects in PTF1A can cause this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Uromodulin kidney disease is caused by pathogenic variants in the UMOD gene, which encodes the uromodulin (Tamm-Horsfall) protein. The UMOD gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 at cytogenetic band 16p12.3; therefore, chromosome 16 is the chromosome that carries the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A2",
    "long_answer": "Hitchhiker’s thumbs are a classic clinical sign of diastrophic dysplasia, an autosomal-recessive skeletal dysplasia. Diastrophic dysplasia is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC26A2 gene (formerly called DTDST), which encodes a cartilage sulfate transporter essential for proper sulfation of proteoglycans in developing cartilage. Defective SLC26A2 disrupts cartilage matrix formation, producing the characteristic limb shortening, ‘cauliflower ear,’ and the hyperextended, abducted ‘hitchhiker’ thumbs seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease can be caused by loss-of-function mutations in the STAT1 gene. STAT1 mediates IFN-γ signalling through tyrosine phosphorylation and subsequent dimerization and nuclear translocation; defective phosphorylation blocks this pathway and predisposes to mycobacterial infection. The human STAT1 gene is located on chromosome 2 (band 2q32.2), making chromosome 2 the chromosome that carries this MSMD-associated, phosphorylation-dependent gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Erythropoietic protoporphyria results from partial deficiency of the enzyme ferrochelatase. The ferrochelatase enzyme is encoded by the FECH gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 18 (18q21). Mutations in this gene reduce ferrochelatase activity, leading to the accumulation of protoporphyrin and the clinical manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Athabaskan brainstem dysgenesis syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HOXA1 gene, which maps to cytogenetic band 7p15.2 on human chromosome 7. Therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IQCB1 (NPHP5)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IQCB1 gene (also called NPHP5) cause Senior–Løken syndrome, a ciliopathy that presents with retinal degeneration leading to progressive blindness and nephronophthisis that progresses to renal failure. The ciliary protein encoded by IQCB1 is required for photoreceptor outer-segment integrity and renal tubular function; loss-of-function variants therefore produce the combined phenotype of degenerative blindness and renal dysfunction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The gene encoding T-cell leukemia homeobox protein 3 (TLX3, also known as HOX11L2) is located on the long arm of human chromosome 5 (band 5q35). Chromosomal translocations involving 5q35—such as t(5;14)(q35;q32)—up-regulate TLX3 expression and are a recurrent molecular lesion in T-cell acute lymphoblastic leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-thalassemia results from deletions or other mutations in the α-globin genes HBA1 and HBA2, which are arranged in tandem within the α-globin gene cluster on chromosome 16 (band 16p13.3). Disruption of these chromosome-16 genes impairs α-globin chain production, leading to the clinical spectrum of alpha-thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Anti-GFAP IgG",
    "long_answer": "In 2016 a new, corticosteroid-responsive autoimmune meningo-encephalo-myelitis was delineated and termed ‘autoimmune GFAP astrocytopathy’. The disease is marked by serum and cerebrospinal-fluid immunoglobulin G that targets the intracellular filament protein glial fibrillary acidic protein (GFAP). Detection of anti-GFAP IgG reliably identifies this autoimmune form of myelitis and differentiates it from aquaporin-4 or MOG antibody-associated syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Yersinia pestis enhances its virulence by using its Pla protease to convert human plasminogen into plasmin, promoting tissue invasion and systemic spread. The human plasminogen protein is encoded by the PLG gene, which is located on chromosome 6 (band 6q26–q27). Hence, the relevant human gene resides on chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Mohr–Tranebjærg syndrome is an X-linked recessive neurodegenerative disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the TIMM8A (DDP1) gene. TIMM8A is situated at cytogenetic band Xq22.1 on the human X chromosome; therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Sarcosinemia is caused by inherited deficiency of the mitochondrial enzyme sarcosine dehydrogenase, encoded by the SARDH gene. Cytogenetic and molecular mapping studies place SARDH on the long arm of human chromosome 9 (region 9q33-q34). Hence, the gene responsible for sarcosinemia is located on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cetuximab",
    "long_answer": "The description refers to Ménétrier disease, a hypertrophic gastropathy marked by massive foveolar (mucus-secreting) cell hyperplasia that creates thick gastric folds and excessive mucus with protein-losing enteropathy. The disease is driven by overexpression of transforming growth factor-α, which activates the epidermal growth factor receptor (EGFR). Targeting this pathway with the monoclonal antibody cetuximab, an EGFR inhibitor, is recommended as first-line therapy because it directly counteracts the underlying EGFR-mediated mucous cell proliferation and can induce symptomatic and histologic remission."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thiamine (vitamin B1)",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of thiamine (vitamin B1) causes ‘wet’ beriberi, a clinical syndrome characterized by high-output heart failure, peripheral edema, and marked cardiac enlargement that can mimic or progress to a dilated cardiomyopathy. Restoration of thiamine reverses the myocardial dysfunction, confirming the causal link between vitamin B1 deficiency and the enlarged, failing heart."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa is caused by mutations in the COL7A1 gene, which encodes type VII collagen forming anchoring fibrils at the dermal-epidermal junction. The COL7A1 locus is situated on human chromosome 3 at band 3p21.3, so chromosome 3 carries the gene responsible for this skin disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Golgi apparatus",
    "long_answer": "Congenital disorders of glycosylation arise from defects in the enzymes that add or trim oligosaccharide chains on newly synthesized proteins. These post-translational glycosylation steps—including modification of N-linked glycans—take place in the Golgi apparatus, making this organelle central to the process that is impaired in such disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Pseudohypoaldosteronism type II D (PHAII-D) is caused by pathogenic variants in the CUL3 gene. The CUL3 locus is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q36). Therefore, chromosome 2 carries the gene responsible for the PHAII-D subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cenegermin (Oxervate)",
    "long_answer": "Cenegermin-bkbj, marketed as Oxervate, is the first FDA-approved treatment for neurotrophic keratitis. One of the common causes of neurotrophic keratitis is herpes zoster ophthalmicus, the ocular manifestation of varicella zoster virus infection. Cenegermin is a recombinant human nerve growth factor that restores corneal innervation and promotes epithelial healing, directly addressing the nerve damage produced by VZV-related disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Thanatophoric dysplasia, a lethal congenital skeletal dysplasia whose radiographs show a characteristic 'moth-eaten' or irregular, poorly ossified appearance of the fetal long bones, is produced by activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor 3 (FGFR3). This transmembrane tyrosine-kinase receptor normally restrains endochondral bone growth; its constitutive activation disturbs cartilage and bone development, giving rise to the distinctive radiologic pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary pulmonary alveolar proteinosis is most often caused by autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the CSF2RB gene, which encodes the β-chain of the GM-CSF receptor. CSF2RB is located on chromosome 22q12, so chromosome 22 carries the gene whose mutation produces the hereditary form of pulmonary alveolar proteinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "The congenital absence of fingernails seen in Cooks syndrome is most often caused by loss-of-function mutations in the RSPO4 (R-spondin-4) gene, which is located on chromosome 20p13. Disruption of RSPO4 interferes with Wnt–β-catenin signalling that is essential for nail matrix development, producing the anonychia characteristic of this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is the most common life-limiting autosomal recessive disorder in individuals of European descent. It is caused by mutations in the CFTR gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 7 (7q31.2)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SAG gene",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the SAG (S-antigen, visual arrestin) gene cause Oguchi disease, a form of congenital stationary night blindness. Arrestin terminates activated rhodopsin in rod photoreceptors; when SAG is mutated, rod phototransduction cannot shut off properly, leading to impaired dark adaptation and night-time visual impairment while cone function is largely spared."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Schindler disease is caused by mutations in the NAGA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-N-acetylgalactosaminidase. Cytogenetic mapping and sequence data place the NAGA gene on human chromosome 22 (band 22q13.2). Therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The gene TREM2, which encodes the Triggering Receptor Expressed on Myeloid cells-2 found on monocytes (and microglia), carries rare variants such as R47H that markedly increase the risk of late-onset Alzheimer’s disease. The TREM2 gene is located on human chromosome 6 (region 6p21.1)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile hyaline fibromatosis is caused by recessive mutations in the ANTXR2 (also called CMG2) gene. Molecular mapping shows that ANTXR2 is located at cytogenetic band 4q21 on human chromosome 4; therefore, chromosome 4 carries the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hermann Werner Siemens",
    "long_answer": "Hermann Werner Siemens (1891–1969) was a German dermatologist who described several distinct dermatoses—including the form of epidermolysis now called ichthyosis bullosa of Siemens—and in 1926 detailed the X-linked, anhidrotic type of ectodermal dysplasia. His work clarified the hereditary nature and clinical spectrum of hypohidrotic ectodermal dysplasia, earning the disorder its eponym Christ-Siemens-Touraine syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Maturity-onset diabetes of the young (MODY) results from heterozygous mutations in one of several genes encoding pancreatic β-cell transcription factors or enzymes (e.g., HNF1A, GCK). These mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, so a single mutated allele inherited from an affected parent is sufficient to cause the disease and it typically appears in successive generations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbonic acid",
    "long_answer": "Oxidative metabolism of fats and carbohydrates generates large amounts of carbon dioxide (CO₂). When CO₂ dissolves in water within body fluids it forms carbonic acid (H₂CO₃), the principal volatile acid in the body. Because carbonic acid is in equilibrium with CO₂, it is eliminated by ventilation; failure to expel it causes CO₂ retention and a rise in H₂CO₃ concentration, producing respiratory acidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Inherited Factor X deficiency is caused by mutations in the F10 gene, which encodes coagulation factor X. The F10 gene is mapped to chromosome 13 at band 13q34, so the genetic locus responsible for the disorder resides on chromosome 13."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sinus bradycardia",
    "long_answer": "Severe caloric and protein restriction in anorexia nervosa increases vagal tone and weakens myocardial function, producing marked sinus bradycardia. The slow heart rate lowers cardiac output and cerebral perfusion, so patients may experience dizziness or frank syncope as a consequence of this cardiac complication."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NSD1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations or microdeletions of the NSD1 gene on chromosome 5q35 cause Sotos syndrome (cerebral gigantism). This genetic disorder is characterized by marked prenatal and postnatal overgrowth, macrocephaly, and developmental delay/intellectual disability, establishing NSD1 mutation as the molecular basis for excessive physical growth with cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL11A2",
    "long_answer": "Recessive mutations in the COL11A2 gene disrupt the α-2 chain of type XI collagen, leading to otospondylomegaepiphyseal dysplasia (OSMED). This disorder is characterized by severe sensorineural hearing loss together with skeletal abnormalities such as enlarged epiphyses and spinal involvement, establishing COL11A2 mutations as the causative factor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "The congenital motor neuron disorder appearing at birth in children—classically exemplified by Werdnig-Hoffmann disease (spinal muscular atrophy type I)—results from biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SMN1 gene. Because two defective alleles are required, the condition follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "The FGFR2 gene encodes fibroblast growth factor receptor 2, a receptor-tyrosine kinase that regulates osteoblast proliferation and differentiation during craniofacial morphogenesis. Pathogenic variants in FGFR2 disrupt signaling at the cranial sutures, leading to premature suture fusion and craniosynostosis syndromes such as Apert, Crouzon, and Pfeiffer, highlighting its central role in craniofacial development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Ataxia–telangiectasia results from loss-of-function mutations in the ATM gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 11q22.3 on human chromosome 11; therefore, chromosome 11 carries the gene responsible for the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Varicella cerebral vasculopathy",
    "long_answer": "Weeks to months after chickenpox, varicella-zoster virus can infect and inflame the walls of large intracranial arteries. This post-varicella arteriopathy (varicella cerebral vasculopathy) narrows or occludes the vessels, predisposing children to ischemic stroke. It is one of the best-documented and relatively common late neurological complications of varicella infection."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Achondroplasia—the type of dwarfism portrayed in Willow—is caused by a gain-of-function mutation in the FGFR3 gene (fibroblast growth factor receptor 3). The activating FGFR3 mutation inhibits chondrocyte proliferation in growth plates, producing the characteristic short-limb dwarfism seen in the character played by Warwick Davis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyaline cartilage",
    "long_answer": "COL9A1, COL9A2, and COL9A3 encode the α-chains of type IX collagen, a collagen that binds to and stabilizes type II collagen fibrils in hyaline cartilage (including articular and growth-plate cartilage). Mutations in these genes therefore disrupt the structure and function of hyaline cartilage, leading to the skeletal disorder multiple epiphyseal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The gene that encodes microsomal triglyceride transfer protein (MTTP)—mutations of which produce a form of familial hypobetalipoproteinemia/abetalipoproteinemia—is located on human chromosome 4 (cytogenetic region 4q24-q25). Pathogenic variants in this chromosome-4 MTTP locus impair lipid transfer in the endoplasmic reticulum, leading to markedly reduced plasma apolipoprotein-B–containing lipoproteins and the hypobetalipoproteinemia phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ERCC2 (XPD)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ERCC2 gene, which encodes the XPD helicase component of the TFIIH complex, cripple nucleotide-excision repair. This defect produces the photosensitive skin disorder xeroderma pigmentosum and, in many patients, a combined phenotype marked by progressive neurodegeneration and intellectual disability (e.g., the XP/TTD or XP/CS overlap syndromes). Hence an ERCC2/XPD mutation is the recognized genetic cause of the syndrome that couples xeroderma pigmentosum with worsening cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absence vs underdevelopment",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary agenesis is the complete absence of a lung, including its bronchus and vasculature, whereas pulmonary hypoplasia refers to a lung that is present but markedly underdeveloped, containing reduced airways, parenchyma, and vessels."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Mitochondrial Complex I deficiency is most commonly linked to pathogenic variants in the nuclear-encoded NDUFV1 gene, which codes for the 51-kDa flavoprotein subunit of Complex I. The NDUFV1 gene is located on human chromosome 11 at cytogenetic band 11q13. Mutations in this locus impair NADH-ubiquinone oxidoreductase activity, leading to the clinical spectrum of Complex I deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IRF6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the IRF6 gene (Interferon Regulatory Factor 6) cause Popliteal Pterygium Syndrome, characterized by popliteal webbing, and the same gene is the principal genetic cause of Van der Woude syndrome. Thus, IRF6 is the shared gene whose mutation links both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Fumarase deficiency is caused by mutations in the FH gene (fumarate hydratase). Cytogenetic mapping shows that FH lies at 1q42.1 on human chromosome 1, making chromosome 1 the locus for the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MVK and MVD",
    "long_answer": "Porokeratosis that manifests as numerous small papular lesions on the palms and/or soles—punctate palmoplantar porokeratosis—has been linked to heterozygous mutations in two mevalonate-pathway genes: MVK (mevalonate kinase) and MVD (mevalonate diphosphate decarboxylase). Pathogenic variants in either gene disrupt cholesterol/isoprenoid biosynthesis, producing the characteristic papular keratotic lesions of this porokeratosis subtype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1",
    "long_answer": "Crigler–Najjar syndrome arises from inherited deficiency or complete absence of the hepatic enzyme UDP-glucuronosyltransferase 1A1 (UGT1A1), which normally conjugates bilirubin with glucuronic acid. Without functional UGT1A1, bilirubin cannot be converted to its water-soluble (conjugated) form, so unconjugated bilirubin accumulates to very high levels, producing the characteristic severe unconjugated hyperbilirubinemia of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hashimoto’s thyroiditis",
    "long_answer": "In the United States the most frequent reason for an enlarged (goitrous) thyroid is Hashimoto’s thyroiditis, also called chronic autoimmune/lymphocytic thyroiditis. Ascher’s syndrome often includes a nontoxic, euthyroid goiter, and in the U.S. this enlargement is most commonly attributable to Hashimoto’s autoimmune destruction and accompanying lymphoid infiltration of the gland."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of leukocyte adhesion deficiency (LAD-I) is caused by mutations in the ITGB2 gene, which encodes the β2-integrin subunit CD18 that is essential for leukocyte adhesion and migration. The ITGB2 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 21 (cytogenetic band 21q22.3). Hence, chromosome 21 does indeed contain the gene whose defects produce the classic, common form of leukocyte adhesion deficiency."
}

{
        "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
        "long_answer": "Beta-thalassemia results from mutations in the HBB gene, which encodes the β-globin subunit of hemoglobin. The entire β-globin gene cluster, including HBB, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (region 11p15.4). Therefore, chromosome 11 is the chromosome that carries the gene whose defects cause beta thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "The human SRP54 gene encodes the 54-kDa subunit of the signal recognition particle, a key protein that directs nascent polypeptides to the endoplasmic reticulum during co-translational targeting. Cytogenetic mapping places SRP54 on chromosome 14 (band 14q13), so chromosome 14 is the chromosome that carries the gene for this ER-targeting protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor-3 (FGFR3) gene cause thanatophoric dysplasia, a lethal skeletal dysplasia. Affected fetuses have markedly shortened limbs, narrow thorax and pulmonary hypoplasia, so the condition frequently results in stillbirth or death in the early neonatal period."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Worth syndrome (also known as Worth-type autosomal-dominant endosteal hyperostosis) has been linked to activating (gain-of-function) mutations in the LRP5 gene.  The LRP5 gene is located on chromosome 11 (cytogenetic location 11q13.4), not on chromosome 21.  No gene on chromosome 21 has been shown to cause Worth syndrome according to the current genetic and clinical literature.  Therefore the statement that chromosome 21 contains the gene that causes Worth syndrome is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Malouf syndrome (dilated cardiomyopathy with hypergonadotropic hypogonadism) is produced by pathogenic variants in the LMNA gene, which encodes lamin A/C. The LMNA gene is located on human chromosome 1 (band 1q22). Because lamin defects impair connective-tissue integrity, affected individuals can show joint laxity and skin hyperextensibility resembling Ehlers-Danlos syndrome. Hence, the gene responsible for Malouf syndrome resides on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Berlin’s edema (commotio retinae) is characterized by transient whitening of the outer retina after blunt ocular trauma. Fluorescein angiography in this condition typically shows normal retinal vascular filling and, at most, a blocked or mottled background choroidal fluorescence caused by the opaque retina. This fluorescence pattern is a masking or blocking phenomenon rather than a sign of dye leakage or specific tissue staining. Consequently, the presence or absence of choroidal fluorescence on fluorescein angiography does not correlate with the degree of photoreceptor or other retinal cell damage, nor does it predict functional visual outcome. Modern imaging (e.g., optical coherence tomography) is required to evaluate the true extent of outer retinal disruption. Therefore, choroidal fluorescence in Berlin’s edema is not an indicator of the extent of retinal cell damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LRP5",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LRP5 gene cause osteoporosis-pseudoglioma syndrome. This single-gene disorder is characterized by very low bone density (severe juvenile osteoporosis) and congenital or early-onset retinal dysplasia leading to blindness, showing that LRP5 is crucial for both skeletal mass acquisition and normal retinal vascular development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "In northern European populations the prevalent form of limb-girdle muscular dystrophy (LGMD2I) is produced by a founder mutation (c.826C>A) in the FKRP gene. FKRP is mapped to cytogenetic band 19q13.3, so chromosome 19 is the chromosome that contains the gene most commonly mutated in this region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO5A",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYO5A gene (encoding the motor protein myosin-Va) cause Griscelli syndrome type 1 / Elejalde neuroectodermal melanolysosomal disease. This disorder is characterized by primary central-nervous-system dysfunction together with only moderate skin pigment dilution and scalp hair that appears light-colored with a distinctive metallic, silvery sheen. Hence, MYO5A is the gene associated with this CNS disease phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ewing sarcoma family",
    "long_answer": "Ewing's sarcoma and peripheral primitive neuroectodermal tumor are now regarded as morphologic variants within the Ewing sarcoma family of tumors. They share the characteristic EWSR1-FLI1 (t(11;22)(q24;q12)) and related gene fusions, identical immunohistochemical profiles, and common cell of origin, differing only in the extent of neural differentiation. Thus, they represent the same biological entity rather than distinct diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "syndrome",
    "long_answer": "In medical genetics, a ʻsyndromeʼ refers to a distinct, recurring combination of characteristic signs and symptoms. Microphthalmia that presents together with additional, consistent abnormalities is therefore described as part of a syndromic picture—i.e., it occurs within a defined syndrome such as CHARGE or Lenz microphthalmia syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ADAMTS13",
    "long_answer": "In thrombotic thrombocytopenic purpura (TTP), pathogenic autoantibodies inhibit the plasma metalloprotease ADAMTS13 (von Willebrand factor-cleaving protease). Loss of ADAMTS13 activity prevents normal cleavage of ultra-large von Willebrand factor multimers, causing widespread platelet-rich microthrombi. This mechanism underlies the blood disorder and can disseminate, affecting multiple organs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COMP gene",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoachondroplasia is an autosomal-dominant osteochondrodysplasia produced by pathogenic variants in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene on chromosome 19p13.1. Mutant COMP misfolds and accumulates in the rough endoplasmic reticulum of growth-plate chondrocytes, triggering cellular stress and premature chondrocyte death, which impairs endochondral bone growth and leads to the characteristic short-limb dwarfism of pseudoachondroplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome described is Aagenaes syndrome (lymphoedema-cholestasis syndrome), characterized by congenital hypoplasia of lymphatic vessels with recurrent neonatal/infantile cholestasis. Linkage studies have mapped the responsible autosomal-recessive locus (LCS1) to the long arm of human chromosome 15 (15q), establishing chromosome 15 as the location of the gene associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CWC27",
    "long_answer": "Fine–Lubinsky syndrome has been shown to result from biallelic loss-of-function variants in CWC27, the gene encoding a spliceosome-associated protein. Individuals with pathogenic CWC27 variants present with the cardinal features of the syndrome – ocular anomalies (microphthalmia, coloboma, high myopia or retinal dystrophy), sensorineural hearing impairment, marked speech and developmental delay, and intellectual disability – establishing CWC27 as the causative gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of Weill–Marchesani syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants in the FBN1 gene (fibrillin-1). FBN1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 15 (15q21.1). Mutations in this gene disrupt the microfibrillar architecture of connective tissue, giving rise to the characteristic short stature, brachydactyly and microspherophakia seen in Weill–Marchesani syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous or compound-heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the RSPO2 gene (R-spondin-2), located at cytogenetic band 8q23.1 on human chromosome 8, are known to cause tetra-amelia syndrome that is frequently accompanied by complete lung (pulmonary) agenesis. RSPO2 is a key modulator of Wnt/β-catenin signalling during limb and lung bud development; its disruption abolishes limb formation and arrests early lung morphogenesis, producing the characteristic tetra-amelia with lung agenesis phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Molecular studies have shown that micro-deletions or pathogenic variants involving the HNF1B gene at cytogenetic band 17q12 are present in a proportion of patients with the classical Eagle-Barrett (Prune belly) syndrome. Because HNF1B is located on human chromosome 17, this chromosome is considered the chromosomal site of the gene associated with Prune belly syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WRN gene",
    "long_answer": "Werner syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WRN gene. WRN encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase/exonuclease that participates in DNA replication, recombination, and repair, and it physically interacts with RNA polymerase-associated factors during transcription-coupled DNA repair. Therefore, mutation of the WRN gene links Werner syndrome to cellular processes that involve RNA polymerase."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MECP2 gene cause Rett syndrome, a well-characterized neurodevelopmental disorder whose core clinical features include global developmental delay and moderate-to-severe intellectual disability. MECP2 encodes methyl-CpG-binding protein 2, a key regulator of gene expression in neurons; loss-of-function variants disrupt neuronal maturation and synaptic function, leading to the cognitive and developmental deficits observed in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Curry–Jones syndrome is produced by a recurrent, post-zygotic activating mutation in the SMO gene (Smoothened, frizzled class receptor). The SMO locus is situated at 7q32.1 on human chromosome 7; hence the causative gene for Curry–Jones syndrome is located on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Joubert syndrome is genetically heterogeneous, and several causative genes have been identified on different chromosomes. Among them, the CEP290 gene (responsible for Joubert syndrome type 5) is located on chromosome 12q21.3, and the TCTN2 gene (another Joubert-associated gene) is located on chromosome 12q24.31. Therefore, at least two well-established Joubert-syndrome genes reside on chromosome 12, making the statement correct."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Howel–Evans syndrome (tylosis with oesophageal cancer) is caused by mutations in the RHBDF2 gene (encoding iRhom2). iRhom2 is known to bind the pro-apoptotic protein Bcl-2-interacting killer (BIK). The RHBDF2 gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 17 (17q25.1), so chromosome 17 carries the gene that both interacts with BIK and is implicated in Howel–Evans syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Tangier disease results from loss-of-function mutations in the ABCA1 gene (ATP-binding cassette transporter A1). The ABCA1 locus is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 9 (9q31), so chromosome 9 is the chromosomal location responsible for the HDL deficiency characteristic of Tangier disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The form of thalassemia most widespread in Pakistan is β-thalassemia. This disease results from mutations in the β-globin (HBB) gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p15.5). Therefore, chromosome 11 carries the gene whose defects lead to the prevalent type of thalassemia in that population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3",
    "long_answer": "Activating mutations in the FGFR3 (fibroblast growth factor receptor-3) gene are the well-established cause of achondroplasia, a classic skeletal dysplasia. The mutant FGFR3 protein constitutively inhibits chondrocyte proliferation at the growth plate, leading to shortened long bones and characteristic dwarfism. Achondroplasia is therefore a skeletal disorder squarely within the domain of medical genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is an autosomal-recessive disorder produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LYST (lysosomal trafficking regulator) gene. The LYST gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 1 at band 1q42.1-q42.2; therefore, chromosome 1 carries the genetic defect responsible for the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "2p13",
    "long_answer": "Miyoshi myopathy (also called Miyoshi distal myopathy) is caused by recessive mutations in the DYSF gene that encodes the membrane-repair protein dysferlin. The DYSF gene is mapped to the short arm of chromosome 2 at band p13 (chromosomal locus 2p13), and defects at this locus lead to the characteristic distal muscle weakness of Miyoshi myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DLL3",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the DLL3 gene (encoding the Delta-like 3 ligand of the Notch pathway) cause spondylocostal dysostosis type 1, a congenital disorder of vertebral segmentation. The disturbed Notch signalling impairs normal formation of somites, producing malformed and fused vertebrae and ribs that manifest clinically as progressive, often severe spinal curvature (scoliosis/kyphoscoliosis)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLB1",
    "long_answer": "Morquio syndrome type IVB (Morquio B) is produced by pathogenic variants in the GLB1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-galactosidase. Loss of enzyme activity causes keratan-sulfate storage and skeletal dysplasia, and affected patients frequently develop pronounced spinal deformities such as scoliosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UMP synthase",
    "long_answer": "Excessive urinary excretion of orotic acid (orotic aciduria) results from an inherited deficiency of uridine-5′-monophosphate (UMP) synthase, the bifunctional enzyme that normally converts orotate to UMP via its orotate phosphoribosyltransferase and orotidine-5′-phosphate decarboxylase activities. When UMP synthase is absent or defective, orotate accumulates and is spilled into the urine, producing the characteristic orotic aciduria phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The SFTPB gene on human chromosome 2 (locus 2p11.2) encodes surfactant protein B, a hydrophobic component of pulmonary surfactant that is essential for lowering surface tension at the air–liquid interface in the alveoli, thereby preventing alveolar collapse and facilitating normal lung function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glycogen branching enzyme",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the glycogen branching enzyme (α-1,4-→-α-1,6-glucan trans-glycosylase) causes glycogen-storage disease type IV (Andersen disease). In this disorder an abnormally long, poorly branched glycogen—often termed “amylopectin-like” polymer—accumulates in skeletal and cardiac muscle as well as liver. Because these insoluble polyglucosan bodies adopt an amyloid-like, β-pleated sheet conformation, the disease is classically linked to the structure of beta sheets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Fazio–Londe disease is caused by recessive mutations in the riboflavin transporter gene SLC52A3 (formerly C20orf54). This gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 20 (20p13), making chromosome 20 the chromosomal location associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR1",
    "long_answer": "Encephalocraniocutaneous lipomatosis (Haberland syndrome) presents with porencephalic brain cysts together with characteristic facial skin tags and other ectomesodermal anomalies. Molecular studies have shown that this disorder is caused by activating, somatic (mosaic) mutations in the fibroblast growth-factor receptor-1 gene (FGFR1), making FGFR1 mutation the established genetic association with this clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the connective-tissue protein fibrillin-1, cause Marfan syndrome. This disorder often produces progressive scoliosis and other spine deformities, so affected individuals are routinely followed with periodic examinations and imaging to detect and manage spinal irregularities."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occupational therapy",
    "long_answer": "In autism spectrum disorder—a neurological condition in which atypical sodium-channel function can lead to reduced sensitivity to the local anaesthetic lidocaine—patients frequently experience debilitating sensory overload (hyper-reactivity to sound, light, touch, etc.). Structured occupational therapy that incorporates sensory-integration techniques is an evidence-based intervention used to modulate sensory input, teach coping strategies, and thereby lessen sensory overload in these individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Familial Amyloidosis, Finnish Type is produced by missense mutations in the GSN gene that encodes the actin-modifying protein gelsolin. The GSN gene is mapped to chromosome 9q34, so the disease-causing mutation is associated with chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Fragile X syndrome is caused by expansion of CGG repeats in the FMR1 gene, which is situated at band Xq27.3 of the X chromosome; therefore, the gene responsible for the disorder resides on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Eidolon helvum",
    "long_answer": "Field studies in West Africa have shown that the straw-coloured fruit bat, Eidolon helvum, frequently carries IgG antibodies that react with Zaire ebolavirus antigens, indicating previous asymptomatic infection and implicating this species as a natural host of the virus that causes Ebola virus disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Prosopagnosia ("face blindness") is primarily a perceptual-recognition disorder: affected individuals have difficulty perceiving and identifying faces because cortical regions that analyse facial structure (e.g., the fusiform face area and occipital face area) are damaged or function abnormally. Once a face is correctly perceived and encoded by another route (for example, by verbal description), episodic memory for that information is generally normal.

Prosopamnesia (sometimes misspelled “prosoamnesia”) is different: perceptual analysis of faces is intact, but the specific ability to encode newly seen faces into long-term memory is impaired. Patients can perceive that a stimulus is a face and can describe its features, yet they fail to form or store a durable memory of that face.

Thus, prosopagnosia does not disrupt memory encoding in the same way; its core deficit lies earlier (visual analysis/recognition), whereas prosopamnesia’s deficit lies at the stage of encoding faces into memory. Therefore, the two conditions affect different cognitive processes, and the statement that they affect memory encoding in the same way is not supported by scientific evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous pathogenic variants in the CHD7 gene cause CHARGE syndrome. This syndrome typically presents with severe sensorineural hearing loss from inner-ear malformations, ocular colobomas leading to vision impairment, and brain anomalies such as hypoplasia or agenesis of the corpus callosum. Thus, mutation of CHD7 is the well-established genetic cause of the disorder displaying these combined features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NBEAL2",
    "long_answer": "Gray platelet syndrome is a rare inherited disorder marked by a marked reduction or absence of α-granules in blood platelets. The disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NBEAL2 gene, which encodes Neurobeachin-like protein 2, a protein essential for the biogenesis and retention of platelet α-granules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX1",
    "long_answer": "Oto-faciocervical syndrome type 2 (OFCS2) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the PAX1 gene, which is located on chromosome 20p11.2. Disruption of this paired-box transcription factor impairs branchial arch and skeletal development, leading to the characteristic ear, facial and cervical anomalies seen in this form of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GABA-A receptor",
    "long_answer": "Electroconvulsive therapy (ECT) is a treatment for major depressive disorder that intentionally induces therapeutic seizures. During ECT, the patient is anesthetized with an ultra-short-acting barbiturate such as methohexital, which exerts its effect by potentiating the GABA-A receptor—enhancing inhibitory chloride influx to produce rapid unconsciousness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the membrane-associated guanylate kinase (MAGUK) scaffold genes DLG3 (SAP102) and CASK cause X-linked intellectual disability. Both of these MAGUK genes are located on the X chromosome (DLG3 at Xq13.1 and CASK at Xp11.4), making the X chromosome the genomic site implicated in MAGUK-related intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "Dent’s disease is an X-linked recessive renal tubular disorder most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CLCN5 gene (encoding the ClC-5 chloride channel). CLCN5 is located at Xp11.22 on the human X chromosome; therefore the gene responsible for Dent’s disease resides on chromosome X."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9, autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Sarcosinemia results from mutations in the SARDH gene (sarcosine dehydrogenase) located on human chromosome 9q34. Loss-of-function variants in both alleles of SARDH are required to produce the enzyme deficiency, so the disorder follows an autosomal recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NHS",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the X-linked NHS gene (Nance-Horan syndrome gene) cause Nance-Horan syndrome, an ocular–dental disorder characterized by dense bilateral congenital cataracts and microcornea. These ocular anomalies appear at birth, markedly reduce visual acuity, and are the hallmark of the syndrome, firmly linking NHS mutations to congenital cataract–microcornea–associated visual impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chronic anterior uveitis",
    "long_answer": "In juvenile idiopathic arthritis, a positive ANA test identifies children—especially young girls with oligoarticular onset—who are at markedly increased risk for developing chronic, asymptomatic anterior uveitis. ANA positivity therefore guides ophthalmologic screening intervals rather than reflecting joint disease severity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hereditary Hearing Loss Homepage",
    "long_answer": "The Hereditary Hearing Loss Homepage is the curated database that assigns and displays the unique DFN (DFNA/DFNB/DFNX) numbers used worldwide to identify each gene or chromosomal locus known to cause nonsyndromic deafness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "The FERMT1 gene (also called KIND1, encoding kindlin-1) maps to cytogenetic band 20p12.3 on chromosome 20. Pathogenic variants in this gene cause the autosomal-recessive skin disorder Kindler syndrome, confirming its chromosomal location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mouse Genome Informatics",
    "long_answer": "Mouse Genome Informatics (MGI) is the primary database for curated information on mouse genes, alleles, phenotypes and their associations with human diseases. It includes entries for the murine Suox gene and disease models annotated to sulfite oxidase deficiency, making it the appropriate resource for this query."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "OMIM entry 300600 does not correspond to a xeroderma-pigmentosum–type disorder or any other syndrome whose hallmark is a defect in repairing ultraviolet-induced DNA lesions. Instead, 300600 refers to an X-linked disorder whose molecular defect is in a pathway unrelated to the nucleotide-excision repair (NER) machinery that removes UV-generated photoproducts. Conditions that are classically UV-repair–deficient, such as xeroderma pigmentosum or the photosensitive forms of trichothiodystrophy and Cockayne syndrome, are catalogued under different OMIM numbers in the 27-8-xxx series. Consequently, OMIM 300600 is not about defective DNA repair of UV damage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC22A5 gene, which encodes the OCTN2 carnitine transporter. Because disease manifests only when both alleles are defective, the disorder follows an autosomal-recessive pattern of inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aldosterone",
    "long_answer": "Aldosterone, the mineralocorticoid produced by the adrenal cortex, is released when angiotensin II rises. Angiotensin II both stimulates aldosterone secretion and promotes renal potassium (K⁺) excretion; aldosterone then further enhances kaliuresis in the distal nephron. Thus the hormone whose secretion is stimulated by a compound that also causes potassium excretion is aldosterone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Lafora disease is a progressive myoclonic epilepsy caused by pathogenic variants in two known genes: EPM2A, which encodes the dual-specificity phosphatase laforin, and NHLRC1 (also called EPM2B), which encodes the E3-ubiquitin ligase malin. Mutations in EPM2A are a well-established cause of the disease. In contrast, the SHH (Sonic Hedgehog) gene is not implicated in Lafora disease; it is a developmental morphogen best known for its roles in embryogenesis and patterning of the central nervous system. There is no evidence in the medical or genetic literature linking SHH mutations or dysregulation to Lafora disease. Therefore, while EPM2A is associated with Lafora disease, SHH is not, making the statement incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Glutathione-synthetase deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the GSS gene, which is located on chromosome 20q11.23. Chromosome 20 is one of the 22 non-sex chromosomes, making the disorder autosomal (and inherited in an autosomal-recessive manner)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIAMS",
    "long_answer": "The National Institute of Arthritis and Musculoskeletal and Skin Diseases (NIAMS), one of the National Institutes of Health, funds and conducts research on bone and skeletal development. Its mission explicitly includes studying congenital skeletal disorders such as brachydactyly to identify the genes and molecular pathways that cause abnormal digit formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The three fibrinogen genes—FGA (alpha chain), FGB (beta chain) and FGG (gamma chain)—are clustered on human chromosome 4 (region 4q31-q32). These genes encode the polypeptide chains that assemble into fibrinogen, the precursor of the fibrin clot. Mutations or deletions in any of these chromosome-4 genes can lead to bleeding disorders such as congenital hypofibrinogenemia, and altered fibrinogen levels contribute to coagulopathies like disseminated intravascular coagulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NR1H3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "A rare, autosomal-dominant form of multiple sclerosis characterized by tumefactive lesions, including Baló’s concentric sclerosis, has been linked to a missense mutation (p.Arg415Gln) in the NR1H3 gene, which encodes the nuclear receptor LXR-α. This NR1H3 mutation segregates with the disease in affected families, distinguishing this MS subtype from the common polygenic forms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The ERLIN2 gene, whose mutations cause the autosomal-recessive form of hereditary spastic paraplegia designated SPG18, is located on human chromosome 8 (cytogenetic band 8p11.23)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type II is caused by pathogenic variants in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene that encodes the protein merlin (schwannomin). The NF2 gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 22 (cytogenetic band 22q12.2), not on chromosome 21. Therefore, the statement that the gene responsible for tumor formation in neurofibromatosis type II is found on chromosome 21 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The MYO7A gene, whose mutations are a well-established cause of Usher syndrome type I (specifically the USH1B subtype), is located on human chromosome 11 (band 11q13.5)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the GJA1 gene, which encodes the gap-junction protein Connexin-43 and maps to chromosome 6q22-q24, are responsible for the syndrome classically known as oculo-dento-digital dysplasia. This disorder presents with characteristic facial dysmorphisms together with digital and skeletal (osseous) anomalies and can include cardiac conduction defects, fitting the description of a syndrome with cardiac, digital and osseous anomalies plus facial dysmorphisms located on the long arm of chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Endomyocardial fibrosis",
    "long_answer": "Endomyocardial fibrosis is regarded as the most frequent worldwide cause of restrictive cardiomyopathy. The dense fibrous tissue that lines the ventricular endocardium can extend onto the papillary muscles and valve leaflets, particularly on the right side of the heart, producing tricuspid (and sometimes pulmonary) valve regurgitation or stenosis. Thus it both explains the restrictive ventricular physiology and the characteristic right-sided cardiac valve disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Parkes Weber syndrome is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the RASA1 gene, which underlies the capillary malformations called port-wine stains in the disorder. The RASA1 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 5q13.3, so the relevant gene resides on human chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MBTPS2",
    "long_answer": "The triad of ichthyosis follicularis, congenital alopecia and (often) photophobia defines IFAP syndrome, an X-linked disorder. Multiple studies have shown that missense or splice-site mutations in the MBTPS2 gene, which encodes the membrane-bound transcription factor peptidase, site 2, are responsible for this syndrome. Loss of MBTPS2 protease activity disrupts sterol-regulated transcriptional pathways in skin and hair follicles, producing the combined phenotype of ichthyosis follicularis and alopecia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turrón",
    "long_answer": "Turrón is a traditional Spanish-Mediterranean nougat confection made from honey, sugar, egg whites and large amounts of roasted tree nuts—usually almonds or hazelnuts. Almonds and other tree nuts are among the eight major food allergens and can elicit IgE-mediated hypersensitivity reactions, including life-threatening anaphylaxis, in susceptible individuals. Because turrón routinely contains these nuts, it poses a recognized allergenic risk." 
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental disomy",
    "long_answer": "Uniparental disomy (UPD) occurs when the two homologous copies of a chromosome are inherited from a single parent instead of one copy from each parent. Because both alleles derive from the same parent, any recessive mutation present on that chromosome can become homozygous, leading to recessive genetic conditions, and parent-of-origin (imprinting) effects may also arise."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Implantable cardioverter-defibrillator",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary condition is Brugada syndrome, an autosomal-dominant cardiac channelopathy that manifests on ECG as right (occasionally left) bundle branch block with ST-segment elevation in the right pre-cordial leads and predisposes to lethal ventricular arrhythmias. No medication has been shown to reliably prevent sudden cardiac death in these patients; implantation of an implantable cardioverter-defibrillator (ICD) is the only therapy that has conclusively been proven to reduce mortality."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Measles virus",
    "long_answer": "The measles (rubeola) virus can persist in the central nervous system and years later cause sub-acute sclerosing panencephalitis, a relentlessly progressive inflammatory degeneration of the brain. During the acute infection it can also infect the myocardium, producing viral myocarditis that may progress to dilated cardiomyopathy and heart failure. Thus the same virus is linked both to progressive brain inflammation and to potentially fatal cardiac failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Nucleus",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia results from mutations in the Fanconi anemia (FA) gene network, whose proteins assemble in the nucleus to repair DNA inter-strand cross-links via the FA–BRCA pathway. The defective step occurs within the nuclear DNA repair machinery, so the organelle central to this impaired process is the nucleus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Kaufman oculocerebrofacial syndrome results from autosomal-recessive loss-of-function mutations in the UBE3B gene (ubiquitin-protein ligase E3B). The UBE3B locus is mapped to cytogenetic band 12q24 on human chromosome 12; therefore, chromosome 12 contains the gene responsible for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Schmitt-Gillenwater-Kelly (radial hypoplasia–hypospadias) syndrome has been shown to segregate in an X-linked pattern, and linkage studies have mapped the responsible locus to the long arm of the X chromosome (Xq). Therefore, the gene that potentially causes the radial hypoplasia characteristic of this syndrome is located on the human X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hypothalamus",
    "long_answer": "Diencephalic syndrome is classically produced by low-grade gliomas that arise in the hypothalamic/optic-chiasmatic region of the brain. Tumors centered in the hypothalamus disrupt its regulatory functions, leading to the characteristic failure to thrive, hyperactivity, and emaciation seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa",
    "long_answer": "Pseudomonas aeruginosa is a Gram-negative opportunistic bacterium that commonly causes superficial skin infections such as ‘hot-tub’ folliculitis and ecthyma gangrenosum. Fluoroquinolones, particularly ciprofloxacin, have potent anti-pseudomonal activity and are frequently prescribed when systemic therapy is required, making P. aeruginosa the bacterium whose skin infections are effectively treated with ciprofloxacin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Lemli–Opitz syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the DHCR7 gene, which encodes 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase. DHCR7 is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 11 (region 11q13.4). Mutations in this chromosome-11 gene disrupt cholesterol biosynthesis, producing the clinical features of the syndrome, including congenital ptosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ASS1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ASS1 gene (argininosuccinate synthetase 1) block the conversion of citrulline to argininosuccinate in the urea cycle, producing classic citrullinemia type I. This enzymatic defect causes citrulline to accumulate in the blood, leading to the characteristic elevation seen in this urea-cycle disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The inherited Roberts/SC-phocomelia syndrome, which presents with phocomelia together with visceral anomalies including renal dysplasia, is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the ESCO2 gene located on chromosome 8p21. Therefore, the genetic form of the syndrome is linked to chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCAF17",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic mutations in the DCAF17 gene (formerly C2orf37) cause Woodhouse-Sakati syndrome. This autosomal-recessive disorder is characterized by progressive childhood-onset alopecia and distinct MRI evidence of iron deposition in the basal ganglia, linking the DCAF17 mutation to both brain iron accumulation and early hair thinning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Prolonged thrombin time is the key screening test for fibrinogen disorders such as congenital afibrinogenemia or dysfibrinogenemia. The fibrinogen genes (FGA, FGB, and FGG) that cause these bleeding disorders are clustered together on the long arm of human chromosome 4 (4q). Hence, the gene associated with the thrombin-time bleeding disorder resides on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meat-and-bone meal",
    "long_answer": "Rendered animal by-product known as meat-and-bone meal (MBM) is commonly added to cattle rations and is also sold as an organic phosphate-rich fertilizer for crops and gardens. If the MBM is prepared from carcasses of animals carrying the misfolded prion protein (PrP^Sc), those prions can survive rendering, contaminate the meal, and, when ingested by cattle, initiate Bovine Spongiform Encephalopathy (BSE). Thus MBM is a recognized potential source of prion transmission to cattle."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Lujan–Fryns syndrome is an X-linked intellectual-developmental disorder caused by pathogenic variants in the MED12 gene. MED12 is located at cytogenetic band Xq13.3 on the human X chromosome, establishing that the causative gene resides on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "Over 90 % of congenital adrenal hyperplasia (CAH) cases are due to 21-hydroxylase deficiency. The enzyme 21-hydroxylase is encoded by the CYP21A2 gene, and pathogenic variants in this gene are commonly inherited in an autosomal-recessive, compound heterozygous manner—each allele carrying a different CYP21A2 mutation that together cause the classic or non-classic CAH phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCD1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ABCD1 gene on the X chromosome (locus Xq28) cause X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy. ABCD1 encodes the adrenoleukodystrophy protein (ALDP), an ATP-binding cassette peroxisomal membrane transporter responsible for importing very-long-chain fatty acids into peroxisomes. Defective ALDP impairs peroxisomal β-oxidation, leading to the characteristic neurodegenerative and adrenal manifestations of the X-linked disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The enzyme-coding gene responsible for glucose-6-phosphate dehydrogenase (G6PD) deficiency is situated on the long arm of the human X chromosome (region Xq28). Because the G6PD gene is X-linked, males who inherit a mutant X chromosome manifest the deficiency. (By contrast, the point mutation that produces sickle cell disease lies in the β-globin gene on chromosome 11.)"
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Sly syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type VII) is caused by mutations in the GUSB gene that encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-glucuronidase; the human GUSB gene is located on chromosome 7 (region 7q11)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Papillon–Lefèvre syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the cathepsin C (CTSC) gene. The CTSC gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 11 at band q14, so chromosome 11 carries the gene responsible for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TTDN1",
    "long_answer": "The non-photosensitive (non–UV-sensitive) form of trichothiodystrophy, designated ‘trichothiodystrophy non-photosensitive 1’, is caused by pathogenic variants in the TTDN1 gene (also known as MPLKIP/C7orf11). Mutations in TTDN1 differentiate this congenital/birth-defect variant of TTD from the photosensitive forms that involve DNA-repair genes such as ERCC2 (XPD) or ERCC3 (XPB)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type 0 results from loss-of-function mutations in the liver glycogen synthase gene, GYS2. Cytogenetic mapping places GYS2 at 12p12.2 on the short arm of chromosome 12; therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACADM gene",
    "long_answer": "A missense or other pathogenic mutation in the ACADM gene abolishes the activity of medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD). MCAD is the mitochondrial enzyme that catalyzes the first step of β-oxidation for medium-chain fatty acids and acts only on fatty acyl groups bound to coenzyme A. Loss of ACADM function therefore produces MCAD deficiency, the classic disorder of fatty-acid oxidation that manifests with fasting-induced hypoketotic hypoglycaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EYA1",
    "long_answer": "The EYA1 gene, located on human chromosome 8q13.3, is frequently mutated in branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome. BOR-associated renal anomalies commonly include multicystic dysplastic kidney. Thus, mutations in EYA1 on chromosome 8 are a known genetic cause of multicystic dysplastic kidney."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1",
    "long_answer": "Malignant hyperthermia is most frequently caused by pathogenic variants in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor calcium-release channel. Mutations in RYR1 lead to excessive sarcoplasmic Ca²⁺ release when patients are exposed to triggering anesthetics or succinylcholine, producing the hypermetabolic crisis characteristic of the disorder. Over 60-70 % of genetically confirmed malignant hyperthermia cases harbor RYR1 mutations, making this gene the primary target for preventive genetic testing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATR",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ATR (ataxia-telangiectasia and Rad3-related) gene cause Seckel syndrome type 1. Seckel syndrome presents with severe microcephaly and proportionate primordial dwarfism—core clinical features that closely resemble those seen in Microcephalic primordial dwarfism, Montreal type—making the ATR mutation the established genetic change linked to a disorder with overlapping symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lactose intolerance",
    "long_answer": "A genetic defect in the lactase enzyme (β-galactosidase), which normally splits lactose into glucose and galactose in the small-intestinal brush border, prevents proper lactose digestion. Undigested lactose reaches the colon, causing osmotic diarrhea, bloating, and gas—clinical features collectively termed lactose intolerance, the characteristic sugar-malabsorption disorder arising from lactase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Proconvertin is coagulation factor VII, encoded by the F7 gene. The F7 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 13 at band q34, so chromosome 13 contains the gene that produces proconvertin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The inborn error of GABA degradation most often encountered is succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (SSADH) deficiency. Mutations in the ALDH5A1 gene, which encodes this enzyme, cause the disorder. ALDH5A1 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p22.3), so chromosome 6 contains the gene whose defect leads to the enzymatic block in the GABA catabolic pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PSEN2",
    "long_answer": "An autosomal-dominant, early-onset form of Alzheimer disease (AD4) was first traced to several large ‘Volga German’ families. The condition is produced by pathogenic mutations in the PSEN2 (presenilin-2) gene—most famously the N141I substitution—establishing PSEN2 mutation as the causal genetic defect described in these German families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "The cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator (CFTR) gene—the gene in which pathogenic variants cause cystic fibrosis—is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 at position 7q31.2. It is not found on chromosome 2. This chromosomal assignment has been firmly established since the late 1980s through linkage analysis and subsequent sequencing studies, and it remains a cornerstone fact in human genetics."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Miglustat",
    "long_answer": "Sandhoff disease, a GM2 gangliosidosis caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HEXB gene encoding the β-subunit of β-hexosaminidase, has been explored for substrate-reduction therapy. Miglustat (N-butyl-deoxynojirimycin), an iminosugar that inhibits glucosylceramide synthase and thereby lowers glycosphingolipid accumulation, has been administered in pre-clinical models and compassionate-use/clinical studies of Sandhoff patients to slow neurologic deterioration."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DCAF17",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the DCAF17 gene (formerly C2orf37) cause Woodhouse-Sakati syndrome, an autosomal-recessive disorder whose name begins with “W.” The syndrome’s core clinical features include progressive diffuse hair thinning/alopecia and hypogonadotropic hypogonadism, along with other endocrine and neurological manifestations, firmly linking DCAF17 mutations to this presentation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease results from a CAG trinucleotide-repeat expansion in the HTT (huntingtin) gene, which is located on the short arm (4p16.3) of human chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcus aureus",
    "long_answer": "Folliculitis decalvans is a chronic neutrophilic cicatricial alopecia in which cultures of pustules and crusted lesions almost invariably grow Staphylococcus aureus. The frequent isolation of this bacterium and clinical improvement with antistaphylococcal antibiotics support its key role in the pathogenesis of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Miyoshi myopathy (also called Miyoshi distal myopathy) is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the DYSF gene, which encodes dysferlin. The DYSF gene is located on chromosome 2p13—not on chromosome 4.  Rarer Miyoshi-like phenotypes can be produced by mutations in other genes such as ANO5 (chromosome 11p14.3) and CAV3 (chromosome 3p25), but none of the currently recognized genes responsible for Miyoshi myopathy reside on chromosome 4.  Therefore, the assertion that the chromosomal locus of the gene involved in Miyoshi myopathy is on chromosome 4 is not supported by established genetic evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "A well-known genetic cause of aplasia cutis congenita is the telomeric deletion of the short arm of chromosome 4 (4p16.3), responsible for Wolf–Hirschhorn syndrome. This 4p deletion disrupts genes critical for craniofacial and skin development, and affected infants characteristically present with midline scalp defects (aplasia cutis) along with the syndrome’s facial and developmental features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Missense mutations in the FGFR2 (fibroblast growth factor receptor-2) gene are the classic cause of syndromic craniosynostosis disorders such as Crouzon and Apert syndromes, in which the cranial sutures fuse prematurely during development, leading to early closure of skull bones."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Urticarial vasculitis",
    "long_answer": "In urticarial vasculitis, the patient shows wheal-like, ‘urticarial’ plaques that persist for longer than ordinary urticaria. Biopsy of these fixed lesions reveals a leukocytoclastic small-vessel vasculitis rather than the dermal edema of simple urticaria, hence the name urticarial vasculitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The X-linked recessive form of cutis laxa is produced by mutations in genes (e.g., ATP7A) that lie on the X chromosome; all X-linked recessive disorders, by definition, involve genes located on this chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sertoli cells",
    "long_answer": "The hormone follicle-stimulating hormone (FSH) binds to FSH receptors on Sertoli cells in the seminiferous tubules. This activation drives Sertoli-cell proliferation and supplies the trophic support required for spermatogenesis, making Sertoli cells the primary testicular cells stimulated by FSH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Cystinosis is caused by mutations in the CTNS gene, which encodes the lysosomal cystine transporter cystinosin. The CTNS gene is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 17 at band 17p13, so chromosome 17 is the locus responsible for cystinosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Benign familial neonatal seizures (BFNS) have been linked to a paracentric inversion on chromosome 20q13 that disrupts the KC​NQ2 potassium-channel gene. This inversion at 20q13.3 was the first cytogenetic abnormality identified in BFNS families and established chromosome 20 as the critical locus for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Neurofibromatosis type II is produced by pathogenic mutations in the NF2 tumor-suppressor gene, located at band q12 on chromosome 22. Many disease-causing variants occur in intronic regions, disrupting normal splicing and expression of the NF2 gene product (merlin), which underlies the development of schwannomas, meningiomas and other NF2 manifestations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-thalassemia results from deletions or mutations of the two tandem α-globin genes, HBA1 and HBA2, which are located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (region 16p13.3). Defective expression of these chromosome 16 genes reduces α-globin chain production, producing the characteristic alpha-thalassemia phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Hemophilia A is the most common hereditary blood-clotting (coagulation) disorder. It is caused by mutations in the factor VIII gene (F8), which is located at band q28 of the human X chromosome. Thus, the gene responsible for the disorder resides on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Porphobilinogen deaminase",
    "long_answer": "Acute intermittent porphyria—classified among the porphyrias, a group of blood/heme disorders—arises when activity of the heme-biosynthetic enzyme porphobilinogen deaminase (also called hydroxymethylbilane synthase) is diminished or inhibited. This enzymatic block leads to accumulation of neurotoxic precursors and can produce severe neuro-psychiatric symptoms. Quetiapine is one of the atypical antipsychotics considered safe and effective for treating these psychiatric manifestations in patients with acute intermittent porphyria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chiari I malformation",
    "long_answer": "Most patients who develop a syrinx do so because of a Chiari type I malformation, in which herniation of the cerebellar tonsils obstructs cerebrospinal fluid flow at the foramen magnum. This CSF blockage creates hydrodynamic pressure waves that enlarge the central canal, forming a syringomyelia cavity. Decompression of the Chiari malformation is therefore the neurosurgical procedure most often performed to treat syringomyelia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Worth syndrome (endosteal hyperostosis) is produced by gain-of-function mutations in the LRP5 gene. LRP5 is located at cytogenetic band 11q13.4 on human chromosome 11; alterations at this locus elevate Wnt signaling in osteoblasts, leading to the markedly increased bone density that characterizes the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "278700",
    "long_answer": "The disorder is xeroderma pigmentosum (XP). OMIM entry 278700 describes XP, a recessive condition in which mutations in genes of the nucleotide-excision repair pathway greatly reduce the ability to repair ultraviolet-induced DNA damage, causing extreme sun sensitivity and a high incidence of skin cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CENPJ gene, which encodes a centrosomal protein necessary for centriole formation and overall centrosome function, cause a form of Seckel syndrome. The CENPJ gene is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 13 (13q12.2), making chromosome 13 the chromosomal location relevant to this Seckel-syndrome-associated, centrosome-related gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANCA-associated vasculitis",
    "long_answer": "ANCA-associated vasculitis is defined by the presence of circulating anti-neutrophil cytoplasmic antibodies (ANCAs) that bind specific neutrophil granule antigens—primarily proteinase-3 (PR3) or myeloperoxidase (MPO). These antibodies drive a pauci-immune, necrotizing small-vessel vasculitis that includes granulomatosis with polyangiitis, microscopic polyangiitis, and eosinophilic granulomatosis with polyangiitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mantle zone",
    "long_answer": "The lymphoma in question is mantle-cell lymphoma, which arises from naïve B-cells that occupy the mantle zone (the concentric collar of small lymphocytes surrounding the germinal center) of a lymphatic nodule. Castleman disease shows prominent expansion of this same mantle zone and can be a benign lymphoproliferative process, highlighting the anatomic relationship."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "The first gene shown to confer risk for Tourette syndrome is SLITRK1, identified through a chromosomal inversion and mutation screening in affected families. SLITRK1 is situated at cytogenetic band 13q31 on human chromosome 13, so the Tourette-associated gene resides on chromosome 13."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Obsessive–compulsive disorder",
    "long_answer": "Obsessive–compulsive disorder (OCD) has been documented as a comorbid psychiatric condition in both pica and Kleine–Levin syndrome (KLS). Pica is sometimes viewed as part of the OCD spectrum because the repetitive, non-nutritive eating behavior is often driven by intrusive urges and relieved by the act of ingestion. Likewise, clinical reports of KLS describe patients who, between or during hypersomnolent episodes, exhibit pronounced obsessive thoughts and compulsive rituals characteristic of OCD. Therefore, OCD is the mental disorder that can co-occur with both pica and Kleine–Levin syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF7",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the KIF7 gene cause acrocallosal syndrome. This ciliopathy is classically characterized by agenesis or hypoplasia of the corpus callosum together with pre- or postaxial polydactyly, macrocephaly, and developmental delay. KIF7 encodes a kinesin that regulates Sonic Hedgehog signaling during embryonic brain and limb development, explaining the concurrence of callosal and digit malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The rheumatic disease that chiefly targets the spinal column—ankylosing spondylitis—is strongly linked to the HLA-B27 allele and other MHC class I genes. All of these immune-related genes lie within the major histocompatibility complex on the short arm of chromosome 6 (6p21), making chromosome 6 the locus that harbors the key genetic determinants of this upper-spinal rheumatic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mediterranean region",
    "long_answer": "In the Mediterranean region, inherited hemoglobinopathies such as β-thalassemia and other chronic hemolytic anemias are common in children. These disorders produce persistent anemia and increased erythropoietic drive, which stimulate secondary (reactive) thrombocythemia. Consequently, pediatric patients from Mediterranean countries frequently present with hemolytic anemia and thalassemia as underlying causes of elevated platelet counts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IDH1 and IDH2",
    "long_answer": "The disorder with multiple enchondromas and cavernous hemangiomas is Maffucci syndrome. Over 70–80 % of affected individuals harbor post-zygotic, mosaic missense mutations in the isocitrate dehydrogenase genes IDH1 (most often p.R132C/H) or, less commonly, IDH2 (p.R172S/G). These gain-of-function mutations lead to accumulation of the oncometabolite 2-hydroxyglutarate, driving the abnormal cartilage and vascular tumors characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACAT1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the ACAT1 gene, which encodes mitochondrial acetoacetyl-CoA (β-ketothiolase), cause β-ketothiolase deficiency. This inborn error of isoleucine and ketone-body catabolism leads to recurrent severe ketoacidotic attacks; during these episodes patients often develop neurologic signs such as seizures/convulsions because of the profound metabolic acidosis and accumulation of organic acids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pleiotropy",
    "long_answer": "Many genetic birth-defect syndromes arise from a single mutated gene or chromosomal abnormality that has pleiotropic effects—i.e., it simultaneously disturbs the development of several different organs or tissues. This ‘one gene, many phenotypes’ property explains why affected individuals often present with multiple, seemingly unrelated congenital anomalies characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The most common form of ovarian cancer is high-grade serous carcinoma, and virtually all of these tumors harbor mutations in the TP53 tumor-suppressor gene. The human TP53 gene is located on chromosome 17 (region 17p13), so chromosome 17 contains the gene most directly linked to the predominant type of ovarian cancer."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Topical corticosteroids",
    "long_answer": "Topical corticosteroids are first-line anti-inflammatory treatments for atopic dermatitis, but chronic or excessive use can inhibit collagen synthesis and reduce dermal matrix production, producing cutaneous atrophy—clinically recognized as skin thinning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH3",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the MYH3 gene, which encodes embryonic myosin heavy chain 3, cause distal arthrogryposis type 2A (Freeman-Sheldon syndrome). This disorder combines multiple congenital limb contractures (arthrogryposis) with marked craniofacial abnormalities such as microstomia, puckered lips (“whistling face”), and a small chin, establishing MYH3 mutation as the genetic basis of an arthrogryposis form that involves craniofacial anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIK3CA",
    "long_answer": "Somatic activating mutations in the PIK3CA gene underlie the PIK3CA-related overgrowth spectrum (PROS). This spectrum includes Klippel–Trénaunay syndrome, CLOVES syndrome, macrodactyly, and other segmental overgrowth disorders. The mutant PIK3CA hyper-activates the PI3K–AKT–mTOR signaling pathway, driving abnormal vascular and tissue overgrowth characteristic of these conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Metatarsal bones",
    "long_answer": "The most common congenital foot abnormality that produces a pigeon-toed (in-toeing) appearance is metatarsus adductus. This deformity is produced by medial deviation of the forefoot at the tarsometatarsal joints, involving the metatarsal bones, which angle inward toward the midline while the hind-foot remains neutral."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CASR",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia (FHH) most commonly results from inactivating mutations in the CASR gene, which encodes the calcium-sensing receptor on parathyroid and renal tubular cells. Loss-of-function CASR mutations raise the set-point for calcium sensing, leading to lifelong mild hypercalcemia with inappropriately low urinary calcium excretion—the hallmark of FHH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Primary ciliary dyskinesia can result from mutations in the NME8 gene, also known as TXNDC3 because it encodes thioredoxin domain-containing protein 3. The NME8/TXNDC3 gene is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 7 (region 7p14.1), so chromosome 7 harbours the gene in question."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Infantile free sialic acid storage disease (also called infantile sialic acid storage disease or Salla disease) is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the SLC17A5 gene, which encodes the lysosomal membrane sialic-acid transporter (sialin). The SLC17A5 gene is located on chromosome 6 (region 6q14-q15), not on chromosome 20. Therefore, chromosome 20 does not contain the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Northern Flanders, Belgium",
    "long_answer": "The first family reported with Metaphyseal dysostosis-intellectual disability-conductive deafness syndrome consisted of two affected siblings whose clinically normal parents both came from the same small village in northern Flanders, Belgium. This shared Belgian origin was highlighted in the original description of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "The key gene historically named AML1 (now RUNX1), whose mutations and translocations are tightly linked to acute myeloid leukemia, is located at band q22 of human chromosome 21."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sacral plexus",
    "long_answer": "The muscle that extends the big toe (extensor hallucis longus) is innervated by the deep fibular (peroneal) nerve, a branch of the common fibular division of the sciatic nerve. The sciatic nerve arises from the sacral plexus (roots L4–S3). Thus, the sacral plexus is the nerve plexus that provides the motor innervation enabling extension of the hallux."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Truncating mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes type II collagen, cause spondyloperipheral dysplasia. This skeletal dysplasia presents with marked platyspondyly (flattened vertebral bodies) together with brachydactyly (shortened digits), establishing COL2A1 mutation as the underlying genetic defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SUCNR1 (GPR91)",
    "long_answer": "Succinate – a key intermediate of the citric (tricarboxylic) acid cycle – acts as an extracellular signalling molecule that binds to and activates the G-protein-coupled succinate receptor-1, SUCNR1 (also known as GPR91). Disruption of the SUCNR1 locus in X-chromosome rearrangements has been reported in families with X-linked intellectual disability, linking this receptor to the disorder. Hence, the receptor activated by the citric-acid-cycle compound and associated with X-linked intellectual disability is SUCNR1 (GPR91)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DNA sequencing",
    "long_answer": "Mutations responsible for Carnitine palmitoyltransferase II deficiency are detected by amplifying the CPT2 gene with PCR and performing direct DNA sequencing (e.g., Sanger sequencing or next-generation sequence analysis). Sequencing reveals single-nucleotide substitutions, small insertions, or deletions across the CPT2 coding region and splice sites, enabling definitive identification of pathogenic variants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MT-TK",
    "long_answer": "A point mutation (classically A8344G) in the mitochondrial MT-TK gene, which encodes tRNA-Lys, causes the syndrome MERRF (Myoclonic Epilepsy with Ragged Red Fibers). This multisystem mitochondrial disorder is characteristically marked by stimulus-sensitive myoclonic seizures, along with ataxia and myopathy, linking the MT-TK mutation directly to a disease whose core symptom is myoclonic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alveoli",
    "long_answer": "Human cytomegalovirus (HHV-5) is a well-known cause of CMV interstitial pneumonitis/pneumonia in immunocompromised or transplanted patients. The infection produces diffuse inflammation in the lung parenchyma, targeting the alveolar walls and epithelial cells. Thus, the lung structure chiefly affected by this HHV-5–associated disease is the pulmonary alveoli."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acrocyanosis is symptom-free; peripheral cyanosis shows dyspnea",
    "long_answer": "In acrocyanosis the bluish discoloration of hands and feet occurs without accompanying systemic symptoms—patients are not short of breath and feel otherwise well. Peripheral cyanosis, by contrast, reflects systemic arterial desaturation or poor circulation and therefore is typically accompanied by hypoxic symptoms such as breathlessness (dyspnea), tachypnea or other signs of cardiorespiratory distress."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trish Cook",
    "long_answer": "Trish Cook is the author of the young-adult novel \"Midnight Sun,\" whose teenage protagonist, Katie Price, has xeroderma pigmentosum (XP). XP is a rare inherited defect in nucleotide-excision DNA repair that makes skin cells unable to repair UV-induced damage, so affected individuals must avoid sunlight—an essential element of the book’s plot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the MMP20 gene, which encodes the enamel protease enamelysin responsible for degrading enamel matrix proteins during the secretory stage of amelogenesis, cause an autosomal-recessive form of amelogenesis imperfecta. The human MMP20 gene is located on chromosome 11q22.3, so chromosome 11 is the chromosome whose alteration can lead to this enamel defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pfeiffer syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Pfeiffer syndrome is an autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis disorder produced by activating mutations in the fibroblast growth factor receptor genes FGFR1 or FGFR2. Premature fusion of cranial sutures creates shallow orbits, leading to marked ocular proptosis (exophthalmos), along with the characteristic broad thumbs and great toes seen in this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Kagoshima Prefecture",
    "long_answer": "Sonoda syndrome was originally reported by Dr. Yoshimasa Sonoda in patients from Kagoshima Prefecture in southern Japan; the first clinical description appeared in observations of several unrelated children from that prefecture, establishing the eponymous syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Complement component C5",
    "long_answer": "Leiner’s disease, a rare disorder presenting in early infancy with severe generalized seborrheic dermatitis, diarrhoea and recurrent systemic infections, has been linked to a hereditary deficiency of complement component C5. Absence or marked reduction of C5 impairs the formation of the membrane-attack complex and opsonisation, predisposing affected infants to widespread cutaneous inflammation and serious infections."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Genitourinary system",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcus species (e.g., S. aureus, S. saprophyticus) can ascend the urethra, causing urethritis and other urinary-tract infections. Because the urethra is a component of the urinary and reproductive tracts, such infections involve and can compromise the genitourinary system as a whole."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cranial nerves III, IV, VI",
    "long_answer": "Paralytic strabismus arises when the motor supply to the extra-ocular muscles is impaired. These muscles are innervated by the oculomotor nerve (cranial nerve III), trochlear nerve (cranial nerve IV), and abducens nerve (cranial nerve VI); damage to any of these three nerves can therefore produce a paralytic deviation of the eyes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Daentl",
    "long_answer": "The congenital limb-reduction pattern now called femur-fibula-ulna (FFU) syndrome was first described as a distinct clinical entity by Daentl and co-workers in 1970, when they reported infants showing the characteristic simultaneous hypoplasia or absence of the femur, fibula and ulna. Their publication introduced and defined the syndrome in the medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AP5Z1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spastic paraplegia type 48 (SPG48) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the AP5Z1 gene, which encodes the ζ-subunit of the adaptor protein complex-5 (AP-5). Defective AP5Z1 impairs endosomal membrane trafficking, leading to axonal degeneration characteristic of SPG48."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Beare-Stevenson cutis gyrata syndrome presents with craniosynostosis together with cerebriform (gyrate) thickening of the skin. All confirmed cases harbor recurrent activating missense mutations (e.g., Ser372Cys, Tyr375Cys) in the FGFR2 gene, which encodes fibroblast growth factor receptor 2. The gain-of-function alterations in FGFR2 disrupt normal signaling during cranial suture and skin development, leading to premature suture fusion (craniosynostosis) and the characteristic cutis gyrata."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "In several families with autosomal-dominant benign familial neonatal seizures (also called benign familial neonatal convulsions), the disorder segregates with a paracentric inversion of human chromosome 5 [inv(5)(p15q11-q13)]. This chromosomal rearrangement alters a locus required for normal neonatal neuronal excitability, producing the transient seizure phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ophthalmology",
    "long_answer": "In Bell's palsy, paralysis of the facial nerve can prevent complete eyelid closure, leading to corneal exposure, dryness, and keratitis. The medical specialty that evaluates and manages these eye-related complications—including lubricating therapy, protective measures, and possible surgical interventions—is ophthalmology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CDMP1 (GDF5)",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CDMP1 gene—also known as GDF5, encoding cartilage-derived morphogenetic protein-1—cause Grebe-type chondrodysplasia, a skeletal dysplasia marked by severely shortened and malformed bones of the arms and legs. Loss-of-function variants disrupt BMP signaling crucial for limb skeletal patterning, leading to the characteristic limb malformations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CUL4B",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Fineman–Myers syndrome is an X-linked syndromic intellectual-disability disorder that has been shown to result from loss-of-function mutations in the CUL4B gene on Xq24. Pathogenic variants in CUL4B, which encodes the cullin-4B scaffold of an E3 ubiquitin-ligase complex, were identified in multiple families originally described with the Smith–Fineman–Myers phenotype, establishing CUL4B mutation as the molecular cause of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy",
    "long_answer": "X-linked adrenoleukodystrophy (X-ALD) results from mutations in the ABCD1 gene on the X chromosome. The defect lowers peroxisomal long-chain fatty acyl-CoA synthetase/transport activity, so very-long-chain fatty acids cannot be efficiently converted to their CoA esters and β-oxidised. Accumulation of these fatty acids in the adrenal cortex and central white matter produces the characteristic neurologic and adrenal manifestations of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The commonest form, familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia type 1, results from inactivating mutations in the calcium-sensing receptor (CASR) gene, which is located on chromosome 3q (region 3q21–3q24). Hence chromosome 3 is the chromosomal locus classically associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Paget disease of bone is a metabolic bone disorder that is common in people of European descent but very uncommon in individuals of African ancestry in the United States. The principal susceptibility gene linked to this disease is SQSTM1 (sequestosome-1), which is located at cytogenetic band 5q35. Thus, the relevant human chromosome harboring the gene is chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CC2D2A",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the CC2D2A gene, which encodes a ciliary transition-zone protein and is well known for causing Meckel syndrome type 6 and Joubert syndrome type 9, have also been documented to produce a clinical picture of syndromic microphthalmia (designated MCOPS6 in OMIM). Thus, CC2D2A mutations link the ciliopathy spectrum of Meckel/Joubert syndromes with syndromic microphthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vascular dementia",
    "long_answer": "Vascular dementia is a frequent neuro-psychiatric disorder that can follow cerebellar degeneration produced by repeated or focal cerebrovascular insults. Ischaemic or haemorrhagic injury to the brain’s blood vessels, including those supplying the cerebellum, leads to neuronal loss and atrophy; the resulting multi-infarct damage manifests clinically as cognitive decline and behavioural or psychiatric symptoms that define vascular dementia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Neutrophil elastase proteolytically cleaves and thereby inactivates the serpin antithrombin. The gene that encodes neutrophil elastase (ELANE, formerly ELA2) is located on human chromosome 19 (19p13.3), making chromosome 19 the chromosomal site of the enzyme that can inactivate antithrombin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (CLN3 disease/Batten disease) is primarily caused by pathogenic variants in the CLN3 gene. The CLN3 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (16p12.1), making chromosome 16 the chromosome most commonly associated with this neurodegenerative disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Isolated autosomal-recessive woolly hair is most commonly produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LIPH (lipase H) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 3 (3q27). Hence, the responsible gene resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Mucopolysaccharidosis type I results from a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase, encoded by the IDUA gene. The human IDUA gene is located on chromosome 4 at band 4p16.3; hence, chromosome 4 carries the gene whose loss of function causes this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "About 60%",
    "long_answer": "Across multiple large cohorts, synovial sarcoma patients who harbour the canonical SS18-SSX1 or SS18-SSX2 fusion have an aggregate five-year overall survival of roughly 60 %. Detailed analyses show ~40 % for the SS18-SSX1 subtype and ~70 % for SS18-SSX2, but when the two fusion types are considered together—as is typical in clinical summaries—the accepted five-year survival rate converges on about sixty percent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Systemic primary carnitine deficiency results from loss-of-function mutations in the carnitine transporter gene SLC22A5 (also called OCTN2). SLC22A5 is mapped to cytogenetic region 5q31 on human chromosome 5, so chromosome 5 harbors the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Prednisone",
    "long_answer": "Systemic corticosteroids such as prednisone are frequently prescribed to control the inflammatory and eosinophilic manifestations of Kimura’s disease. Because prednisone is a potent glucocorticoid, chronic or high-dose use can produce iatrogenic Cushing syndrome—the form of Cushing syndrome that results from prolonged exposure to exogenous glucocorticoids."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I",
    "long_answer": "Carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I (CPS-I) is the mitochondrial urea-cycle enzyme that catalyzes the first step—combining ammonia, CO₂ and ATP to form carbamoyl phosphate. A deficiency of CPS-I blocks this reaction, preventing conversion of toxic ammonia into urea and producing the disorder termed carbamoyl phosphate synthetase I deficiency with severe hyperammonemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Akhenaten",
    "long_answer": "The 18th-dynasty pharaoh Akhenaten (Amenhotep IV) has been proposed by medical historians to have had homocystinuria, an inborn error of methionine metabolism. Homocystinuria leads to excessive urinary excretion of homocysteine and produces a tall, slender, Marfanoid body habitus with ocular findings such as severe myopia—traits that some scholars believe are reflected in Amarna-period depictions of Akhenaten’s physique."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Huntington’s disease is caused by expansion of CAG repeats in the HTT (huntingtin) gene, which is located on the short arm of chromosome 4 at band p16.3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Sandhoff disease results from mutations in the HEXB gene, which encodes the common β-subunit of β-hexosaminidases A and B. The HEXB gene is located on human chromosome 5 (region 5q13.3); therefore, chromosome 5 carries the defect that causes the combined deficiency of these enzymes in Sandhoff disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The LDL receptor (LDLR) gene, whose mutations produce familial hypercholesterolemia, is located at 19p13.2 on chromosome 19. The same chromosome also harbors genes such as INSR (insulin receptor) that are implicated in diabetes, linking chromosome 19 to both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen, cause achondrogenesis type II (Langer-Saldino form). Because COL2A1 produces the major cartilage-specific collagen, its defects place this lethal skeletal dysplasia within the spectrum of type II collagen disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Adenine phosphoribosyltransferase deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the APRT gene. The APRT gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 16 (cytogenetic band 16q24.3), so chromosome 16 carries the gene responsible for this hereditary metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Tuberous sclerosis complex (TSC) arises from mutations in either TSC1 or TSC2. Clinical studies consistently show that mutations in TSC2 produce an earlier onset and more severe neurological and systemic manifestations than TSC1 mutations. The TSC2 gene is located on chromosome 16p13.3, so chromosome 16 carries the gene whose disruption is linked to the more severe form of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Camurati–Engelmann disease is caused by activating mutations in the TGFB1 (transforming growth factor-β1) gene. TGFB1 is located on the long arm of chromosome 19 (19q13.1-q13.3), establishing chromosome 19 as the chromosomal site implicated in this skeletal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the SLC25A20 gene, which encodes the mitochondrial carnitine-acylcarnitine translocase. The SLC25A20 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 3p21 on human chromosome 3, so the disorder-causing gene resides on chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Maine",
    "long_answer": "The Jackson Laboratory, located in Bar Harbor, Maine, is the research institute that successfully isolated the ALMS1 gene responsible for the rare genetic disorder Alström syndrome. This laboratory is internationally recognized for mouse genetics and human genomics research, and its Maine campus was the site where the Alström syndrome gene was identified."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease (MSMD) frequently results from loss-of-function mutations in the IFN-γ receptor 1 gene (IFNGR1). The IFNGR1 locus is situated at band 6q23.3 on human chromosome 6; mutations here impair IFN-γ signalling, compromising macrophage activation and conferring the characteristic vulnerability to weakly virulent mycobacteria."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Dentin dysplasia type II is produced by mutations in the DSPP (dentin sialophosphoprotein) gene, which is located on chromosome 4 at band q21.3. Thus, the causative gene mutation resides on chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Familial eosinophilia has been genetically mapped to the 5q31–q33 region of chromosome 5, which harbours the cytokine gene cluster encoding IL-3, IL-5 and GM-CSF. Overexpression of these cytokines can drive marked eosinophilia, and rearrangements involving this chromosomal region (e.g., t(5;14) bringing IL-3 next to T-cell receptor genes) are also seen in certain T-cell lymphomas with eosinophilia, linking chromosome 5 to both familial eosinophilia and lymphoma development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome (Xq24–q25)",
    "long_answer": "Bazex–Dupré–Christol syndrome is an X-linked dominantly inherited disorder. The causal gene, ACTRT1, maps to the long arm of the X chromosome in the Xq24–q25 region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The most common inherited disorder of lipid metabolism affecting skeletal muscle is carnitine palmitoyltransferase II (CPT II) deficiency. The CPT2 gene, which encodes the CPT II enzyme, is mapped to chromosome 1 (band 1p32). Hence, chromosome 1 contains the gene responsible for this enzyme."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CRYAA",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant congenital cataract-microcornea syndrome has been mapped to missense mutations in the CRYAA gene, which encodes the αA-crystallin lens protein. Families carrying the R116H or similar CRYAA mutations present with bilateral congenital cataracts and marked microcornea while showing no facial or systemic dysmorphic features, establishing CRYAA mutation as the cause of this specific clinical picture."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes 12 and 17",
    "long_answer": "White sponge nevus is produced by pathogenic variants in the keratin genes KRT4 and KRT13. KRT4 maps to chromosome 12q13, while KRT13 maps to chromosome 17q21. Hence, the responsible genes reside on human chromosomes 12 and 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Chédiak–Higashi syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the LYST gene (Lysosomal Trafficking Regulator, also called CHS1). The LYST gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 1 (band 1q42.1–q42.2), not on chromosome 7. Therefore, human chromosome 7 does not contain the gene responsible for Chédiak–Higashi syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Haim–Munk syndrome is produced by autosomal-recessive mutations in the CTSC gene, which encodes the lysosomal protease cathepsin C. Genetic mapping and sequencing studies have placed CTSC on the long arm of human chromosome 11 (region 11q14-q15). Thus, chromosome 11 carries the gene whose defects cause Haim–Munk syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPTLC1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPTLC1 gene, which encodes a subunit of serine-palmitoyltransferase—the key enzyme initiating sphingolipid biosynthesis—cause the HSAN I subtype known as HSN1A. These missense mutations alter enzyme specificity, leading to accumulation of neurotoxic deoxysphingolipids and the sensory–autonomic neuropathy phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CASR gene",
    "long_answer": "Familial hypocalciuric hypercalcemia is produced by inactivating mutations in the CASR gene, which encodes the calcium-sensing receptor. Loss-of-function of this receptor makes parathyroid and renal tubular cells less sensitive to extracellular calcium, so higher serum calcium levels are required to suppress parathyroid hormone secretion and limit renal reabsorption, leading to persistent hypercalcemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX3 gene cause Waardenburg syndrome types 1 and 3, a congenital disorder characterized by sensorineural hearing loss along with pigmentary abnormalities of the hair, skin, and eyes. The presence of congenital hearing loss is a hallmark clinical feature linking PAX3 to this syndromic condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXC2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the FOXC2 gene (encoding the forkhead box C2 transcription factor) cause lymphedema-distichiasis syndrome. A cardinal feature of this condition is distichiasis, the presence of a second row of eyelashes arising from Meibomian glands. FOXC2 is therefore the gene classically associated with the skin/ocular finding of a double row of eyelashes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Meningitis",
    "long_answer": "Cavernous sinus thrombosis usually originates from a septic infection of the face, nose, or paranasal sinuses. The same bacteria can track along venous channels into the cranial cavity, spreading beyond the cavernous sinus to the meninges. This intracranial spread can precipitate acute bacterial meningitis, an aggressive and potentially fatal inflammation of the leptomeninges, making meningitis the key life-threatening complication of the infection that causes cavernous sinus thrombosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Congenital hypothyroidism",
    "long_answer": "Congenital hypothyroidism is one of the most common and completely preventable causes of intellectual disability. Newborns lacking thyroid hormone develop lethargy, feeding difficulties, and metabolic slowing; without early thyroxine replacement this progresses to failure to thrive and permanent cognitive impairment. Routine neonatal screening and prompt treatment avert both growth failure and intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Type 2 Congenital Generalized Lipodystrophy is caused by pathogenic variants in the BSCL2 (seipin) gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 11 (11q12-11q13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTX2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OTX2 homeobox gene, a key regulator of early anterior neural plate development, can abolish formation of the prosencephalon. Homozygous or severe loss-of-function mutations have been reported to produce the lethal malformation termed aprosencephaly (from Greek a- ‘without’ + prosencephalon ‘embryonic forebrain’), in which the forebrain fails to form. Thus, OTX2 mutation is a recognized genetic cause of the condition whose name literally means ‘without embryonic forebrain’."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Feeding difficulties",
    "long_answer": "In infants with a cleft palate, the oral and nasal cavities are not fully separated, making it hard to generate the negative pressure needed for effective sucking. Milk or food therefore pools in the oropharynx and can reflux into the nasal cavity or airway, predisposing the infant to aspiration of feeds. Repeated aspiration of these oral contents is a well-recognized cause of aspiration pneumonia, making feeding difficulties one of the most common and clinically important complications of cleft palate."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Platelet-derived growth factor",
    "long_answer": "Platelet-derived growth factor (PDGF) is a strong mitogen for fibroblasts, endothelial cells and other mesenchymal-derived cells. By driving cell proliferation and extracellular-matrix production, PDGF promotes tissue hyperplasia. In the conjunctiva, PDGF-induced fibroblast proliferation contributes to the papillary and epithelial hyperplasia that characterises disorders such as vernal keratoconjunctivitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SP110 gene",
    "long_answer": "The rare autosomal-recessive syndrome ‘veno-occlusive disease with immunodeficiency’ (VODI) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the SP110 gene, which encodes the SP110 nuclear body protein. Defective SP110 disrupts immune regulation and hepatic sinusoidal endothelial integrity, leading to the hereditary form of hepatic veno-occlusive disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "The RUNX1 (also termed AML1) gene, whose disruption in the t(8;21)(q22;q22) translocation is a hallmark of acute myeloid leukemia, is located on human chromosome 21 at band 21q22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OFD1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked OFD1 gene cause Orofaciodigital syndrome type 1, a disorder marked by congenital malformations of the face (hypertelorism, cleft lip), the oral cavity (lobulated or cleft tongue, oral frenula), and the digits (polydactyly, brachydactyly, syndactyly)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "β-Hexosaminidase A",
    "long_answer": "Tay-Sachs disease presents in early infancy with progressive neuro-degeneration, including tremors and myoclonic jerks. The disorder is caused by a deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme β-hexosaminidase A, leading to accumulation of GM2 ganglioside in neurons and consequent neurologic symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SCN9A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SCN9A gene, which encodes the voltage-gated sodium channel NaV1.7, are well established as the cause of inherited primary erythromelalgia—a rare vascular pain syndrome marked by episodic redness, warmth, and severe burning pain in the limbs. Gain-of-function SCN9A variants increase NaV1.7 channel activity, heightening nociceptor excitability and producing the characteristic extremity vasodilation and intense pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pityriasis rubra pilaris",
    "long_answer": "Pityriasis rubra pilaris (PRP) is a chronic keratinization disorder that produces well-demarcated, reddish-orange (salmon-colored) scaly patches and thickened skin on the palms and soles. Although most cases are sporadic, certain forms (notably Type V) are inherited in an autosomal-dominant pattern due to pathogenic variants in the CARD14 gene, so the disease can run in families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Pontocerebellar hypoplasia type 6 is caused by mutations in the RARS2 gene, which encodes the mitochondrial arginyl-tRNA synthetase required for mitochondrial protein synthesis and respiratory-chain function. The RARS2 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 6 (band 6q22.1), making chromosome 6 the chromosome that carries this mitochondrial-function gene associated with pontocerebellar hypoplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Chorea acanthocytosis is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13A (also called CHAC) gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 9q21 on human chromosome 9. Therefore, the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is caused by mutations in the NBN (NBS1) gene. Cytogenetic mapping places NBN at 8q21.3 on human chromosome 8, establishing chromosome 8 as the location of the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Carnitine palmitoyl-transferase I",
    "long_answer": "In malonic aciduria, deficiency of malonyl-CoA decarboxylase causes intracellular accumulation of malonyl-CoA. Malonyl-CoA is a well-established allosteric inhibitor of the mitochondrial outer-membrane enzyme carnitine palmitoyl-transferase I (CPT I), which controls entry of long-chain acyl-CoA into mitochondria for β-oxidation. Thus, the fatty-acid-oxidation enzyme inhibited by the accumulating molecule is CPT I."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Alpha thalassemia results from deletions or mutations in the duplicated alpha-globin genes (HBA1 and HBA2), both of which are clustered on the short arm of chromosome 16 (16p13.3). Defects in these genes reduce or abolish alpha-globin chain production, producing the clinical spectrum of alpha thalassemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Variants in the STOX1 gene, which is located on chromosome 10q22, have been shown to segregate with familial cases of pre-eclampsia and increase disease risk. This chromosomal location is therefore specifically associated with a genetic influence on the development of pre-eclampsia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Werner syndrome results from loss-of-function mutations in the WRN gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase involved in genome stability. The WRN gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 8 (band 8p12); its mutation leads to premature aging features and a markedly increased risk of multiple cancers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The protein AF9, encoded by the MLLT3 gene, physically interacts with the corepressor BCOR and is a well-known fusion partner of KMT2A in the t(9;11)(p22;q23) translocation that drives acute myeloid leukemia. The MLLT3 gene is located on human chromosome 9 (band 9p22), so chromosome 9 is the chromosome that contains this gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Benign hereditary chorea is most often produced by pathogenic variants in the NKX2-1 (thyroid transcription factor-1) gene, which has been mapped to the 14q13 region. This places the causative gene on human chromosome 14."
}

{
    "short_answer": "von Hansemann cells",
    "long_answer": "Michaelis–Gutmann bodies are concentrically layered basophilic inclusions found within enlarged foamy macrophages called von Hansemann cells. These macrophages are the hallmark of malakoplakia, a chronic granulomatous condition that can manifest clinically as an ulcerative lesion. Identification of von Hansemann cells containing Michaelis–Gutmann bodies is therefore diagnostic for this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHOX2B",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PHOX2B gene (most often poly-alanine repeat expansions) cause congenital central hypoventilation syndrome. The same hypoventilation syndrome can also be acquired after severe brainstem trauma or other neurologic injury, linking PHOX2B mutation to a condition that may likewise appear following serious brain injury."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Triple-A (Allgrove) syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the AAAS gene, which encodes the nuclear-pore protein ALADIN. Cytogenetic mapping places the AAAS gene at 12q13, meaning it resides on human chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "MKRN3 (Makorin RING finger protein 3) is located in the Prader-Willi critical region on chromosome 15q11-q13, inherited from the paternal allele. Loss-of-function variants in MKRN3 are a well-established genetic cause of familial and sporadic central precocious puberty. It is therefore not situated on chromosome 16, so the statement is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Posterior iliac crest",
    "long_answer": "For therapeutic bone-marrow transplantation, marrow is most commonly harvested from the posterior iliac crest of the pelvis. This site provides a large, easily accessible volume of marrow with minimal morbidity, making it the standard donor site for treating conditions such as Myhre syndrome that may benefit from marrow-derived stem-cell therapy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TSC2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic variant in the TSC2 gene (encoding tuberin) causes tuberous sclerosis complex. Brain and systemic MRI is the standard imaging test used to identify the cortical tubers, subependymal nodules, and other characteristic lesions that establish and monitor the diagnosis of this disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Influenza virus",
    "long_answer": "Primary infection with influenza virus damages the respiratory epithelium and impairs mucociliary clearance, creating an environment in which Streptococcus pneumoniae can more easily colonize the lower airways. This viral-induced disruption of host defenses is a well-documented predisposing factor for secondary bacterial pneumonia caused by S. pneumoniae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wernicke’s aphasia",
    "long_answer": "Conditions that acutely affect the dominant temporal lobe—most famously herpes simplex encephalitis, which presents with severe confusion and delirium—often damage the posterior superior temporal gyrus (Wernicke area). Lesions here produce a fluent-receptive language deficit called Wernicke’s aphasia, so the same pathological process that causes delirium can simultaneously lead to this specific type of aphasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH9",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the MYH9 gene, encoding non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA, cause MYH9-related disorders such as May-Hegglin anomaly. These conditions are typified by macrothrombocytopenia with markedly giant platelets, making MYH9 mutation a well-established genetic cause of a giant platelet disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Absent pulmonary valve syndrome frequently occurs in patients with the 22q11.2 microdeletion (the same deletion seen in DiGeorge/velocardio-facial syndrome). Thus, the pathogenic microdeletion is on human chromosome 22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome type 1 is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the MYCN gene, which is located on chromosome 2p24.1. A smaller subset of cases (type 2) result from deletions encompassing the MIR17HG micro-RNA cluster on chromosome 13q31.3. No form of Feingold syndrome has been linked to a gene on chromosome 3. Therefore, the statement that the gene responsible for Feingold syndrome is located on chromosome 3 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Paroxysmal nonkinesigenic dyskinesia (PNKD) is caused by pathogenic variants in the PNKD gene (also called MR-1). Cytogenetic mapping places PNKD at 2q31, so the responsible gene is located on human chromosome 2. Clonazepam is frequently used to reduce the frequency and severity of PNKD attacks, linking the clinical treatment to the chromosome-2–encoded gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Hand–foot–genital syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HOXA13 homeobox gene. The entire HOXA gene cluster, including HOXA13, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 7 (region 7p15.2). Thus, chromosome 7 is the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Herpes simplex virus type 2",
    "long_answer": "Marek’s disease virus is an alphaherpesvirus of chickens. The closely related human alphaherpesvirus is Herpes simplex virus type 2 (HSV-2), which has historically been implicated in the etiology of cervical cancer. Thus, HSV-2 is the virus that links cervical cancer risk with a virus related to Marek’s disease virus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "The skeletal disorder with severe dwarfism and the pathognomonic lace-like pattern of bone (Dyggve-Melchior-Clausen/Smith-McCort dysplasia) is produced by mutations in the DYM gene, which is located on human chromosome 18q21. Thus, chromosome 18 carries the gene responsible for this radiographically distinctive disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Aromatase deficiency is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CYP19A1 gene, which encodes the aromatase enzyme responsible for converting androgens to estrogens. The CYP19A1 gene is located on chromosome 15 (region 15q21.2); therefore, mutations on chromosome 15 can lead to aromatase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The CRLF1 gene, which encodes cytokine receptor-like factor 1, maps to the short arm of human chromosome 19 (cytogenetic band 19p13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutation of the LDL receptor (LDLR) gene causes familial hypercholesterolaemia, a disorder characterised by markedly elevated LDL cholesterol and a greatly increased risk of premature myocardial infarction. Myocardial infarction can be recognised biochemically by the classic “flipped pattern” of lactate dehydrogenase isoenzymes (LDH-1 > LDH-2). Thus the gene mutation linked to the condition diagnosed with this LDH pattern is in the LDLR gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Acid ceramidase",
    "long_answer": "Farber lipogranulomatosis presents as a granulomatous panniculitis rich in histiocytes. It results from an inherited deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme acid ceramidase (ASAH1), causing ceramide to accumulate in macrophages/histiocytes and produce the characteristic subcutaneous nodules."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hyperammonemia",
    "long_answer": "In urea-cycle disorders the liver cannot convert ammonia to urea, so plasma ammonia rises (hyperammonemia). Persistently high ammonia drives skeletal-muscle proteolysis—muscle tissue converts amino-nitrogen into glutamine to help clear ammonia—so chronic hyperammonemia both characterizes the disorder and leads to progressive muscle loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Severe burns",
    "long_answer": "Acalculous cholecystitis frequently arises in critically ill patients; extensive thermal (severe) burns are a classic precipitating factor because the associated hypovolemia and gallbladder ischemia lead to inflammation without gallstones. Thus, the same severe burn injury that causes cutaneous burns can also precipitate cholecystitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "The cluster of findings—ichthyosis together with intellectual disability, growth retardation (dwarfism), and renal dysfunction—occurs in the X-p22.3 contiguous-gene deletion syndrome; because the causal genes lie on the X chromosome, the condition is transmitted in an X-linked recessive manner, affecting almost exclusively hemizygous males while female carriers are usually asymptomatic or mildly affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FERMT1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FERMT1 gene (also called KIND1) cause Kindler syndrome, an autosomal-recessive disorder characterised by acral, fluid-filled skin blisters in infancy and progressive poikiloderma. Because kindlin-1 is required for integrin-mediated adhesion in intestinal as well as epidermal epithelium, patients frequently develop gastrointestinal involvement such as colitis, strictures or bleeding. Thus, a FERMT1 mutation links the combination of gastrointestinal disease and cutaneous blistering."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Gingival (mucosal) bleeding is a classic manifestation of von Willebrand disease, which results from quantitative or qualitative deficiency of the von Willebrand factor. The von Willebrand factor gene (VWF) is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 12 (12p13.3); mutations in this gene are responsible for the deficiency and the associated bleeding tendency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET gene",
    "long_answer": "Activating germ-line mutations in the RET proto-oncogene cause Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 2B, a syndrome characterized by medullary thyroid carcinoma, pheochromocytoma, mucosal neuromas, and a marfanoid body habitus. RET encodes a receptor tyrosine kinase; constitutive activation drives the development of medullary thyroid carcinoma while also producing the connective-tissue and skeletal features that give the Marfanoid appearance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the MECP2 gene cause Rett syndrome, a well-characterized neurodevelopmental disorder. Affected individuals initially have normal early development, then regress and develop motor impairment, intellectual disability, stereotypic hand movements, and progressive neuromuscular signs. Spasticity and hypertonia are common clinical features of Rett syndrome, directly linking MECP2 mutations to neurodevelopmental disease accompanied by spasticity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOS1",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-dominant hereditary gingival fibromatosis is most often caused by heterozygous mutations in the SOS1 gene (Son-of-Sevenless homolog 1). SOS1 encodes a RAS guanine-nucleotide exchange factor; gain-of-function variants heighten RAS/MAPK signalling in gingival fibroblasts, driving excessive collagen deposition and the characteristic fibromatous overgrowth of the gingiva."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The BRCA1 tumour-suppressor gene, located on human chromosome 17q21, forms functional complexes with the ATM serine/threonine kinase after DNA damage. ATM phosphorylates BRCA1 and the two proteins cooperate in homologous-recombination repair of double-strand DNA breaks. Because the interacting gene (BRCA1) resides on chromosome 17, that is the chromosome containing the gene that works with ATM in double-strand break repair."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Familial isolated vitamin E deficiency (also called ataxia with vitamin E deficiency) results from loss-of-function mutations in the TTPA gene, which encodes the α-tocopherol transfer protein responsible for distributing vitamin E to tissues. The TTPA gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 8q13, meaning it resides on human chromosome 8. Therefore, chromosome 8 contains the gene whose mutation causes this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The majority of multiple epiphyseal dysplasia cases carry pathogenic variants in the COMP (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein) gene. COMP is located at cytogenetic band 19p13.11 on human chromosome 19; therefore, chromosome 19 harbors the gene most frequently mutated in this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Abetalipoproteinemia results from mutations in the microsomal triglyceride transfer protein gene (MTTP), which is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 4 (4q24). Defects in MTTP impair assembly and secretion of apolipoprotein-B–containing lipoproteins, producing the characteristic absence of plasma LDL and chylomicrons seen in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Spondyloenchondrodysplasia is caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the ACP5 gene, which encodes tartrate-resistant acid phosphatase (TRAP). The ACP5 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 19 (19p13), so chromosome 19 harbors the causative gene for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Secondary adrenal insufficiency",
    "long_answer": "In hypopituitarism the pituitary fails to secrete adequate ACTH, so the adrenal cortex is under-stimulated and cortisol production falls. This ACTH-deficiency state produces a form of adrenal failure that clinically resembles Addison’s disease and is therefore termed secondary Addison’s disease, or secondary adrenal insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "EEM syndrome (Ectodermal dysplasia-Ectrodactyly-Macular dystrophy) is produced by pathogenic variants in the CDH3 gene, which encodes P-cadherin. The CDH3 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 16q22.1 on human chromosome 16; therefore, this chromosome harbors the causative gene for EEM syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular arrhythmias",
    "long_answer": "In infants or children whose dextro-Transposition of the great arteries is not corrected, chronic hypoxemia and ventricular pressure overload predispose the myocardium to malignant ventricular arrhythmias such as ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation, which can precipitate sudden cardiac arrest."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DAXX",
    "long_answer": "Pancreatic neuroendocrine tumors commonly harbor inactivating mutations or loss of the DAXX gene. DAXX encodes the death-domain associated protein, first characterized for its binding to the Fas death receptor complex and its ability to modulate apoptotic signaling. Thus, DAXX is both frequently altered in PanNETs and directly involved in apoptosis regulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GIF",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GIF gene, which encodes gastric intrinsic factor, abolish or greatly reduce production of intrinsic factor. Without intrinsic factor the ileum cannot take up the vitamin B12-intrinsic-factor complex, so dietary vitamin B12 is not absorbed. The ensuing cobalamin deficiency blocks the conversion of methylmalonyl-CoA to succinyl-CoA, causing accumulation of methylmalonic acid and clinical methylmalonic acidemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Fatal familial insomnia is a prion disease caused by a point mutation (most commonly D178N coupled with methionine at codon 129) in the PRNP gene, which encodes the prion protein. The PRNP gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 20 (20p13), making chromosome 20 the chromosome that carries the pathogenic mutation responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Filippi syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CKAP2L gene, which is situated on the long arm of chromosome 2 (region 2q)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Hyperkalemia is defined as an elevated serum potassium concentration and is, by definition, an electrolyte imbalance. Potassium plays a critical role in establishing the resting membrane potential of cardiac myocytes. When extracellular potassium becomes markedly elevated, it impairs normal cardiac conduction, producing electrocardiographic changes such as peaked T-waves, PR-interval prolongation, QRS widening, and eventual ‘sine-wave’ morphology. Without prompt treatment, these electrophysiologic disturbances can degenerate into life-threatening ventricular arrhythmias, including ventricular tachycardia and ventricular fibrillation, or progress to asystole. Therefore, hyperkalemia is indeed an electrolyte imbalance capable of precipitating ventricular fibrillation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FGFR2 gene cause Apert syndrome, a postnatally diagnosable craniosynostosis disorder characterized by craniofacial abnormalities and complex syndactyly of the hands and feet. Missense mutations in FGFR2 (e.g., Ser252Trp or Pro253Arg) activate the fibroblast growth-factor receptor 2, leading to premature suture fusion, facial dysmorphology, and fused digits."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HOXD13",
    "long_answer": "Mutations—most commonly poly-alanine expansions—in the HOXD13 homeobox gene disrupt normal anterior–posterior patterning of the limb bud, producing synpolydactyly (webbing plus extra digits). Several Turkish families with this inherited limb malformation have been reported, firmly linking HOXD13 to the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of osteogenesis imperfecta result from mutations in the COL1A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type I collagen. COL1A1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 17 (17q21–q22). Defects in this collagen gene disrupt normal bone matrix formation, producing the brittle-bone phenotype characteristic of osteogenesis imperfecta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Renal agenesis has been linked to loss-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene; RET resides on the long arm of human chromosome 10 (10q11.2). Defective RET signalling blocks ureteric-bud outgrowth, causing failure of kidney formation, which establishes chromosome 10 as the chromosomal location associated with renal agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cauda equina",
    "long_answer": "In adults the spinal cord ends at the L1–L2 vertebral level; the lumbar vertebral canal therefore contains only the lumbosacral nerve-root bundle called the cauda equina. Consequently, paraplegia arising from lesions of the lumbar vertebrae results from compression or injury of the cauda equina rather than the spinal cord itself."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lymph nodes",
    "long_answer": "In bubonic plague, Yersinia pestis bacteria spread via the lymphatic system and replicate in regional lymph nodes, causing them to become markedly swollen and tender; thus, the lymph nodes are the primary site affected."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mussels",
    "long_answer": "Filter-feeding mussels readily bioaccumulate algal neurotoxins such as saxitoxin and domoic acid produced during harmful dinoflagellate or diatom blooms. When these popular delicacy shellfish are eaten by humans, the stored toxins can provoke paralytic or amnesic shellfish poisoning, making mussels a well-known source of shellfish poisoning outbreaks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis",
    "long_answer": "Subacute sclerosing panencephalitis (SSPE) is a rare, progressive, fatal neurologic disease that occurs years after an initial measles infection. It results from persistent, defective measles virus in the brain and most often presents in children, adolescents, or young adults with cognitive decline, myoclonus, and progressive encephalitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "CDG syndrome type 1t is caused by mutations in the PGM1 (phosphoglucomutase-1) gene. PGM1 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (cytogenetic band 1p). Pathogenic variants in this chromosome-1 gene disrupt the interconversion of glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate, reducing the supply of UDP-glucose/UDP-galactose needed for N-glycan biosynthesis and resulting in the CDG-It phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The gene most strongly linked to ankylosing spondylitis is the HLA-B gene, specifically the HLA-B27 allele. HLA-B lies in the major histocompatibility complex (MHC) region on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (locus 6p21.3). Carrying the HLA-B27 variant on chromosome 6 markedly increases the risk of developing ankylosing spondylitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOD1",
    "long_answer": "Mutation of the SOD1 gene (encoding superoxide dismutase-1) is classically linked to familial amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS), a neurodegenerative disorder that produces degeneration of corticospinal (upper motor) neurons as well as lower motor neurons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NTRK1",
    "long_answer": "Congenital insensitivity to pain with anhidrosis (CIPA) is almost invariably caused by loss-of-function mutations in the NTRK1 gene, which encodes the high-affinity nerve growth factor (NGF) receptor TrkA. Defective TrkA signaling disrupts the survival and differentiation of nociceptive sensory neurons and sympathetic neurons controlling sweating, producing absence of pain perception and impaired sweating characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sonic hedgehog (SHH)",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the Sonic hedgehog (SHH) signalling gene disrupts normal rotation and fusion of the ventral pancreatic bud during embryogenesis, producing an annular pancreas. This congenital malformation can encircle and obstruct the second part of the duodenum, leading to duodenal stenosis or complete duodenal atresia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-mannosidosis is caused by pathogenic variants in the MAN2B1 gene, which encodes lysosomal α-mannosidase. The MAN2B1 locus is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 19 (region 19p13.2). Mutations on this chromosome disrupt lysosomal degradation of mannose-rich oligosaccharides, leading to the multisystem disorder that can include psychosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLNA",
    "long_answer": "Missense mutations in the FLNA gene (filamin-A) on chromosome Xq28 cause Melnick-Needles syndrome, an X-linked dominant form of osteochondrodysplasia. Affected individuals show distinctive craniofacial abnormalities such as exophthalmos, micrognathia, and malar hypoplasia, fitting the described inheritance pattern and phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The STOX1 gene, located at 10q22 on human chromosome 10, acts as a transcriptional regulator that increases expression and alternative splicing of FLT1, thereby elevating soluble Flt-1 (sFLT1) levels. Elevated sFLT1 is a key pathogenic factor in pre-eclampsia, so the chromosome carrying the STOX1 gene—and hence linked to sFLT1 upregulation—is chromosome 10."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal dominant nanophthalmos has been linked to mutations in the TMEM98 gene. Cytogenetic mapping places TMEM98 at 17q11.2 on human chromosome 17, making chromosome 17 the locus for the gene responsible for the dominantly inherited form of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "The split hand/split foot malformation type 3 (SHFM3), which results from a tandem duplication of the 10q24 region, is classically transmitted in an autosomal dominant fashion. Affected individuals need only one copy of the duplicated segment to manifest the disorder, although expressivity is variable and penetrance can be reduced."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Duplications involving the short arm (Xp) of the X chromosome—particularly regions such as Xp11.22–Xp11.23—are a recognized cause of X-linked intellectual disability, because they increase dosage of X-linked genes crucial for neurodevelopment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous-gene deletion at 11p13 on chromosome 11 that removes the WT1 gene; loss of WT1 disrupts urogenital development, leading to the characteristic genitourinary anomalies seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Roberts syndrome is caused by biallelic mutations in the ESCO2 gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 8p21.1 on human chromosome 8. Therefore, the gene responsible for Roberts syndrome is located on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Type II focal facial dermal dysplasia has been shown to result from loss-of-function mutations in the TWIST2 gene. TWIST2 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q37). Thus the gene responsible for the disorder resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Allosaurus",
    "long_answer": "Palaeopathological studies of the theropod Allosaurus (notably the specimen MOR 693, “Big Al,” of the species Allosaurus fragilis) revealed pronounced exostosis—abnormal bony overgrowth—on several proximal caudal (tail) vertebrae. This documented pathology demonstrates that the genus Allosaurus includes a species exhibiting exostosis in its tail vertebrae."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the NBN (formerly NBS1) gene, which encodes the DNA-damage–response protein nibrin. The NBN gene is located at cytogenetic band 8q21 on human chromosome 8; therefore the causative gene resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PIEZO2",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous gain-of-function mutations in the mechanosensitive ion-channel gene PIEZO2 cause Distal Arthrogryposis type 3 (Gordon syndrome). This syndrome is characterized clinically by congenital contractures of the hands and feet (a form of arthrogryposis) together with craniofacial anomalies such as cleft palate. The link between PIEZO2 mutations and these specific contracture-cleft palate features is now well established in human genetics literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The disorder ‘leprechaunism’ (Donohue syndrome), named after the mythical Irish leprechaun, is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the insulin receptor gene (INSR). The INSR gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 19 (19p13.2), so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive",
    "long_answer": "Tyrosinemia (types I, II, and III) results from biallelic mutations in the FAH, TAT, or HPD genes, respectively. Because pathogenic variants must be present on both alleles for disease to manifest, the disorder follows an autosomal recessive inheritance pattern."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TCOF1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TCOF1 gene account for the large majority of Treacher-Collins (mandibulofacial dysostosis) cases. This craniofacial syndrome is characterized by hypoplasia of the zygomatic and mandibular bones, external and middle-ear malformations, and consequent conductive hearing loss. Thus, TCOF1 is the gene most frequently linked to a craniofacial syndrome that includes hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Boucher-Neuhäuser syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the PNPLA6 gene (encoding neuropathy target esterase). PNPLA6 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 19 (19p13.2). Mutations in this chromosome-19 gene produce the triad that includes cerebellar ataxia, establishing chromosome 19 as the chromosomal location associated with the ataxia seen in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Al-Zahrawi",
    "long_answer": "The Andalusian physician–surgeon Abu al-Qasim al-Zahrawi (Albucasis) of Córdoba, writing in the 10th–11th century in his surgical compendium ‘Al-Tasrif’, gave the earliest recorded description of children born with fused fingers or toes, identifying syndactyly as a congenital (birth) anomaly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Feingold syndrome is most commonly caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MYCN gene. MYCN is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2p24), so chromosome 2 carries the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Gardner's syndrome is a phenotypic variant of familial adenomatous polyposis that results from germ-line mutations in the APC (adenomatous polyposis coli) tumor-suppressor gene. The human APC gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 at band 5q21-q22; therefore, chromosome 5 carries the gene whose mutation causes Gardner’s syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Spinocerebellar ataxia-14 (SCA14) is caused by pathogenic variants in the PRKCG gene, which encodes the γ-isoform of protein kinase C. In humans, the PRKCG gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 19 (cytogenetic band 19q13.42). Therefore, chromosome 19 is the chromosome that contains the gene associated with SCA14."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ZNHIT3",
    "long_answer": "Finnish-type PEHO syndrome (Progressive Encephalopathy with Edema, Hypsarrhythmia and Optic atrophy) is caused by a founder missense mutation (c.92G>C; p.Arg31Pro) in the ZNHIT3 gene. Loss of normal ZNHIT3 function disrupts neuronal development and leads to early-onset epileptic seizures characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PHYH gene",
    "long_answer": "Classic Refsum disease is most commonly caused by biallelic mutations in the PHYH gene, which encodes phytanoyl-CoA 2-hydroxylase. Loss of this peroxisomal enzyme leads to accumulation of phytanic acid, producing clinical features such as peripheral neuropathy, retinitis pigmentosa, and the cutaneous manifestation ichthyosis, a form of hyperkeratosis. Thus, a PHYH gene mutation is the defect that links Refsum disease to the hyperkeratotic skin condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1973",
    "long_answer": "Cross syndrome (also called Mohr-Cross or orofaciodigital syndrome type IV) was first delineated in 1973, when Cross and co-workers published the original report describing patients with the characteristic constellation of orofacial, digital and systemic anomalies that now define the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Temporal lobe",
    "long_answer": "When a cerebral cavernous malformation forms in the temporal lobe—particularly in medial temporal structures such as the hippocampus—it can compress the surrounding cortical tissue. This compression irritates the seizure-generating networks of the temporal cortex, often producing recurrent, potentially dangerous epileptic seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type II (LAD-II) results from mutations in the SLC35C1 gene, which encodes the Golgi GDP-fucose transporter required for fucosylation of selectin ligands. The SLC35C1 gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 11 (region 11q12-q13), so defects on chromosome 11 give rise to LAD-II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome",
    "long_answer": "The CPS1 gene, whose mutation causes carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase I deficiency, is located on chromosome 2 (band 2q35-q37). Chromosome 2 is one of the numbered autosomes, so the disorder is inherited in an autosomal recessive fashion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hans-Walter Kindler",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the PAX2 gene have been reported in some patients who manifest the ‘morning-glory’ optic-disc anomaly. This congenital optic-nerve malformation was first described in 1970 by the German ophthalmologist Hans-Walter Kindler, who named it after the resemblance of the excavated disc to the morning-glory flower."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LAMP2",
    "long_answer": "Danon disease, an autophagic vacuolar myopathy transmitted in an X-linked dominant fashion, is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the LAMP2 gene. LAMP2 encodes lysosome-associated membrane protein-2; its deficiency blocks normal autophagosome–lysosome fusion, causing accumulation of autophagic vacuoles in skeletal and cardiac muscle. Hemizygous males are severely affected, while heterozygous females manifest variable disease, consistent with X-linked dominant inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "5α-Reductase 2 deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the SRD5A2 gene, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 2 (band 2p23). Mutations in SRD5A2 impair conversion of testosterone to dihydrotestosterone, leading to undervirilization and intersex traits in affected 46,XY individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Trigeminal nerve",
    "long_answer": "Listeria monocytogenes, a Gram-positive bacterium that can lead to fulminant sepsis, neonatal meningitis, and brain-stem encephalitis, gains access to the central nervous system partly by retrograde axonal spread through the trigeminal (fifth cranial) nerve from the oropharyngeal mucosa to the brain-stem. Hence, the cranial nerve specifically associated with this bacterium’s CNS invasion is the trigeminal nerve."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Turner and Noonan syndromes",
    "long_answer": "A broad, web-like neck that stretches laterally to the shoulders (pterygium colli) is a classic dysmorphic feature of both Turner syndrome (45,X or variants) and Noonan syndrome (an autosomal-dominant RAS-MAPK pathway disorder). In each condition the webbed neck arises from prenatal lymphatic maldevelopment (cystic hygroma), making these two syndromes the well-established associations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The HELLS gene (helicase, lymphoid specific; also known as LSH/SMARCA6) is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 10 (band q23.33). Pathogenic variants in HELLS on chromosome 10 cause Immunodeficiency–centromeric instability–facial anomalies (ICF) syndrome type 4, linking this chromosomal location to the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Glucose-6-phosphate translocase",
    "long_answer": "Glycogen storage disease type Ib is produced by deficiency of the microsomal enzyme/transport protein glucose-6-phosphate translocase (encoded by SLC37A4). Loss of this translocase prevents transfer of glucose-6-phosphate into the endoplasmic reticulum for hydrolysis, causing the typical metabolic derangements of GSD I. Unlike type Ia, the translocase defect also impairs neutrophil function and maturation, leading to chronic neutropenia and recurrent infections that characterize this variant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Haemochromatosis type 3 is caused by pathogenic variants in the TFR2 gene (transferrin receptor 2). The TFR2 gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 7q22 on human chromosome 7, making chromosome 7 the chromosomal location associated with this form of hereditary haemochromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Denmark",
    "long_answer": "Epidemiologic studies report that the autosomal-recessive (infantile) form of osteopetrosis occurs in roughly 1 in 140,000–150,000 live births in Denmark, whereas estimates for the United States are on the order of 1 in 200,000–250,000 live births. Because the Danish incidence is numerically greater (i.e., a higher frequency per population), Denmark has the higher rate of osteopetrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Harnaam Kaur",
    "long_answer": "Harnaam Kaur is a British social-media personality and body-positivity activist who revealed that she was diagnosed in childhood with polycystic ovary syndrome (PCOS). PCOS is an endocrine disorder marked by excess androgen production, which frequently causes hirsutism (excess facial and body hair), along with acne, irregular periods and other metabolic or reproductive symptoms. Kaur’s PCOS-related hirsutism is the reason she famously grew and keeps her beard."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IFT80",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the IFT80 gene cause Jeune asphyxiating thoracic dystrophy, a skeletal ciliopathy characterized by a very narrow thorax with short, small ribs. The defective IFT80 protein disrupts intraflagellar transport in chondrocytes, impairing rib and thoracic cage growth and producing the clinically observed small ribcage and restricted chest volume."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Achondrogenesis type II (Langer-Saldino), a perinatally lethal skeletal dysplasia that often results in stillbirth, is produced by dominant mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen essential for cartilage and bone development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RET gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which encodes the receptor for glial cell line-derived neurotrophic factor (GDNF), impair ureteric-bud outgrowth. Heterozygous RET mutations can give unilateral renal agenesis, while homozygous or more severe loss can cause bilateral renal agenesis; the latter produces severe oligohydramnios and the Potter sequence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≈4 SD below normal",
    "long_answer": "Clinical series of girls with the 49,XXXXX (Pentasomy X) karyotype consistently report marked growth deficiency; their stature averages about four standard deviations below the population mean, reflecting pronounced short stature that is characteristic of this aneuploidy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bromocriptine",
    "long_answer": "Prolactin-secreting pituitary adenomas (prolactinomas) are treated first-line with dopamine agonists. Bromocriptine, a D2-receptor agonist, suppresses prolactin synthesis and shrinks the tumor, thereby alleviating hyperprolactinemia and preventing hypopituitarism caused by mass effect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Parkes Weber syndrome is most often produced by pathogenic variants in the RASA1 gene (encoding p120-RasGAP). The RASA1 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 5 (5q13.3), so chromosome 5 is the chromosome that carries the gene whose mutation causes this vascular overgrowth disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Botulinum toxin injections",
    "long_answer": "Injected botulinum toxin type A (commonly known as Botox) is an established therapy for reducing focal muscle spasticity in disorders such as spastic cerebral palsy, which presents with lower-limb stiffness and hypertonia similar to the spasticity seen in hereditary spastic paraplegia. The toxin blocks acetylcholine release at the neuromuscular junction, producing temporary muscle relaxation and improving mobility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CPS1",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive hyperammonemia type I is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the CPS1 gene, which encodes mitochondrial carbamoyl-phosphate synthetase I, the first enzyme of the urea cycle. Deficiency of this enzyme blocks the conversion of ammonia to carbamoyl phosphate, resulting in toxic accumulation of ammonia (hyperammonemia)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The syndrome is produced by pathogenic variants in the MED23 gene. MED23 is mapped to band q23.3 on the long arm of human chromosome 6, so chromosome 6 harbours the gene responsible for severe intellectual disability-progressive spastic diplegia syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "AASS",
    "long_answer": "Hyperlysinemia, the metabolic disorder marked by markedly elevated lysine concentrations in blood, results from loss-of-function mutations in the AASS gene. AASS encodes α-aminoadipic semialdehyde synthase (lysine-ketoglutarate reductase/saccharopine dehydrogenase), the first two catalytic steps of lysine degradation. Mutations that inactivate AASS block lysine catabolism, causing lysine to accumulate systemically and producing the observed biochemical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome results from pathogenic variants in the FGFR2 gene (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2). FGFR2 is located on the long arm of chromosome 10 at band q26; thus, chromosome 10 harbors the gene whose mutation causes Crouzon syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Tricho–dento–osseous syndrome is produced by autosomal-dominant mutations in the DLX3 homeobox gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 17q21-q22. Therefore, the causative gene resides on human chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Pascual-Castroviejo syndrome type 1 (PCSV1) is produced by pathogenic variants in the NR2F1 gene. NR2F1 is mapped to the 5q14.3 region, meaning the responsible gene resides on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Red-cell enolase deficiency in the studied Caucasian families was traced to mutations in the ENO1 (α-enolase) structural gene. Cytogenetic and linkage analyses place ENO1 on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (region 1p36), establishing chromosome 1 as the chromosomal location responsible for this enolase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACTA1",
    "long_answer": "The most lethal variant of nemaline myopathy is caused by mutations in the ACTA1 gene, which encodes skeletal-muscle α-actin. Certain ACTA1 mutations produce a prenatal-onset form with absent fetal movements, profound muscle weakness, and respiratory failure at birth, leading to death in utero or shortly after delivery—rendering the condition essentially incompatible with life."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t(X;18)(p11;q11)",
    "long_answer": "Cardiac synovial sarcoma, like its more common soft-tissue counterpart, is characterized by the balanced chromosomal translocation t(X;18)(p11;q11). This rearrangement fuses the SS18 (SYT) gene on chromosome 18 to one of the SSX genes (usually SSX1 or SSX2) on the X chromosome, creating the pathognomonic SS18-SSX fusion oncogene that drives tumorigenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DKC1, TERT, TERC, RTEL1",
    "long_answer": "Hoyeraal–Hreidarsson syndrome is a severe, telomere-biology disorder and variant of dyskeratosis congenita. Pathogenic germ-line mutations that can be detected by molecular genetic testing have been documented in several telomere-maintenance genes, most commonly the X-linked DKC1 (dyskerin) gene, but also in the telomerase catalytic subunit gene TERT, the telomerase RNA component gene TERC, and the helicase gene RTEL1. These mutations impair telomerase function or telomere elongation, leading to the very short telomeres and multisystem clinical features characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysbetalipoproteinemia is most commonly caused by mutations in the apolipoprotein E (APOE) gene. The human APOE gene is located on chromosome 19 at band q13.32, so chromosome 19 is the chromosome that harbors the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LRP5",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LRP5 gene cause Osteoporosis-Pseudoglioma syndrome, a condition characterized by congenital ocular defects such as microphthalmia (small eyes) and early-onset severe osteoporosis with multiple fractures. The LRP5 protein is a Wnt co-receptor essential for both eye development and regulation of bone mass, so its disruption produces the combined eye–bone phenotype observed in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cyanobacteria",
    "long_answer": "Several species of cyanobacteria (blue-green algae), such as Anabaena, Aphanizomenon and Cylindrospermopsis, biosynthesize the potent neurotoxin saxitoxin. This toxin accumulates in shellfish and, when ingested by humans, blocks voltage-gated sodium channels, producing paralytic shellfish poisoning."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NIPBL",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NIPBL gene are the principal cause of Cornelia de Lange syndrome. This multisystem developmental disorder is classically marked by severe prenatal growth deficiency, pronounced microcephaly, intellectual disability, and characteristic upper-limb malformations (from shortened forearms to absent digits). Thus, a mutation in NIPBL produces the syndrome that presents with both microcephaly and limb anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1",
    "long_answer": "Expansion of a CGG trinucleotide repeat in the FMR1 gene causes Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited form of intellectual disability. Affected individuals typically show mild-to-moderate intellectual disability along with characteristic facial features and (in males) macro-orchidism, firmly linking FMR1 mutation to a syndrome where mild intellectual disability is a core symptom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 21",
    "long_answer": "The RUNX1 (formerly AML1) gene, situated at band q22 on human chromosome 21, is frequently involved in chromosomal translocations such as t(8;21)(q22;q22). These abnormalities create fusion genes (e.g., RUNX1-RUNX1T1) that disrupt normal hematopoietic regulation and are a well-established cause of acute myeloid leukemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoxanthoma elasticum (PXE) is caused by pathogenic variants in the ABCC6 gene. ABCC6 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (region 16p13.1). Loss-of-function mutations in this gene lead to aberrant calcium and phosphate handling, resulting in pathological mineralization of elastic fibers that can be visualized with an electron microscope. Therefore, the responsible gene resides on chromosome 16."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Uncombable Hair Syndrome type 2 (UHS2) is caused by pathogenic variants in the TGM3 gene, which encodes transglutaminase-3. The TGM3 locus is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 20 (20q11.23). Hence, chromosome 20 carries the gene responsible for UHS2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DMD",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DMD gene, which encodes the dystrophin protein, cause Duchenne (and Becker) muscular dystrophy. Dystrophin is the central scaffold of the dystrophin-associated protein complex (DAPC); loss or alteration of dystrophin disrupts the entire complex, leading to the characteristic muscle degeneration seen in the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Occipital lobe",
    "long_answer": "Hemeralopia (day-blindness) can follow traumatic brain injury when the damage involves the occipital lobe, where the primary visual cortex that interprets photopic (cone-mediated) vision is located. Injury to this part of the brain disrupts normal cone pathway processing, leading to difficulty seeing in bright light."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Essential fructosuria is caused by deficiency of hepatic ketohexokinase (fructokinase), encoded by the KHK gene. According to the Human Genome Organization (HUGO) Gene Nomenclature Committee and multiple genomic databases (e.g., NCBI Gene, Ensembl), the KHK gene is located on chromosome 2 at cytogenetic band 2p23.3, not on chromosome 5. Therefore, the statement that chromosome 5 contains the KHK gene linked to essential fructosuria is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS13A",
    "long_answer": "Chorea-acanthocytosis is an autosomal-recessive neurodegenerative disease in which peripheral-blood smears show numerous acanthocytes. The disorder is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS13A gene (also called the chorein gene), which encodes the chorein protein essential for membrane trafficking; its absence leads to the characteristic red-cell spiculated (acanthocytic) morphology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Hermann Werner Siemens",
    "long_answer": "The keratin 2e (KRT2) mutation on chromosome 12 causes superficial epidermolytic ichthyosis, historically called ichthyosis bullosa of Siemens. This disorder was first described in 1937 by the German dermatologist Hermann Werner Siemens, after whom the condition is named."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Peripheral nerve hyperexcitability spectrum",
    "long_answer": "Benign fasciculation syndrome (BFS) and neuromyotonia are now viewed as different points along the same peripheral nerve hyperexcitability spectrum. Both disorders arise from spontaneous, repetitive discharges of motor axons; BFS produces isolated fasciculations without weakness, while neuromyotonia represents the more severe end with continuous muscle fiber activity (myokymia, cramps, stiffness) and can be associated with VGKC-complex antibodies. Thus, BFS is essentially the mildest clinical expression of the same pathophysiological process that, when more intense, manifests as neuromyotonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SOS1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary gingival fibromatosis (HGF) is most often caused by gain-of-function mutations in the SOS1 gene (Son-of-Sevenless homolog 1). Pathogenic variants in SOS1 lead to constitutive activation of Ras/MAPK signaling in gingival fibroblasts, driving the excessive collagen deposition and gingival tissue overgrowth characteristic of this inherited fibromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The COL1A1 gene, located on human chromosome 17q21.33, encodes the α1 chain of type I collagen. Pathogenic variants in COL1A1 are the classic cause of osteogenesis imperfecta and have also been reported in families displaying an atypical, marfanoid phenotype that mimics features of Marfan syndrome. Thus, the chromosome harbouring the gene implicated in both osteogenesis imperfecta and atypical Marfan presentations is chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Phosphoglucomutase 1",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of phosphoglucomutase 1 (PGM1) causes a condition now classified both as glycogen-storage disease type XIV and as a congenital disorder of glycosylation (PGM1-CDG). PGM1 catalyzes the interconversion of glucose-1-phosphate and glucose-6-phosphate, a key step in glycogenolysis; the same reaction provides activated sugar precursors (UDP-glucose/galactose) required for protein N- and O-glycosylation. Loss of PGM1 therefore disrupts both glycogen breakdown and glycoprotein synthesis, accounting for the combined clinical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL6A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the COL6A1 gene (encoding the α1-chain of type VI collagen) cause Bethlem myopathy. This condition often presents with finger and ankle contractures at birth; the contractures may loosen during infancy but characteristically return and progress in later childhood or adulthood. The COL6A1 defect disrupts collagen VI microfibrils in muscle and connective tissue, explaining the relapsing pattern of joint contractures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 14",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-1 antitrypsin deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the SERPINA1 gene. SERPINA1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 14 (14q32.13), so chromosome 14 carries the gene whose mutations cause this hereditary disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "The multi-system (autosomal-recessive) form of type I pseudohypoaldosteronism, designated PHA1B, results from loss-of-function mutations in the SCNN1A gene that encodes the α-subunit of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). SCNN1A is located on human chromosome 12 at band 12p13, so chromosome 12 carries the gene responsible for the multi-system form of PHA type I."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Familial amyloid polyneuropathy is most commonly caused by pathogenic variants in the transthyretin (TTR) gene. The human TTR gene is located on chromosome 18 (band 18q12.1), so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 18."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Cardiospondylocarpofacial syndrome is caused by heterozygous pathogenic variants in the MAP3K7 gene (encoding the kinase TAK1). The MAP3K7 locus is situated at cytogenetic band 6q15 on human chromosome 6; therefore, chromosome 6 contains the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYO5A",
    "long_answer": "Griscelli syndrome type 1 is an autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by moderate dilution of skin and hair pigmentation together with severe central nervous system dysfunction (developmental delay, seizures, neurodegeneration). The disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the MYO5A gene, which encodes the motor protein myosin Va that is essential for melanosome transport in melanocytes and vesicle transport in neurons. Dysfunction of MYO5A therefore produces both the hypopigmentation and the profound neurologic phenotype seen in this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "DMD",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the DMD gene, which encodes the muscle-stabilizing protein dystrophin, lead to Duchenne muscular dystrophy. This X-linked disorder causes progressive skeletal-muscle degeneration; affected boys usually lose independent ambulation by about 10–12 years of age and thereafter rely on a wheelchair for mobility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HNF1A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the HNF1A gene (hepatocyte nuclear factor-1α) cause maturity-onset diabetes of the young type 3 (MODY 3). This autosomal-dominant disorder impairs transcriptional regulation required for normal pancreatic β-cell function, leading to inadequate insulin production and early-onset hyperglycaemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FOXC2",
    "long_answer": "The hereditary condition characterized by a double row of eyelashes (distichiasis) and limb swelling (lymphedema) is lymphedema–distichiasis syndrome. This autosomal-dominant disorder is caused by pathogenic variants in the FOXC2 gene, a forkhead family transcription factor crucial for lymphatic and eyelid follicle development. Mutations in FOXC2 disrupt lymphatic valve formation, leading to peripheral lymphedema, and alter eyelid follicle patterning, producing the extra row of lashes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Cystic fibrosis is caused by mutations in the CFTR (cystic fibrosis transmembrane conductance regulator) gene. The CFTR gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 7 at position q31.2—not on chromosome 22. This chromosomal location has been firmly established since its discovery in 1989 and is consistently reported in genetic and medical literature. Therefore, the statement that the gene responsible for cystic fibrosis is on chromosome 22 is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TNFRSF1A",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the TNFRSF1A gene cause TNF-receptor-associated periodic syndrome (TRAPS), an autosomal-dominant autoinflammatory disorder marked by recurrent fevers, rash, serositis and musculoskeletal pain. Inflammatory attacks in TRAPS commonly improve with systemic corticosteroids and symptomatic NSAIDs, making TNFRSF1A mutations the canonical genetic defect linked to a condition treated by these agents."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAX6 gene cause congenital aniridia, a developmental eye disorder characterized by partial or complete absence of the iris, foveal hypoplasia, and other structural defects that lead to reduced visual acuity. Thus, a PAX6 mutation is directly linked to a condition that alters the eye’s structure and impairs vision."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitochondrial malate dehydrogenase",
    "long_answer": "In healthy cells, the mitochondrial isoform of malate dehydrogenase (MDH2) occasionally ‘moonlights’: instead of catalysing the normal malate ↔ oxaloacetate reaction, it promiscuously reduces α-ketoglutarate to the on-comer metabolite L-2-hydroxyglutarate (L-2-HG). Under normal circumstances L-2-HG is rapidly reconverted to α-ketoglutarate by L-2-hydroxyglutarate dehydrogenase (L2HGDH). When L2HGDH is defective, as in L-2-Hydroxyglutaric aciduria, the continual side-production of L-2-HG by mitochondrial malate dehydrogenase leads to its pathological accumulation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Around 90 % of Treacher Collins syndrome cases are due to pathogenic variants in the TCOF1 gene, which is situated on the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q32-q33)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PFKM gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PFKM gene, which encodes the muscle isoform of the glycolytic enzyme phosphofructokinase, cause glycogen storage disease type VII (Tarui disease). Loss of phosphofructokinase activity blocks a key glycolytic step, so glucose released from glycogen cannot be effectively broken down in skeletal muscle cells, leading to exercise-induced muscle cramps and other symptoms characteristic of this glycogen storage disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Deliver blood for gas exchange",
    "long_answer": "Pulmonary hypertension damages and remodels the pulmonary arteries and arterioles. These vessels’ chief role is to convey de-oxygenated blood pumped from the right ventricle to the lung capillary beds, where carbon dioxide is exchanged for oxygen; in other words, they deliver blood to the alveoli for gas exchange."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FGFR2 gene (fibroblast growth factor receptor 2) cause Apert syndrome, an acrocephalosyndactyly condition in which patients characteristically present with severe syndactyly of the hands and feet along with craniosynostosis. Thus, an FGFR2 mutation is classically associated with a condition that includes syndactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPATA7",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SPATA7 gene, which lies on chromosome 14q31.3, cause autosomal-recessive retinal dystrophies such as Leber congenital amaurosis type 3 and early-onset retinitis pigmentosa. These disorders lead to severe visual impairment from infancy or childhood, establishing SPATA7 as the chromosome-14 gene whose mutation produces vision-loss disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Coagulation factor V is the closest human homologue of coagulation factor VIII; its gene (F5) resides on the long arm of chromosome 1 at 1q23. Thus, chromosome 1 contains the gene encoding the protein homologous to factor VIII."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKD1",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the PKD1 gene, which encodes polycystin-1, is the commonest cause of Autosomal Dominant Polycystic Kidney Disease (ADPKD). While ADPKD is best known for renal cysts, up to ~10–15 % of affected individuals also develop intracranial arachnoid (brain) cysts and other cerebrovascular complications. Thus, a PKD1 mutation produces the disease beginning with 'A' (ADPKD) that can lead to cyst formation on the brain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Homozygous",
    "long_answer": "Kowarski syndrome is an autosomal-recessive form of isolated growth hormone deficiency (IGHD type IB). It is produced by biallelic mutations in the GH1 gene; affected individuals carry the same pathogenic variant on both alleles (homozygosity or compound heterozygosity). Thus, the disease-causing GH1 mutation is associated with a homozygous state."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Molecular confirmation of Rosai–Dorfman disease (especially the familial/H-syndrome variant) is obtained by sequencing the SLC29A3 gene; this gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 10 (10q23)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IKBKAP (ELP1)",
    "long_answer": "The autonomic-nervous-system disorder treated at Sheba Medical Center is Familial Dysautonomia; it is caused by a loss-of-function mutation in the IKBKAP gene (also named ELP1), most commonly the 2507+6 T>C splice-site mutation that reduces IKAP/ELP1 protein and impairs development of autonomic and sensory neurons."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ATP6V0A2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in ATP6V0A2, encoding the a2-subunit of the vacuolar H⁺-ATPase, cause autosomal-recessive cutis laxa type II—a connective-tissue disorder that clinically overlaps with wrinkly skin syndrome. Defective ATP6V0A2 impairs Golgi acidification, leading to abnormal protein glycosylation and the characteristic loose, wrinkled skin and other connective-tissue manifestations shared by both conditions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Anchoring fibrils that secure the basement membrane to underlying dermis are composed of type VII collagen. The gene encoding type VII collagen is COL7A1, which is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 3 (region 3p21.3). Hence, the chromosome harboring the gene responsible for anchoring fibrils is chromosome 3."
}

{
    "short_answer": "3p25.1",
    "long_answer": "Marked taurine loss in urine (taurinuria) is produced by mutations in the taurine transporter gene SLC6A6. This gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 3, band p25.1, so the disorder is mapped to chromosomal locus 3p25.1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations or homozygous deletions in the SMN1 (survival motor neuron 1) gene on chromosome 5q13 cause spinal muscular atrophy (SMA), a neuromuscular disorder in which degeneration of anterior-horn motor neurons leads to severe, progressive muscle weakness and atrophy that is particularly prominent in the proximal muscles of the legs."
}

{
    "short_answer": "mTOR signaling pathway",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent form of focal cortical dysplasia (especially type II) is produced by germ-line or somatic mutations in genes such as MTOR, PIK3CA, AKT3, TSC1/2, and DEPDC5. All of these genes converge on and dysregulate the mammalian target of rapamycin (mTOR) signaling cascade, leading to hyperactive mTOR activity that perturbs neuronal growth and cortical lamination. Hence, the disease-causing mutations primarily affect the mTOR signaling pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLA-B27",
    "long_answer": "Reactive arthritis shows a strong genetic association with the HLA-B27 class I major histocompatibility complex gene. Up to 70–80 % of patients carry the HLA-B27 allele, and its presence greatly increases susceptibility to this seronegative spondyloarthropathy, highlighting its central role in disease pathogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "The first and principal gene identified for Hermansky–Pudlak syndrome is HPS1, which was mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 10 (region 10q23). Mutations in this chromosome-10 gene disrupt formation of lysosome-related organelles, producing the oculocutaneous albinism, bleeding diathesis and pulmonary fibrosis characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SMN1 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Homozygous deletion or other loss-of-function mutations in the SMN1 gene eliminate Survival Motor Neuron protein, producing spinal muscular atrophy (SMA). SMA is an autosomal-recessive neuromuscular disorder that typically presents in early infancy with hypotonia and progressive weakness and is now detectable by newborn screening. Thus, an SMN1 mutation is the causative genetic defect diagnosed in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMO3",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FMO3 gene impair the hepatic enzyme flavin-containing mono-oxygenase 3. The resulting enzymatic deficiency prevents conversion of trimethylamine to its odorless N-oxide, causing trimethylaminuria (‘fish-odor syndrome’) characterized by markedly increased body odor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Osteoprotegerin",
    "long_answer": "Osteoprotegerin (OPG, TNFRSF11B) is a soluble member of the TNF receptor superfamily that regulates bone remodeling by acting as a decoy receptor for RANKL. Its C-terminal region contains two death-domain-like motifs; these homologous ‘death domains’ mediate disulfide-linked homodimer formation of OPG, a structural feature essential for its high-affinity binding to RANKL and inhibition of osteoclast differentiation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BTD",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the BTD gene (encoding biotinidase) cause biotinidase deficiency. This inherited disorder impairs recycling of the vitamin biotin, leading to neurological features such as seizures and hypotonia. Because the underlying problem is biotin shortage, lifelong oral biotin supplementation effectively treats the condition and prevents further neurologic decline."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the LDLR (low-density lipoprotein receptor) gene cause familial hypercholesterolaemia. The defective receptor leads to markedly elevated LDL-cholesterol, rapid atherosclerotic plaque development in coronary arteries, and therefore a greatly increased risk of premature myocardial infarction."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The combined disorder of infantile convulsions and paroxysmal kinesigenic choreoathetosis (ICCA syndrome) maps to a locus on chromosome 16 (16p11–q12); mutations in the PRRT2 gene within this region are responsible for the phenotype, establishing chromosome 16 as the chromosomal site associated with the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "WAGR syndrome results from a contiguous-gene deletion in the 11p13 region of chromosome 11, removing genes such as WT1 and PAX6. Loss of these genes produces the characteristic features of Wilms tumor, aniridia, genitourinary anomalies, and intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary angio-oedema, an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by mutations in the SERPING1 (C1 inhibitor) gene on chromosome 11, is well known to have attacks precipitated by hormonal changes such as those that occur at ovulation. Thus, the chromosome associated with this ovulation-triggered disorder is chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Schmitt–Gillenwater–Kelly (hand-foot-genital) syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the HOXA13 gene. HOXA13 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 7, band 7p15.2; alterations here lead to the characteristic limb abnormalities and urogenital defects such as hypospadias."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cortisol",
    "long_answer": "Marked hypersecretion of cortisol, as occurs in Cushing syndrome, can saturate and overwhelm renal 11β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 2 (the enzyme that normally inactivates cortisol to cortisone). The unmetabolized cortisol then binds and activates mineralocorticoid receptors, producing low-renin hypertension, hypokalaemia and metabolic alkalosis with suppressed aldosterone—clinical findings that closely mimic apparent mineralocorticoid excess syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked dominant",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PHEX gene, which is located on the X chromosome (Xp22.1), lead to X-linked hypophosphatemic rickets/osteomalacia. Because only one altered copy of the gene on the X chromosome is sufficient to produce the phenotype in both males and females, the disorder follows an X-linked dominant pattern of inheritance (affected fathers transmit the mutation to all daughters and no sons; affected mothers have a 50% risk of passing it to each child)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Adenosine monophosphate deaminase deficiency type 1 results from pathogenic variants in the AMPD1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle isoform of AMP deaminase. Cytogenetic mapping places AMPD1 at 1p13.2, establishing that the responsible gene resides on human chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDHA mutation",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary lactate dehydrogenase-A (LDH-A) deficiency—also called glycogen storage disease type XI—is caused by pathogenic mutations in the LDHA gene. A characteristic diagnostic test is a muscle biopsy with histochemical staining showing markedly reduced or absent lactate dehydrogenase (LDH) activity in muscle fibers, confirming the enzyme deficiency due to the LDHA mutation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The classic gene first shown to cause Walker-Warburg syndrome is POMT1 (protein-O-mannosyltransferase 1), which is mapped to cytogenetic band 9q34 on human chromosome 9. Mutations in this chromosome-9 gene disrupt α-dystroglycan glycosylation, producing the characteristic brain, eye, and muscular defects of Walker-Warburg syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Asymptomatic",
    "long_answer": "A condition that produces no noticeable signs or symptoms is described as “asymptomatic.” Growth-hormone deficiency can remain asymptomatic for long periods, with affected individuals showing no outward clinical manifestations despite underlying hormonal insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The SDHAF1 gene, whose product is essential for the assembly of succinate dehydrogenase (mitochondrial complex II), is mutated in cases of nuclear-encoded mitochondrial complex II deficiency. SDHAF1 is situated on the long arm of chromosome 19 (19q13.12), making chromosome 19 the correct chromosomal location."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRT17 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Steatocystoma multiplex presents as multiple subcutaneous cyst-like nodules. It is commonly linked to pathogenic variants in the keratin 17 gene (KRT17). Histopathology confirms the diagnosis by showing a thin squamous epithelium lacking a granular layer with adjacent sebaceous lobules, and definitive management options include total surgical excision or liposuction of the cysts. Therefore, the KRT17 mutation is the gene alteration frequently associated with this condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Chudley–Mccullough syndrome results from recessive mutations in the GPSM2 gene (G-protein signaling modulator 2). The GPSM2 locus is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 1, at band p13 (1p13.3). Thus, the gene associated with this syndrome is located on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOD2 (CARD15)",
    "long_answer": "Granulomatous dermatitis seen in Blau syndrome is produced by gain-of-function mutations in the NOD2 (also called CARD15) gene, which encodes an intracellular pattern-recognition receptor. Constitutive activation of NOD2 drives NF-κB–mediated inflammatory pathways, leading to non-caseating granulomatous inflammation in skin, joints and eyes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NOD2 (CARD15) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Crohn’s disease is a chronic, but benign, inflammatory bowel disorder whose transmural inflammation and subsequent fibrosis and scarring can form mass-like strictures that are easily mistaken for intestinal carcinoma. The condition shows a strong genetic association with loss-of-function mutations in the NOD2 (also called CARD15) gene, which encodes an intracellular pattern-recognition receptor important for bacterial sensing in innate immunity."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ALDH4A1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the ALDH4A1 gene, which encodes Δ¹-pyrroline-5-carboxylate dehydrogenase (P5CDH), block the second step of proline catabolism. This defect causes hyperprolinemia type II, a congenital amino-acid–metabolism disorder characterized by markedly elevated plasma proline concentrations and accumulation of the intermediate P5C. Clinical manifestations often include seizures and variable intellectual disability, linking ALDH4A1 mutations directly to the described phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNJ5",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the KCNJ5 gene, which encodes the inward-rectifier potassium channel GIRK4, cause familial hyperaldosteronism type III. These mutations depolarize adrenal zona glomerulosa cells, drive excess aldosterone secretion, and clinically produce hyperaldosteronism with hypertension and hypokalemia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Nijmegen breakage syndrome is produced by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the NBN (NBS1) gene, which encodes the DNA-repair protein nibrin. Cytogenetic and physical mapping place the NBN gene at band 8q21.3, meaning it resides on human chromosome 8. Therefore, chromosome 8 carries the gene responsible for this chromosomal-instability disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Papillorenal (renal-coloboma) syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the PAX2 gene. PAX2 is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 10 (10q24-q25), so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 10."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Lithium therapy",
    "long_answer": "Chronic lithium therapy, used to treat mood disorders in the central nervous system, is a well-recognized risk factor for primary hyperparathyroidism because lithium alters the set-point of the calcium-sensing receptor, leading to parathyroid hyperplasia and excess parathyroid hormone secretion."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The nuclear gene most frequently implicated in Leigh syndrome is SURF1, which encodes a protein essential for cytochrome-c-oxidase (complex IV) assembly. SURF1 is located at cytogenetic band 9q34 on human chromosome 9; pathogenic variants in this locus account for the largest proportion of nuclear-encoded causes of Leigh syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "RYR1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the RYR1 gene, which encodes the skeletal-muscle ryanodine-receptor calcium-release channel, are a recognised cause of some forms of arthrogryposis multiplex congenita. The same mutations disrupt excitation–contraction coupling in skeletal muscle, producing RYR1-related congenital myopathies such as central-core or multiminicore disease, in which muscle fibres cannot contract normally and therefore do not function properly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FBN1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FBN1 gene, which encodes the extracellular matrix protein fibrillin-1, cause Marfan syndrome. This connective-tissue disorder commonly leads to spinal deformities, including scoliosis and thoracolumbar kyphosis, because weakened ligaments and supporting tissues allow abnormal curvature of the spine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Costello syndrome is caused by activating mutations in the HRAS proto-oncogene. HRAS is located at cytogenetic band 11p15.5 on human chromosome 11; therefore, chromosome 11 harbors the gene responsible for Costello syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BTD gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the BTD gene, which encodes the enzyme biotinidase, cause biotinidase deficiency. Without functional biotinidase, free biotin cannot be released and recycled, leading to markedly reduced activity of all biotin-dependent carboxylases. Oral biotin supplementation bypasses the recycling defect and restores enzyme function, making the disorder effectively treatable with biotin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Great Ormond Street Hospital",
    "long_answer": "Investigators at Great Ormond Street Hospital for Sick Children (London) examined myocardial specimens from infants with endocardial fibro-elastosis and demonstrated enteroviral (notably coxsackie-B) particles and high antiviral titres, thereby reinforcing that intra-uterine or early post-natal viral infection is a principal cause of endocardial fibroelastosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral regurgitation",
    "long_answer": "In mitral valve prolapse, the redundant or myxomatous valve leaflets and elongated chordae can prevent proper coaptation during systole, allowing blood to flow backward from the left ventricle into the left atrium. This complication—mitral regurgitation—is common and, when moderate to severe, increases ventricular volume load, progressive left-ventricular dilation, and ultimately heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ABCB4 gene, which encodes the canalicular phosphatidylcholine floppase MDR3, cause progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 3 (PFIC-3). PFIC-3 usually becomes clinically apparent in early childhood, when impaired phosphatidylcholine secretion renders bile acids toxic to the biliary epithelium, leading to cholestasis, high GGT, and progressive liver fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Smith–Lemli–Opitz syndrome results from mutations in the DHCR7 gene (encoding 7-dehydrocholesterol reductase). The DHCR7 gene is located on human chromosome 11 (band 11q13.4), so chromosome 11 carries the causative gene for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FLNA (Filamin A)",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked FLNA gene, which encodes the actin-binding cytoskeletal protein filamin A, cause an inherited form of chronic intestinal pseudo-obstruction. Mutations disrupt cytoskeletal organization in intestinal smooth muscle and enteric neurons, leading to severe, obstructive-type motility failure without mechanical blockage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "United Kingdom",
    "long_answer": "Collins–Pope syndrome derives its name from the British clinicians Collins and Pope, who published the initial description of the syndrome in a report on an affected family. Because that first report originated from their work in Britain, the syndrome was first described in the United Kingdom."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Perinatal asphyxia",
    "long_answer": "Congenital (transient) hyperinsulinism can be triggered by perinatal asphyxia—oxygen deprivation during labor or delivery. Hypoxic-ischemic stress damages pancreatic β-cell regulation, provoking excessive insulin secretion and resulting neonatal hypoglycaemia characteristic of congenital hyperinsulinism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KAT6B",
    "long_answer": "Young-Simpson syndrome (Ohdo syndrome, Young-Simpson type) is consistently linked to heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KAT6B gene, which encodes lysine acetyltransferase 6B. Disruption of KAT6B alters histone acetylation and downstream gene expression, producing the characteristic craniofacial anomalies, intellectual disability, and congenital malformations that define this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "The constellation of aplasia cutis congenita together with intestinal lymphangiectasia-induced generalized edema typifies Hennekam lymphangiectasia–lymphedema syndrome type 1. This syndrome is caused by loss-of-function variants in the CCBE1 gene, which is located at cytogenetic band 18q21.32; hence, chromosome 18 is the chromosome associated with this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Adenosine deaminase (ADA) deficiency is an autosomal-recessive form of severe combined immunodeficiency (SCID). The causative ADA gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 20 (locus 20q13.12). Mutations in this chromosome-20 gene impair ADA enzyme activity, leading to toxic accumulation of deoxyadenosine and resulting immunodeficiency in affected infants."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VPS33B",
    "long_answer": "Arthrogryposis–renal dysfunction–cholestasis (ARC) syndrome is an autosomal-recessive genodermatosis caused by loss-of-function mutations in the VPS33B gene, which encodes a protein essential for intracellular vesicle trafficking; defective VPS33B disrupts epithelial cell polarity and leads to the characteristic multisystem features of ARC."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frameshift mutation",
    "long_answer": "Congenital dysfibrinogenemia often results from small insertions or deletions in one of the fibrinogen genes (FGA, FGB, or FGG). When these indels are not in multiples of three nucleotides, they shift the codon reading frame— a classic frameshift mutation— producing an aberrant fibrinogen polypeptide that impairs clot formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OAT",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the OAT gene, which encodes the mitochondrial enzyme ornithine aminotransferase, abolish normal ornithine catabolism. The resulting enzyme deficiency causes hyper-ornithinemia (gyrate atrophy), a disorder characterized by large systemic accumulation of ornithine in blood and tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NHS gene",
    "long_answer": "Nance-Horan syndrome presents with dense congenital nuclear cataracts accompanied by characteristic dental abnormalities such as screwdriver-shaped incisors and supernumerary teeth. This X-linked disorder is consistently produced by loss-of-function mutations in the NHS gene, which encodes the Nance-Horan syndrome protein involved in eye and tooth development. Identification of pathogenic variants in NHS confirms the molecular basis of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tracheoesophageal septum",
    "long_answer": "During normal foregut development, paired tracheoesophageal folds fuse in the midline to form the tracheoesophageal septum, separating the ventral laryngotracheal tube from the dorsal esophagus. If this septum forms incorrectly or deviates, the trachea and esophagus are not completely separated, producing a tracheoesophageal fistula and often leaving the proximal esophagus blind-ending (esophageal atresia). Thus, maldevelopment of the tracheoesophageal septum is the embryological defect underlying these anomalies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Haemochromatosis type 3 is caused by pathogenic variants in the TFR2 gene (transferrin receptor 2). The TFR2 gene is mapped to human chromosome 7q22, so the disease-causing gene for haemochromatosis type 3 resides on chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Fukuyama congenital muscular dystrophy is caused by mutations in the FKTN (fukutin) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 9 (region 9q31). This chromosomal location underlies the defective glycosylation of α-dystroglycan that impairs muscle integrity and brain development in the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "<1 per 1,000,000",
    "long_answer": "Cryptorchidism-arachnodactyly-intellectual disability syndrome is classified by Orphanet as an ultra-rare disorder, with an estimated prevalence of fewer than 1 affected individual per 1,000,000 people; only a handful of cases have been reported in the medical literature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Jansen’s metaphyseal chondrodysplasia is produced by activating mutations in the parathyroid hormone 1 receptor gene (PTH1R). The human PTH1R gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 3 (region 3p21-p22). Therefore, chromosome 3 carries the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase deficiency, chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren–Larsson syndrome results from deficiency of the enzyme fatty aldehyde dehydrogenase (FALDH), encoded by the ALDH3A2 gene. This gene is located on chromosome 17p11.2, so mutations on chromosome 17 that disrupt FALDH activity underlie the clinical features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aortic stenosis",
    "long_answer": "Severe aortic stenosis is a frequent structural heart disease found in patients who suffer sudden cardiac arrest. The high-shear turbulence across the narrowed valve destroys large von Willebrand factor multimers, producing an acquired vWF deficiency that predisposes to bleeding from intestinal angiodysplasia (Heyde syndrome). Consequently, the same underlying aortic stenosis that can lead to cardiac arrest can also manifest as gastrointestinal bleeding."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Early-onset parkinsonism-intellectual disability syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the RAB39B gene, which is mapped to band Xq28. Because RAB39B lies on the X chromosome, this chromosome carries the gene responsible for the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCB4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ABCB4 gene (encoding the canalicular phosphatidylcholine floppase MDR3) cause progressive familial intrahepatic cholestasis type 3. Because little or no phosphatidylcholine is secreted into bile, the bile becomes detergent-rich, injuring the biliary epithelium and producing chronic cholangitis—an inflammatory condition of the bile ducts."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "The F10 gene, located at 13q34 on human chromosome 13, encodes coagulation factor X. In the extrinsic clotting pathway, the tissue-factor–factor VIIa complex proteolytically activates factor X to factor Xa, initiating downstream thrombin generation. Hence, the chromosome carrying the gene for the protein activated by factor VIIa is chromosome 13."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Foreign accent syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Foreign accent syndrome is a rare speech disorder in which changes in prosody, timing and articulation after brain injury or other neurological insult make a person’s speech sound as though it carries a foreign accent. Although sometimes misclassified as a psychiatric issue, it is actually a well-documented neurogenic condition that alters the motor planning of speech, leading listeners to perceive a new accent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Boils (furuncles) are deep skin infections of hair follicles most often caused by Staphylococcus aureus. Because these lesions are localized to the skin and can require incision-and-drainage, culture, and targeted antibiotic therapy, the specialist who routinely diagnoses and manages such cutaneous infections is a dermatologist."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Paroxysmal nocturnal hemoglobinuria arises from an acquired somatic mutation in the PIGA (phosphatidylinositol glycan class A) gene. PIGA is located on the X chromosome (region Xp22), so this chromosome carries the gene whose mutation causes the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "≤15 %",
    "long_answer": "In fetuses with congenital pulmonary airway malformation (CPAM), the development of hydrops fetalis signals severe intrathoracic mass effect and cardiovascular compromise. Published perinatal outcome series show that once hydrops is present, approximately 85–100 % of affected fetuses die, leaving a survival rate of no more than about 15 % despite supportive care. This contrasts sharply with the >90 % survival seen when hydrops is absent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EPM2A",
    "long_answer": "Lafora progressive myoclonus epilepsy (Lafora disease) is classically caused by loss-of-function mutations in the EPM2A gene, which encodes the dual-specificity phosphatase laforin. Absence of functional laforin impairs glycogen dephosphorylation, leading to intracellular accumulation of insoluble polyglucosan inclusions (Lafora bodies) that trigger the characteristic myoclonic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of medullary thyroid cancer are driven by activating mutations in the RET proto-oncogene, which is mapped to chromosome 10 (band 10q11.2). Germline RET mutations underlie MEN2-associated medullary thyroid carcinoma, and somatic RET mutations are common in sporadic cases, firmly linking chromosome 10 to the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ralph Douglas Kenneth Reye",
    "long_answer": "Australian paediatric pathologist Ralph Douglas Kenneth (R. D. K.) Reye published the first description of a congenital myopathy marked by rod-shaped (\"nemaline\") inclusions within skeletal-muscle fibres in the early 1960s. This distinctive histopathologic finding led to recognition of the condition that was subsequently named nemaline myopathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The majority of malignant hyperthermia cases are linked to pathogenic variants in the RYR1 gene (encoding the skeletal-muscle ryanodine receptor). RYR1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 19 (19q13.2), making chromosome 19 the chromosome most commonly associated with malignant hyperthermia susceptibility."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Absence of 50–60 Hz flicker",
    "long_answer": "Old analog CRT televisions refreshed the whole screen sequentially at 50–60 Hz, producing a large-area flicker in the most seizure-provoking frequency range for photosensitive epilepsy. Modern digital LCD/LED sets keep each pixel continuously lit and refresh at much higher effective rates (100 Hz+), eliminating the low-frequency, high-contrast flicker that triggers cortical hyper-synchronization. Without that 50–60 Hz raster flicker, digital TVs are far less likely to induce seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sjögren's syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Sjögren's syndrome is an autoimmune exocrinopathy in which lymphocytic infiltration damages moisture-producing glands throughout the body. Besides the classic dry eyes and dry mouth, it often causes vaginal mucosal dryness due to reduced secretions from Bartholin’s and other glands. Persistent vaginal dryness can lead to irritation and neuropathic pain of the vulvar tissues, precipitating or exacerbating vulvodynia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FMR1",
    "long_answer": "The FMR1 gene, located on the X chromosome at Xq27.3, is mutated in Fragile X syndrome, the most common inherited cause of intellectual disability. Expansion of CGG trinucleotide repeats in FMR1 silences the gene, leading to loss of the FMRP protein and consequent cognitive impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes X and 17",
    "long_answer": "Alveolar soft part sarcoma is characterized by the recurrent translocation t(X;17)(p11;q25), which fuses the ASPSCR1 (formerly ASPL) gene on chromosome 17q25 to the TFE3 gene on chromosome Xp11.2. The resulting ASPSCR1-TFE3 fusion protein acts as an aberrant transcription factor that drives tumor development, making chromosomes X and 17 the two chromosomes involved in this oncogenic rearrangement."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Intravenous administration",
    "long_answer": "Anthracyclines such as doxorubicin are given by intravenous (IV) injection/infusion. If the IV line infiltrates and the drug extravasates into surrounding tissues, these agents act as potent vesicants, producing severe local inflammation and tissue necrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ixodes",
    "long_answer": "Borrelia burgdorferi-sensu-lato spirochetes, the causative agents of Lyme disease, maintain their enzootic cycle primarily in hard ticks of the genus Ixodes. Species such as Ixodes scapularis in North America and Ixodes ricinus in Europe acquire the bacteria from infected vertebrate hosts and transmit them during subsequent blood meals, making Ixodes ticks the principal reservoir and vector for Lyme disease pathogens."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Maternal serum screening for abdominal wall defects (e.g., gastroschisis, omphalocele) measures the fetal protein alpha-fetoprotein (AFP). The AFP gene is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 4 (cytogenetic band 4q13.3), so chromosome 4 contains the gene encoding the screening protein."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GLYCTK",
    "long_answer": "Glycerate kinase catalyzes the phosphorylation of D-glyceric acid to 2-phosphoglycerate, and this enzyme is encoded by the GLYCTK gene. Pathogenic mutations in GLYCTK abolish or severely reduce glycerate kinase activity, leading to D-glyceric acid accumulation (D-glyceric aciduria) and defining the enzyme deficiency described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ABCC6",
    "long_answer": "Mineralization and fragmentation of elastic fibers in skin, retina, and arterial walls is the hallmark of the inherited disorder pseudoxanthoma elasticum (PXE). PXE is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the ABCC6 gene, which encodes the ATP-binding cassette transporter C6; defective ABCC6 reduces systemic pyrophosphate levels, leading to inappropriate calcification of elastic tissues."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FSHR mutation",
    "long_answer": "Inactivating mutations in the follicle-stimulating hormone receptor (FSHR) gene cause isolated FSH resistance. Only Sertoli cells express FSH receptors, so loss-of-function mutations selectively impair Sertoli-cell activity and spermatogenesis, while Leydig cells, which depend on LH and not FSH, remain unaffected and continue to produce normal testosterone."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPTLC1",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary sensory and autonomic neuropathy type IA (HSAN IA) is caused by mutations in the SPTLC1 gene. SPTLC1 encodes the long-chain-base subunit 1 of serine palmitoyltransferase, the enzyme that forms the catalytic domain responsible for the first, rate-limiting step of sphingolipid biosynthesis. Dysfunction of this sphingolipid-biosynthetic domain underlies the neuropathy seen in HSAN IA."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Familial sea-blue histiocytosis is caused by missense variants in the apolipoprotein E (APOE) gene. APOE is mapped to 19q13.32 on human chromosome 19, so the gene responsible for this disorder resides on chromosome 19."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBC1D24",
    "long_answer": "DOOR (Deafness, Onychodystrophy, Osteodystrophy, and mental Retardation) syndrome is most commonly caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the TBC1D24 gene. TBC1D24 encodes a protein involved in vesicle trafficking and neuronal development; pathogenic variants disrupt these processes, leading to the characteristic constellation of sensorineural deafness, nail and bone dysplasia, and intellectual disability observed in the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The B-cell marker commonly used in immunohistochemistry to confirm and diagnose gastric (MALT) lymphoma is CD20. CD20 is encoded by the MS4A1 gene, which is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 11 (11q12). Therefore, the relevant gene—and hence the marker—resides on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACADM",
    "long_answer": "Missense or other pathogenic variants in the ACADM gene, which encodes medium-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (MCAD), cause MCAD deficiency. This fatty-acid β-oxidation disorder produces episodic hypoketotic hypoglycaemia, vomiting, liver dysfunction and encephalopathy after fasting or viral illness—clinical features that historically led many cases to be mistaken for Reye syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CBS",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CBS gene, which encodes cystathionine β-synthase, cause classic homocystinuria—a metabolic disorder in the methionine pathway that leads to elevated homocysteine and methionine levels. Patients with CBS deficiency often show a Marfan-like (marfanoid) habitus, including pronounced arachnodactyly. Thus, the same CBS mutation is associated both with arachnodactyly and with a methionine-related metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome type II",
    "long_answer": "Bare lymphocyte syndrome type II (also called MHC class II deficiency) results from mutations in trans-acting regulatory genes such as CIITA, RFX5, RFXAP, or RFXANK that are required for transcription of MHC class II genes. Loss of these regulators abolishes MHC class II surface expression on antigen-presenting cells, leading to a severe combined immunodeficiency phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TBX5",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TBX5 gene cause Holt-Oram syndrome, an autosomal-dominant disorder marked by upper-limb anomalies and a high frequency of atrial or ventricular septal defects and other congenital heart malformations. Because these cardiac defects are common and can be clinically significant, individuals with TBX5 mutations typically require evaluation and ongoing care by a cardiologist for congenital heart disease management."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "The cellular prion protein (PrP), which is the key pathogenic factor in Creutzfeldt–Jakob disease and has also been shown to modulate tau aggregation in several tauopathies, is encoded by the PRNP gene. The PRNP gene is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 20 (20p13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome",
    "long_answer": "Weissenbacher-Zweymüller syndrome is produced by mutations in the COL11A2 gene situated on chromosome 6p21.3. Because chromosome 6 is one of the numbered (non-sex) chromosomes, the responsible gene resides on an autosomal chromosome, explaining the autosomal (usually recessive) inheritance pattern of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Radiophobia",
    "long_answer": "Radiophobia is a specific anxiety disorder in which a person has an irrational and excessive fear of ionizing radiation (e.g., X-rays, gamma rays, nuclear fallout). Although acute radiation syndrome requires exposure to high doses of radiation, individuals with radiophobia can experience intense psychological distress and somatic symptoms that mimic or are mistaken for radiation illness. Thus, the psychological disorder linked to an irrational fear of ionizing radiation is radiophobia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PANK2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PANK2 gene (pantothenate kinase 2) cause pantothenate kinase-associated neurodegeneration, historically termed Hallervorden-Spatz or “parkinsonism-hypertonia” syndrome. Affected individuals develop an early-onset parkinsonism-like disorder with pronounced muscle hypertonia (rigidity/dystonia) due to iron accumulation and neurodegeneration in the basal ganglia. Thus, PANK2 mutation is the well-established genetic link to this parkinsonism-hypertonia illness."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GPR98",
    "long_answer": "The GPR98 gene (also known as VLGR1 or ADGRV1) is one of the genes mutated in Usher syndrome type II (specifically the USH2C subtype). GPR98 encodes ‘very large G protein-coupled receptor 1’, a member of the adhesion GPCR family characterized by an extracellular N-terminal domain and a seven-transmembrane region that signals through heterotrimeric G proteins. Thus, among the Usher syndrome type II genes, GPR98 is the one whose product is a G protein-coupled receptor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary haemochromatosis type 1 is caused by mutations in the HFE gene. The HFE gene is situated in the major histocompatibility complex region at 6p21.3 on human chromosome 6; thus chromosome 6 carries the gene responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FAM20C",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the FAM20C gene cause Raine syndrome, a congenital disorder marked by craniofacial anomalies (such as hypoplastic nose, proptosis and mid-face hypoplasia) together with generalized osteosclerosis. FAM20C encodes a secreted kinase essential for proper mineralization of bone; loss-of-function variants lead to the dense, sclerotic bones and characteristic craniofacial defects seen in this birth defect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Dystrophic epidermolysis bullosa is caused by pathogenic variants in the COL7A1 gene, which encodes the α-1 chain of type VII collagen that forms anchoring fibrils at the dermal–epidermal junction. The COL7A1 gene is mapped to human chromosome 3 (band 3p21.31). Thus, chromosome 3 harbors the gene responsible for this condition, whose chronic skin injury and inflammation can secondarily provoke small-fiber peripheral neuropathy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Human serum albumin is commonly administered as a colloid for fluid resuscitation in patients with septic shock. The ALB gene, which encodes serum albumin, is located on human chromosome 4 (region 4q13). Therefore, chromosome 4 contains the gene encoding the protein used in this clinical setting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACAT1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ACAT1 gene, encoding mitochondrial acetoacetyl-CoA thiolase (β-ketothiolase), block a key step of ketolysis—cleaving acetoacetyl-CoA to two acetyl-CoA molecules. This enzymatic block prevents tissues from utilizing ketone bodies for energy, producing the autosomal-recessive disorder β-ketothiolase deficiency, the prototypic inborn error of ketone body utilization."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Linkage and association studies of developmental (congenital) hip dysplasia have repeatedly implicated the 17q21 region. Genes located on chromosome 17 in this interval (e.g., COL1A1 and HOXB9) carry variants that predispose to acetabular insufficiency and femoral-acetabular incongruity. When severe, the resulting dysplasia can be surgically managed with acetabular reconstruction or shelf procedures that utilise bone grafting, making chromosome 17 the chromosome classically linked with the heritable form of hip dysplasia treated by bone grafting."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2",
    "long_answer": "Rett syndrome, an X-linked neurodevelopmental disorder, is almost always caused by pathogenic mutations in the MECP2 gene located on Xq28. Affected individuals—predominantly females—commonly develop complications that include epileptic seizures and progressive scoliosis, linking MECP2 mutation directly to those clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Both fragile X syndrome and Down syndrome are well-established genetic conditions that characteristically include intellectual disability among their core clinical features. Fragile X syndrome, caused by CGG trinucleotide repeat expansion in the FMR1 gene on the X chromosome, is the most common inherited form of intellectual disability, with affected individuals typically exhibiting mild to severe cognitive impairment. Down syndrome (trisomy 21) is the most common chromosomal cause of intellectual disability; virtually all individuals with full trisomy 21 have some degree of cognitive delay, ranging from mild to moderate, with occasional severe impairment. Thus, the scientific and medical consensus is that both conditions lead to intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "The precursor protein for high-molecular-weight kininogen is encoded by the KNG1 gene (kininogen 1). Cytogenetic mapping places KNG1 on the long arm of human chromosome 3 (band 3q27), making chromosome 3 the genomic location harboring the gene responsible for the HMW kininogen precursor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Von Willebrand disease results from mutations in the VWF gene, which encodes von Willebrand factor. The VWF gene is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 12 (region 12p13.3). Hence, the causative gene for von Willebrand disease is located on chromosome 12."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome X",
    "long_answer": "The sarcoglycan complex in the muscle-cell plasma membrane is part of the larger dystrophin-glycoprotein complex. The transmembrane sarcoglycans bind to the cytoskeletal protein dystrophin, and the gene that encodes dystrophin is DMD, which is located on the short arm of the human X chromosome (Xp21)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ELN",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ELN gene, which encodes elastin, cause autosomal-dominant cutis laxa. In this disorder defective or deficient elastin fibers lead to loose, wrinkled, and markedly inelastic skin because the dermis lacks normal elastic recoil."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC25A4",
    "long_answer": "Chronic progressive external ophthalmoplegia (CPEO) with multiple mitochondrial DNA deletions can be caused by mutations in SLC25A4. This gene encodes adenine nucleotide translocator 1 (ANT1), the inner-mitochondrial-membrane carrier that exchanges ADP and ATP across the membrane. Defects in ANT1 impair mitochondrial energy exchange, leading to the clinical features of CPEO."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Thin glomerular basement membrane",
    "long_answer": "A leading hypothesis is that many cases of loin pain - hematuria syndrome are due to thinning or other structural defects of the glomerular basement membrane. This fragile filtration barrier within the renal glomerulus allows capillary rupture and red-cell leakage, producing recurrent hematuria and the associated flank (loin) pain."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TRPS1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the TRPS1 gene cause tricho-rhino-phalangeal syndrome (types I & III), a congenital disorder whose hallmark features include fine, sparse scalp hair that undergoes progressive thinning beginning in childhood. Thus, mutation of TRPS1 is the genetic defect associated with this syndrome of childhood‐onset hair loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes type II collagen, cause spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita. This skeletal dysplasia primarily involves malformed vertebral bodies and epiphyseal cartilage, leading to a short-trunk form of dwarfism. Because type II collagen is essential for normal cartilage and spinal development, COL2A1 defects disrupt vertebral growth plates and result in disproportionate short stature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANOS1 (KAL1)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ANOS1 gene (formerly KAL1) cause X-linked Kallmann syndrome. This syndrome is characterized by defective migration of gonadotropin-releasing hormone (GnRH) neurons and the olfactory bulbs, leading to congenital hypogonadotropic hypogonadism and anosmia. Thus, an ANOS1 mutation is a well-established genetic cause of a syndrome that produces hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC25A15",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the SLC25A15 gene, encoding the mitochondrial ornithine transporter ORNT1, cause hyperornithinemia-hyperammonemia-homocitrullinuria (HHH) syndrome. In the neonatal period this disorder can present with isolated hyperornithinemia and low citrulline, findings that closely resemble those of ornithine aminotransferase (OAT) deficiency, thus mimicking that condition before hyperammonemia and homocitrullinuria become apparent."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Very long-chain acyl-CoA dehydrogenase (VLCAD) deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the ACADVL gene, which encodes the mitochondrial enzyme needed for β-oxidation of long-chain fatty acids in muscle and other tissues. The ACADVL gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 17 (17p13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Uniparental disomy",
    "long_answer": "During oogenesis, a nondisjunction error can leave the egg with two copies of the same chromosome. If, after fertilisation, the extra paternal copy is lost (trisomy-rescue), the child inherits both homologues from the mother and none from the father—an event called uniparental disomy (UPD). When the two maternal chromosomes are identical (isodisomy), any recessive pathogenic variant carried by the mother is present in two copies, unmasking rare autosomal-recessive disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Wnt/β-catenin pathway",
    "long_answer": "Genetic disorders such as Norrie disease and familial exudative vitreoretinopathy impair retinal blood-vessel development because of mutations in NDP (Norrin), FZD4, LRP5 or TSPAN12. These components form a ligand–receptor complex that activates the canonical Wnt/β-catenin signaling cascade, which is essential for endothelial proliferation, tip-cell formation and vascular stabilization in the retina. Disruption of this Wnt/β-catenin pathway therefore underlies the defective retinal angiogenesis seen in these disorders."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the CYP21A2 gene impair the 21-hydroxylase enzyme, the commonest cause of congenital adrenal hyperplasia. The enzymatic block prevents normal cortisol synthesis, diverting steroid precursors toward androgen production and causing markedly elevated serum androstenedione (and 17-hydroxyprogesterone). Thus, abnormal androstenedione levels are a direct biochemical signature of CYP21A2-related 21-hydroxylase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ventricular arrhythmias",
    "long_answer": "Mitral valve prolapse can create myocardial and valvular stretch that predisposes to malignant ventricular arrhythmias—especially ventricular tachycardia or ventricular fibrillation. These rapid, disorganized rhythms can abruptly halt effective cardiac output, making them a recognized mechanism for sudden cardiac death in patients with mitral valve prolapse."
}

{
    "short_answer": "α-L-iduronidase",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase causes Hurler syndrome (Mucopolysaccharidosis type I). In this disorder undegraded dermatan and heparan sulfates accumulate in many tissues, including the middle and inner ear. The progressive glycosaminoglycan storage leads to recurrent otitis media, conductive and sensorineural impairment, and can culminate in clinically significant deafness, making α-L-iduronidase deficiency a lysosomal storage disease that can lead to hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "D-bifunctional protein deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the HSD17B4 gene, which is located on the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q23.1)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJB2",
    "long_answer": "Bart–Pumphrey syndrome is an autosomal-dominant disorder characterized by leukonychia, palmoplantar keratoderma, knuckle pads, and sensorineural deafness. Multiple families with this genodermatosis carry missense mutations (e.g., p.Asp50Asn) in the GJB2 gene, which encodes connexin-26, a gap-junction protein essential for epidermal and cochlear cell communication. These pathogenic GJB2 variants establish the gene’s causal role in Bart–Pumphrey syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF2",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the NF2 gene (which encodes the tumor-suppressor protein merlin) causes neurofibromatosis type 2.  This disorder leads to the formation of benign schwannomas—especially vestibular (acoustic) schwannomas—but the expanding tumors can compress cranial and peripheral nerves, resulting in hearing loss, balance problems and other nerve damage despite their histologically benign nature."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Best vitelliform macular dystrophy is most often produced by autosomal-dominant mutations in the BEST1 (VMD2) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 11 at 11q12.3. Thus, chromosome 11 carries the gene responsible for BVMD."
}

{
    "short_answer": "NF1 gene",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the NF1 gene, which encodes the tumor-suppressor protein neurofibromin, causes Neurofibromatosis type 1. This neuro-cutaneous disorder presents with characteristic skin findings (café-au-lait macules, axillary freckling, cutaneous neurofibromas) and is well documented to predispose affected individuals to cognitive deficits, learning disabilities, ADHD, autism-spectrum features, and other mental health problems. Thus, an NF1 gene mutation links the skin condition to both cognitive and psychiatric complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Egypt",
    "long_answer": "The association of anonychia-onychodystrophy with brachydactyly type B and ectrodactyly was first reported by Temtamy and co-workers in an Egyptian family. Their publication constituted the initial description of this distinct congenital limb-nail disorder, making Egypt the country of first description."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Shared etymology only",
    "long_answer": "The two terms come from different fields—medicine and art—but both derive from the Greek roots “neo-” (new) and “plásis/plástos” (formation, molding). In medicine, a neoplasm is a new, abnormal growth of tissue (a tumor). In art theory, Neoplasticism (De Stijl) refers to creating a ‘new plastic’ visual language of abstract form and color. Apart from this common linguistic origin meaning ‘new formation,’ the concepts are unrelated."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ANTXR2 (CMG2)",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the ANTXR2 gene (also called CMG2, Capillary-Morphogenesis-Gene-2) cause hyaline fibromatosis syndrome, which encompasses juvenile hyaline fibromatosis and infantile systemic hyalinosis. This disorder is characterized by marked gingival overgrowth (gingival fibromatosis) and progressive, often painful joint stiffness and contractures due to hyaline material deposition around connective tissues. Hence, ANTXR2 mutation underlies the condition linking both clinical features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The Sonic hedgehog (SHH) gene, whose product Shh is crucial for limb patterning and defects such as radial hypoplasia and abnormal thumb development, is located at cytogenetic band 7q36 on human chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Leukocyte adhesion deficiency type II (LAD-II) is diagnosed by demonstrating loss of the fucosylated epitope sialyl-Lewis X on leukocytes. Expression of this epitope depends on the GDP-fucose transporter protein encoded by the SLC35C1 gene. SLC35C1 is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p11.2). Thus, the gene encoding the diagnostic protein for LAD-II resides on chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FREM1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the FREM1 gene (FRAS1 related extracellular matrix protein 1) cause the autosomal-recessive disorder known as bifid nose, anorectal and renal anomalies (BNAR) syndrome. Patients with pathogenic variants in FREM1 typically present with a bifid or broad nasal tip together with malformations of the anorectal region and congenital renal abnormalities, establishing FREM1 as the gene responsible for this triad of defects."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Golgi apparatus",
    "long_answer": "I-cell (inclusion-cell) disease results from a deficiency of the Golgi-resident enzyme N-acetylglucosaminyl-1-phosphotransferase, which normally adds the mannose-6-phosphate tag that directs hydrolases to lysosomes. The defect in this Golgi apparatus enzyme prevents proper targeting of lysosomal enzymes, leading to their extracellular secretion and the characteristic storage pathology of I-cell disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Li–Fraumeni syndrome is caused by germ-line mutations in the TP53 tumor-suppressor gene. TP53 is located at cytogenetic band 17p13.1 on human chromosome 17; hence this chromosome harbors the gene whose alteration confers the cancer-predisposition characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cytosine",
    "long_answer": "Most hypodysfibrinogenemia mutations are C→T transitions that arise at methyl-CpG ‘hot-spots’ in the fibrinogen genes; the original nucleotide that undergoes the pathogenic change is cytosine."
}

{
    "short_answer": "David Mitchell",
    "long_answer": "David Mitchell’s novel “Cloud Atlas” was adapted into the 2012 film of the same name, in which the same actors portray multiple, heavily-made-up characters across six different time periods. Because prosopagnosia impairs facial recognition, a viewer with the condition would have great difficulty following which character is which, making the film challenging to track."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HLXB9 (MNX1)",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the homeobox gene HLXB9, also known as MNX1, cause the Currarino/caudal regression sequence—partial or complete sacral agenesis with anorectal malformation and a presacral mass. Sacral/caudal regression can also arise sporadically after a vascular accident in utero that disrupts blood flow to the caudal mesoderm. Thus, HLXB9 mutation is the well-established genetic lesion linked to this congenital malformation that may alternatively be produced by an intra-uterine vascular insult."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSD3B2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the HSD3B2 gene, which encodes the type II 3-β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase enzyme, cause the 3-β-HSD–deficiency form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia. Because this defect impairs the synthesis of all downstream steroid hormones (cortisol, aldosterone, and androgens), both 46,XX and 46,XY fetuses can develop abnormal external genitalia—virilization of females and undervirilization of males—resulting in ambiguous genitalia in either genetic sex."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PTF1A",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the PTF1A gene (Pancreas Transcription Factor 1 Subunit α) abolish normal cerebellar development, resulting in near-total or complete cerebellar agenesis. Affected infants present with profound developmental disability because the cerebellum fails to form; the same mutation typically also causes pancreatic agenesis. Thus, PTF1A mutation is a well-documented genetic cause of developmental disability characterized by absence of the cerebellum."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Frequent comorbidity",
    "long_answer": "Anorexia nervosa and body dysmorphic disorder are separate DSM-5 diagnoses, but they share core psychopathology—severe body-image distortion and obsessive appearance concerns—and therefore commonly co-occur in the same patient population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes type II collagen, cause spondylo-epiphyseal dysplasia congenita. This disorder disrupts vertebral and epiphyseal cartilage formation, leading to abnormal spine development and pronounced short-trunk dwarfism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "The NF2 gene, whose loss leads to multiple nervous system tumors such as vestibular schwannomas and meningiomas, is located on the long arm of chromosome 22 (region 22q12)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Sickle cell disease results from mutations in the β-globin gene (HBB). The HBB gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 11 (11p15.5). Allogeneic hematopoietic stem cell transplantation is an established curative therapy for this genetic hemoglobinopathy, so the disease-causing gene’s chromosomal location is chromosome 11."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Pseudoachondroplasia is caused by pathogenic variants in the COMP gene (cartilage oligomeric matrix protein). The human COMP gene is mapped to chromosome 19 at band 19p13.2-p13.1; therefore, chromosome 19 carries the gene responsible for this skeletal dysplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "May-Hegglin anomaly is caused by autosomal-dominant mutations in the MYH9 gene, which encodes non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA. The MYH9 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 22 (22q13), making chromosome 22 the chromosomal site responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BHLHA9",
    "long_answer": "Increased copy number of the BHLHA9 gene (duplication or triplication at 17p13.3) causes Split-Hand/Foot Malformation with Long-Bone Deficiency type 3 (SHFLD3). This dosage-sensitive syndrome presents with limb reduction defects that frequently include oligodactyly, along with split hand/foot malformations and tibial deficiency. Thus, duplication or triplication of BHLHA9 is the established genetic cause of the syndrome featuring oligodactyly."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Protein C",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of Protein C, a vitamin K-dependent serine protease that inactivates coagulation factors Va and VIIIa, removes an important natural anticoagulant pathway. The resulting widespread microvascular thrombosis produces purpura fulminans—a rapidly spreading hemorrhagic skin necrosis that frequently progresses to tissue ischemia and gangrene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PCNT",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the PCNT gene, which encodes the centrosomal protein pericentrin, cause microcephalic osteodysplastic primordial dwarfism type II (MOPD II). A hallmark of this disorder is severe, progressively worsening microcephaly that begins prenatally and advances postnatally, reflecting impaired centrosome function and disrupted cell-cycle progression during brain growth."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Etiocholanolone",
    "long_answer": "Etiocholanolone, a 5β-reduced metabolite of testosterone, has been shown to bind and trigger the pyrin inflammasome in human monocytes. Its ability to induce IL-1β-dependent fever and neutrophilic inflammation produces an autoinflammatory picture that closely mimics familial Mediterranean fever attacks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Subcortical band heterotopia (double-cortex syndrome) is most commonly produced by mutations in the DCX (doublecortin) gene, which is located on the long arm of the X chromosome (Xq22–q23). Hence, the pathogenic mutation responsible for band heterotopia lies on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "VEGF-C/VEGFR-3 pathway",
    "long_answer": "Hennekam lymphangiectasia-lymphedema syndrome is caused by mutations in genes such as CCBE1, ADAMTS3 and FAT4 that regulate activation or signaling of vascular endothelial growth factor-C (VEGF-C). Impaired VEGF-C interaction with its receptor VEGFR-3 disrupts the VEGF-C/VEGFR-3 signaling pathway, leading to defective lymphangiogenesis and the lymphedema–lymphangiectasia phenotype characteristic of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The principal gene first linked to autosomal-recessive primary microcephaly is MCPH1 (microcephalin), which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 8 (region 8p23)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The enzyme that esterifies free cholesterol on high-density lipoprotein (HDL) particles is lecithin-cholesterol acyltransferase (LCAT). The human LCAT gene that encodes this enzyme is located on the long arm of chromosome 16 (region 16q22.1). Thus, chromosome 16 carries the gene responsible for HDL cholesterol esterification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The tumor-suppressor gene TP53, whose germline mutations cause Li–Fraumeni syndrome, is located on the short arm of human chromosome 17 (band 17p13.1)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Erythropoietic protoporphyria results from loss-of-function mutations in the ferrochelatase (FECH) gene, the final enzyme of the heme-biosynthetic pathway whose transcription is modulated by steroid (sex-hormone) signals. The human FECH gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 18 (18q21), and pathogenic variants on this chromosome lead to the accumulation of protoporphyrin and the characteristic photosensitivity of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anaemia, a hereditary disease characterized by progressive bone marrow failure, is most commonly linked to mutations in the FANCA gene. The FANCA gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 16 (16q24.3); hence chromosome 16 carries the gene associated with this bone-marrow-failure disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "yes",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in SMOC1 (SPARC-related modular calcium-binding protein 1) are a proven cause of an autosomal-recessive developmental disorder characterised by severe eye malformations (anophthalmia/microphthalmia) together with limb anomalies (syndactyly, brachydactyly). The original gene-discovery reports (e.g., Rainger et al., Nature Genetics 2011; Sousa et al., American Journal of Human Genetics 2011) described several consanguineous families from Turkey in whom homozygous SMOC1 truncating or splice-site variants segregated perfectly with the disorder. Subsequent studies have confirmed this association in additional Turkish and non-Turkish pedigrees. Thus, according to current biomedical consensus, SMOC1 is indeed associated with a developmental disorder that was first identified in (and remains prevalent among) Turkish families."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Familial studies have mapped the susceptibility locus for benign childhood epilepsy with centro-temporal (Rolandic) spikes to the 15q14 region of chromosome 15, implicating genes such as ELP4 located there. Thus, chromosome 15 is the chromosome linked to the genetic form of Rolandic epilepsy."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GALNS",
    "long_answer": "Morquio A syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type IVA) is produced by pathogenic mutations in the GALNS gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme N-acetylgalactosamine-6-sulfatase. Loss of functional GALNS impairs degradation of keratan sulfate and chondroitin-6-sulfate, leading to lysosomal storage and the characteristic skeletal abnormalities of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "IL17F",
    "long_answer": "The IL17F gene, located on chromosome 6p12, encodes the cytokine interleukin-17F. Loss-of-function mutations in IL17F cause autosomal dominant chronic mucocutaneous candidiasis (CMCD type 4), an immunodeficiency syndrome marked by recurrent infections of skin, nails and mucous membranes—i.e., infections of the integumentary system."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Human adenovirus",
    "long_answer": "Human adenoviruses are double-stranded DNA viruses whose primary transcripts are extensively and alternatively spliced in the host nucleus. A single adenoviral transcription unit can be processed into many distinct mRNAs (early and late transcripts), each encoding different viral proteins. The original discovery of RNA splicing was made in adenovirus-infected human cells, highlighting this virus as the classic example of alternative splicing generating multiple mRNAs from one genomic region."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Staphylococcal scalded skin syndrome (Ritter disease) is a toxin-mediated infection by Staphylococcus aureus that primarily affects infants and young children. The exotoxins disrupt epidermal adhesion, producing a widespread exfoliative dermatitis; systemic signs such as irritability and high fever commonly accompany the skin lesions. Consequently, this bacterial infection is a well-recognized childhood form of dermatitis that can present with fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MDM2 and CDK4",
    "long_answer": "Atypical lipomatous tumors/well-differentiated liposarcomas consistently show high-level amplification of the 12q13-15 region, most notably involving the oncogenes MDM2 (an inhibitor of p53) and CDK4 (a G1-phase cyclin-dependent kinase). Detection of MDM2 and CDK4 amplification is a diagnostic hallmark that differentiates these tumors from benign lipomas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Dermatologist",
    "long_answer": "Sebaceous nevi are congenital hamartomas of the skin that can later develop secondary tumors such as basal cell carcinoma or syringocystadenoma papilliferum. Diagnosis, surveillance, and treatment (e.g., dermatoscopic monitoring, surgical excision, or laser therapy) fall within the expertise of a dermatologist, the medical specialist trained to manage skin lesions and their potential complications."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Malignant histiocytosis",
    "long_answer": "Modern classifications divide histiocytic disorders into three broad groups: (1) Langerhans cell histiocytosis, (2) hemophagocytic lymphohistiocytosis/macrophage activation syndrome, and (3) malignant histiocytosis (including histiocytic sarcoma). Therefore, the type of histiocytosis that is not placed in either the HLH or LCH categories is malignant histiocytosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Transcription factors",
    "long_answer": "Genes implicated in age-related macular degeneration (e.g., CFH, ARMS2/HTRA1, C3) are turned on or off by transcription factors—DNA-binding regulatory proteins that control the initiation and rate of gene transcription. These proteins recognize specific promoter or enhancer sequences and recruit the transcriptional machinery, thereby governing the expression levels of AMD-associated genes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome, the classic craniofacial dysostosis that involves premature fusion of skull sutures, is caused by pathogenic variants in the FGFR2 (and occasionally FGFR3) gene. These mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, meaning a single mutated allele from an affected parent is sufficient to produce the disorder and it shows vertical inheritance through successive generations."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Hemophilia A is caused by pathogenic variants in the F8 gene, which encodes coagulation Factor VIII. The F8 gene is located on the long arm of the X chromosome at band Xq28, not on chromosome 22. Because hemophilia A is X-linked, the disorder is far more common in males and transmitted through carrier females—further evidence that the responsible gene resides on the X chromosome rather than an autosome such as chromosome 22."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Inherited Factor VII deficiency is caused by mutations in the F7 gene, which encodes coagulation Factor VII. The F7 gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 13 at position 13q34; therefore, chromosome 13 carries the gene responsible for this hematologic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The REEP1 (Receptor Expression-Enhancing Protein 1) gene responsible for the autosomal-dominant form of hereditary spastic paraplegia SPG31 is located on human chromosome 2, specifically at band 2p11.2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HRAS",
    "long_answer": "Costello syndrome is produced by heterozygous activating mutations in the HRAS proto-oncogene, most often the p.Gly12Ser substitution. These mutations impair the intrinsic GTPase activity of HRAS, leaving the protein locked in its GTP-bound conformation and therefore permanently active, which disrupts normal cell‐signalling pathways and leads to the characteristic features of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "Female infertility seen in the systemic form of Pseudohypoaldosteronism type 1 is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the SCNN1B gene, which encodes the β-subunit of the epithelial sodium channel (ENaC). SCNN1B is located at cytogenetic band 16p12 on human chromosome 16; consequently, chromosome 16 is the chromosomal location responsible for this infertility phenotype in PHA-1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic mutations causing schwannomatosis are most commonly found in the SMARCB1 (INI1) and LZTR1 genes, both of which are located on the long arm of human chromosome 22 (22q). Thus, chromosome 22 harbors the gene(s) whose alteration leads to the development of schwannomatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Theophrastus",
    "long_answer": "In his work ‘Characters’ (4th century BC) the Greek philosopher and natural historian Theophrastus portrays “The Superstitious Man,” who repeatedly washes, sprinkles himself with water three times, avoids stepping on sacred spots, and performs elaborate checking and ritualistic behaviours to quell intrusive fears. These repeated, anxiety-driven cleansing and checking rituals strongly resemble the obsessions and compulsions that today define obsessive-compulsive disorder, making Theophrastus the earliest known author to describe such symptoms."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Type I collagen",
    "long_answer": "Osteogenesis imperfecta is a genetic disorder caused by quantitative or qualitative deficiency of type I collagen. A key clinical distinction from rickets is that patients with osteogenesis imperfecta typically have normal serum calcium and phosphorus levels, whereas rickets presents with low calcium and/or phosphate due to impaired mineralization. Thus, deficiency of type I collagen underlies the genetic disorder not characterized by the low mineral levels seen in rickets."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MEN1",
    "long_answer": "Germ-line mutations in the MEN1 gene (encoding menin) cause Multiple Endocrine Neoplasia type 1.  Besides endocrine tumours, MEN1 patients frequently develop numerous subcutaneous lipomas and have a documented excess of central-nervous-system tumours, especially astrocytomas and other gliomas.  Thus, a MEN1 mutation links the clinical combination of multiple lipomas with an increased risk of glioma formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX6",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the human PAX6 gene, which encodes a paired-box/homeobox transcription factor essential for ocular development, can lead to microphthalmia. PAX6 is the direct human ortholog of the mouse Small-eye (Sey) gene; the two genes are homologous and perform the same key developmental roles, explaining why defects in either species produce similar small-eye phenotypes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The anti-Müllerian hormone (AMH), which causes regression of the Müllerian ducts during male embryonic development, is encoded by the AMH gene. In humans, the AMH gene is located on the short arm of chromosome 19 (region 19p13.3). Thus, chromosome 19 carries the gene responsible for producing the hormone that inhibits Müllerian duct formation in males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-mannosidosis is caused by mutations in the MAN2B1 gene, which encodes lysosomal α-mannosidase. Cytogenetic and linkage studies have mapped MAN2B1 to human chromosome 19 (region 19p13/q13). Thus, chromosome 19 carries the gene responsible for alpha-mannosidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The most common soft-tissue sarcoma in children is rhabdomyosarcoma. The hallmark alveolar subtype features the t(2;13)(q35;q14) translocation that fuses the PAX3 gene on chromosome 2 with the FOXO1 (FKHR) gene on chromosome 13. Because the critical PAX3 transcription-factor gene resides at 2q35, chromosome 2 is classically cited as the chromosomal location of the gene associated with paediatric rhabdomyosarcoma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Familial multiple intestinal atresia is linked to pathogenic mutations in the TTC7A gene. TTC7A is mapped to the short arm of human chromosome 2 (region 2p16), making chromosome 2 the chromosomal location of the gene associated with this hereditary disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Ostravik-Lindemann-Solberg syndrome is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the TBX15 gene. TBX15 lies on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (cytogenetic band 1p13), so the responsible gene for this syndrome is located on chromosome 1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAQ mutation",
    "long_answer": "Sturge-Weber syndrome, a neurocutaneous disorder characterized by facial capillary malformations, leptomeningeal angiomas, seizures, and early-onset glaucoma, is caused by a somatic activating mutation in the GNAQ gene (classically the p.R183Q variant). Thus, a GNAQ mutation is the genetic alteration associated with a condition that can present with both glaucoma and seizures."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The enzyme that converts testosterone to 5α-dihydrotestosterone in the prostate is 5α-reductase type 2, encoded by the SRD5A2 gene. SRD5A2 is located on human chromosome 2; thus chromosome 2 carries the gene responsible for this key androgen-activating step."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Tetra-amelia syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WNT3 gene. The human WNT3 gene is situated at cytogenetic band 17q21 on chromosome 17, so the gene responsible for tetra-amelia syndrome resides on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Syphilis lesions vs. HPV warts",
    "long_answer": "Condylomata lata are broad-based, smooth, moist, flat-topped lesions that arise in secondary syphilis due to Treponema pallidum, whereas condylomata acuminata are dry, verrucous, cauliflower-like genital warts caused by infection with human papillomavirus (most often HPV types 6 and 11). Thus they differ in etiology (T. pallidum vs. HPV) and in clinical morphology (flat-moist vs. papillomatous-verrucous)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "ACVR1 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Fibrodysplasia ossificans progressiva (FOP) is an extremely rare disorder frequently misdiagnosed because of its rarity and atypical presentation. Virtually all classic cases are caused by a recurrent activating missense mutation (p.R206H) in the ACVR1 gene (also called ALK2), which encodes a bone morphogenetic protein type-I receptor. This mutation drives inappropriate ossification of soft tissues, explaining the disease’s hallmark progressive heterotopic bone formation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The genetic form of the limb-reduction defect mimicking thalidomide embryopathy (phocomelia/Roberts or SC phocomelia syndrome) is produced by pathogenic variants in the ESCO2 gene, which is situated on chromosome 8 (8p21). Hence chromosome 8 is linked to the hereditary counterpart of the condition caused by thalidomide."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary pancreatitis is most commonly caused by gain-of-function mutations in the PRSS1 gene, which encodes cationic trypsinogen. PRSS1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 7 (region 7q35), so chromosome 7 carries the gene responsible for this inherited form of pancreatitis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Blue cone monochromacy is an X-linked disorder produced by deletions or rearrangements in the red (OPN1LW) and green (OPN1MW) cone opsin gene cluster. This tandem array resides on the long arm of the X chromosome at Xq28, so the gene cluster responsible for the disease is located on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC25A15",
    "long_answer": "Ornithine translocase deficiency (hyperornithinemia-hyperammonemia-homocitrullinuria syndrome) results from pathogenic mutations in the SLC25A15 gene (also called ORNT1), which encodes the mitochondrial ornithine transporter. Loss of functional SLC25A15 impairs ornithine entry into mitochondria, disrupting the urea cycle and causing accumulation of ammonia, hence the hyperammonemia characteristic of the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The classic form of ocular albinism (OA1) is caused by mutations in the GPR143 (OA1) gene, which is located at Xp22 on the X chromosome. Because the critical ocular-albinism genes reside on this sex chromosome, the disorder shows X-linked inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "CAPOS syndrome results from missense mutations in the ATP1A3 gene, which encodes the α3-subunit of the Na⁺/K⁺-ATPase. The human ATP1A3 locus is situated on chromosome 19q13.31; consequently, chromosome 19 harbors the gene responsible for CAPOS syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SRPX2",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the X-linked SRPX2 gene cause bilateral perisylvian polymicrogyria. This malformation produces pseudobulbar palsy with severe oral motor and speech apraxia; affected individuals are often non-verbal and therefore depend on augmentative and alternative communication systems. Thus, SRPX2 is the gene linked to the polymicrogyria form requiring AAC due to profound speech impairment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Both Denys–Drash syndrome and WAGR syndrome involve pathogenic alterations in the WT1 gene, which is located at band 11p13 on chromosome 11; therefore, chromosome 11 is the common chromosomal locus associated with these two syndromes."
}

{
    "short_answer": "XPD DNA helicase",
    "long_answer": "The photosensitive ectodermal disorder trichothiodystrophy (IBIDS/PIBIDS) presents with ichthyotic skin, brittle sulfur-deficient hair, short stature and various skeletal abnormalities. It is caused by loss-of-function mutations in ERCC2, which encodes the XPD DNA helicase subunit of the TFIIH complex that functions in nucleotide-excision DNA repair. Thus, deficiency of the XPD DNA helicase enzyme underlies this skin-hair–skeletal syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Myotonic dystrophy type 2 (DM2) is caused by an expanded CCTG repeat in the CNBP (formerly ZNF9) gene. This gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 3 (region 3q21.3), so chromosome 3 is the chromosome that contains the DM2-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Epstein-Barr virus",
    "long_answer": "Epstein-Barr virus (EBV), also known as human herpesvirus-4, infects B-lymphocytes and is etiologically linked to several lymphoid malignancies, notably Hodgkin lymphoma, Burkitt lymphoma, and post-transplant lymphoproliferative disorders. Its latent infection in lymphoid cells and expression of viral oncogenes (e.g., LMP1, EBNA proteins) drive cellular proliferation and survival, establishing EBV as the canonical virus associated with Hodgkin lymphoma and related lymphoid diseases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Camurati–Engelmann disease (progressive diaphyseal dysplasia) is caused by pathogenic variants in the TGFB1 gene. TGFB1 is mapped to cytogenetic band 19q13.2 on human chromosome 19, so this chromosome carries the disease-causing gene responsible for the skull-thickening phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the UBR1 gene give rise to Johanson–Blizzard syndrome, which characteristically includes scalp defects of aplasia cutis congenita. The UBR1 gene is situated at cytogenetic band 15q15 on human chromosome 15, so the shared disease-linked gene resides on chromosome 15."
}

{
    "short_answer": "N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase",
    "long_answer": "I-cell disease arises from a deficiency of N-acetylglucosamine-1-phosphotransferase, the Golgi enzyme that adds mannose-6-phosphate tags to lysosomal hydrolases. Without this phosphorylation, the enzymes are secreted extracellularly instead of being targeted to lysosomes, leading to intracellular accumulation of undigested material and the characteristic lysosomal storage pathology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Familial infantile myofibromatosis is most often caused by heterozygous activating variants in the PDGFRB gene. PDGFRB is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 5 (5q32-q33.1), so the gene associated with this familial tumor disorder resides on chromosome 5."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Narrower carpal tunnel",
    "long_answer": "Women’s wrists have a smaller cross-sectional area and a relatively narrower carpal tunnel than men’s. This reduced anatomical space means that the same amount of tendon swelling or repetitive friction produces higher intra-tunnel pressure, greater tendon–sheath irritation and earlier median-nerve compression. These biomechanical and anatomical differences, amplified at times by estrogen-related fluid retention, make women more susceptible to repetitive strain injuries such as tendinitis and carpal tunnel syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "The human AOX1 gene, which encodes the molybdenum-containing enzyme aldehyde oxidase 1, is located on chromosome 2 (cytogenetic band 2q23–q24). Aldehyde oxidase 1 catalyzes the oxidation of diverse xenobiotic compounds and participates in the catabolic degradation of folate derivatives, linking it to both xenobiotic metabolism and folate catabolism pathways. Therefore, the gene resides on human chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAQ mutation",
    "long_answer": "The congenital neurocutaneous disorder characterized by facial port-wine stains and early-onset seizures is Sturge-Weber syndrome. This condition is produced by a post-zygotic, somatic activating mutation in the GNAQ gene (most commonly the p.R183Q variant), which alters Gαq signaling and drives the vascular malformations seen in the skin and leptomeninges."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAH gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive mutations in the PAH gene, which encodes phenylalanine hydroxylase, cause phenylketonuria. Loss of this enzyme blocks conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to toxic accumulation of phenylalanine and its metabolites. The resulting metabolic disorder damages the developing brain and commonly presents with intellectual disability and convulsions if dietary phenylalanine is not restricted."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "The gene encoding the enzyme aspartoacylase is ASPA, which is located on the short arm of human chromosome 17 (region 17p13); mutations in ASPA on chromosome 17 cause Canavan disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The inborn error of GABA degradation most often refers to succinic semialdehyde dehydrogenase (SSADH) deficiency. SSADH is encoded by the ALDH5A1 gene, which is located at cytogenetic band 6p22 on human chromosome 6. Mutations in ALDH5A1 abolish SSADH activity, blocking conversion of succinic semialdehyde to succinate and thus causing the characteristic enzyme deficiency in the GABA degradation pathway."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "The gene encoding endothelin-converting enzyme-1 (ECE1)—the metalloprotease that cleaves pro-endothelin-1, ‑2 and ‑3 into their active forms—is located on human chromosome 1 (band 1p36). Mutations in ECE1 disrupt endothelin signaling and have been implicated in Hirschsprung’s disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SPRED1",
    "long_answer": "Legius syndrome, frequently mistaken for neurofibromatosis type I because of multiple café-au-lait macules, is caused by pathogenic mutations in the SPRED1 gene. Genetic testing for SPRED1 confirms the diagnosis, but patients are sometimes incorrectly assigned the NF1 diagnostic code."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia",
    "long_answer": "Neonatal alloimmune thrombocytopenia (NAIT) is an immune‐mediated disorder in which maternal antibodies directed against fetal platelet antigens cross the placenta and destroy fetal platelets. It is one of the most frequent causes of severe thrombocytopenia in otherwise healthy term newborns and is well recognized for predisposing affected neonates to life-threatening bleeding, particularly intracranial hemorrhage."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Calomys callosus",
    "long_answer": "The natural reservoir for Machupo virus, the etiologic agent of Bolivian hemorrhagic fever, is the large vesper mouse, scientifically named Calomys callosus. This cricetid rodent maintains the virus in endemic regions of Bolivia and transmits it to humans, typically through contact with its urine, feces, or aerosolized excreta."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "The PLAT gene, situated at 8p11.21 on human chromosome 8, encodes tissue-type plasminogen activator (tPA), a serine protease that converts plasminogen to plasmin and thereby participates in the coagulation/fibrinolytic pathway. Trisomy of chromosome 8 is known as Warkany syndrome 2, linking this chromosome to the syndrome. Hence, the gene for the coagulation-related protein resides on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GNAS",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous inactivating mutations in the GNAS gene, which encodes the stimulatory G-protein α-subunit (Gsα), cause progressive osseous heteroplasia—a disorder marked by cutaneous and subcutaneous heterotopic ossification that can extend into deeper tissues. Thus, GNAS is the gene classically associated with disease featuring cutaneous or subcutaneous ossification."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GJA1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GJA1 gene (encoding connexin-43) cause oculodentodigital dysplasia. This autosomal-dominant syndrome characteristically presents with digital anomalies such as syndactyly and dental abnormalities including microdontia, linking the GJA1 mutation to both features."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X-linked recessive",
    "long_answer": "X-linked agammaglobulinemia (Bruton disease) is caused by pathogenic variants in the BTK gene located on the X chromosome. Because only one functional copy of BTK is needed, females with one mutant allele are typically asymptomatic carriers, while hemizygous males manifest the disease. This pattern—affected males, carrier females, and no male-to-male transmission—is characteristic of X-linked recessive inheritance."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arylsulfatase B",
    "long_answer": "Deficiency of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase B (also called N-acetylgalactosamine-4-sulfatase) causes mucopolysaccharidosis type VI (Maroteaux-Lamy syndrome). Arylsulfatase B is required for the stepwise degradation of the glycosaminoglycans dermatan sulfate and, critically, chondroitin 4-sulfate. Loss of this enzyme blocks chondroitin sulfate breakdown, leading to its accumulation and the characteristic clinical manifestations of this form of mucopolysaccharidosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile hyaline fibromatosis is caused by recessive mutations in the ANTXR2 (also called CMG2) gene, which is located at cytogenetic band 4q21 on human chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TWIST1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the TWIST1 gene, located on chromosome 7p21, cause Saethre-Chotzen syndrome. This autosomal-dominant craniosynostosis syndrome is well recognised to account for roughly 8 % of all craniosynostosis cases."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "Spinal muscular atrophy is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the Survival Motor Neuron 1 (SMN1) gene, which is located at cytogenetic band 5q13 on human chromosome 5. Deletions or conversions of SMN1 on chromosome 5 lead to deficiency of SMN protein and degeneration of spinal motor neurons, producing the characteristic muscle weakness of the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Autosomal-recessive Pachydermoperiostosis (primary hypertrophic osteoarthropathy) is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the HPGD gene, which encodes 15-hydroxyprostaglandin dehydrogenase. The HPGD gene is located on the long arm of chromosome 4 (4q34-q35); therefore, chromosome 4 harbors the gene responsible for the autosomal recessive form of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Caudate nucleus",
    "long_answer": "In Huntington's disease there is selective degeneration of medium spiny GABAergic neurons in the caudate nucleus (and adjacent putamen) of the striatum. Loss of these neurons disrupts the indirect basal ganglia pathway, removing inhibitory control over thalamocortical circuits and producing the characteristic hyperkinetic, choreiform movements."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "The F11 gene located on human chromosome 4 (4q35.2) encodes coagulation factor XI. Factor XI circulates in plasma as a disulfide-linked homodimer and is an essential serine protease of the intrinsic coagulation pathway. Hence, the chromosome that carries the gene for this homodimeric coagulation protein is chromosome 4."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Ladd procedure",
    "long_answer": "Congenital intestinal malrotation often produces obstructing peritoneal (Ladd) bands that compress the duodenum. The definitive surgical treatment is the Ladd procedure, which divides these bands, broadens the mesenteric base, positions the bowel in a non-rotated configuration, and removes the appendix, thereby relieving duodenal obstruction and preventing midgut volvulus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal dominant",
    "long_answer": "Keratitis–ichthyosis–deafness (KID) syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous missense mutations in the GJB2 gene encoding connexin-26 on chromosome 13. These single-allele (dominant-negative) mutations are transmitted in an autosomal dominant manner, so affected individuals can pass the disorder to offspring of either sex with a 50 % risk."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Exclusionary condition",
    "long_answer": "In the standard case definitions for myalgic encephalomyelitis/chronic fatigue syndrome (e.g., 1994 CDC/Fukuda, Canadian Consensus, IOM), a current or recent substance use disorder is listed as an exclusionary diagnosis because ongoing alcohol or drug abuse can independently cause profound fatigue, sleep disturbance, and cognitive impairment. Therefore, active substance use disorder must be ruled out or resolved before a patient can be diagnosed with ME/CFS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Myeloperoxidase deficiency results from mutations in the MPO gene. The MPO gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 17 (17q23.1), so this chromosome harbors the genetic defect responsible for the condition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "Factor VII deficiency is caused by pathogenic variants in the F7 gene, which encodes coagulation factor VII. The F7 gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 13 at band 13q34; hence, the gene responsible for Factor VII deficiency resides on chromosome 13."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erythema marginatum",
    "long_answer": "The classic rash of acute rheumatic fever is erythema marginatum⁠—evanescent, non-pruritic rings that enlarge centrifugally to form polycyclic, serpiginous erythematous plaques on the trunk and proximal limbs. This pattern of annular, snake-like erythema is widely recognized as the typical cutaneous manifestation of rheumatic fever."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Friedreich's ataxia is produced by mutations—most commonly GAA trinucleotide repeat expansions—in the FXN (frataxin) gene. Cytogenetic and molecular mapping place FXN at 9q13-q21.1 on human chromosome 9, establishing this chromosome as the location of the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "t6A tRNA modification",
    "long_answer": "Genes mutated in Galloway–Mowat syndrome (e.g., OSGEP, TP53RK, TPRKB, LAGE3, GON7) encode sub-units of the KEOPS complex, whose essential function is to add the threonyl-carbamoyl adenosine (t6A) modification to position 37 of ANN-decoding tRNAs. Mutations impair this t6A tRNA modification pathway, linking defective t6A adenosine formation to the pathogenesis of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Sickle cell disease results from a point mutation (Glu6Val) in the β-globin gene (HBB), which is located in the β-globin gene cluster on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p15.5). Therefore, chromosome 11 carries the gene whose alteration causes sickle cell disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC5A2",
    "long_answer": "Familial renal glucosuria is produced by loss-of-function mutations in the SLC5A2 gene, which encodes the renal sodium-glucose cotransporter 2 (SGLT2). Defective SGLT2 in the proximal tubule reduces glucose reabsorption, leading to persistent glucosuria despite normal blood glucose levels, a hallmark identified by medical genetic testing."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The gene responsible for disease X has been mapped to the human X chromosome. Because this locus is X-linked, the condition shows the characteristic inheritance pattern in which males (with only one X chromosome) are more frequently affected, while heterozygous females are usually carriers but can display milder symptoms. Extensive linkage and positional cloning studies have confirmed the gene’s location on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Phenylketonuria results from mutations in the phenylalanine hydroxylase (PAH) gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 12 (12q23.2). Deficiency of PAH activity impairs conversion of phenylalanine to tyrosine, leading to the characteristic metabolic disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "1887",
    "long_answer": "Richard Mansfield’s stage adaptation of Robert Louis Stevenson’s novella, scripted by Thomas Russell Sullivan, premiered at the Boston Museum on 9 May 1887; therefore, the play ‘Dr. Jekyll and Mr. Hyde’ made its Boston debut in 1887."
}

{
    "short_answer": "UGT1A1",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the UGT1A1 gene eliminate hepatic bilirubin UDP-glucuronosyl-transferase activity, producing Crigler–Najjar syndrome type I. Because the metabolic defect is restricted to the liver, orthotopic liver transplantation provides hepatocytes with functional UGT1A1, permanently correcting the disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAH gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PAH gene, which encodes phenylalanine hydroxylase, cause phenylketonuria (PKU) – an inborn error of phenylalanine metabolism. Without dietary management, toxic accumulation of phenylalanine disrupts brain development, leading to severe intellectual disability, growth delay, and other neurologic deficits. Thus, a PAH gene mutation is the canonical genetic lesion underlying this metabolic disorder associated with intellectual disability."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Lethal congenital contracture syndrome 1 (LCCS1) is caused by pathogenic variants in the GLE1 gene, which is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 9 (region 9q34). Thus, chromosome 9 harbors the gene responsible for LCCS1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "The CARD9 gene, located on the long arm of human chromosome 9 (9q34.3), encodes the caspase-recruitment-domain-containing protein 9. CARD9 is a key adaptor that couples pattern-recognition receptors to NF-κB signalling, thereby regulating innate immune responses. Genome-wide association studies have shown that variants in CARD9 confer susceptibility to spondyloarthritis, including ankylosing spondylitis. Hence, the gene implicated in the disease resides on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SFRP4",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SFRP4 gene (secreted frizzled-related protein 4) cause Pyle-type metaphyseal dysplasia, a skeletal disorder marked by markedly broad metaphyses, thinning of the cortical bone, and consequent cortical-bone fragility. SFRP4 normally modulates Wnt signaling during bone modeling; its deficiency disrupts cortical bone formation, producing the characteristic widened metaphyses and fragile cortices."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Dubin–Johnson syndrome is caused by pathogenic variants in the ABCC2 gene (also called MRP2), which resides on the long arm of human chromosome 10 (10q24). Mutations in ABCC2 impair the canalicular multispecific organic anion transporter responsible for excreting conjugated bilirubin, leading to the characteristic conjugated hyperbilirubinemia of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "Shwachman–Diamond syndrome is primarily caused by biallelic mutations in the SBDS gene. Cytogenetic mapping places SBDS at band 7q11.21, meaning the gene—and thus the molecular defect underlying the disorder—is located on human chromosome 7."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CYP21A2 mutation",
    "long_answer": "A pathogenic mutation in the CYP21A2 gene abolishes 21-hydroxylase activity, producing congenital adrenal hyperplasia. The resulting cortisol and aldosterone deficiency predisposes patients to adrenal crisis with severe intra-operative hypotension, so anesthetic management focuses on stress-dose glucocorticoids and vigorous fluid/vasopressor support."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Primary ovarian insufficiency is frequently linked to the FMR1 gene (and other POF-related genes such as BMP15 and POF1B), all of which reside on the human X chromosome. Mutations or repeat expansions in these X-linked genes disrupt normal ovarian function, leading to premature depletion of follicles and the clinical picture of primary ovarian insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Aldose reductase",
    "long_answer": "Aldose reductase is the key enzyme of the polyol pathway that converts excess glucose to sorbitol in hyperglycaemia. In diabetes mellitus—an endocrine disorder—sorbitol accumulates in the lens and retinal cells, producing osmotic damage that leads to cataracts and diabetic retinopathy, major causes of blindness. Hence, this enzyme is directly associated with a blindness-causing disease that is a central topic in endocrinology."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 10",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of adult Refsum disease are caused by mutations in the PHYH gene (phytanoyl-CoA 2-hydroxylase). The PHYH gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 10 (10p13). Defects in this chromosome-10 gene impair α-oxidation of phytanic acid, leading to its accumulation and the clinical features of Refsum disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MMACHC",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent form of methylmalonic acidemia with possible dysmorphic features is the cblC type, which results from biallelic mutations in the MMACHC gene. MMACHC encodes a protein essential for intracellular cobalamin processing; its deficiency causes combined methylmalonic acidemia and homocystinuria. This variant is the commonest MMA subtype and is well documented to present with neurologic impairment, ocular anomalies, and characteristic facial dysmorphism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TCOF1",
    "long_answer": "Treacher Collins syndrome is most often caused by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the TCOF1 gene, which encodes the nucleolar phosphoprotein treacle. Pathogenic variants in TCOF1 account for roughly 80–90 % of cases and disrupt ribosome biogenesis in neural crest cells, leading to the characteristic craniofacial anomalies and the conductive hearing loss seen in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "OTC gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the ornithine transcarbamylase (OTC) gene disrupt the first intra-mitochondrial step of the urea cycle. Loss of OTC enzyme activity prevents conversion of carbamoyl phosphate and ornithine to citrulline, so nitrogen cannot be effectively incorporated into urea. As a result, ammonia and other neurotoxic metabolites accumulate in the bloodstream, producing the classic hyperammonemic urea cycle disorder known as OTC deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "The most frequent cause of Mendelian susceptibility to mycobacterial disease is loss-of-function mutation of the IL12RB1 gene, which encodes the β1 chain of the interleukin-12/23 receptor essential for IFN-γ–mediated immunity to mycobacteria. IL12RB1 is situated on the long arm of human chromosome 19 (19q13.1), making chromosome 19 the chromosome whose mutation predisposes to this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Achondrogenesis type II (Langer-Saldino), a lethal congenital skeletal dysplasia and hence a birth defect, is caused by mutations in the COL2A1 gene. COL2A1 encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen, the principal collagen of cartilage; pathogenic variants disrupt cartilage matrix formation, leading to the severe dwarfism and pulmonary hypoplasia characteristic of this form of achondrogenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosome (chromosome 1)",
    "long_answer": "The dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase (DPYD) gene that causes dihydropyrimidine dehydrogenase deficiency is situated on human chromosome 1 (band 1p22), which is an autosomal chromosome rather than a sex chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary spherocytosis caused by defects in erythrocyte membrane protein 4.2 results from mutations in the EPB42 gene. Cytogenetic and sequence mapping place EPB42 at band q15-q21 on human chromosome 15, establishing this chromosome as the location whose mutations lead to the disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CD40LG mutation",
    "long_answer": "X-linked Hyper-IgM syndrome is caused by mutations in the CD40 ligand gene (CD40LG). Because defective CD40L on T-helper cells cannot engage CD40 on B cells, class-switch recombination fails. Consequently IgM remains normal or elevated, while switched isotypes—including IgE—are markedly reduced, giving the characteristic pattern of low IgE with increased IgM."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EMG1",
    "long_answer": "Biallelic mutation of the EMG1 gene (classically the p.Asp86Gly variant) causes Bowen-Conradi syndrome, a ribosomopathy in which defective EMG1 disrupts 18S rRNA processing and small-subunit ribosome assembly. This impairment of ribosome biogenesis produces a lethal syndrome characterized by profound prenatal and postnatal growth failure, microcephaly, and early death."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "Heterozygous mutations in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1-chain of type II collagen, are the established cause of spondylo-epimetaphyseal dysplasia. In this skeletal disorder the vertebrae (spine) and both the metaphyses and epiphyses—the two regions near the ends of long bones—are abnormal, reflecting the critical role of type II collagen in cartilage and growth-plate development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "There are multiple reputable online medical resources that provide accurate, peer-reviewed or expert-reviewed information on rare conditions such as Trevor disease (dysplasia epiphysealis hemimelica). Examples include:  • Orphanet – a European reference portal dedicated to information on rare diseases.  • OMIM (Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man) – a Johns Hopkins/NIH database cataloguing genetic disorders.  • GARD (Genetic and Rare Diseases Information Center) run by the U.S. National Institutes of Health.  • PubMed/MedlinePlus – the National Library of Medicine’s searchable literature and patient-oriented summaries.  • UpToDate, Medscape, BMJ Best Practice, and peer-reviewed journal articles accessible through many platforms.  Because reliable information on Trevor disease is available from these and other sources, the claim that there is only one accurate online resource is incorrect."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Liver transplantation",
    "long_answer": "Severe (primary) hyperoxaluria results from a hepatic enzyme deficiency that causes massive over-production of oxalate, leading to nephrocalcinosis and eventual kidney failure. Replacing the liver corrects the metabolic defect and therefore is the definitive, first-line treatment; when renal function is already lost, it is performed as a combined liver–kidney transplant."
}

{
    "short_answer": "WRN gene",
    "long_answer": "Werner syndrome, often called adult-onset progeria, is a very rare autosomal-recessive disorder characterized by accelerated aging and a markedly increased risk of malignancies, especially osteosarcoma and other sarcomas. The disease is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the WRN gene, which encodes a RecQ family DNA helicase essential for DNA repair and genomic stability; its deficiency leads to chromosomal instability, premature cellular senescence, and the clinical features of premature aging and cancer predisposition."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Giza",
    "long_answer": "The cited investigation sampled feral cats captured in the city of Giza, Egypt, and serological testing (e.g., the modified agglutination test) showed a very high proportion of cats carrying antibodies against Toxoplasma gondii, demonstrating a high seroprevalence in that urban feral population."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KCNJ11",
    "long_answer": "Activating mutations in the KCNJ11 gene, which encodes the Kir6.2 subunit of the pancreatic β-cell ATP-sensitive potassium (KATP) channel, are a well-established cause of permanent neonatal diabetes mellitus (PNDM) and also give rise to the MODY13 subtype of maturity-onset diabetes of the young. Thus the same KCNJ11 mutations underlie both PNDM and a form of MODY."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Mitral regurgitation",
    "long_answer": "In severe mitral valve prolapse, the valve leaflets fail to coapt properly, allowing blood to leak backward from the left ventricle into the left atrium during systole—this is mitral regurgitation. Chronic significant mitral regurgitation increases left-atrial and left-ventricular volume load, eventually causing ventricular dilation, reduced ejection performance, and symptomatic heart failure."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Sheetal Devi",
    "long_answer": "Indian para-archer Sheetal Devi was born with phocomelia, a rare limb-development disorder that can arise from mutations in the ESCO2 gene located on chromosome 8. Despite being born without arms because of this chromosome-8–linked condition, she competes internationally by shooting the bow with her feet and has become one of India’s leading para-archers."
}

{
    "short_answer": "COL2A1",
    "long_answer": "A gain-of-function or loss-of-function mutation in the COL2A1 gene, which encodes the α1 chain of type II collagen, disrupts normal cartilage and growth-plate development. The resulting disorder, spondyloepiphyseal dysplasia congenita, presents clinically with a very short trunk and neck together with shortened limbs—an archetypal short-trunk dwarfism phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TPM2",
    "long_answer": "Distal arthrogryposis, a subtype of arthrogryposis that produces congenital contractures limited mainly to the hands and feet, is most often caused by heterozygous mutations in the TPM2 gene. TPM2 encodes β-tropomyosin, a thin-filament protein of fast skeletal muscle; pathogenic variants alter sarcomeric function, decrease fetal limb movement, and result in the characteristic hand- and foot-contractures seen in this form of arthrogryposis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Marfanoid–progeroid–lipodystrophy syndrome is caused by heterozygous truncating mutations in the FBN1 gene. FBN1 is located at cytogenetic band 15q21.1 on human chromosome 15, making this the chromosome that carries the gene responsible for the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "Aldosterone synthase is encoded by the CYP11B2 gene. Genetic mapping and sequence analysis place CYP11B2 on the long arm of human chromosome 8 (band q24), where it lies adjacent to the related CYP11B1 gene. Hence, the gene encoding aldosterone synthase is located on chromosome 8."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TAS1R1",
    "long_answer": "Umami taste—the savoury flavour elicited by the glutamate moiety of monosodium glutamate (MSG)—is detected by a heterodimeric Class C G-protein-coupled receptor formed by the T1R1 and T1R3 subunits. The subunit that confers specificity for L-glutamate is T1R1, and it is encoded by the TAS1R1 gene. Hence TAS1R1 is the gene that encodes the receptor responsive to the glutamate present in MSG."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Factor XIII’s catalytic A subunit carries the transglutaminase activity that creates ε-(γ-glutamyl)-lysine isopeptide bonds in fibrin, stabilizing blood clots. The human gene encoding this A subunit, F13A1, is located on the short arm of chromosome 6 (6p24-p25)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KHK (fructokinase) mutation",
    "long_answer": "Essential (benign) fructosuria is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the KHK gene, which encodes hepatic fructokinase. Because fructose cannot be phosphorylated, it is freely excreted in urine, where it reacts with copper-based ‘reducing sugar’ dipsticks and other screening assays. These tests are designed to detect glucose, so the presence of fructose produces a false-positive glucosuria result and the patient may be mistakenly labelled as having diabetes. Thus, the KHK mutation underlies a condition that is often misdiagnosed owing to false positives in routine urine tests."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Proline",
    "long_answer": "Properdin contains hydroxyproline residues. During properdin biosynthesis, selected proline residues in the nascent polypeptide are hydroxylated (a vitamin-C–dependent reaction). Therefore, the amino acid whose incorporation and subsequent modification are critical for producing properdin—and whose involvement is highlighted in the context of properdin deficiency—is proline."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 16",
    "long_answer": "The juvenile (Batten disease) variant of neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis is caused by mutations in the CLN3 gene. CLN3 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 16 (16p12.1). Thus, chromosome 16 contains the gene responsible for this juvenile NCL form that can manifest with pathological storage material in muscle as well as neuronal tissue."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis type VI) is an extremely rare lysosomal-storage disorder, with only a few hundred documented cases worldwide. A review of reliable public sources, medical literature, news reports, and the self-disclosures of well-known social-media personalities reveals no YouTuber who has publicly confirmed or been reliably reported to have this condition. Because no verifiable evidence exists that any recognized YouTube content creator has Maroteaux–Lamy syndrome, the correct answer is “no.”"
}

{
    "short_answer": "KIF7",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the kinesin family member 7 (KIF7) gene are the established cause of acrocallosal syndrome. This hereditary ciliopathy is clinically defined by agenesis or severe hypoplasia of the corpus callosum together with pre- or post-axial polydactyly, craniofacial anomalies and neurodevelopmental delay, making KIF7 mutations the canonical genetic explanation for the coexistence of polydactyly and corpus callosum agenesis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 8",
    "long_answer": "11β-hydroxylase deficiency is caused by mutations in the CYP11B1 gene (encoding the 11β-hydroxylase enzyme). The CYP11B1 gene is located at cytogenetic band 8q24.3 on human chromosome 8; thus chromosome 8 carries the gene responsible for this form of congenital adrenal hyperplasia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PKD1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the PKD1 gene, encoding polycystin-1, are the primary cause of autosomal-dominant polycystic kidney disease. Loss of functional polycystin-1 alters signalling and structure in renal tubular epithelial cells, producing cysts that distort the nephron and impair proper renal tubule function."
}

{
    "short_answer": "22q11.2 microdeletion",
    "long_answer": "Tetralogy of Fallot commonly appears in DiGeorge/velocardio-facial syndrome, which is caused by a microdeletion at chromosome 22q11.2. Loss of this chromosomal segment disrupts genes critical for pharyngeal arch and cardiac outflow-tract development, predisposing to conotruncal defects such as Tetralogy of Fallot."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1",
    "long_answer": "Infantile neuronal ceroid lipofuscinosis (INCL) is produced by mutations in the CLN1 gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme palmitoyl-protein thioesterase 1 (PPT1). PPT1 hydrolyzes thioester-linked palmitate from S-acylated proteins; the liberated palmitate is subsequently utilized in sphingolipid pathways, including the de novo synthesis of ceramide. Thus, PPT1 is the enzyme both functionally connected to ceramide production and genetically responsible for INCL."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Tibia",
    "long_answer": "Ectrodactyly with tibia aplasia/hypoplasia is characterized by split hand/foot malformation together with partial or complete absence (aplasia) or under-development (hypoplasia) of the tibia—the larger of the two bones of the lower leg (tibia and fibula). Thus, the tibia is the larger bone that is frequently missing in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "KRIT1",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the KRIT1 gene (also known as CCM1) are a well-documented cause of familial cavernous hemangiomas. KRIT1 encodes a scaffolding protein that helps maintain endothelial junction integrity; loss-of-function variants disrupt vascular stability, leading to the thin-walled, blood-filled channels characteristic of cavernous hemangioma."
}

{
    "short_answer": "GAA gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the GAA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme acid α-glucosidase, cause Pompe disease (glycogen storage disease type II). Lack of this enzyme leads to massive glycogen accumulation in cardiac and respiratory muscles; infants rapidly develop hypertrophic cardiomyopathy and respiratory weakness. Without treatment, most patients succumb to cardiac failure or pneumonia, typically before the age of three."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Hereditary coproporphyria results from deficient activity of coproporphyrinogen III oxidase, encoded by the CPOX gene. Cytogenetic and molecular studies have mapped CPOX to band q11.2-q12 on human chromosome 3, establishing chromosome 3 as the chromosomal location responsible for this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "607465",
    "long_answer": "Congenital dyserythropoietic anemia type I is most commonly caused by mutations in the CDAN1 gene (codanin-1). In the Online Mendelian Inheritance in Man (OMIM) database, the entry for this gene is *607465, which is the OMIM number specifically assigned to CDAN1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Human enolase deficiencies reported to date are caused by mutations in the genes that encode the α-enolase (ENO1) or β-enolase (ENO3) isoforms. ENO1 is located on chromosome 1p36.23, and ENO3 is located on chromosome 17p13.3. No enolase-coding gene associated with a deficiency phenotype resides on chromosome 2. Therefore, the statement that the enolase deficiency gene is found on chromosome 2 is not supported by established genetic evidence."
}

{
    "short_answer": "BCOR",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the X-linked BCOR (BCL6 corepressor) gene give rise to oculo-facio-cardio-dental (OFCD) syndrome. OFCD is allelic—and therefore genetically related—to Lenz microphthalmia syndrome, as both disorders are produced by BCOR mutations. Disruption of BCOR’s corepressor function affects eye, cranio-facial, cardiac and dental development and can include intellectual disability in affected individuals."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The condition known as X-linked sideroblastic anemia with spinocerebellar ataxia (XLSA/A) is caused by pathogenic variants in the ABCB7 gene, which is mapped to the Xq13 region. Because ABCB7 is located on the X chromosome, that chromosome carries the gene responsible for the combined phenotype of sideroblastic anemia and spinocerebellar ataxia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Cementum",
    "long_answer": "Cementum is the calcified layer covering the tooth root to which the periodontal ligament fibers are anchored. It is secreted by cementoblasts, which remain viable on the root surface and can lay down additional layers of cementum if the tooth root is damaged, thus aiding repair and continued ligament attachment."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Juvenile hyaline fibromatosis (also called hyaline fibromatosis syndrome) is an autosomal-recessive disorder caused by biallelic pathogenic variants in the ANTXR2 gene (formerly called CMG2). The ANTXR2 gene is located on chromosome 4 at band 4q21.21, not on chromosome 3. Therefore chromosome 3 does not contain the gene responsible for juvenile hyaline fibromatosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher disease, marked by pathological accumulation of glucocerebroside in tissues (including the kidneys), is caused by mutations in the GBA gene encoding lysosomal β-glucocerebrosidase. The GBA gene resides on human chromosome 1q21, so chromosome 1 is the location that harbors the causative gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "Pitt–Hopkins syndrome is caused by haplo-insufficiency or pathogenic variants in the TCF4 gene (Transcription Factor 4), which is mapped to cytogenetic band 18q21.2 on human chromosome 18. Therefore, the disease-associated gene resides on chromosome 18."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Very long-chain acyl-coenzyme A dehydrogenase (VLCAD) deficiency results from mutations in the ACADVL gene. The human ACADVL gene is mapped to chromosome 17 (cytogenetic band 17p13), so all pathogenic variants causing VLCAD deficiency are located on chromosome 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "LDLR gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Mutations in the LDLR gene, located on chromosome 19p13.2, impair the function of the LDL receptor that clears low-density lipoprotein (LDL) particles from the bloodstream. Loss or reduction of receptor activity leads to familial hypercholesterolemia, a metabolic disorder characterized by markedly elevated LDL cholesterol levels and increased risk of premature atherosclerotic cardiovascular disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Alpha-mannosidosis results from mutations in the MAN2B1 gene, which encodes lysosomal α-mannosidase. The MAN2B1 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 19 (19p13.2), so chromosome 19 carries the gene whose defects cause this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Enolase deficiency in humans is caused by pathogenic variants in the ENO1 gene, which encodes the α-enolase isoform essential for the glycolytic conversion of 2-phosphoglycerate to phosphoenolpyruvate. ENO1 is located on the short arm of human chromosome 1 (1p36), so mutations on chromosome 1 underlie the glucose-metabolism defect seen in enolase deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "PAX5",
    "long_answer": "Reed-Sternberg cells in classical Hodgkin’s lymphoma, including the Hodgkin type that can arise during Richter’s transformation of chronic lymphocytic leukaemia, almost always retain weak nuclear expression of the B-cell lineage transcription factor PAX5 (paired-box gene 5). Detection of PAX5 is a standard diagnostic feature used to confirm the B-cell origin of these cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Vitamin B3 (niacin)",
    "long_answer": "Pellagra is a disease characterized by dermatitis, diarrhea, and dementia; it arises from a deficiency of vitamin B3—also called niacin. Therapeutically, the condition is treated by replenishing the missing vitamin, most commonly using its amide form, nicotinamide, which is rapidly converted in the body to the active coenzymes NAD⁺/NADP⁺. Thus, deficiency of vitamin B3 is the cause of the disease alleviated by nicotinamide supplementation."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosomes 12 and 17",
    "long_answer": "White sponge nevus is an autosomal-dominant disorder caused by mutations in the epithelial keratin genes KRT4 and KRT13. KRT4 is mapped to chromosome 12q13, and KRT13 is mapped to chromosome 17q21–q22. Therefore, the pathogenic mutations involve chromosomes 12 and 17."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 13",
    "long_answer": "The RB1 tumor-suppressor gene, whose inactivation is frequently associated with osteosarcoma as well as retinoblastoma, is located on human chromosome 13 at band q14."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The CUL7 gene, located at 6p21.1 on human chromosome 6, is responsible for most cases of 3-M syndrome when mutated. The CUL7 protein physically associates with the tumour-suppressor p53 and is needed for efficient p53-mediated transcriptional activation and apoptosis; thus chromosome 6 carries the gene whose loss causes 3-M syndrome and that operates as a p53 cofactor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CAMT-to-MDS progression",
    "long_answer": "Congenital amegakaryocytic thrombocytopenia is not only a platelet-failure disorder; over time many patients develop trilineage marrow failure that can evolve into myelodysplastic syndrome (and sometimes AML). Thus CAMT is a congenital condition that predisposes to, and can clinically progress into, MDS."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Alcohol (ethanol)",
    "long_answer": "Ethanol, the active drug in alcoholic beverages, is a well-established carcinogen. Chronic alcohol consumption markedly increases the risk of laryngeal cancer and is also causally linked to cancers of the oral cavity, pharynx, esophagus, liver, colorectum and female breast, making it a significant multi-site cancer risk factor."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Menkes disease",
    "long_answer": "Menkes disease (also called Menkes kinky hair syndrome) is a genetic disorder of copper transport caused by ATP7A mutations. The resulting systemic copper deficiency leads to characteristic sparse, brittle, twisted (pili torti) hair, making this hair abnormality directly associated with the underlying copper metabolism disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FGFR3 mutation",
    "long_answer": "Crouzon syndrome with acanthosis nigricans is caused by a recurrent missense mutation (Ala391Glu) in the FGFR3 gene. This activating FGFR3 mutation drives premature fusion of cranial sutures (craniosynostosis) and the skin changes characteristic of acanthosis nigricans, linking the two features within the same genetic syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "The protein that stabilizes fibrin clots is the A-subunit of coagulation factor XIII (factor XIIIa). Its gene, F13A1, is expressed by the factor XIIIa-positive dermal dendritic cells that give rise to dermatofibromas. The F13A1 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p24.2), so chromosome 6 carries the gene associated with this clot-stabilizing protein and the formation of dermatofibromas."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "The Dystrophin (DMD) gene resides on the X chromosome, specifically at the cytogenetic band Xp21.2-p21.1. Because it is X-linked, mutations in this gene cause Duchenne and Becker muscular dystrophies to follow an X-linked inheritance pattern, predominantly affecting males."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 2",
    "long_answer": "Schimke immuno-osseous dysplasia (Schimke syndrome) is caused by biallelic loss-of-function mutations in the SMARCAL1 gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 2q35 on human chromosome 2. Therefore, the gene associated with Schimke syndrome resides on chromosome 2."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Erich Urbach",
    "long_answer": "The rare disorder lipoid proteinosis—now known to result from loss-of-function mutations in the ECM1 gene—was first described in 1929 by Austrian dermatologist Erich Urbach (with later co-description by Camillo Wiethe). His original report of ‘lipoidosis cutis et mucosae’ laid the clinical foundation for what is today called Urbach–Wiethe disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HSD17B3",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the HSD17B3 gene lead to 17β-hydroxysteroid dehydrogenase type 3 deficiency. This enzyme performs the key step of converting androstenedione into testosterone within Leydig cells of the testes, so its absence markedly reduces testicular testosterone production and causes a 46,XY disorder of sex development."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Dyschromatosis symmetrica hereditaria is caused by pathogenic variants in the ADAR (adenosine deaminase, RNA-specific) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 1 (cytogenetic band 1q21). Mutations in this chromosome-1 gene disrupt RNA editing in melanocytes, leading to the characteristic mottled pigmentation of DSH."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Inherited Protein S deficiency results from mutations in the PROS1 gene, which is located on the long arm of human chromosome 3 (region 3q11-q11.2). Alterations in this chromosomal locus impair synthesis of functional Protein S, leading to the clinical coagulation disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 1",
    "long_answer": "Gaucher disease results from mutations in the GBA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme β-glucocerebrosidase. Loss of this enzyme leads to glucocerebroside accumulation in macrophages within bone marrow and other tissues. The GBA gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 1 (region 1q21), so chromosome 1 carries the disease-causing gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Pulmonary embolism",
    "long_answer": "Congenital dysfibrinogenemia can predispose to venous thrombo-embolism; when thrombi lodge in the pulmonary arteries as pulmonary emboli, they can chronically obstruct the pulmonary vascular bed and progress to chronic thromboembolic pulmonary hypertension. Thus, pulmonary embolism is a recognized complication of dysfibrinogenemia that can ultimately cause pulmonary hypertension."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MECP2 duplication syndrome",
    "long_answer": "Duplication of the MECP2 gene, which is located on chromosome Xq28, produces MECP2 duplication syndrome. This X-linked disorder is characterized by severe intellectual disability, profound developmental delay, hypotonia and other neurologic deficits, establishing the causal link between the Xq28 gene duplication and the clinical phenotype."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Autosomal recessive StAR mutations",
    "long_answer": "Lipoid congenital adrenal hyperplasia occurs when both copies of the steroidogenic acute regulatory (StAR) gene carry loss-of-function mutations. This autosomal recessive defect blocks cholesterol transport into mitochondria, crippling steroid hormone synthesis. In 46,XY fetuses the lack of testicular androgen production leads to undervirilized (female-appearing) external genitalia, while both sexes develop severe adrenal insufficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 7",
    "long_answer": "The gene that causes cystic fibrosis is CFTR (Cystic Fibrosis Transmembrane Conductance Regulator). It is located on the long arm (q arm) of chromosome 7 at cytogenetic band 7q31.2. Mutations in this chromosome-7 gene disrupt chloride ion transport in epithelial cells, producing the characteristic pulmonary and gastrointestinal manifestations of cystic fibrosis."
}

{
    "short_answer": "X chromosome",
    "long_answer": "Classic ocular albinism (Nettleship-Falls type) is inherited in an X-linked pattern because the causative OA1/GPR143 gene is situated at Xp22 on the X chromosome; therefore, the genes responsible for ocular albinism are located on the X chromosome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 19",
    "long_answer": "Myotonic dystrophy type 1 (DM1) is caused by a CTG trinucleotide-repeat expansion in the DMPK (dystrophia myotonica protein kinase) gene. The DMPK gene is located at cytogenetic band 19q13.3 on human chromosome 19, making chromosome 19 the chromosomal location responsible for DM1."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Familial chordoma is caused by germ-line duplication or activating variants of the brachyury gene (TBXT, also called T). This gene is mapped to cytogenetic band 6q27; hence, the predisposing locus for familial chordoma resides on human chromosome 6."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Carey-Fineman-Ziter syndrome is now known to result from biallelic loss-of-function variants in the MYMK gene (also called TMEM8C, encoding the muscle-specific fusogen myomaker). The human MYMK gene is mapped to the long arm of chromosome 9 (9q34), so chromosome 9 contains the gene responsible for this syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "National Center for Biotechnology Information",
    "long_answer": "The National Center for Biotechnology Information (NCBI) hosts and curates a suite of integrated biological databases—such as Gene, ClinVar, OMIM, and GenBank—that catalogue sequence, variant, and clinical data on genes implicated in disorders like MELAS (e.g., MT-TL1, MT-ND5). Researchers can query NCBI to retrieve up-to-date molecular and clinical information relevant to the genetic basis of MELAS syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Benign childhood epilepsy with centrotemporal spikes (also called Rolandic epilepsy) has been mapped to a susceptibility locus on chromosome 11p13. Variants within the ELP4 region on 11p13 are consistently associated with the centrotemporal spikes seen on EEG, making chromosome 11 the chromosome linked to this often benign genetic epilepsy syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "Hydrolethalus syndrome is classically caused by recessive mutations in the HYLS1 gene. The HYLS1 gene is located on the long arm of human chromosome 11 (11q24), so chromosome 11 harbors the causative gene for Hydrolethalus syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "no",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the NF1 gene cause Neurofibromatosis type 1 (NF1). The core clinical hallmarks of NF1 include café-au-lait macules, skinfold freckling, cutaneous and plexiform neurofibromas, Lisch nodules of the iris, skeletal dysplasia, learning difficulties, and an increased risk of certain tumors (e.g., optic pathway gliomas, malignant peripheral nerve-sheath tumors). While some NF1 complications—such as intracranial tumors or gastrointestinal neurofibromas—can secondarily lead to nausea or vomiting in an individual patient, vomiting is not a defining or characteristic feature used to recognize or diagnose the NF1 disorder. Therefore the statement that an NF1 gene mutation results in “a disorder characterized by vomiting” is not accurate according to established biomedical consensus."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 15",
    "long_answer": "Tay-Sachs disease is a lysosomal storage disorder in which progressive accumulation of GM2 ganglioside causes relentless damage to neurons in the brain and spinal cord. The disease results from loss-of-function mutations in the HEXA gene, which encodes the α-subunit of β-hexosaminidase A. The HEXA gene is located on chromosome 15 (band 15q23–q24); therefore, chromosome 15 carries the gene whose mutation produces this neurodegenerative lysosomal storage disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 3",
    "long_answer": "Protein S deficiency results from pathogenic variants in the PROS1 gene, which encodes the anticoagulant protein S. The PROS1 gene is located on the short arm of human chromosome 3 (region 3p11.1-q11.2); therefore, chromosome 3 is the chromosome associated with Protein S deficiency."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Arylsulfatase G",
    "long_answer": "Sanfilippo syndrome type E (mucopolysaccharidosis IIIE) is produced by recessive mutations in the ARSG gene. These mutations abolish or markedly reduce the activity of the lysosomal enzyme arylsulfatase G, leading to impaired degradation of heparan sulfate and the characteristic neurodegenerative features of Sanfilippo disease."
}

{
    "short_answer": "TAZ gene",
    "long_answer": "Mutation of the X-linked TAZ (tafazzin) gene causes Barth syndrome, a congenital disorder of cardiolipin remodeling that typically presents with dilated or hypertrophic cardiomyopathy in infancy and, in adolescence/adulthood, primary testicular failure leading to hypergonadotropic hypogonadism."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis",
    "long_answer": "Amyotrophic lateral sclerosis (ALS) is a progressive motor-neuron degenerative disease that weakens the bulbar and oropharyngeal muscles. This impairs the swallowing (dysphagia) and cough reflexes, so food or saliva can be aspirated into the lungs, frequently resulting in aspiration pneumonia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "CHD7",
    "long_answer": "Pathogenic variants in the CHD7 gene, located on chromosome 8q12.2, are the established cause of CHARGE syndrome. This multisystem disorder frequently presents with craniofacial anomalies that include cleft lip and/or cleft palate, in addition to the characteristic coloboma, heart defects, choanal atresia, growth-retardation, genital, and ear anomalies. Thus, mutation of CHD7 on chromosome 8 is the recognised genetic basis for the cleft lip/palate–containing syndrome described."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
    "long_answer": "Immunodeficiency–centromeric instability–facial anomalies (ICF) syndrome is caused by loss-of-function mutations in the DNA methyltransferase gene DNMT3B. The human DNMT3B locus is mapped to band q11.2 of chromosome 20, so the disease-causing gene resides on chromosome 20."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 12",
    "long_answer": "Type 1 Kabuki syndrome is produced by heterozygous loss-of-function mutations in the KMT2D (formerly MLL2) gene, which is mapped to cytogenetic band 12q13.12 on human chromosome 12. Thus, chromosome 12 carries the pathogenic gene responsible for this form of syndromic microphthalmia."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Follicular hyperkeratosis",
    "long_answer": "Night blindness is a hallmark of vitamin A deficiency. Lack of vitamin A also disrupts normal keratinization in the epidermis, producing follicular hyperkeratosis (phrynoderma)—a rough, ‘goose-flesh’ eruption of dry, gritty papules around hair follicles on the extensor surfaces of the skin."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The TCOF1 gene, mutations of which cause Treacher-Collins craniofacial abnormalities, is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 5 (region 5q32-q33.1)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "EYA1",
    "long_answer": "Branchio-oto-renal (BOR) syndrome, a disorder of first- and second-branchial-arch development, is most commonly produced by heterozygous mutations in the EYA1 gene. EYA1 encodes a nuclear transcription-factor co-activator that partners with SIX homeobox proteins to regulate gene programs essential for ear, branchial arch and renal morphogenesis; loss-of-function variants disrupt this transcriptional control and give rise to the characteristic branchial clefts, ear anomalies and renal defects of the syndrome."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 5",
    "long_answer": "The autosomal dominant form of craniometaphyseal dysplasia is produced by pathogenic variants in the ANKH gene (ankylosis, progressive homolog), which is situated on the short arm of human chromosome 5 (5p15)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Optic nerve (CN II)",
    "long_answer": "Compression of the optic nerve, the second cranial nerve, damages its axons and blood supply, producing optic neuropathy. This neurological disorder manifests as visual field loss, reduced acuity, or even blindness, as seen with pituitary adenomas, meningiomas, or orbital masses that mechanically compress CN II."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 17",
    "long_answer": "Most cases of osteogenesis imperfecta result from mutations in the COL1A1 gene encoding the α1-chain of type I collagen; COL1A1 is located on the long arm of human chromosome 17 (region 17q21–q22)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "HBB",
    "long_answer": "The HBB gene encodes the β-globin chains of hemoglobin. Mutations in HBB (such as the Glu6Val substitution that produces hemoglobin S) cause sickle cell disease and β-thalassemia. These hemoglobinopathies distort or reduce hemoglobin, directly impairing the oxygen-carrying capacity of red blood cells."
}

{
    "short_answer": "SLC26A3 gene mutation",
    "long_answer": "Loss-of-function mutations in the SLC26A3 gene, which encodes the intestinal chloride–bicarbonate exchanger (also called DRA), abolish chloride absorption in the distal ileum and colon. This defect produces congenital chloride diarrhea, an autosomal-recessive disorder that presents in newborns with profuse, watery diarrhea and severe electrolyte disturbances."
}

{
    "short_answer": "FANCA",
    "long_answer": "Fanconi anemia, a congenital hypoplastic (aplastic) anemia, is most commonly caused by biallelic mutations in the FANCA gene. Loss of FANCA disrupts the Fanconi anemia DNA-repair pathway, making cells hypersensitive to DNA interstrand cross-linking agents (e.g., mitomycin C) and predisposing affected individuals to acute leukemia and solid tumors. Thus, FANCA links congenital anemia with both cancer susceptibility and cellular sensitivity to DNA crosslinks."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The gene most frequently mutated in hypertrophic cardiomyopathy is MYBPC3 (myosin-binding protein C, cardiac type). MYBPC3 resides on the short arm of human chromosome 11 (11p11.2). Pathogenic variants in this gene account for the largest proportion of familial HCM cases, making chromosome 11 the chromosome that harbors the most commonly mutated HCM gene."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 9",
    "long_answer": "Familial dysautonomia (Riley–Day syndrome) is caused by recessive mutations in the IKBKAP (also called ELP1) gene, which is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 9 (region 9q31). Therefore, the gene responsible for familial dysautonomia resides on chromosome 9."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 4",
    "long_answer": "Hurler syndrome (mucopolysaccharidosis I) is caused by mutations in the IDUA gene, which encodes the lysosomal enzyme α-L-iduronidase required for the degradation of glycosaminoglycans. The IDUA gene is located at band p16.3 of human chromosome 4; therefore chromosome 4 carries the gene whose defect leads to this glycosaminoglycan-breakdown disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "MYH9",
    "long_answer": "May-Hegglin anomaly belongs to the spectrum of MYH9-related macrothrombocytopenias. Pathogenic variants in the MYH9 gene, which encodes non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA, disrupt platelet cytoskeletal function, producing giant platelets, thrombocytopenia, and Döhle-like leukocyte inclusions characteristic of this disorder."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 22",
    "long_answer": "Epstein syndrome belongs to the group of MYH9-related disorders. The causative gene, MYH9 (encoding non-muscle myosin heavy chain IIA), is mapped to the long arm of human chromosome 22 at band q12.3. Hence, chromosome 22 carries the gene whose mutation produces Epstein syndrome’s characteristic macrothrombocytopenia, nephritis, and hearing loss."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 6",
    "long_answer": "Studies of peanut-allergic cohorts have repeatedly linked variation in HLA class II genes—especially HLA-DQB1 and related alleles—to the IgE-mediated immune response that drives peanut allergy. These HLA genes reside within the major histocompatibility complex on the short arm of human chromosome 6 (6p21). Thus, chromosome 6 is the chromosomal location of the gene(s) that modulate the immune response in peanut allergies."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 11",
    "long_answer": "The WT1 tumor-suppressor gene, whose mutation accounts for a subset of Wilms’ tumor cases, is located on the short arm of chromosome 11 (region 11p13)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 18",
    "long_answer": "The NPC1 gene, mutations of which cause Niemann-Pick disease type C—a lysosomal storage disorder characterized by accumulation of unesterified cholesterol in late endosomes/lysosomes—is located on human chromosome 18 (band 18q11-q12)."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Annonaceae",
    "long_answer": "The custard-apple or Annonaceae family (e.g., Annona muricata, soursop) is epidemiologically linked to an atypical parkinsonism that presents with supranuclear gaze palsy and other clinical and neuropathological features closely resembling progressive supranuclear palsy. The association is attributed to the mitochondrial complex-I inhibitor annonacin concentrated in Annonaceae fruits and herbal infusions."
}

{
    "short_answer": "Chromosome 20",
